            "heading": "The Mystery of the Turmeric Stain on a White Napkin",
            "headingHinglish": "Safed rumaal par haldi ka jaadu",
            "body": "It was Sunday afternoon lunch. Leo was sitting beside his aunt, Dr. Clara, enjoying a warm bowl of yellow curry and rice. While chatting, his spoon slipped and a drop of golden-yellow curry landed squarely on his crisp white cotton napkin, leaving a **bright yellow stain**.\n\nLeo hurried over to the sink and rubbed a bar of **hand soap** directly onto the spot. The moment the soapy lather touched the turmeric stain, Leo's eyes widened in disbelief: the bright yellow spot suddenly turned a **deep reddish-brown**!\n\n\"Aunt Clara, look! The moment I rubbed soap on it, the yellow turmeric stain turned reddish-brown! Did I ruin the napkin forever?\" Leo asked nervously.\n\nDr. Clara, who worked as a quality-control chemist at a pharmaceutical lab, walked over to the sink with a smile. First, she had Leo rinse the napkin thoroughly with plenty of running water, and then she brought a **small wedge of fresh lemon** from the kitchen and squeezed two drops of juice onto the reddish-brown spot. In the blink of an eye, the reddish-brown colour vanished and the stain turned back to **yellow**!\n\nLeo was amazed: \"Reddish-brown with soap, and back to yellow with lemon juice? Does turmeric have a built-in colour switch inside it?\"\n\nDr. Clara chuckled, \"You can think of it just like a switch, Leo! In chemistry, substances like this are called **indicators**. Soap contains a **base** and lemon juice contains an **acid**, and turmeric changes its colour to announce which one it has just met.\" Let's start with Leo's napkin and understand how this entire detective game works.",
            "bodyHinglish": "Sunday dopahar ka khana chal raha tha. Shaunak apni chachi Ms. Chitnis ke paas baith kar garam-garam dal-chawal kha raha tha. Baaton-baaton mein chammach hila aur peeli dal ki ek boond Shaunak ke safed suti rumaal (cotton handkerchief) par gir gayi. Rumaal par **chamakdar peela daag** lag gaya.\n\nShaunak turant washbasin ki taraf bhaaga aur daag par **nahane ka sabun** ragad diya. Jaise hi sabun ka jhaag haldi ke daag se chhua, Shaunak ki aankhein phati reh gayin: peela daag achanak **gehra laal-bhoora (reddish-brown)** ho gaya!\n\n\"Chachi, dekhiye! Sabun lagate hi peeli haldi laal kaise ho gayi? Rumaal to aur kharab ho gaya!\" Shaunak ne ghabra kar kaha.\n\nMs. Chitnis, jo ek dawa-company ki quality lab mein kaam karti thin, hanste hue washbasin ke paas aayin. Unhone pahle rumaal ko khub saare paani se dhulwaya, aur phir rasoi se **nimbu ka ek chhota tukda** laakar us laal-bhoore daag par do boond ras nichod diya. Palak jhapakte hi laal-bhoora rang gayab ho gaya aur daag wapas **peela** ho gaya!\n\nShaunak hairaan tha: \"Sabun se laal-bhoora, aur nimbu se wapas peela? Chachi, haldi ke andar koi switch laga hai kya?\"\n\nMs. Chitnis muskurayin, \"Switch hi samajh lo, beta! Chemistry mein aisi cheezon ko **indicator** kehte hain. Sabun mein **base** hai aur nimbu mein **acid**, aur haldi apna rang badal kar chugli kar deti hai ki uske saamne kaun aaya hai.\" Chalo, Shaunak ke rumaal se shuru karke is poore khel ko samajhte hain."
