          {
            "heading": "Part A: Kitchen Acids and Bases vs. Laboratory Chemicals",
            "headingHinglish": "Part A: Rasoi ke acids aur bases banaam lab ke chemicals",
            "body": "Historically, people first recognised acids and bases by their **taste** and **feel**:\n\n1. **Acids:** The word *acid* comes from the Latin word *acidus*, meaning **sour**. Lemons, oranges, tamarind, unripe green apples, plain yoghurt (curd), and vinegar all owe their tangy sourness to natural (organic) acids.\n2. **Bases:** If you rub a pinch of baking soda between wet fingers, it feels **soapy or slippery**, and safe household bases taste **bitter**.\n\n> **The Golden Safety Rule:** Tasting lemon juice or yoghurt in the kitchen is completely normal, but **you must NEVER taste or touch any chemical from an unknown bottle or in a science laboratory with bare hands!** Laboratory mineral acids (such as HCl, H₂SO₄, and HNO₃) and strong bases (such as NaOH and KOH) burn skin and tissue severely—they are **corrosive**. That is why chemists rely on **indicators**, never on their tongue or fingers, to identify chemicals.\n\n### Which Natural Acid Is Found Where?\n\nThis reference table is worth memorising because it links everyday foods and stings to the specific organic acids inside them:\n\n| Natural Source | Acid Present | Notable Fact |\n|---|---|---|\n| Vinegar | Acetic acid (CH₃COOH) | Gives pickles and salad dressings their sharp tang |\n| Lemons and Oranges (Citrus fruits) | Citric acid | Provides a sour taste alongside Vitamin C |\n| Tamarind and Grapes | Tartaric acid | Also used as the dry acid component in baking powder |\n| Tomatoes and Spinach | Oxalic acid | Useful for removing stubborn rust stains from fabrics |\n| Sour milk or Yoghurt (Curd) | Lactic acid | Produced by bacteria fermenting milk sugar (lactose) |\n| Apples | Malic acid | Present in higher amounts in crisp, unripe apples |\n| Red ant sting and Stinging nettle leaf | Methanoic acid (Formic acid) | Causes the sharp burning pain and swelling after a sting |\n\nAnd here are some familiar **bases** along with their chemical formulas (built using the Class 9 Chapter 3 criss-cross rule, [language-of-chemistry-part5.md](../../../class9/chemistry/language-of-chemistry/language-of-chemistry-part5.md)):\n\n- **Sodium hydroxide (NaOH)** and **Potassium hydroxide (KOH):** used in making soap and paper (extremely strong and corrosive).\n- **Calcium hydroxide, Ca(OH)₂ (Slaked lime):** used for whitewashing walls and neutralising acidic soil.\n- **Magnesium hydroxide, Mg(OH)₂ (Milk of Magnesia):** a gentle white suspension taken as an antacid to relieve stomach acidity.\n- **Ammonium hydroxide, NH₄OH:** used in many household glass-cleaning sprays (has a sharp, pungent smell; keep away from eyes).",
            "bodyHinglish": "Shuruat mein logon ne cheezon ko unke **swaad** aur **chhune ke ehsaas** se pehchana tha:\n\n1. **Acids:** Shabd *acid* Latin shabd *acidus* se aaya hai, jiska matlab hai **khatta (sour)**. Nimbu, santra, imli, kachcha aam, dahi aur sirka (vinegar), in sab mein prakritik (organic) acids hote hain.\n2. **Bases:** Khaane wala meetha soda (baking soda) agar ungliyon ke beech paani ke saath maslo to **chikna (soapy/slippery)** lagta hai, aur swaad mein **kadwa (bitter)** hota hai.\n\n> **Sabse zaroori suraksha niyam (Safety Rule):** Rasoi mein nimbu ya dahi chakhna aam baat hai, par **science lab mein ya kisi anjaan botal ki kisi bhi cheez ko KABHI chakhna (taste) ya nange haath se chhuna mana hai!** Lab ke mineral acids (jaise HCl, H₂SO₄, HNO₃) aur bases (jaise NaOH, KOH) chamdi ko jala dete hain (**corrosive** hote hain). Isliye pehchaan ke liye hum jeebh ya ungli nahi, balki **indicators** ka upyog karte hain.\n\n### Kudrat ke khazaane mein kaun-sa acid kahan milta hai?\n\nYe table yaad rakhne mein bahut kaam aati hai, kyunki ye rozmarra ki cheezon ko unke andar ke organic acid se jodti hai:\n\n| Prakritik srot (Source) | Usmein paya jaane wala Acid | Khaas baat |\n|---|---|---|\n| Sirka (Vinegar) | Acetic acid (CH₃COOH) | Achaar aur chaat mein khattapan deta hai |\n| Nimbu aur Santra (Citrus fruits) | Citric acid | Vitamin C ke saath-saath khatta swaad |\n| Imli (Tamarind) aur Angoor | Tartaric acid | Baking powder banane mein bhi kaam aata hai |\n| Tamatar (Tomato) aur Palak | Oxalic acid | Kapdon par lage zang ke daag hatane mein bhi upyogi |\n| Khatta doodh ya Dahi (Curd) | Lactic acid | Doodh ke lactose se bacteria banate hain |\n| Seb (Apple) | Malic acid | Kache seb mein zyada hota hai |\n| Laal cheenti (Ant sting) aur Bichchhu-booti (Nettle leaf) | Methanoic acid (Formic acid) | Dank lagne par jalan aur dard isi acid se hota hai |\n\nAur kuch jaane-pehchane **bases** aur unke formula (Class 9 Ch 3 ke criss-cross niyam se, [language-of-chemistry-part5.md](../../../class9/chemistry/language-of-chemistry/language-of-chemistry-part5.md)):\n\n- **Sodium hydroxide (NaOH)** aur **Potassium hydroxide (KOH):** sabun aur paper banane mein (bahut tez/corrosive).\n- **Calcium hydroxide, Ca(OH)₂ (Slaked lime / bujha chuna):** paan mein lagne wala chuna aur deewaron ki safedi (whitewash).\n- **Magnesium hydroxide, Mg(OH)₂ (Milk of magnesia):** pet ki acidity door karne wali safed dawa (antacid).\n- **Ammonium hydroxide, NH₄OH:** khidki ke kaanch saaf karne wale kuch sprays mein (tez gandh hoti hai, aankh se door rakhein)."
          },
          {
            "heading": "Part B: Colour-Changing Detectives: Natural and Synthetic Indicators",
            "headingHinglish": "Part B: Rang badalne wale jasoos: Prakritik aur Synthetic Indicators",
            "body": "When we cannot taste a liquid, we add a drop of an **indicator** to the solution—or dip a strip of indicator paper into a drop of it. Indicators fall into two broad categories: **natural indicators** extracted from plants, and **synthetic indicators** manufactured in laboratories.\n\n### 1. Litmus: The Most Famous Natural Indicator\n\nLitmus is a **purple** dye extracted from **lichens** (small composite organisms formed by a partnership between a fungus and an alga, growing on rocks and tree bark). When a solution is neither acidic nor basic (such as pure distilled water), litmus keeps its natural **purple** colour. For convenience in the lab, litmus is supplied as paper strips pre-dyed in two colours: **Blue litmus paper** and **Red litmus paper**.\n\n- An **acid** turns **blue** litmus **red**. *(Easy memory trick: **A**cid → **B**lue to **R**ed, or **ABR**—red is the colour of danger!)*\n- A **base** turns **red** litmus **blue**. *(Trick: **B**ase → **B**lue!)*\n- In a **neutral** solution (such as pure water or common salt solution), neither blue litmus nor red litmus changes colour.\n\n### 2. Turmeric, Hibiscus (China Rose), and Red Cabbage\n\n- **Turmeric:** Its natural colour is **yellow**. In both acidic and neutral solutions it **remains yellow**, but the moment it meets a **base**, it turns **reddish-brown**! That is exactly what happened on Leo's napkin: soap (a base) turned the turmeric stain reddish-brown, and lemon juice (an acid) neutralised the soap's base and restored the yellow colour.\n- **Hibiscus (China rose) extract:** Steeping red hibiscus petals in warm water produces a pale pink extract. It turns **dark pink (magenta)** in an **acid** and **green** in a **base**!\n- **Red cabbage extract:** Naturally purplish-red. It turns **red** in an **acid** and **greenish-yellow** in a **base**.\n\n> **Activity 1.1 (At home with an adult): Make a Turmeric Greeting Card.**\n> Mix a spoonful of turmeric powder with a little water to make a thick yellow paste. Spread it evenly over a sheet of white card stock and let it dry—your **turmeric indicator paper** is ready! Now dissolve a little **laundry soap** in a small bowl of water (do not drink it or bring it near your eyes). Dip a cotton swab into the soapy water and paint a flower on the yellow card: wherever the soap touches, a **reddish-brown** flower blooms! Next, dip a fresh cotton swab in **lemon juice** and touch the centre of the reddish-brown flower: that spot immediately turns back to **yellow**. Wash your hands when finished.\n\n### 3. Two Essential Lab Synthetic Indicators: Phenolphthalein and Methyl Orange\n\nIn school chemistry laboratories, these two synthetic indicators are used all the time:\n\n- **Phenolphthalein:** Its solution is **colourless** like water. In an **acidic** or **neutral** solution it **remains colourless**, but as soon as it meets a **base**, it turns a vibrant **pink**!\n- **Methyl orange:** Its neutral solution is **orange**. In an **acid**, it turns **red (or pinkish-red)**, and in a **base**, it turns **yellow**!\n\nHere is a complete summary chart of all 5 major indicators at a glance:\n\n```\n   INDICATOR COLOUR WHEEL (ACID vs. NEUTRAL vs. BASE)\n\n   Indicator          In Acid            In Neutral         In Base\n   --------------------------------------------------------------------------\n   Litmus             Red                Purple             Blue\n   Turmeric           Yellow             Yellow             Reddish-brown\n   Hibiscus           Dark Pink/Magenta  Pale Pink          Green\n   Phenolphthalein    Colourless         Colourless         Pink\n   Methyl Orange      Red                Orange             Yellow\n```\n\n> **Figure 1.1** – Colours of the five main indicators in acidic, neutral, and basic media. Notice that both turmeric and phenolphthalein show the same colour in acidic and neutral solutions (yellow and colourless, respectively), so telling an acid apart from a neutral liquid using only one of them requires a clever two-step trick (which we will see in Part D).",
            "bodyHinglish": "Jab hum chakh nahi sakte, to hum ghol mein ek boond **indicator** daalte hain, ya indicator laga kaagaz dubote hain. Indicator do tarah ke hote hain: **prakritik (natural)** jo paudhon se milte hain, aur **manav-nirmit (synthetic)** jo lab mein bante hain.\n\n### 1. Litmus: sabse mashhoor prakritik indicator\n\nLitmus **lichen** (fungus aur shaival ki saajhedaari se bane chhote jeev, jo chattano aur pedon ki chhaal par ugte hain) se nikala gaya ek **baingani (purple)** rang ka ark (dye) hai. Jab na koi acid ho na base (jaise shuddh paani), to litmus ka asali rang **purple** hi rehta hai. Lab mein suvidha ke liye ise do rang ke kaagaz ki pattiyon (strips) mein banaya jaata hai: **Blue litmus paper** aur **Red litmus paper**.\n\n- **Acid** neele (blue) litmus ko **laal (red)** kar deta hai. *(Yaad rakhne ki chhoti trick: **A**cid → **B**lue to **R**ed, yaani **ABR**; khatre ka rang laal!)*\n- **Base** laal (red) litmus ko **neela (blue)** kar deta hai. *(Trick: **B**ase → **B**lue!)*\n- Agar **neutral** ghol (jaise shuddh paani ya namak ka paani) ho, to na neela litmus badalta hai, na laal litmus.\n\n### 2. Haldi (Turmeric), Gudhal (China Rose) aur Lal Patta-gobhi (Red Cabbage)\n\n- **Haldi (Turmeric):** Asli rang **peela (yellow)**. Acid aur neutral paani mein **peela hi rehta hai**, par **base** milte hi **laal-bhoora (reddish-brown)** ho jaata hai! Yahi Shaunak ke rumaal par hua tha: sabun (base) ne haldi ko laal-bhoora kiya, aur nimbu (acid) ne sabun ke base ko khatam karke wapas peela kar diya.\n- **Gudhal ke phool (China rose) ka ghol:** Garam paani mein gudhal ki pankhudiyan bhigone par halka gulabi ark milta hai. Ye **acid** mein **gehra gulabi (dark pink / magenta)** aur **base** mein **hara (green)** ho jaata hai!\n- **Lal patta-gobhi (Red cabbage) ka ark:** Asli rang baingani-laal. **Acid** mein **laal (red)** aur **base** mein **hara/peela (greenish-yellow)** ho jaata hai.\n\n> **Activity 1.1 (ghar par adult ke saath): Haldi ka greeting card.**\n> Ek chammach haldi powder mein thoda paani milakar gaadha peela paste banao. Ise ek safed chart paper par phaila kar sukhne do — tumhara **haldi indicator paper** taiyaar hai! Ab ek katori mein thoda sa **kapde dhone ke sabun ka paani** lo (ise peena ya aankh ke paas le jaana mana hai). Rui ki teeli (cotton bud) sabun ke paani mein dubokar peele card par phool banao: jahan-jahan sabun lagega, wahan **laal-bhoora** phool ubhar aayega! Ab doosri saaf cotton bud ko **nimbu ke ras** mein dubokar us laal phool ke beech mein chhuo: wahan rang wapas **peela** ho jaayega. Kaam ke baad haath dho lo.\n\n### 3. Lab ke do pakke Synthetic Indicators: Phenolphthalein aur Methyl Orange\n\nSchool lab mein sabse zyada ye do manav-nirmit indicators dikhte hain:\n\n- **Phenolphthalein:** Iska khud ka ghol paani jaisa **rangheen (colourless)** hota hai. **Acid** ya **neutral** ghol mein ye **rangheen (colourless)** hi rehta hai, par jaise hi **base** milta hai, ye khubsurat **gulabi (pink)** ho jaata hai!\n- **Methyl orange:** Iska asali ghol **narangi (orange)** hota hai. **Acid** mein ye **laal (red/pinkish-red)** ho jaata hai, aur **base** mein **peela (yellow)** ho jaata hai!\n\nSabhi 5 mukhya indicators ka poora chart ek nazar mein:\n\n```\n   INDICATOR KA RANG-CHAKRA (ACID vs NEUTRAL vs BASE)\n\n   Indicator          Acid mein          Neutral mein       Base mein\n   --------------------------------------------------------------------------\n   Litmus             Laal (Red)         Baingani (Purple)  Neela (Blue)\n   Haldi (Turmeric)   Peela (Yellow)     Peela (Yellow)     Laal-bhoora\n   Gudhal             Gehra Gulabi       Halka Gulabi       Hara (Green)\n   Phenolphthalein    Rangheen           Rangheen           Gulabi (Pink)\n   Methyl Orange      Laal (Red)         Narangi (Orange)   Peela (Yellow)\n```\n\n> **Figure 1.1** – Paanch pramukh indicators ke rang: Acid, Neutral aur Base mein. Dhyan do ki Haldi aur Phenolphthalein dono Acid aur Neutral mein ek hi jaisa rang dikhate hain (peela aur rangheen), isliye sirf unse Acid aur Neutral ke beech farq karne ke liye ek chhota step aur lagta hai (Part D mein dekhenge)."
          },
          {
            "heading": "Part C: Identifying by Smell: Olfactory Indicators",
            "headingHinglish": "Part C: Naak se pehchaan: Olfactory Indicators",
            "body": "Suppose a student is visually impaired. Can they still identify acids and bases independently in the chemistry lab? Absolutely! Certain substances have a **characteristic odour (smell)** that remains intact in an acid, but **vanishes completely when mixed with a base**. Such substances are called **olfactory indicators** (*olfactory* = relating to the sense of smell).\n\nThe three best-known olfactory indicators are:\n\n1. **Finely chopped onion-scented cloth strips** (testing with lab acid and base is for **observation only — teacher demonstration**): Place clean cotton cloth strips in a sealed plastic bag with finely chopped onions overnight in the refrigerator (this preparation step can be done **at home with an adult**). By morning, the cloth strips absorb a strong onion smell.\n   - Put a few drops of a **dilute acid** (such as lemon juice or dilute HCl) on one strip: the sharp onion **smell remains**.\n   - Put a few drops of a **base** (such as soapy water, or dilute NaOH handled by the teacher in the lab) on another strip and rinse lightly with water: the onion **smell disappears completely**!\n2. **Vanilla extract** (the flavouring used in cakes and ice cream):\n   - When shaken with an **acid**, the sweet vanilla **aroma stays unchanged**.\n   - When shaken with a **base (NaOH)**, the sweet vanilla **aroma vanishes**.\n3. **Clove oil:**\n   - In an **acid**, the warm, spicy smell of clove **remains intact**.\n   - In a **base (NaOH)**, the smell of clove **disappears (or becomes undetectable)**.\n\n**One-line rule:** For onion, vanilla extract, and clove oil alike—their smell **STAYS in an Acid**, and **VANISHES in a Base**!\n\n> **Activity 1.2 (Observation only — teacher demonstration).** The teacher takes small amounts of dilute HCl and dilute NaOH in two separate test tubes, adds 2 drops of **vanilla extract** to each, and shakes them gently. When wafting the air gently toward the nose from the HCl tube, the sweet scent of vanilla is clearly detected, whereas the NaOH tube has lost its vanilla scent completely. *(Safety note: Never place your nose directly over the mouth of a laboratory test tube; always fan a little vapour gently toward your nose with your hand—a technique called **wafting**.)*",
            "bodyHinglish": "Maan lo kisi vidyarthi ko dikhayi nahi deta (visually impaired student hai). Kya wo lab mein acid aur base ki pehchaan kar sakta hai? Bilkul! Kuch padarth aise hote hain jinki **khas gandh (smell)** acid mein to bani rehti hai, par **base milte hi gayab ho jaati hai**. Inhe **olfactory indicators** (*olfactory* = soonghne se juda) kehte hain.\n\nTeen sabse achhe olfactory indicators:\n\n1. **Barik kata pyaaz (Onion)-basa kapda** (acid aur base wala test **sirf dekhne ke liye (teacher demo)**): Ek saaf suti kapde ki pattiyon ko barik kate pyaaz ke saath plastic thaili mein raat bhar band karke fridge mein rakh do (ye hissa **ghar par adult ke saath** ho sakta hai). Subah pattiyon mein pyaaz ki tez gandh bas jaati hai.\n   - Ek patti par **patla acid** (jaise nimbu ka ras ya dilute HCl) daalo: pyaaz ki **gandh bani rehti hai**.\n   - Doosri patti par **base** (jaise sabun ka ghol, ya lab mein teacher dwara dilute NaOH) daalo aur paani se halka dho lo: pyaaz ki **gandh bilkul gayab ho jaati hai**!\n2. **Vanilla essence** (jo cake aur ice-cream mein dalta hai):\n   - **Acid** ke saath hilane par vanilla ki meethi **khushboo bani rehti hai**.\n   - **Base (NaOH)** ke saath hilane par vanilla ki **khushboo gayab ho jaati hai**.\n3. **Laung ka tel (Clove oil):**\n   - **Acid** mein laung ki **gandh bani rehti hai**.\n   - **Base (NaOH)** mein laung ki **gandh khatam (ya bahut halki) ho jaati hai**.\n\n**Niyam ek line mein:** Pyaaz, vanilla aur laung ke tel — teeno ki gandh **Acid mein BANI REHTI hai**, aur **Base mein GAYAB ho jaati hai**!\n\n> **Activity 1.2 (sirf dekhne ke liye, teacher demo).** Teacher do test tubes mein thoda-thoda dilute HCl aur dilute NaOH lete hain. Dono mein 2-2 boond **vanilla essence** daal kar hilaate hain. HCl wali tube ke paas haath se hawa jhalane (wafting) par vanilla ki khushboo saaf aati hai, jabki NaOH wali tube se vanilla ki khushboo gayab ho chuki hoti hai. *(Safety: Lab mein kisi bhi tube ko naak se chipka kar nahi soonghte; haath se hawa apni naak ki taraf jhalate hain, jise **wafting** kehte hain.)*"
          },
          {
            "heading": "Part D: Solved Examples: Detective Puzzles",
            "headingHinglish": "Part D: Solved Examples: Jasoosi Paheliyan (Detective Puzzles)",
            "body": "### Example 1. Three Unlabelled Test Tubes and Only Red Litmus Paper!\n\n**Problem:** You are given three unlabelled test tubes—**A, B, and C**. One contains **dilute HCl (an acid)**, one contains **NaOH solution (a base)**, and one contains **pure distilled water (neutral)**. You are given **only a single strip of red litmus paper** (and no other indicator!). How will you identify all three liquids?\n\n**Solution (Step-by-step):**\n1. **Step 1 (Find the Base):** Tear the red litmus strip into three small pieces (or use three separate corners of the strip). Place one drop from tubes A, B, and C onto the three separate pieces. Whichever drop turns the **red litmus blue** must be the **base (NaOH)**! Suppose **Tube B** turns it blue. Then **B = Base (NaOH)**.\n2. **Step 2 (You now have Blue Litmus paper!):** The piece of paper that just turned **blue** can now act as your **blue litmus paper**!\n3. **Step 3 (Distinguish the Acid from Water):** Tear this newly blue piece into two halves and place a drop from each of the remaining tubes, **A and C**, onto them. Whichever tube turns the blue piece back to **red** is the **acid (HCl)**! And whichever tube leaves the blue piece unchanged is **pure distilled water (neutral)**!\n\n**Check:** Did we need any outside indicator? No! First Red → Blue identified the Base, then that same Blue → Red identified the Acid, and no change identified the Neutral liquid ✅.\n\n---\n\n### Example 2. Identifying All Three Tubes (Acid, Base, Water) Using Only Turmeric Paper\n\n**Problem:** Once again you have three unlabelled test tubes—**X, Y, and Z** (one containing an acid, one a base, and one pure water)—and this time you have **only yellow turmeric paper**. How will you identify all three?\n\n**Solution (Step-by-step):**\n1. **Step 1:** Place one drop from each tube (X, Y, and Z) onto three separate spots on the yellow turmeric paper. In both acid and water, turmeric **stays yellow**, but in a **base**, turmeric turns **reddish-brown**. Suppose **Tube Z** turns the turmeric spot reddish-brown. That proves **Z = Base**!\n2. **Step 2:** Now use two reddish-brown spots (made with drops from Tube Z). Add a few drops of **Tube X** to the first reddish-brown spot, and a few drops of **Tube Y** to the second.\n   - The **acid** will neutralise the base on the spot and turn the reddish-brown colour right back to **yellow**!\n   - **Pure water** will not turn the reddish-brown spot back to yellow (it may only wet the paper slightly).\n\n**Check:** Turmeric (yellow) + Base (Z) → Reddish-brown; Reddish-brown + Acid → Back to Yellow (just like Leo's napkin and the lemon juice!) ✅.\n\n---\n\n### Example 3. Matching Phenolphthalein, Methyl Orange, and Clove Oil Clues\n\n**Problem:** When phenolphthalein is added to an unknown solution **P**, it remains **colourless**, and when methyl orange is added to **P**, it turns **red**. In another unknown solution **Q**, phenolphthalein turns **pink**, and the smell of clove oil **disappears**. Identify the chemical nature of **P** and **Q**.\n\n**Solution:**\n- **Solution P:** Phenolphthalein stays colourless (meaning P is not a base; it could be acidic or neutral). Methyl orange turns **red**—and methyl orange turns red **only in an acid** (it stays orange in a neutral liquid). Therefore, **Solution P = Acidic**.\n- **Solution Q:** Phenolphthalein turns **pink** and the smell of clove oil **disappears**—both observations are unmistakable signatures of a **base**. Therefore, **Solution Q = Basic**.\n\n**Check:** Both identifications match Figure 1.1 and the rules in Part C 100% ✅.\n\n---\n\n### Example 4. Calculating Indicator Testing Volumes for a Class\n\n**Problem:** In a school laboratory, a class of 40 students has to test 5 different household liquids (lemon juice, vinegar, soapy water, limewater, and salt water) using **4 indicators** (blue litmus solution, red litmus solution, phenolphthalein, and methyl orange). If students work in pairs (2 students per group) and each test requires **2 mL** of the liquid in a separate test tube, what total volume (in mL) of each household liquid must be prepared for the entire class?\n\n**Solution:**\n- Total students = 40; students per group = 2 → Total groups = 40 ÷ 2 = **20 groups**.\n- Each group tests a liquid with 4 indicators in separate test tubes → 1 group needs **4 samples** of each liquid.\n- Each sample uses 2 mL → Volume of each liquid needed per group = 4 × 2 mL = **8 mL**.\n- Total volume of each liquid needed for all 20 groups = 20 × 8 mL = **160 mL**.\n\n**Check:** 160 mL ÷ 2 mL = 80 test tubes (per liquid); 80 ÷ 4 indicators = 20 groups; 20 × 2 = 40 students ✅.",
            "bodyHinglish": "### Example 1. Teen bina-label ki test tubes aur sirf Red Litmus!\n\n**Sawaal:** Tumhare paas teen test tubes **A, B aur C** hain. Ek mein **dilute HCl (acid)** hai, ek mein **NaOH (base)** hai, aur ek mein **shuddh paani (neutral)** hai. Tumhein **sirf ek Red Litmus paper ki patti** di gayi hai (aur koi indicator nahi!). Tum teeno tubes ki pehchaan kaise karoge?\n\n**Hal (Step-by-step):**\n1. **Qadam 1 (Base dhoondho):** Red litmus patti ke teen chhote tukde kar lo (ya patti ka ek kona-kona bari-bari dubo). Teeno tubes A, B, C ki ek-ek boond alag-alag tukde par daalo. Jis tube ki boond se **Red litmus Neela (Blue)** ho jaaye, wo pakka **Base (NaOH)** hai! Maan lo **Tube B** ne use neela kar diya. To **B = Base (NaOH)**.\n2. **Qadam 2 (Ab tumhare paas Blue litmus ban gaya!):** Jis tukde ka rang abhi-abhi **neela (blue)** hua hai, wahi ab tumhara **Blue litmus paper** ban gaya!\n3. **Qadam 3 (Acid aur Paani alag karo):** Is naye bane neele tukde ke do hisse karo aur bachi hui dono tubes **A aur C** ki boond daalo. Jo tube is neele tukde ko wapas **Laal (Red)** kar de, wo **Acid (HCl)** hai! Aur jis tube se neela tukda neela hi rahe, wo **Shuddh Paani (Neutral)** hai!\n\n**Jaanch:** Kya humein bahar se doosra indicator laga? Nahi! Pehle Red → Blue (Base mila), phir usi Blue → Red (Acid mila), aur jisme koi badlav nahi wo Neutral ✅.\n\n---\n\n### Example 2. Sirf Haldi (Turmeric) se teeno tubes (Acid, Base, Paani) pehchanna\n\n**Sawaal:** Phir se wahi teen tubes **X, Y aur Z** (ek mein Acid, ek mein Base, ek mein shuddh Paani) hain, aur is baar tumhare paas **sirf Haldi paper (peela)** hai. Kaise pehchanoge?\n\n**Hal (Step-by-step):**\n1. **Qadam 1:** Teeno tubes X, Y, Z ki ek-ek boond haldi paper ke teen alag hisson par daalo. Acid aur paani dono mein haldi **peeli** hi rehti hai, par **Base** mein haldi **laal-bhoori (reddish-brown)** ho jaati hai. Maan lo **Tube Z** ne haldi ko laal-bhoora kar diya. To **Z = Base** pakka ho gaya!\n2. **Qadam 2:** Ab us **laal-bhoore** ho chuke haldi ke daag ke do hisse karo. Ek par **Tube X** ki kuch boondein daalo, aur doosre par **Tube Y** ki.\n   - **Acid** us laal-bhoore base ko neutralise karke haldi ko turant wapas **peela** kar dega!\n   - **Paani** se laal-bhoora rang wapas peela nahi hoga (sirf halka phail sakta hai).\n\n**Jaanch:** Haldi (peeli) + Base (Z) → Laal-bhoora; Laal-bhoora + Acid → Wapas Peela (bilkul Shaunak ke rumaal aur nimbu ki tarah!) ✅.\n\n---\n\n### Example 3. Phenolphthalein aur Methyl Orange ka milaan\n\n**Sawaal:** Ek anjaan ghol **P** mein phenolphthalein daalne par wo **rangheen** rehta hai, aur methyl orange daalne par **laal** ho jaata hai. Doosre ghol **Q** mein phenolphthalein **gulabi (pink)** ho jaata hai, aur laung ke tel (clove oil) ki gandh **gayab** ho jaati hai. P aur Q ki prakriti (nature) batao.\n\n**Hal:**\n- **Ghol P:** Phenolphthalein rangheen hai (matlab P base nahi hai; wo acid ya neutral ho sakta hai). Methyl orange **laal** ho gaya — methyl orange sirf **acid** mein laal hota hai (neutral mein narangi rehta hai). Isliye **Ghol P = Acidic**.\n- **Ghol Q:** Phenolphthalein **gulabi** ho gaya aur clove oil ki gandh **gayab** ho gayi — ye dono baatein **base** ki pakki pehchaan hain. Isliye **Ghol Q = Basic**.\n\n**Jaanch:** Figure 1.1 aur Part C ke niyam se dono pehchaan 100% mail khaati hain ✅.\n\n---\n\n### Example 4. Indicator drops ka hisaab\n\n**Sawaal:** Ek school lab mein 40 vidyarthiyon ke group ko 5 alag-alag gholon (nimbu ras, sirka, sabun ka paani, chuna paani, aur namak ka paani) ki jaanch **4 indicators** (blue litmus, red litmus, phenolphthalein aur methyl orange) se karni hai. Agar 2 vidyarthi milkar 1 set banate hain aur har test mein ghol ki **2 mL** matra lagti hai, to har ghol ka kul kitna aayatan (mL mein) poore class ke liye chahiye?\n\n**Hal:**\n- Kul vidyarthi = 40; har group mein 2 vidyarthi → Kul groups = 40 ÷ 2 = **20 groups**.\n- Har group ek ghol ko 4 indicators se alag-alag test tube mein jaanchta hai → 1 group ko ek ghol ke **4 samples** chahiye.\n- Har sample mein 2 mL lagta hai → 1 group ke liye ek ghol = 4 × 2 mL = **8 mL**.\n- Poore 20 groups ke liye har ghol ki kul matra = 20 × 8 mL = **160 mL**.\n\n**Jaanch:** 160 mL ÷ 2 mL = 80 test tubes (har ghol ke liye); 80 ÷ 4 indicators = 20 groups; 20 × 2 = 40 vidyarthi ✅."
          },
          {
            "heading": "Common Pitfalls to Avoid",
            "headingHinglish": "Aam galtiyan (bachke rehna)",
            "body": "1. **Mistake 1: Assuming \"Phenolphthalein is colourless, so the liquid must be an acid.\"**\n   Phenolphthalein remains colourless in **both acidic and neutral** solutions! A colourless result only proves that the liquid is **not a base**; you still need blue litmus or methyl orange to distinguish an acid from a neutral liquid.\n2. **Mistake 2: Reversing the colour change of turmeric.**\n   Turmeric turns reddish-brown in a **base**, not in an acid! In an acid, turmeric keeps its natural **yellow** colour.\n3. **Mistake 3: Writing that olfactory indicators keep their smell in a base.**\n   It is the exact opposite! Onion, vanilla extract, and clove oil **keep their characteristic smell in an acid** and **lose their smell in a base** (such as NaOH).\n4. **Mistake 4: Thinking the natural (neutral) colour of litmus is blue or red.**\n   The raw neutral dye extracted from lichens is **purple**. Blue and red litmus papers are pre-treated in the factory for laboratory convenience.\n5. **Mistake 5: Tasting or dipping bare fingers into laboratory liquids.**\n   Laboratory acids and bases are strongly corrosive; never taste or touch them with bare hands.",
            "bodyHinglish": "1. **Galti 1: \"Phenolphthalein rangheen hai, to ghol pakka Acid hi hoga\" maan lena.**\n   Phenolphthalein **Acid** mein bhi rangheen rehta hai aur **Neutral (shuddh paani)** mein bhi rangheen rehta hai! Isliye sirf phenolphthalein ke rangheen rehne se ye sabit hota hai ki ghol **Base nahi hai**, par Acid aur Neutral mein farq karne ke liye blue litmus ya methyl orange chahiye.\n2. **Galti 2: Haldi ke rang-badlav ko ulta likhna.**\n   Haldi **Base** mein laal-bhoori hoti hai, Acid mein nahi! Acid mein haldi apna asali **peela** rang hi rakhti hai.\n3. **Galti 3: Olfactory indicators mein \"Base mein gandh rehti hai\" likh dena.**\n   Bilkul ulta! Pyaaz, vanilla aur clove oil — teeno ki gandh **Acid mein bani rehti hai** aur **Base (jaise NaOH) mein gayab ho jaati hai**.\n4. **Galti 4: Litmus ka asali (neutral) rang neela ya laal samajhna.**\n   Lichen se nikle litmus ka asali neutral rang **baingani (purple)** hota hai. Blue aur Red litmus papers to lab ki suvidha ke liye pehle se rang kar banaye jaate hain.\n5. **Galti 5: Lab mein kisi ghol ko chakh kar ya ungli dubokar dekhna.**\n   Lab ke acids aur bases tez corrosive hote hain; unhe kabhi chakhna ya nange haath se chhuna nahi chahiye."
          },
          {
            "heading": "Quick Recap",
            "headingHinglish": "Quick Recap",
            "body": "- Edible, safe **organic acids** (such as citric acid in lemons, lactic acid in yoghurt, tartaric acid in tamarind, and acetic acid in vinegar) taste sour; **bases** (such as baking soda and soap) taste bitter and feel soapy or slippery.\n- Laboratory **mineral acids** (HCl, H₂SO₄, HNO₃) and strong bases (NaOH, KOH) burn skin severely (**corrosive**), so they must never be tasted or touched.\n- **Litmus** (from lichens): Acid turns Blue litmus **Red**; Base turns Red litmus **Blue**; Neutral shows no change (natural extract is **purple**).\n- **Turmeric:** **Yellow** in Acid and Neutral; **Reddish-brown** in Base.\n- **Hibiscus (China rose):** **Dark Pink (Magenta)** in Acid; **Green** in Base.\n- **Phenolphthalein:** **Colourless** in Acid and Neutral; **Pink** in Base.\n- **Methyl Orange:** **Red** in Acid; **Orange** in Neutral; **Yellow** in Base.\n- **Olfactory indicators** (Onion, Vanilla extract, Clove oil): Smell **stays intact** in Acid; smell **vanishes** in Base.",
            "bodyHinglish": "- Khaane wale safe **organic acids** (jaise nimbu mein citric acid, dahi mein lactic acid, imli mein tartaric acid, sirke mein acetic acid) swaad mein khatte hote hain; **bases** (jaise baking soda, sabun) kadwe aur chhune mein chikne (soapy) hote hain.\n- Lab ke **mineral acids** (HCl, H₂SO₄, HNO₃) aur strong bases (NaOH, KOH) chamdi jala dete hain (**corrosive**), isliye unhe kabhi chakhna ya chhuna nahi chahiye.\n- **Litmus** (lichen se): Acid → Blue ko **Red** karta hai; Base → Red ko **Blue** karta hai; Neutral mein koi badlav nahi (asali ark **purple**).\n- **Haldi (Turmeric):** Acid aur Neutral mein **Yellow**; Base mein **Reddish-brown**.\n- **Gudhal (China rose):** Acid mein **Dark Pink (Magenta)**; Base mein **Green**.\n- **Phenolphthalein:** Acid aur Neutral mein **Colourless**; Base mein **Pink**.\n- **Methyl Orange:** Acid mein **Red**; Neutral mein **Orange**; Base mein **Yellow**.\n- **Olfactory indicators** (Pyaaz, Vanilla essence, Clove oil): Acid mein gandh **bani rehti hai**; Base mein gandh **gayab ho jaati hai**."
          },
          {
            "heading": "Practice Questions",
            "headingHinglish": "Practice Questions",
            "body": "1. Name the natural acid present in each of the following: (a) Tamarind, (b) Tomato, (c) Sour curd (yoghurt), (d) Ant sting, (e) Vinegar, (f) Lemon.\n2. Why should a student never taste or touch an unknown liquid in the chemistry laboratory to check whether it is an acid or a base?\n3. What colour will you observe in each of the following cases?\n   (a) A drop of lemon juice is placed on blue litmus paper.\n   (b) A drop of soap solution is placed on a strip of turmeric paper.\n   (c) Two drops of phenolphthalein are added to limewater, Ca(OH)₂(aq).\n   (d) Two drops of methyl orange are added to dilute nitric acid, HNO₃(aq).\n4. You are given three unlabelled test tubes containing distilled water, dilute hydrochloric acid, and potassium hydroxide solution, along with **only a strip of blue litmus paper**. Explain step by step how you will identify all three liquids.\n5. Explain what happens when a white cotton shirt with a yellow curry (turmeric) stain is:\n   (a) rubbed with soap,\n   (b) washed with plenty of water afterwards, and\n   (c) dabbed with a few drops of vinegar.\n6. What are olfactory indicators? Name two olfactory indicators and state what happens to their smell when they are treated separately with (i) dilute HCl and (ii) dilute NaOH solution.\n7. A colourless liquid **M** does not change the colour of phenolphthalein. Can you be certain that **M** is an acid? Explain your reasoning and suggest one extra test to be sure.\n8. Hibiscus (China rose) indicator is added to two test tubes: Tube 1 containing window cleaner (which contains ammonium hydroxide) and Tube 2 containing tamarind water. What colour will appear in Tube 1 and Tube 2?\n9. State whether each statement is True or False, and correct the false ones:\n   (a) The original neutral dye extracted from lichen (litmus) is purple in colour.\n   (b) Methyl orange turns pink-red in a basic solution and yellow in an acidic solution.\n   (c) Clove oil loses its characteristic smell when shaken with dilute sodium hydroxide solution.\n   (d) All acids found in fruits and vegetables are mineral acids.\n10. In a school laboratory, 36 students work in groups of 3. Each group tests 6 different household solutions using 3 liquid indicators (litmus solution, phenolphthalein, and methyl orange) in separate test tubes, using 3 mL of solution per test tube. How many millilitres of each household solution must the lab assistant prepare in total?\n\n### Answers\n\n1. (a) **Tartaric acid** (in tamarind), (b) **Oxalic acid** (in tomato), (c) **Lactic acid** (in sour curd/yoghurt), (d) **Methanoic acid / Formic acid** (in ant sting), (e) **Acetic acid** (in vinegar), (f) **Citric acid** (in lemon).\n\n2. Laboratory acids (such as HCl, H₂SO₄, and HNO₃) and bases (such as NaOH and KOH) are highly **corrosive** and often toxic. Tasting them or touching them with bare skin can cause severe chemical burns to the mouth, tongue, or hands. Therefore, indicators are always used for identification in the lab.\n\n3. (a) Lemon juice contains citric acid, so **blue litmus turns red**.\n   (b) Soap solution is basic, so yellow turmeric paper turns **reddish-brown**.\n   (c) Limewater, Ca(OH)₂, is a base, so phenolphthalein turns **pink**.\n   (d) Dilute HNO₃ is an acid, so methyl orange turns **red**.\n\n4. **Step 1 (Identify the Acid):** Tear the blue litmus strip into three small pieces and place one drop from each test tube onto a separate piece. Only **dilute HCl (the acid)** will turn the blue litmus **red**. This identifies the test tube containing the acid!\n   **Step 2 (You now have Red Litmus paper):** The piece of litmus paper that just turned red can now serve as **red litmus paper**.\n   **Step 3 (Distinguish the Base from Water):** Cut this red piece into two halves and add a drop from each of the two remaining test tubes. The liquid that turns the red piece back to **blue** is **KOH solution (the base)**! The liquid that leaves the red piece unchanged is **distilled water (neutral)** ✅.\n\n5. (a) Curry contains **turmeric**, and soap is **basic**. Upon contact with a base, the turmeric indicator changes colour from yellow to **reddish-brown**.\n   (b) Washing with plenty of water rinses away most of the soap (base), so the reddish-brown colour fades and the stain returns to **yellow**.\n   (c) Vinegar contains **acetic acid**, which immediately neutralises any remaining traces of basic soap and restores the stain to its original **yellow** colour.\n\n6. **Olfactory indicators** are substances whose **odour (smell)** changes in acidic versus basic media, enabling visually impaired students to identify acids and bases by smell. Two examples: **Vanilla extract** and **Clove oil** (or onion-scented cloth).\n   - (i) With **dilute HCl (acid)**: the characteristic smell of both **remains intact** (does not vanish).\n   - (ii) With **dilute NaOH (base)**: the characteristic smell of both **disappears**.\n\n7. **No**, we cannot be certain that **M** is an acid, because phenolphthalein remains **colourless** in **both acidic and neutral** solutions. To be sure, place a drop of **M** on **blue litmus paper** (or add methyl orange): if blue litmus turns **red** (or methyl orange turns red), then **M** is definitely an acid; if there is no colour change, then **M** is neutral ✅.\n\n8. **Tube 1 (Window cleaner = Ammonium hydroxide, a base):** Hibiscus (China rose) indicator turns **green**.\n   **Tube 2 (Tamarind water = Tartaric acid, an acid):** Hibiscus (China rose) indicator turns **dark pink / magenta**.\n\n9. (a) **True** ✅. The neutral litmus extract obtained from lichens is purple.\n   (b) **False** ❌. Methyl orange turns **red** in an **acidic** solution and **yellow** in a **basic** solution.\n   (c) **True** ✅. Clove oil loses its smell when shaken with a base (NaOH).\n   (d) **False** ❌. Acids naturally present in fruits and vegetables are mild **organic acids**, not mineral acids.\n\n10. Total students = 36; 3 students per group → Total groups = 36 ÷ 3 = **12 groups**.\n    Each group tests a solution in 3 separate test tubes (one for each of the 3 indicators) → 1 group needs **3 test tubes** per household solution.\n    Each test tube requires 3 mL → Volume of one household solution per group = 3 × 3 mL = **9 mL**.\n    Total volume of each household solution for all 12 groups = 12 × 9 mL = **108 mL** ✅ (Check: 108 ÷ 3 mL = 36 tubes; 36 ÷ 3 indicators = 12 groups; 12 × 3 = 36 students ✅).\n\n---\n\nHanging his clean white napkin out to dry in the sun, Leo grinned and said, \"Aunt Clara, now I see that turmeric, hibiscus petals, and onions aren't just kitchen ingredients—they're full-time chemistry detectives!\"\n\n**What's Next?** We have seen that all acids turn blue litmus red and all bases turn red litmus blue. But **what single chemical particle do all acids have in common** that makes them behave like acids—and why can't an acid show its acidic nature without water? In the next topic (**Part 2**), we will dive into water to explore the world of H⁺(aq) and OH⁻(aq) ions!",
            "bodyHinglish": "1. Name the natural acid present in each of the following: (a) Tamarind, (b) Tomato, (c) Sour curd, (d) Ant sting, (e) Vinegar, (f) Lemon.\n2. Why should a student never taste or touch an unknown liquid in the chemistry laboratory to check whether it is an acid or a base?\n3. What colour will you observe in each of the following cases?\n   (a) A drop of lemon juice is placed on blue litmus paper.\n   (b) A drop of soap solution is placed on a strip of turmeric paper.\n   (c) Two drops of phenolphthalein are added to limewater, Ca(OH)₂(aq).\n   (d) Two drops of methyl orange are added to dilute nitric acid, HNO₃(aq).\n4. You are given three unlabelled test tubes containing distilled water, dilute hydrochloric acid, and potassium hydroxide solution, along with **only a strip of blue litmus paper**. Explain step by step how you will identify all three liquids.\n5. Explain what happens when a white cotton shirt with a yellow curry (turmeric) stain is:\n   (a) rubbed with soap,\n   (b) washed with plenty of water afterwards, and\n   (c) dabbed with a few drops of vinegar.\n6. What are olfactory indicators? Name two olfactory indicators and state what happens to their smell when they are treated separately with (i) dilute HCl and (ii) dilute NaOH solution.\n7. A colourless liquid **M** does not change the colour of phenolphthalein. Can you be certain that **M** is an acid? Explain your reasoning and suggest one extra test to be sure.\n8. China rose (gudhal) indicator is added to two test tubes: Tube 1 containing window cleaner (which contains ammonium hydroxide) and Tube 2 containing tamarind water. What colour will appear in Tube 1 and Tube 2?\n9. State whether each statement is True or False, and correct the false ones:\n   (a) The original neutral dye extracted from lichen (litmus) is purple in colour.\n   (b) Methyl orange turns pink-red in a basic solution and yellow in an acidic solution.\n   (c) Clove oil loses its characteristic smell when shaken with dilute sodium hydroxide solution.\n   (d) All acids found in fruits and vegetables are mineral acids.\n10. In a school laboratory, 36 students work in groups of 3. Each group tests 6 different household solutions using 3 liquid indicators (litmus solution, phenolphthalein, and methyl orange) in separate test tubes, using 3 mL of solution per test tube. How many millilitres of each household solution must the lab assistant prepare in total?\n\n### Answers\n\n1. (a) **Tartaric acid** (imli mein), (b) **Oxalic acid** (tamatar mein), (c) **Lactic acid** (dahi mein), (d) **Methanoic acid / Formic acid** (cheenti ke dank mein), (e) **Acetic acid** (sirke mein), (f) **Citric acid** (nimbu mein).\n\n2. Lab ke acids (jaise HCl, H₂SO₄, HNO₃) aur bases (jaise NaOH, KOH) bahut **corrosive** (jala dene wale) ya zeherile hote hain. Unhe chakhne ya nange haath se chhune par munh, jeebh ya chamdi buri tarah jal sakti hai. Isliye lab mein hamesha indicators ka hi upyog kiya jaata hai.\n\n3. (a) Nimbu ke ras mein citric acid hota hai, isliye **neela litmus laal (red)** ho jaayega.\n   (b) Sabun ka ghol basic hota hai, isliye peela haldi paper **laal-bhoora (reddish-brown)** ho jaayega.\n   (c) Limewater Ca(OH)₂ ek base hai, isliye phenolphthalein **gulabi (pink)** ho jaayega.\n   (d) Dilute HNO₃ ek acid hai, isliye methyl orange **laal (red)** ho jaayega.\n\n4. **Step 1 (Acid pehchano):** Blue litmus patti ke teen chhote tukde karke teeno tubes ki ek-ek boond daalo. Sirf **dilute HCl (acid)** neele litmus ko **laal (red)** karega. Isse Acid wali tube pehchan mein aa gayi!\n   **Step 2 (Ab laal tukda taiyaar hai):** Jo tukda abhi laal hua hai, wahi ab **Red litmus paper** ka kaam karega.\n   **Step 3 (Base aur Paani alag karo):** Is laal tukde ke do hisse karke bachi hui dono tubes ki boond daalo. Jo tube is laal tukde ko wapas **neela (blue)** kar de, wo **KOH (base)** hai! Aur jis tube se laal tukda laal hi rahe, wo **distilled water (neutral)** hai ✅.\n\n5. (a) Curry mein **haldi (turmeric)** hoti hai aur sabun **basic** hota hai. Base ke sampark mein aate hi haldi indicator apna rang peele se **laal-bhoora (reddish-brown)** kar leta hai.\n   (b) Khoob saare paani se dhone par sabun (base) beh jaata hai, isliye laal-bhoora rang hat kar daag wapas **peela** ho jaata hai.\n   (c) Sirke (vinegar) mein **acetic acid** hota hai, jo bache-khuche sabun ke base ko turant neutralise kar deta hai aur daag ko wapas **peela** kar deta hai.\n\n6. **Olfactory indicators** wo padarth hain jinki **gandh (smell)** acidic aur basic madhyam mein badal jaati hai, isliye visually impaired vidyarthi bhi inse acid aur base pehchan sakte hain. Do udaharan: **Vanilla essence** aur **Clove oil (laung ka tel)** (ya pyaaz).\n   - (i) **Dilute HCl (acid)** ke saath: dono ki khas gandh **bani rehti hai** (khatam nahi hoti).\n   - (ii) **Dilute NaOH (base)** ke saath: dono ki khas gandh **gayab ho jaati hai**.\n\n7. **Nahi**, hum pakka nahi keh sakte ki **M** acid hi hai, kyunki phenolphthalein **acid** aur **neutral** dono tarah ke gholon mein **rangheen (colourless)** hi rehta hai. Pakka karne ke liye **M** ki ek boond **blue litmus paper** par daalo (ya methyl orange daalo): agar blue litmus **laal** ho jaaye (ya methyl orange laal ho jaaye), to **M** pakka acid hai; warna agar koi badlav na ho, to **M** neutral hai ✅.\n\n8. **Tube 1 (Window cleaner = Ammonium hydroxide, base):** China rose indicator **hara (green)** ho jaayega.\n   **Tube 2 (Imli ka paani = Tartaric acid, acid):** China rose indicator **gehra gulabi / magenta (dark pink)** ho jaayega.\n\n9. (a) **True** ✅. Lichen se nikala gaya neutral litmus ark baingani (purple) hota hai.\n   (b) **False** ❌. Methyl orange **acid** mein laal (red) aur **base** mein **peela (yellow)** hota hai.\n   (c) **True** ✅. Base (NaOH) ke saath laung ke tel ki gandh gayab ho jaati hai.\n   (d) **False** ❌. Phalon aur sabziyon mein paaye jaane wale acids **organic acids** (prakritik aur halke acids) hote hain, mineral acids nahi.\n\n10. Kul vidyarthi = 36; har group mein 3 vidyarthi → Kul groups = 36 ÷ 3 = **12 groups**.\n    Har group ek ghol ko 3 alag test tubes mein (3 indicators ke liye) jaanchta hai → 1 group ko har ghol ke **3 test tubes** chahiye.\n    Har test tube mein 3 mL ghol lagta hai → 1 group ke liye ek ghol = 3 × 3 mL = **9 mL**.\n    Poore 12 groups ke liye har ghol ki kul matra = 12 × 9 mL = **108 mL** ✅ (Jaanch: 108 ÷ 3 mL = 36 tubes; 36 ÷ 3 indicators = 12 groups; 12 × 3 = 36 vidyarthi ✅).\n\n---\n\nShaunak ne apna saaf rumaal dhoop mein sukhate hue kaha, \"Chachi, ab samajh aaya ki haldi, gudhal aur pyaaz sirf rasoi ka samaan nahi, poore-poore chemistry ke jasoos hain!\"\n\n**Aage kya?** Humne ye to dekh liya ki saare acids blue litmus ko laal karte hain aur saare bases red litmus ko neela. Par **saare acids ke andar aisi kaun-si ek cheez common hai** jo unhein acid banati hai, aur paani ke bina acid apna asar kyun nahi dikha paata? Agle topic (**Part 2**) mein hum paani ke andar utar kar H⁺(aq) aur OH⁻(aq) ions ki duniya dekhenge!"
          }
