            "explanation": "Pitfall to avoid (\"Roasting and calcination are just two words for the same thing.\"): Incorrect. Compare what happens when two zinc ores are heated: heat ZnS in air and you get a sharp, choking gas (SO₂). Heat ZnCO₃ and you get an odourless gas that turns lime water milky (CO₂). Two different ores, two different gases, two distinct processes: in roasting, oxygen from the air reacts with the ore to oxidise it; in calcination, heat alone decomposes the ore in limited or no air.",
