            "correctIndex": 3,
            "explanation": "Pitfall to avoid (\"If I can balance the equation on paper, carbon will reduce any metal oxide.\"): Suppose a student writes and balances: Al₂O₃ + 3C → 2Al + 3CO. The atoms balance on paper, but balancing an equation does not mean the reaction actually happens in a furnace! Because Al sits high above Fe and Zn in the reactivity series, Al holds onto oxygen far too tightly for carbon to reduce it at ordinary furnace temperatures. Top-group metals (K, Na, Ca, Mg, Al) require electrolysis.",
