            "explanation": "Pitfall to avoid (Believing that any substance that dissolves in water is automatically an electrolyte): *Correction:* Solubility does not equal conductivity! Table sugar (sucrose) dissolves abundantly in water, forming a thick syrup, but because sucrose molecules remain intact and uncharged ($\\text{C}_{12}\\text{H}_{22}\\text{O}_{11}$), the syrup contains zero ions and cannot conduct electricity. To be an electrolyte, a solute must produce freely mobile ions, not merely dissolve.",
            "explanationHinglish": "Bachke rehna (\"Jo paani mein ghul jaaye, wo electrolyte hai.\"): Galat. *Demonstration:* cheeni paani mein khoob ghulti hai, par cheeni ke syrup se LED nahi jalti (Activity 1.1). Cheeni ke poore molecule ghole jaate hain, koi ion nahi banta. Bijli ke liye ions chahiye, ghulna kaafi nahi."
