            "correctIndex": 3,
            "explanation": "Pitfall to avoid (Confusing Gay-Lussac's combining volumes law with the pressure-temperature law): Suppose a friend writes: \"By Gay-Lussac's law, 20 L of H₂ needs 10 L of O₂ because P₁/T₁ = P₂/T₂.\" That formula doesn't even contain volume! That is the Class 9 law where the volume of a single gas is held constant. Here, we need the ratio of volumes. Quick rule: when different gases react and you use equation coefficients, it's the Law of Combining Volumes; when a single gas sample changes pressure and temperature at constant volume, it's the Class 9 law.",
