            "explanation": "Pitfall to avoid (Thinking VD and molecular mass M are the same number): Suppose someone claims the VD of methane is 16. Let's check: at STP, 22.4 L of hydrogen weighs 2 g and 22.4 L of methane weighs 16 g. Their ratio is 16 ÷ 2 = 8, not 16! One litre of methane is 8 times as heavy as one litre of hydrogen. VD is half of M because we compare against diatomic H₂ (M = 2), not a single H atom (mass = 1).",
