            "correctIndex": 0,
            "explanation": "Pitfall to avoid (Mixing up atomic mass (u) and molar mass (g/mol)): The atomic mass of magnesium is 24 u — that is the mass of one single Mg atom (which in grams is only 24 ÷ 6.022 × 10²³ ≈ 3.99 × 10⁻²³ g!). The molar mass is 24 g/mol — the mass of 6.022 × 10²³ atoms (1 mole). If a question asks for the mass of 0.5 mol of Mg, writing \"12 u\" is wrong; the answer is 0.5 × 24 g/mol = 12 g. Use u when talking about single atoms or molecules, and g (or g/mol) when working with moles.",
            "explanationHinglish": "Bachke rehna (\"Atomic mass aur molar mass ek hi cheez hain, unit se kya farq padta hai.\"): Magnesium ka atomic mass 24 u hai: ek atom ka. Ek Mg atom ka asli mass 24 ÷ 6.022 × 10²³ = lagbhag 3.99 × 10⁻²³ g hota hai: bahut chhota. Molar mass 24 g/mol hai: 6.022 × 10²³ atoms ka mass. Sawaal: \"0.5 mol Mg ka mass?\" Galat jawab: 0.5 × 24 u = 12 u (ye ek atom ka hota hai, aur 0.5 mol ke liye bekaar). Sahi: 0.5 × 24 g = 12 g. Gram wala hisaab tab, jab mole ginte ho; u wala jab ek atom ya ek molecule ginte ho. Dono ke number wahi, par ikai alag aur matlab alag."
