            "explanation": "Pitfall to avoid (Assuming that an atom or ion with more electrons is always larger): This is the single most common trap in this topic! Let's test it against real examples: - Mg has 12 electrons and S has 16 electrons. Yet Mg (~160 pm) is much larger than S (~103 pm) — the atom with fewer electrons is larger! Why? Both have 3 shells, and S has 16 protons pulling those 3 shells tighter inward. - Li (3 electrons, ~152 pm) vs. F (9 electrons, ~64 pm): again, the atom with more electrons is much smaller. - O²⁻ (10 electrons) and Mg²⁺ (10 electrons) have the exact same number of electrons, yet their radii are ~140 pm and ~72 pm (Part G). - Only when comparing K (19 electrons, 4 shells, 227 pm) with K⁺ (18 electrons, 3 shells, 138 pm) does more electrons mean larger — because that 19th electron adds a whole new 4th shell! Moral: Atomic and ionic size is governed by number of shells and net nuclear pull (Part A), never by raw electron count alone.",
            "explanationHinglish": "Bachke rehna (\"Jis atom mein zyada electrons hain, wo hamesha bada hota hai.\"): Ye galat hai, aur ye is topic ki sabse badi galti hai. Gin ke dekho: - Mg mein 12 electrons aur S mein 16 electrons. Par Mg lagbhag 160 pm aur S lagbhag 103 pm: kam electron wala bada. Kaaran: dono ke 3 shells hain, par S mein 16 protons hain aur wahi shells zyada khinchi hain. - Li (3 electrons) lagbhag 152 pm, F (9 electrons) lagbhag 64 pm: yahan bhi zyada electron wala chhota. - O²⁻ mein 10 electrons aur Mg²⁺ mein bhi 10 electrons: electrons barabar, par size lagbhag 140 aur 72 pm (Part G). - K⁺ mein 18 electrons aur K mein 19 electrons: zyada electron wala (K) bada hai (227 pm aur 138 pm). Yahan kaaran ye hai ki ek poori shell zyada hai. To size electrons ki ginti se nahi, shells aur bachi hui pull (Part A) se tay hota hai. Electrons ki ginti kabhi-kabhi sahi nateeja deti hai, par sirf sanyog se."
