          {
            "heading": "A Tug-of-War Over a Plush Toy",
            "headingHinglish": "Khilone ki kheenchatani",
            "body": "On Sunday afternoon, Ethan was visiting his uncle Mr. Davies for a family gathering. Ethan's eighteen-month-old cousin Lily was sitting on the rug hugging a small rubber duck. Playfully, Ethan reached out: \"Can I borrow your duck for a second?\" Lily immediately hugged the toy tight against her chest with both arms. When Ethan gave a gentle tug, she held on even tighter.\n\nHalf an hour later, Lily was distracted watching a cartoon, holding the rubber duck loosely at arm's length with just two fingers. Ethan gave the exact same gentle tug, and the toy slipped effortlessly into his hand before Lily even noticed.\n\n\"Uncle, look at that!\" Ethan laughed. \"Earlier it was impossible to budge, and just now it took almost zero effort — same toy, same me!\"\n\nMr. Davies smiled. \"The difference was her **grip**. When the toy was held right against her chest with both arms, it would have taken a lot of force to pull it away. When it was held far out at arm's length with a loose grip, it took very little effort. Atoms work the exact same way: how tightly a nucleus holds onto its outermost electron determines how much **energy** is needed to remove it. And just two factors control that grip — the exact two factors you learned in Topic 1: **distance (shells)** and **nuclear pull**.\"\n\n```\n    Held tightly (close to the chest)     Held loosely (out at arm's length)\n\n         _____                                  _____\n        ( ^ ^ )                                ( ^ ^ )\n         \\___/ [toy]                            \\___/\n          / \\  tight grip                        / \\  ---------- [toy]\n                                                      far out, loose grip\n\n    Takes a lot of effort to remove       Takes very little effort to remove\n    (close & tight = high ionisation      (far & loose = low ionisation\n    energy)                               energy)\n```\n\n> **Figure 2.1** – The plush-toy analogy: the closer and tighter the grip, the more energy it takes to pull the toy away. In an atom, the toy is the outermost electron, and the grip is the inward pull of the nucleus.",
            "bodyHinglish": "Mr. Tembe ke ghar Sunday ko rishtedaaron ki baithak thi. Unka bhanja Hrishikesh chhutti mein aaya hua tha. Baithak mein ek dedh saal ki bachchi apna nanha sa rabar ka khilona pakde baithi thi. Hrishikesh ne mazaak mein haath badhaya: \"Mujhe de do, thodi der ke liye.\" Bachchi ne khilona seene se chipka liya aur dono haathon se kas kar pakad liya. Hrishikesh ne halka sa kheencha, to bachchi aur kas gayi.\n\nThodi der baad bachchi ek kone mein khel rahi thi aur khilona haath ki lambi bahon mein dheele se latka hua tha. Hrishikesh ne wahi halka kheench kiya aur khilona jhat se nikal aaya. Bachchi ko pata bhi nahi chala.\n\n\"Mama, ye kya? Pehle itna mushkil, ab itna aasaan. Khilona wahi, main wahi!\" Hrishikesh hairaan tha.\n\nMr. Tembe hanse. \"Farq **pakad** ka tha. Jab khilona seene ke bilkul paas aur kas ke pakda tha, to nikalne ke liye bahut zor lagta. Jab door aur dheela tha, to bahut kam. Chemistry mein bhi yahi hota hai: ek atom apne baahari electron ko kitni zor se pakde hai, ye tay karta hai ki use nikalne mein kitni **energy** lagegi. Aur is pakad ko tay karne wale do hi cheezein hain, jo tumne pichhle topic mein dekhi: **doori** aur **nuclear pull**.\"\n\n```\n   Kas ke pakda (seene ke paas)        Dheela pakda (haath ki lambaai par)\n\n        _____                                  _____\n       ( ^ ^ )                                ( ^ ^ )\n        \\___/ [khilona]                        \\___/\n         / \\  tight                             / \\  ---------- [khilona]\n                                                    door aur dheela\n\n   Nikalne mein zyada zor              Nikalne mein kam zor\n   (paas aur kas ke = badi ionisation   (door aur dheela = chhoti ionisation\n   energy)                              energy)\n```\n\n> **Figure 2.1** – Khilone ki kheenchatani: pakad jitni kasi aur paas ki ho, nikalne ka zor utna zyada. Atom mein khilona = baahari electron, pakadne wala = nucleus ki kheench."
          },
          {
            "heading": "Key Vocabulary",
            "headingHinglish": "Naye shabd",
            "body": "| Term | Meaning | Example |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Ionisation energy (IE)** | The energy required to remove the most loosely held (outermost) electron from an isolated gaseous atom | For Na → Na⁺ + e⁻, IE is about 496 kJ per mol |\n| **Gaseous atom** | An isolated atom in the gas phase, far away from neighbouring atoms | A single sodium atom in sodium vapour |\n| **First ionisation energy** | The energy needed to remove the first outermost electron from a neutral atom (what is meant when we simply say \"IE\") | About 496 kJ per mol for Na |\n| **Second ionisation energy** | The energy needed to remove a second electron from the **+1 ion** left behind after the first electron is removed | For Na⁺ → Na²⁺ + e⁻, about 4562 kJ per mol |\n| **kJ per mol** | The unit of ionisation energy: the total energy in kilojoules required to remove one electron from every atom in 1 mole of gaseous atoms | 496 kJ per mol |\n| **Cation** | A positively charged ion formed when an atom loses one or more electrons | Na⁺, Mg²⁺, Al³⁺ |\n| **Closed inner shell** | A completely filled electron shell closer to the nucleus | The 2, 8 shells of Na⁺ |",
            "bodyHinglish": "| Shabd | Matlab (simple bhasha mein) | Example |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Ionisation energy (IE)** | Gas ki haalat ke ek akele atom se baahari sabse door wale electron ko nikaalne mein lagne wali energy | Na → Na⁺ + e⁻ ke liye lagbhag 496 kJ per mol |\n| **Gaseous atom** | Aisa atom jo gas ki tarah akela hai, doosre atoms ke bahut paas nahi | Sodium ki bhaap ka ek atom |\n| **First ionisation energy** | Pehla electron nikaalne ki energy (jab sirf IE kaha jaaye to yahi maana jaata hai) | Na ke liye lagbhag 496 kJ per mol |\n| **Second ionisation energy** | Pehla electron nikalne ke baad bache **ion** se doosra electron nikaalne ki energy | Na⁺ → Na²⁺ + e⁻ ke liye lagbhag 4562 kJ per mol |\n| **kJ per mol** | Energy ki ikaai: 1 mol atoms (ginti ka ek bada fixed dher) ke saare atoms se ek-ek electron nikalne ki kul energy, kilojoule mein | 496 kJ per mol |\n| **Cation** | Electron khokar bana positive ion | Na⁺, Mg²⁺, Al³⁺ |\n| **Closed inner shell** | Poori bhari, nucleus ke paas wali shell | Na⁺ ki 2, 8 shells |"
          },
          {
            "heading": "Part A: What Is Ionisation Energy?",
            "headingHinglish": "Part A: Ionisation energy kya hai?",
            "body": "Imagine a single, isolated sodium atom floating in the gas phase. We want to remove its outermost electron. That electron will not fly off on its own — we must **put energy in** from outside to overcome the attractive pull of the positively charged nucleus. The minimum energy required to pull that electron completely away is the **ionisation energy**:\n\n**Na(g) + energy → Na⁺(g) + e⁻**\n\nOr in general form for any element M: **M(g) + energy → M⁺(g) + e⁻**\n\nRemember: because energy must be **supplied** (absorbed) to pull a negative electron away from a positive nucleus, ionisation energy is always a **positive** quantity — removing an electron always costs energy, never happens for free.\n\n```\n      Before: Neutral Na atom                 After: Na⁺ ion + free electron\n\n       +11 nucleus                             +11 nucleus\n      shells: 2, 8, 1                          shells: 2, 8\n         o                                                     o --> e⁻ (removed)\n      ( o )  ( o o ... )                       ( o )  ( o o ... )\n       1 outer electron                         only the 2, 8 shells remain\n\n      11 protons, 11 electrons                11 protons, 10 electrons\n      net charge = 0                          net charge = +1\n```\n\n> **Figure 2.2** – Na → Na⁺ + e⁻: the single outermost electron is removed, leaving 11 protons and 10 electrons (a +1 cation). The energy supplied to remove that electron is the first ionisation energy.\n\n**Why must the atom be in the gaseous state?** In a solid or liquid, neighbouring atoms are packed closely around each atom and tug on its electrons too. To measure the true grip of **one single atom's nucleus** on its own electron without interference from neighbours, the atom must be isolated in the gas phase.\n\n**Why is the unit kJ per mol?** The energy required to remove one electron from a single atom is tinier than a trillionth of a trillionth of a joule. Instead of writing awkward tiny numbers, chemists measure the energy needed to remove one electron from **every atom in 1 mole** (6.022 × 10²³ atoms — our counting unit from Class 9 and Chapter 5), expressed in kilojoules per mole (**kJ per mol**, where 1 kJ = 1000 J).\n\n**Second ionisation energy (in one line):** After the first electron is removed to leave an M⁺ ion, the energy required to remove a *second* electron (M⁺ → M²⁺ + e⁻) is called the **second ionisation energy** — and it is always larger than the first (in Part C we will see just how much larger!).",
            "bodyHinglish": "Maan lo ek sodium ka atom gas ki tarah akela hai. Isse baahari ek electron nikaalna hai. Ye khud nahi nikalega: bahar se **energy deni padegi**, kyunki nucleus us electron ko kheench raha hai. Jitni energy dene par ye electron nikal jaaye, wahi **ionisation energy** hai.\n\n**Na (gas) + energy → Na⁺ (gas) + e⁻**\n\nYe general roop mein: **M (g) + energy → M⁺ (g) + e⁻**\n\nYaad rakho: yahan energy **andar jaati hai** (kharch hoti hai), isliye ionisation energy hamesha **positive** hoti hai: electron ko nikalne ke liye mehnat lagti hai, mufat mein nahi milta.\n\n```\n     Pehle: Na atom                         Baad mein: Na⁺ ion aur electron\n\n      +11 nucleus                             +11 nucleus\n     shells: 2, 8, 1                          shells: 2, 8\n        o                                                     o --> e⁻ (bahar)\n     ( o )  ( o o ... )                       ( o )  ( o o ... )\n      1 electron baahar                        ab sirf 2 aur 8 bache\n\n     11 proton, 11 electron                   11 proton, 10 electron\n     kul charge 0                             kul charge +1\n```\n\n> **Figure 2.2** – Na → Na⁺ + e⁻: baahari akela electron nikal gaya, 11 proton wahi hain, ab 10 electron bache, isliye ion +1 hai. Nikalne mein lagi energy hi ionisation energy hai.\n\n**Gas ki haalat kyun?** Agar atom thos mein doosre atoms ke bahut paas ho, to padosi atoms bhi us electron par asar daalte hain. Ek **akele** atom ki ionisation energy tabhi saaf-saaf naapi ja sakti hai jab wo gas ki tarah akela ho. Bas itna hi yaad rakho.\n\n**Ikaai kJ per mol kya hai?** Ek atom se ek electron nikaalne ki energy itni chhoti hoti hai ki use sidhe likhna asaan nahi. Isliye chemist **1 mol atoms** (bahut bade, fixed ginti ka dher: Class 9 aur Chapter 5 ka mole) ke saare atoms se ek-ek electron nikalne ki kul energy likhte hain. Ikaai kilojoule per mol (kJ per mol) hai. Ek kilojoule = 1000 joule. Isse zyada ikaai ka matlab yahan nahi chahiye.\n\n**Doosri ionisation energy (ek line):** pehla electron nikalne ke baad jo ion bachta hai, usse ek aur electron nikaalne ki energy **doosri ionisation energy** kehlaati hai, aur wo hamesha pehli se badi hoti hai (Part C mein dekhenge ki kitni badi)."
          },
          {
            "heading": "Part B: Periodic Trends: Down a Group and Across a Period",
            "headingHinglish": "Part B: Trends: group mein aur period mein",
            "body": "Let's apply our two core ideas. Think of Lily's grip on the rubber duck: when the electron is held close with a strong nuclear pull, removing it costs a lot of energy; when it sits far out behind many shielding shells, removing it is much easier.\n\n### Down a Group: Ionisation Energy Decreases\n\nLook at Group 1: Li, Na, K, ..., Cs. As we move down the group, **more electron shells are added**, placing the outermost electron farther and farther from the nucleus behind more layers of inner-shell **shielding**. Because the nucleus's grip weakens down the group, removing the outer electron becomes easier and easier.\n\nApproximate first ionisation energies (in kJ per mol): **Li 520, Na 496, K 419, Cs 376.**\n\n```\n    Group 1: First Ionisation Energy (approx.)     1 # = 20 kJ per mol\n\n    Li   ##########################   520\n    Na   #########################    496\n    K    #####################        419\n    Cs   ###################           376\n\n    Going down: more shells, outer electron farther out, IE decreases\n```\n\n> **Figure 2.3** – First ionisation energy decreases down Group 1: Li holds its outer electron tightest, while Cs holds its outer electron most loosely.\n\nWe see the exact same trend in Group 2: Mg is about 738 kJ per mol and Ca (one row below Mg) is about 590 kJ per mol — Ca's outer electrons sit one shell farther out, so they are easier to remove.\n\n### Across a Period: Ionisation Energy Increases\n\nMoving from left to right across a period, the number of shells stays constant while **the number of protons increases**. As a result, the inward nuclear pull grows stronger, the atom shrinks (Topic 1), and the outer electrons are held more tightly. Therefore, **ionisation energy increases across a period**, reaching its **highest peak at the noble gas** at the end of the row.\n\nPeriod 3: **Na ~496, Mg ~738, Ar ~1521 kJ per mol.**\nPeriod 2: **Li ~520, Ne ~2081 kJ per mol.**\n\n```\n    Period 3 (from start to end)                   1 # = 50 kJ per mol\n\n    Na   ##########                 496\n    Mg   ###############            738\n    Ar   ##############################   1521     (noble gas: highest in row)\n\n    Period 2\n\n    Li   ##########                 520\n    Ne   ##########################################   2081\n```\n\n> **Figure 2.4** – Across a period, from the alkali metal on the far left (Na, Li) to the noble gas on the far right (Ar, Ne), ionisation energy climbs sharply: same number of shells, more protons, tighter grip.\n\n**A quick note for higher classes:** Between a few adjacent elements in the middle of a period (such as from Mg to Al), there is a small dip in IE caused by subshell structure, which you will study in higher classes. The overall trend across any period remains a strong **increase from left to right**.\n\n**One-Line Summary of the Grip:** Down a group = farther out + more shielding = **lower IE**. Across a period = more protons + same shells = **higher IE**.",
            "bodyHinglish": "Ab wahi do ideas lagate hain. Khilone ki kahani yaad karo: pakad kasi ho (nuclear pull zyada) ya door ho (shells zyada) to nikalne ka zor kitna badalta hai.\n\n### Group mein upar se neeche: IE ghat-ti hai\n\nGroup 1 mein Li, Na, K, ..., Cs. Neeche utarte hue **shells badhti jaati hain**, baahari electron nucleus se door hota jaata hai, aur beech mein zyada shells ka **parda** bhi hota hai. Pakad dheeli hoti jaati hai, isliye electron nikaalna aasan hota jaata hai.\n\nLagbhag values (first IE, kJ per mol): **Li 520, Na 496, K 419, Cs 376.**\n\n```\n   Group 1: pehli ionisation energy (lagbhag)     1 # = 20 kJ per mol\n\n   Li   ##########################   520\n   Na   #########################    496\n   K    #####################        419\n   Cs   ###################           376\n\n   Neeche jao: shells badhti hain, baahari electron door, IE ghat-ti hai\n```\n\n> **Figure 2.3** – Group 1 mein IE neeche utarte hue kam hoti jaati hai: Li ke baahari electron ki pakad sabse kasi, Cs ki sabse dheeli. (Cs sirf group ke order mein naam ke liye hai.)\n\nIsi tarah Group 2 mein Mg lagbhag 738 aur Ca lagbhag 590: Ca neeche hai, usse electron aasani se nikalta hai.\n\n### Period mein baayen se daayen: IE badhti hai\n\nEk hi period mein shells ki ginti wahi rehti hai, par **proton badhte jaate hain**. To nucleus ki kheench badhti hai, atom chhota hota jaata hai (Topic 1), aur baahari electron aur kas ke pakda jaata hai. IE **badhti hai**, aur period ke **end par noble gas mein sabse zyada** hoti hai.\n\nPeriod 3: **Na lagbhag 496, Mg lagbhag 738, Ar lagbhag 1521.**\nPeriod 2: **Li lagbhag 520, Ne lagbhag 2081.**\n\n```\n   Period 3 (shuruat se ant tak)                  1 # = 50 kJ per mol\n\n   Na   ##########                 496\n   Mg   ###############            738\n   Ar   ##############################   1521     (noble gas: sabse oonchi)\n\n   Period 2\n\n   Li   ##########                 520\n   Ne   ##########################################   2081\n```\n\n> **Figure 2.4** – Ek period mein shuruat ke metal (Na, Li) se noble gas (Ar, Ne) tak ionisation energy bahut badh jaati hai: same shells, zyada proton, kasi pakad.\n\n**Ek line aage ki class ke liye:** Period ke beech ke kuch tatvon mein (jaise Mg se Al) IE ek chhote ulte kadam se girti hai, jiska reason aage ki class mein aata hai. Bada trend wahi rehta hai: **period mein badhna**.\n\n**Pakad ka mantra:** group mein neeche = door + parda = IE kam. Period mein aage = zyada proton, wahi shells = IE zyada."
          },
          {
            "heading": "Part C: Removing Electrons One After Another: Successive Ionisation Energies",
            "headingHinglish": "Part C: Ek ke baad ek electron: successive ionisation energies",
            "body": "Now for a fascinating question: once the first electron is removed, what does it cost to remove a second electron, or a third? The numbers reveal the exact reason why **sodium only forms Na⁺ and never Na²⁺**, whereas **magnesium forms Mg²⁺**!\n\n### The Case of Sodium (Na)\n\nSodium's electron configuration is 2, 8, 1. Compare its first and second ionisation energies:\n\n| Which Electron Is Removed? | Ionisation Energy (kJ per mol, approx.) |\n|---|---|\n| 1st electron (the single outer 3rd-shell electron) | 496 |\n| 2nd electron (pulled from the filled 2, 8 inner shell) | 4562 |\n\nRemoving the second electron costs **more than 9 times as much energy** as removing the first (4562 ÷ 496 = 9.2)!\n\n**Why such a massive jump?** The first electron was sitting all alone in the 3rd shell, far from the nucleus and shielded by 10 inner electrons (2, 8). Once it leaves, the second electron must be ripped out of the **completely filled 2nd shell, which lies much closer to the nucleus** with far less shielding! Furthermore, all 11 protons are now pulling on just 10 electrons. Because that inner shell is held with an iron grip, breaking into it requires an enormous leap in energy.\n\n### The Case of Magnesium (Mg)\n\nMagnesium's electron configuration is 2, 8, 2. Look at its first three ionisation energies:\n\n| Which Electron Is Removed? | Ionisation Energy (kJ per mol, approx.) |\n|---|---|\n| 1st electron (from the 3rd shell) | 738 |\n| 2nd electron (the second 3rd-shell electron) | 1451 |\n| 3rd electron (pulled from the filled 2, 8 inner shell) | 7733 |\n\nHere, the second IE is about twice the first (1451 ÷ 738 = 1.97 — a normal increase because you are pulling an electron away from a positively charged Mg⁺ ion). But the **third IE jumps by more than 5.3 times** (7733 ÷ 1451 = 5.3)! The giant leap happens at the **third electron**, right when we try to break into the filled 2, 8 inner shell.\n\n```\n    Successive Ionisation Energies (1 # = 200 kJ per mol, approx.)\n\n    Na   1st    ##                                      496\n         2nd    #######################                       4562  <-- HUGE JUMP!\n\n    Mg   1st    ####                                    738\n         2nd    #######                                1451\n         3rd    #######################################   7733  <-- HUGE JUMP!\n```\n\n> **Figure 2.5** – In sodium, the giant energy jump occurs right after the 1st electron (at the 2nd IE). In magnesium, the first two steps are modest and the giant jump occurs after the 2nd electron (at the 3rd IE). The giant jump always marks the boundary where you break into a filled inner shell.\n\n### What Does This Tell Us?\n\n- **Sodium:** The 1st electron comes off easily (496), while the 2nd is prohibitively expensive (4562). So in chemical reactions, Na loses **only 1 electron** to form **Na⁺** (1 valence electron).\n- **Magnesium:** The first 2 electrons come off at reasonable cost (738 and 1451), while the 3rd is prohibitively expensive (7733). So Mg loses **2 electrons** to form **Mg²⁺** (2 valence electrons).\n- **General Rule for Counting Valence Electrons:** Look for the **giant jump** in successive ionisation energies. **The number of electrons removed *before* the giant jump equals the number of valence electrons!**\n\nIn Class 9, we linked valence electrons to group numbers and ionic charges; successive ionisation energies give us the direct **experimental proof** of that rule!",
            "bodyHinglish": "Ab ek dilchasp sawaal: pehla electron nikal gaya, to doosra? Wo kuch aur zyada mehnat maangega, par kitna zyada? Isi mein poori kahani chhupi hai ki **Na sirf Na⁺ kyun banata hai, Na²⁺ kyun nahi**.\n\n### Na ka case\n\nNa ke shells 2, 8, 1. Lagbhag values:\n\n| Kaun sa electron | Ionisation energy (kJ per mol, lagbhag) |\n|---|---|\n| Pehla (baahari 1 electron) | 496 |\n| Doosra (ab 2, 8 wali bhari shell se) | 4562 |\n\nDoosra electron pehle se **lagbhag 9 guna** mehnga hai (4562 ÷ 496 = 9.2).\n\n**Kyun?** Pehla electron teesri shell ka akela electron tha, nucleus se door aur 2, 8 ke parde ke peeche. Wo nikal gaya. Ab doosra electron **doosri shell ki bhari hui, nucleus ke bilkul paas wali shell** se aana hai. Nucleus mein abhi bhi 11 proton hain, par ab sirf 10 electron unhe baand rahe hain (pehle 11 the). Aur ye electron bahut paas hai, parde ke andar. Pakad bahut kasi hai, isliye energy bahut lagti hai.\n\n### Mg ka case\n\nMg ke shells 2, 8, 2. Lagbhag values:\n\n| Kaun sa electron | Ionisation energy (kJ per mol, lagbhag) |\n|---|---|\n| Pehla | 738 |\n| Doosra | 1451 |\n| Teesra (ab 2, 8 wali bhari shell se) | 7733 |\n\nPehle se doosra lagbhag 2 guna (1451 ÷ 738 = 1.97), par doosre se teesra lagbhag **5.3 guna** (7733 ÷ 1451 = 5.3). Bada chhalaang **teesre electron par** aaya, jab bhari shell tootne lagi.\n\n```\n   Successive IE ke bar (1 # = 200 kJ per mol, lagbhag)\n\n   Na   1st    ##                                      496\n        2nd    #######################                       4562\n\n   Mg   1st    ####                                    738\n        2nd    #######                                1451\n        3rd    #######################################   7733\n\n```\n\n> **Figure 2.5** – Sodium mein pehle electron ke baad ek bahut bada ooncha kadam aata hai (2nd), magnesium mein do chhote kadam ke baad bada ooncha kadam (3rd). Bada kadam tab aata hai jab bhari hui andar ki shell tootne lagti hai.\n\n### Ye kya batata hai?\n\n- Na: pehla aasan, doosra bahut mushkil, isliye Na **sirf 1 electron** deta hai. Valence electron 1 hain, ion **Na⁺**.\n- Mg: pehle do (738, 1451) mein chhota farq, teesra bahut zyada, isliye Mg **2 electron** deta hai. Ion **Mg²⁺**.\n- Isse seedha pata chalta hai ki **valence electron kitne hain**: jahan IE mein **bada chhalaang** aaye, us se pehle jitne electron nikale gaye, utne valence electron.\n\nClass 9 mein valency aur group ka rishta tha: ye usi ka **energy wala saboot** hai."
          },
          {
            "heading": "Part D: Noble Gases Sit at the Highest Peaks",
            "headingHinglish": "Part D: Noble gases sabse oonchi chotiyon par",
            "body": "Noble gases have **completely filled outer shells** (He: 2; Ne: 2, 8; Ar: 2, 8, 8) and the highest proton counts in their respective periods. Because their outer electrons are pulled tightly inward, noble gases have the **highest ionisation energies in their periods**: **He ~2372, Ne ~2081, Ar ~1521 kJ per mol.**\n\nNotice also that as you go **down Group 18** (He → Ne → Ar), ionisation energy decreases because each step down adds a new electron shell — the exact same group trend we saw in Part B.\n\n```\n    Group 18: IE decreases down the group               1 # = 50 kJ per mol\n\n    He   ###############################################   2372\n    Ne   ##########################################        2081\n    Ar   ##############################                    1521\n```\n\n> **Figure 2.6** – He, Ne, and Ar have exceptionally high ionisation energies (which explains why noble gases are chemically unreactive or \"inert\"), while still showing the standard decrease down the group as more shells are added.",
            "bodyHinglish": "Noble gas ka baahari shell **bhari hui** hoti hai (He ki 2, Ne ki 2, 8, Ar ki 2, 8, 8). Electron nucleus ke paas aur kas ke pakde hain, isliye inki IE apne period mein **sabse zyada** hoti hai: **He lagbhag 2372, Ne lagbhag 2081, Ar lagbhag 1521 kJ per mol.**\n\nDhyan do: noble gas ki IE apne **group mein neeche** bhi ghat-ti hai (He > Ne > Ar), kyunki neeche shells badhti hain. Ye group wala trend wahi hai jo Part B mein tha.\n\n```\n   Group 18: IE neeche utarte hue ghat-ti hai          1 # = 50 kJ per mol\n\n   He   ###############################################   2372\n   Ne   ##########################################        2081\n   Ar   ##############################                    1521\n```\n\n> **Figure 2.6** – He, Ne, Ar: IE bahut oonchi hai (isliye ye gas aasani se react nahi karti: Class 9 ka \"inert\" ka ek reason), par neeche utarte hue phir bhi thodi ghat-ti hai."
          },
          {
            "heading": "Part E: How Cations Form and Reactivity in Group 1",
            "headingHinglish": "Part E: Cation kaise bante hain aur Group 1 ki reactivity",
            "body": "### Forming Cations\n\nAn element with a **low ionisation energy** gives up its outer electron(s) easily and readily forms **cations**. That is why the metals of Group 1 and Group 2 (K, Na, Ca, Mg) are the champions of cation formation: their ionisation energies are low. These are the exact cations we met in ionic compounds (Chapter 2) and migrating toward the **cathode** during electrolysis (Chapter 4).\n\n### Reactivity Trend in Group 1\n\nIn Class 9 ([periodic-table-part5.md](../../../class9/chemistry/periodic-table/periodic-table-part5.md)), we learned that alkali metal reactivity increases down the group: **Li < Na < K**. Now we have the exact energy explanation: when an alkali metal reacts, it must **lose its outer electron**. **The lower the ionisation energy, the more easily the atom loses its electron, and the more vigorously the metal reacts!** Because K has a lower IE (419 kJ/mol) than Na (496 kJ/mol), potassium loses its electron more readily and reacts even more violently than sodium. (**Safety Note:** The reaction of alkali metals with water is strictly for reading or a teacher demonstration behind a safety screen — students must never touch or handle sodium or potassium.)",
            "bodyHinglish": "### Cation ka bantaa\n\nJis tatva ki IE **kam** hoti hai, wo electron aasani se chhodta hai aur **cation** banata hai. Isliye Group 1 aur 2 ke tatva (K, Na, Mg, Ca) ke metals cation banane mein maahir hain: inki IE kam hai. Ye wahi cations hain jo Chapter 2 ke ionic compounds mein aur Chapter 4 mein **cathode** ki taraf jaate hain (Electrolysis).\n\n### Group 1 ki reactivity\n\nClass 9 mein hum ne dekha tha ki Li < Na < K reactivity badhti hai ([periodic-table-part5.md](../../../class9/chemistry/periodic-table/periodic-table-part5.md)). Ab uska energy-wala reason: reaction mein alkali metal ko electron **dena** hota hai. **Jis ki IE kam, wo electron jaldi deta hai, isliye zyada tezi se react karta hai.** K ki IE (419) Na ki (496) se kam hai, isliye K, Na se zyada tezi se react karta hai. Ye vyavhaar paani ke saath teacher ke demo mein dikhaya jaata hai: **potassium zyada tez, hinsak tareeke se**. **Sirf padhne ke liye ya sirf teacher demo:** koi student kabhi sodium ya potassium ko nahi chhuega, aur na hi koi prayog karega."
          },
          {
            "heading": "Part F: Solved Examples",
            "headingHinglish": "Part F: Solved examples",
            "body": "### Example 1. Arrange Li, Na, K in Increasing Order of First Ionisation Energy\n\n- All three are in Group 1. Li has 2 shells, Na has 3 shells, and K has 4 shells.\n- Going down the group: more shells → outer electron is farther from the nucleus with more inner-shell shielding → weaker grip.\n- Therefore, IE decreases down the group, so in increasing order: **K < Na < Li**.\n\n**Check:** Approximate values: 419 < 496 < 520 kJ per mol ✅.\n\n### Example 2. Which Has the Higher Ionisation Energy: Mg or Ca?\n\n- Mg has 3 shells (2, 8, 2); Ca has 4 shells (2, 8, 8, 2). Both are in Group 2.\n- Ca's outer electrons sit one shell farther out behind more shielding shells.\n- Therefore, **Mg has the higher ionisation energy** (and Ca has the lower IE).\n\n**Check:** Mg ~738 > Ca ~590 kJ per mol ✅.\n\n### Example 3. Arrange Na, Mg, Ar in Increasing Order Across Period 3\n\n- All three are in Period 3 (3 shells). Protons: Na 11, Mg 12, Ar 18.\n- Same number of shells + more protons = stronger nuclear pull = tighter grip on outer electrons.\n- Increasing order: **Na < Mg < Ar**.\n\n**Check:** 496 < 738 < 1521 kJ per mol ✅ (with the noble gas Ar at the top).\n\n### Example 4. Why Does Sodium Form Na⁺ and Never Na²⁺?\n\n- For Na: 1st IE is ~496 kJ/mol, whereas 2nd IE jumps to ~4562 kJ/mol.\n- After the single 3rd-shell electron is removed, the 2nd electron would have to be pulled from the stable, filled 2nd shell (2, 8) close to the nucleus.\n- Chemical reactions cannot supply that huge 4562 kJ/mol cost, so sodium loses **only 1 electron** to form **Na⁺**.\n\n**Check:** Na → Na⁺ + e⁻ leaves 11 − 1 = 10 electrons and 11 protons, giving a +1 charge ✅.\n\n### Example 5. Why Does Magnesium Form Mg²⁺?\n\n- For Mg: 1st IE is ~738, 2nd IE is ~1451, and 3rd IE jumps to ~7733 kJ/mol.\n- The giant jump occurs after the first two electrons are removed, so Mg loses **2 electrons** to form **Mg²⁺**: Mg → Mg²⁺ + 2e⁻.\n\n**Check:** Removing 2 electrons from (2, 8, 2) leaves the filled (2, 8) core (10 electrons and 12 protons, charge +2) ✅.\n\n### Example 6. Finding Valence Electrons from a Table of Successive IEs\n\nAn unknown element X has the following approximate successive ionisation energies (in kJ per mol): **578, 1817, 2745, 11577.**\n\n- 578 → 1817: about 3.1× increase.\n- 1817 → 2745: about 1.5× increase.\n- 2745 → 11577: about **4.2× increase — a massive jump of nearly 9000 kJ/mol!**\n- Because the giant jump occurs on the 4th electron, the first **3 electrons** are in the outer shell: X has **3 valence electrons**, belongs to **Group 13**, and forms the **X³⁺** ion (this element is aluminium, Al³⁺).\n\n**Check:** Al has configuration (2, 8, 3); removing 3 electrons leaves the (2, 8) core (10 electrons, 13 protons, charge +3) ✅.\n\n### Example 7. Energy Calculations Using Moles\n\n**Question 1:** How much energy is required to remove one electron from every atom in 2 moles of gaseous sodium atoms? (First IE of Na ≈ 496 kJ per mol.)\n\n- Energy = 2 mol × 496 kJ/mol = **992 kJ**.\n- **Check:** 1 mol takes 496 kJ, so 2 mol takes double = 992 kJ ✅.\n\n**Question 2:** How much total energy is required to convert 1 mole of gaseous magnesium atoms completely into Mg²⁺ ions? (Use 1st IE ≈ 738 and 2nd IE ≈ 1451 kJ per mol.)\n\n- Total energy per mole = 738 + 1451 = **2189 kJ**.\n- **Check:** 2189 − 738 = 1451 ✅.\n\n### Example 8. Comparing Group 18 Noble Gases: He, Ne, Ar\n\nWhich noble gas has the highest first ionisation energy among He, Ne, and Ar?\n\n- Helium has only 1 shell (2 electrons right next to the nucleus with zero inner-shell shielding).\n- Moving down to Ne (2 shells) and Ar (3 shells) increases distance and shielding, so IE decreases down the group.\n- Order: **He > Ne > Ar**.\n\n**Check:** ~2372 > ~2081 > ~1521 kJ per mol ✅.\n\n### Example 9. Easiest vs. Hardest to Ionise Across Period 3\n\nAmong Na, Mg, and Ar in Period 3, which atom loses an outer electron most easily, and which loses one with the greatest difficulty?\n\n- **Most easily:** **Na** (lowest IE, 496 kJ/mol).\n- **Greatest difficulty:** **Ar** (highest IE, 1521 kJ/mol).\n\n**Check:** Na is the largest atom in Period 3 (Topic 1), so its outer electron feels the weakest grip ✅.",
            "bodyHinglish": "### Example 1. Li, Na, K ko IE ke order mein likho (sabse kam se sabse zyada)\n\n- Teeno Group 1 mein hain. Li ke 2 shells, Na ke 3, K ke 4.\n- Group mein neeche: shells badhti hain, baahari electron door, parda zyada, pakad dheeli.\n- Isliye IE ghat-ti hai: **K < Na < Li**.\n\n**Jaanch:** values lagbhag 419 < 496 < 520 ✅.\n\n### Example 2. Mg aur Ca mein kiski IE zyada?\n\n- Mg: 3 shells (2, 8, 2). Ca: 4 shells (2, 8, 8, 2). Dono Group 2.\n- Ca ka baahari electron zyada door aur zyada shells ke parde ke peeche hai.\n- Isliye **Mg ki IE zyada**, Ca ki kam.\n\n**Jaanch:** Mg lagbhag 738, Ca lagbhag 590, aur 738 > 590 ✅.\n\n### Example 3. Na, Mg, Ar: period mein badhta hua order\n\n- Teeno Period 3 mein: 3 shells. Proton Na 11, Mg 12, Ar 18.\n- Shells wahi, proton badhe, nuclear pull badhi, electron kas ke pakda hai.\n- Order: **Na < Mg < Ar**.\n\n**Jaanch:** lagbhag 496 < 738 < 1521 ✅. (Ar noble gas hai, isliye sabse oonchi.)\n\n### Example 4. Na⁺ hi kyun, Na²⁺ kyun nahi?\n\n- Na: 1st IE lagbhag 496, 2nd IE lagbhag 4562.\n- Pehle electron ke baad 2nd bahut mehnga, kyunki wo ab bhari hui 2, 8 shell se aata hai.\n- Reaction mein itni zyada energy dene ka koi faayda nahi, isliye Na **sirf ek** electron deta hai.\n\n**Jaanch:** Na → Na⁺ + e⁻ likho. Total electron 11 − 1 = 10 aur proton 11, charge +1 ✅.\n\n### Example 5. Mg²⁺ kyun?\n\n- Mg: 738, 1451, 7733 lagbhag.\n- Pehle do ke baad bada chhalaang. Mg sirf 2 electron deta hai.\n- Mg → Mg²⁺ + 2e⁻.\n\n**Jaanch:** shells 2, 8, 2 mein se 2 electron nikle, bache 2, 8 (10 electron) aur 12 proton, charge +2 ✅.\n\n### Example 6. Ek anjaan tatva: IE ke numbers se valence electron\n\nEk tatva ke lagbhag IE (kJ per mol) hain: **578, 1817, 2745, 11577.**\n\n- 578 → 1817: lagbhag 3.1 guna.\n- 1817 → 2745: lagbhag 1.5 guna.\n- 2745 → 11577: lagbhag **4.2 guna**, bada chhalaang.\n- Bada chhalaang chautha electron nikalte hue aaya, to pehle 3 electron aasani se nikle: **3 valence electron**.\n- Group 13 ka tatva, ion X³⁺. (Ye Al hai: Al³⁺.)\n\n**Jaanch:** Al ke shells 2, 8, 3. 3 nikalne par 2, 8 bachti hai, 10 electron aur 13 proton, charge +3 ✅.\n\n### Example 7. Kitni energy chahiye? (chhota sa numerical)\n\n**Sawaal 1:** Sodium ke 2 mol atoms se ek-ek electron nikalne mein kitni energy lagegi? (Na ki first IE lagbhag 496 kJ per mol.)\n\n- 2 × 496 = **992 kJ**.\n\n**Jaanch:** 1 mol ke liye 496, to 2 mol ke liye double = 992 ✅.\n\n**Sawaal 2:** Magnesium ke 1 mol atoms ko Mg²⁺ ions mein badalne mein kul kitni energy? (Lagbhag 738 + 1451.)\n\n- 738 + 1451 = **2189 kJ**.\n\n**Jaanch:** 2189 − 738 = 1451 ✅.\n\n### Example 8. Group 18 ki tulna\n\nHe, Ne, Ar: kiski IE sabse zyada?\n\n- He ka sirf 1 shell hai, electron nucleus ke bilkul paas aur beech mein koi parda nahi.\n- Neeche utarte hue (Ne, Ar) shells badhti hain, IE ghat-ti hai.\n- Order: **He > Ne > Ar**.\n\n**Jaanch:** lagbhag 2372 > 2081 > 1521 ✅.\n\n### Example 9. Ek hi period ka sabse bada aur sabse chhota\n\nPeriod 3 ke Na, Mg, Ar mein se kis ka atom electron sabse aasani se chhodta hai, kiska sabse mushkil se?\n\n- Sabse aasani se: **Na** (IE sabse kam, 496).\n- Sabse mushkil se: **Ar** (IE sabse zyada, 1521).\n\n**Jaanch:** Na ke atom ka size sabse bada hai (Topic 1), isliye pakad dheeli ✅."
          },
          {
            "heading": "Common Pitfalls to Avoid",
            "headingHinglish": "Aam galtiyan (bachke rehna)",
            "body": "**Mistake 1: Thinking that \"high ionisation energy\" means an atom loses electrons easily.**\n\nThis is completely backwards! Ionisation energy is the **price you must pay** to remove an electron. A high price means the electron is **hard** to remove, not easy! Think back to Lily hugging the rubber duck tightly against her chest: high effort required = hard to take away.\n\n**Let's prove it with three elements:** Na (496), Ne (2081), and K (419). Which atom loses an electron most easily?\n\n- Low IE = easy to remove. K has the lowest price (419), followed by Na (496), while Ne has a sky-high price (2081).\n- Ease of losing an electron: **K > Na > Ne**.\n- Because neon's IE is huge (2081), neon refuses to lose electrons — which is why neon is an unreactive noble gas!\n\n**Two quick self-check questions whenever you feel confused:**\n1. Between K and Ne, which one readily forms a +1 cation? K⁺ (easily); Ne⁺ never forms in normal chemistry.\n2. If \"high IE\" meant \"loses electrons easily,\" then neon would be the most reactive metal in Period 2 — when in reality it is an inert gas!\n\nMemory trick: **Think of IE as a price tag in dollars — the higher the price tag, the harder it is to buy that electron away from the atom.**",
            "bodyHinglish": "**Galti: \"Jiski ionisation energy zyada, wo electron aasani se chhodta hai.\"** Ye ulta hai.\n\nIonisation energy electron **nikalne ki keemat** hai, yaani zor jo lagana padta hai. Keemat zyada = nikalna mushkil. Khilone ki kahani mein: jab khilona kas ke pakda ho, to nikalne mein zyada zor lagta hai; aasani se kabhi nahi nikalta.\n\n**Demonstration:** teen tatva lo: Na (496), Ne (2081), K (419). Kaun electron sabse aasani se chhodega?\n\n- Kam IE = aasan nikalna. K sabse kam (419), phir Na (496), phir Ne (2081).\n- Isliye order electron chhodne ki aasaani mein: **K > Na > Ne**.\n- Ne ki IE sabse zyada hai, to wo electron chhodne mein sabse **kamzor** hai, isliye hi Ne aasani se react nahi karti (Class 9 ka inert gas).\n\n**Do chhote sawaal se jaanch:**\n\n1. K aur Ne: kaun sa cation banata hai? K⁺ (aasani se), Ne⁺ lagbhag kabhi nahi.\n2. Agar IE zyada ka matlab \"aasani se chhodta\" hota, to Ne sabse zyada electron chhodne wala hota, par asli mein wo sabse nishkriya gas hai.\n\nYaad rakhne ka tareeka: **IE ka number paise ki tarah socho: jitni zyada keemat, utna kam aasan.**"
          },
          {
            "heading": "Quick Recap",
            "headingHinglish": "Quick Recap",
            "body": "- **Ionisation energy (IE)** = the energy required to remove the outermost electron from an isolated gaseous atom: M(g) + energy → M⁺(g) + e⁻, measured in **kJ per mol**.\n- Because energy must be put in to overcome nuclear attraction, IE is always positive.\n- **Down a group:** More shells + more shielding → outer electron is farther out → IE **decreases** (e.g., Li 520, Na 496, K 419, Cs 376 kJ/mol).\n- **Across a period:** Same shells + more protons → stronger nuclear pull → IE **increases** (e.g., Na 496, Mg 738, Ar 1521 kJ/mol).\n- **Noble gases** have the highest IE in their respective periods (He 2372, Ne 2081, Ar 1521 kJ/mol).\n- **Successive ionisation energies:** The 2nd IE is always larger than the 1st. A **giant jump** occurs when an electron must be removed from a filled inner shell (2nd IE for Na at 4562; 3rd IE for Mg at 7733).\n- Counting how many electrons are removed *before* the giant jump tells you the number of **valence electrons** and the cation charge (Na⁺, Mg²⁺, Al³⁺).\n- **Low IE = loses electrons easily** = reactive metal that readily forms cations (e.g., K is more reactive than Na because 419 < 496).\n- Never confuse high IE with losing electrons easily — high IE means the atom holds its electrons tightly!",
            "bodyHinglish": "- **Ionisation energy** = gas ki haalat ke ek akele atom se baahari electron nikalne ki energy: M → M⁺ + e⁻. Ikaai kJ per mol.\n- Ye energy **dene** par hi electron nikalta hai, isliye IE positive hoti hai.\n- **Group mein neeche:** shells badhti hain, electron door aur parda zyada, IE **ghat-ti** hai (Li 520, Na 496, K 419, Cs 376, lagbhag).\n- **Period mein aage:** shells wahi, proton zyada, pull zyada, IE **badhti** hai (Na 496, Mg 738, Ar 1521, lagbhag). Beech ke tatvon mein chhote ulte kadam aage ki class mein.\n- **Noble gas** ki IE apne period mein sabse zyada (He 2372, Ne 2081, Ar 1521, lagbhag).\n- **Successive IE:** doosri hamesha pehli se badi. Bada chhalaang tab aata hai jab bhari andar ki shell tootne lagti hai: Na ka 2nd (4562), Mg ka 3rd (7733).\n- Chhalaange se **valence electron** ki ginti aur ion ka charge pata chalta hai: Na⁺, Mg²⁺, Al³⁺.\n- **Kam IE = electron aasani se chhodta hai** = achha cation banane wala metal. Group 1 mein K, Na se zyada tez reactive kyunki IE kam.\n- **Zyada IE ka matlab electron aasani se nikalta hai? Nahi, bilkul ulta.**"
          },
          {
            "heading": "Practice Questions",
            "headingHinglish": "Practice Questions",
            "body": "(All questions are for paper calculation. Reactions of alkali metals with water are strictly for reading or teacher demonstrations.)\n\n1. Arrange K, Ca and Ar in increasing order of first ionisation energy and give the reason in terms of shells and nuclear pull. (K and Ca are in Period 4 and Ar is in Period 3.)\n2. Cs lies below Li in Group 1. Which of the two loses its outer electron more easily? Explain using shells.\n3. An element X has these approximate successive ionisation energies in kJ per mol: 590, 1145, 4912. How many valence electrons does X have? To which group does it belong and what ion does it form? Explain from the numbers.\n4. For lithium, the first ionisation energy is about 520 kJ per mol and the second is about 7298 kJ per mol. Which ion does lithium form and why does it not form Li²⁺?\n5. How much energy is needed to remove one electron from each atom in 3 mol of potassium atoms? (First ionisation energy of K is about 419 kJ per mol.)\n6. How much energy is needed to convert 2 mol of calcium atoms completely into Ca²⁺ ions? (Take the first and second ionisation energies as about 590 and 1145 kJ per mol.)\n7. State True or False and give a reason for each:\n   (a) Neon has a higher first ionisation energy than sodium.\n   (b) Potassium has a higher first ionisation energy than sodium.\n   (c) Noble gases lose electrons the most easily in their period.\n   (d) The second ionisation energy of an atom is smaller than its first.\n   (e) Ionisation energy is released when an atom becomes a cation.\n8. Why is the first ionisation energy of helium the highest among He, Ne and Ar? Use the idea of shells and shielding.\n9. The first ionisation energy drops sharply from argon (about 1521 kJ per mol) to potassium (about 419 kJ per mol), even though potassium has one more proton. Explain why.\n10. A student says: \"Argon has a very high ionisation energy, so argon forms Ar⁺ ions most easily.\" Correct the student and explain.\n11. Na is in Group 1 (Period 3) and Ca is in Group 2 (Period 4). Their first ionisation energies are about 496 and 590 kJ per mol. Using only the idea of ionisation energy, which of the two would you expect to react more vigorously with cold water (described only, never tried)? Explain.\n12. Given the first ionisation energies (approximate, kJ per mol): K 419, Na 496, Ca 590, Mg 738. Arrange the four in order of how easily they lose an electron (easiest first), and explain the order of K and Na, and of Na and Mg, in one line each.\n13. Activity (adult nearby): hold a small soft toy tightly against your chest while a friend gently tries to take it, then hold it loosely at arm's length and let the friend try again. Match each case with an atom (small, strongly pulling or large, loosely held) and say which atom has the higher ionisation energy.\n\n### Answers\n\n1. K and Ca are in Period 4 (4 shells), while Ar is in Period 3 (3 shells). Between K and Ca (same 4 shells), Ca has one more proton, giving a stronger nuclear pull and higher IE: K < Ca. Ar has only 3 shells (outer electrons closer to the nucleus), 18 protons, and a completely filled outer shell, giving it the highest IE of the three. Order: **K (~419) < Ca (~590) < Ar (~1521)**. **Check:** 419 < 590 < 1521 ✅.\n2. **Cs** loses its outer electron much more easily. Cs has many more electron shells (6 shells vs. 2 in Li), so its outer electron is far from the nucleus and heavily shielded by inner shells, making the grip weak. Approximate values: Cs 376 < Li 520 kJ/mol ✅.\n3. 590 → 1145 is a ~1.9× increase, whereas 1145 → 4912 is a **4.3× jump** (breaking into a filled inner shell). Because 2 electrons are removed before the big jump, X has **2 valence electrons**, belongs to **Group 2**, and forms the **X²⁺** ion (this is calcium, Ca²⁺). **Check:** Ca has configuration (2, 8, 8, 2); losing 2 electrons leaves the stable (2, 8, 8) core with charge +2 ✅.\n4. Li has configuration (2, 1) — 1 valence electron. Removing the first electron costs 520 kJ/mol, but removing a second costs 7298 kJ/mol (~14 times as much!) because it must be pulled from the filled 1st shell right next to the nucleus. Therefore, lithium forms **Li⁺** and never Li²⁺. **Check:** 7298 ÷ 520 = 14.0 ✅.\n5. Energy = 3 mol × 419 kJ/mol = **1257 kJ**. **Check:** 1257 ÷ 3 = 419 ✅.\n6. Energy per mole of Ca = 590 + 1145 = 1735 kJ/mol. For 2 moles: 2 × 1735 = **3470 kJ**. **Check:** 3470 ÷ 2 = 1735 ✅.\n7. (a) **True** ✅: Ne (~2081) > Na (~496) because Ne has a filled 2nd shell with 10 protons holding its electrons tightly. (b) **False** ❌: K (~419) has a *lower* IE than Na (~496) because K has 4 shells compared to Na's 3. (c) **False** ❌: noble gases have the *highest* IE in their period, so they lose electrons with the greatest difficulty. (d) **False** ❌: the 2nd IE is always *larger* than the 1st because you are pulling an electron away from a positively charged ion. (e) **False** ❌: ionisation energy must be *absorbed* (put in) to remove an electron, never released.\n8. Helium has only 1 shell (2 electrons very close to the nucleus) and zero inner-shell shielding. Moving down to Ne and Ar adds shells and inner-shell shielding, weakening the grip on the outer electrons. **He (~2372) > Ne (~2081) > Ar (~1521)** ✅.\n9. Argon has 3 shells with a completely filled outer shell (2, 8, 8). Although potassium has 19 protons (one more than Ar), its 19th electron goes into a **brand-new 4th shell** far from the nucleus, shielded by 18 inner electrons (2, 8, 8). The increased distance and shielding outweigh the single extra proton, causing IE to drop sharply from 1521 to 419 kJ/mol. **Check:** 1521 > 419 ✅.\n10. The student has it backwards: **a high ionisation energy means it is very difficult to remove an electron**, not easy. Because Ar has a very high IE (~1521 kJ/mol), forming Ar⁺ requires a huge energy input, so Ar does not form cations in normal chemistry. Metals with low IE (like K and Na) form cations easily.\n11. Because Na has a lower first IE (~496 kJ/mol) than Ca (~590 kJ/mol) — and Na only needs to lose 1 electron whereas Ca must lose 2 — **Na** loses its valence electron more easily and reacts much more vigorously with cold water (matching our Chapter 2 reactivity observations; teacher demonstration only). **Check:** 496 < 590 ✅.\n12. Order of losing an electron (easiest first = lowest IE first): **K (419), Na (496), Ca (590), Mg (738)**. K vs. Na: both are in Group 1, but K has 4 shells vs. Na's 3, so K's outer electron is farther out and easier to remove. Na vs. Mg: both are in Period 3 (3 shells), but Mg has 12 protons vs. Na's 11, pulling its outer shell tighter and raising its IE. **Check:** 419 < 496 < 590 < 738 ✅.\n13. Toy held tightly against the chest = a **small atom with strong nuclear pull**; it has a **higher ionisation energy**. Toy held loosely at arm's length = a **large atom with a distant, shielded outer electron**; it has a **lower ionisation energy**.\n\n---\n\nEthan jotted a two-line summary in his notebook: \"Down a group, IE drops; across a period, IE climbs. Big jump in successive IEs = inner shell reached!\" Mr. Davies nodded: \"Just remember the golden rule: a bigger price tag means it's harder to remove the electron.\"\n\n**What's Next?** In Topic 3, we flip the coin over: what happens when an isolated atom **gains** an extra electron, how much energy is released, and which elements are the most eager to grab an incoming electron — illustrated by an outfielder diving to catch a fly ball!",
            "bodyHinglish": "(Sab sawaal paper par hal karne ke liye hain. Alkali metals aur paani ka reaction sirf padhne ke liye ya teacher demo ke liye hai.)\n\n1. Arrange K, Ca and Ar in increasing order of first ionisation energy and give the reason in terms of shells and nuclear pull. (K and Ca are in Period 4 and Ar is in Period 3.)\n2. Cs lies below Li in Group 1. Which of the two loses its outer electron more easily? Explain using shells.\n3. An element X has these approximate successive ionisation energies in kJ per mol: 590, 1145, 4912. How many valence electrons does X have? To which group does it belong and what ion does it form? Explain from the numbers.\n4. For lithium, the first ionisation energy is about 520 kJ per mol and the second is about 7298 kJ per mol. Which ion does lithium form and why does it not form Li²⁺?\n5. How much energy is needed to remove one electron from each atom in 3 mol of potassium atoms? (First ionisation energy of K is about 419 kJ per mol.)\n6. How much energy is needed to convert 2 mol of calcium atoms completely into Ca²⁺ ions? (Take the first and second ionisation energies as about 590 and 1145 kJ per mol.)\n7. State True or False and give a reason for each:\n   (a) Neon has a higher first ionisation energy than sodium.\n   (b) Potassium has a higher first ionisation energy than sodium.\n   (c) Noble gases lose electrons the most easily in their period.\n   (d) The second ionisation energy of an atom is smaller than its first.\n   (e) Ionisation energy is released when an atom becomes a cation.\n8. Why is the first ionisation energy of helium the highest among He, Ne and Ar? Use the idea of shells and shielding.\n9. The first ionisation energy drops sharply from argon (about 1521 kJ per mol) to potassium (about 419 kJ per mol), even though potassium has one more proton. Explain why.\n10. A student says: \"Argon has a very high ionisation energy, so argon forms Ar⁺ ions most easily.\" Correct the student and explain.\n11. Na is in Group 1 (Period 3) and Ca is in Group 2 (Period 4). Their first ionisation energies are about 496 and 590 kJ per mol. Using only the idea of ionisation energy, which of the two would you expect to react more vigorously with cold water (described only, never tried)? Explain.\n12. Given the first ionisation energies (lagbhag, kJ per mol): K 419, Na 496, Ca 590, Mg 738. Arrange the four in order of how easily they lose an electron (easiest first), and explain the order of K and Na, and of Na and Mg, in one line each.\n13. Activity (adult nearby): hold a small soft toy tightly against your chest while a friend gently tries to take it, then hold it loosely at arm's length and let the friend try again. Match each case with an atom (small, fast-pulling or large, loosely held) and say which atom has the higher ionisation energy.\n\n### Answers\n\n1. K aur Ca Period 4 mein hain (4 shells), Ar Period 3 mein (3 shells). K aur Ca mein shells wahi, par Ca mein ek proton zyada, to pull zyada aur IE zyada: K < Ca. Ar mein sirf 3 shells (electron paas), 18 proton, aur bhari baahari shell, isliye uski IE sabse zyada. Order: **K (lagbhag 419) < Ca (lagbhag 590) < Ar (lagbhag 1521)**. **Jaanch:** 419 < 590 < 1521 ✅.\n2. **Cs** zyada aasani se chhodta hai. Cs mein shells bahut zyada hain, baahari electron nucleus se bahut door aur bahut shells ke parde ke peeche hai, isliye pakad dheeli. Lagbhag values: Cs 376 < Li 520 ✅.\n3. 590 → 1145: lagbhag 1.9 guna. 1145 → 4912: lagbhag 4.3 guna, bada chhalaang. Isliye pehle **2 electron** aasani se nikle: **2 valence electron**, **Group 2**, ion **X²⁺**. (X Ca hai, Ca²⁺.) **Jaanch:** Ca ke shells 2, 8, 8, 2: 2 nikalne par 2, 8, 8 bachti hai, charge +2 ✅.\n4. Li ke shells 2, 1: 1 valence electron. 520 aasan, 7298 bahut mushkil (lagbhag 14 guna), kyunki doosra electron bhari hui paas wali 1st shell se aata. Isliye ion **Li⁺**, Li²⁺ nahi. **Jaanch:** 7298 ÷ 520 = 14.0 ✅.\n5. 3 × 419 = **1257 kJ**. **Jaanch:** 1257 ÷ 3 = 419 ✅.\n6. Har atom ke liye 590 + 1145 = 1735 kJ per mol. 2 mol ke liye 2 × 1735 = **3470 kJ**. **Jaanch:** 3470 ÷ 2 = 1735 ✅.\n7. (a) **True** ✅: Ne lagbhag 2081, Na lagbhag 496, Ne ki shell bhari aur electron kas ke pakda. (b) **False** ❌: K ki IE (419) Na (496) se kam, kyunki K mein shells zyada. (c) **False** ❌: noble gas ki IE sabse zyada hai, isliye wo electron sabse mushkil se chhodte hain. (d) **False** ❌: doosri hamesha badi hoti hai, kyunki ab ion se electron nikalna hai. (e) **False** ❌: cation banne mein energy **lagti** hai (di jaati hai), mukt nahi hoti.\n8. He mein sirf 1 shell hai, electron nucleus ke bilkul paas hain aur beech mein koi doosri shell ka parda nahi. Ne aur Ar mein shells aur parde badhte hain, isliye IE ghat-ti hai. **He lagbhag 2372 > Ne 2081 > Ar 1521** ✅.\n9. Ar mein 3 shells aur sabse baahari shell 8 se bhari hai. K mein 19 proton hain par uska 19th electron ek **nayi, 4th shell** mein akela hai, jo bahut door hai aur 18 electron (2, 8, 8) ke parde ke peeche hai. Doori aur parda is ek extra proton ki kheench ko bahut zyada kam kar dete hain. Isliye IE 1521 se girkar 419 ho gayi. **Jaanch:** 1521 > 419 ✅.\n10. Student galat hai. **Zyada IE ka matlab electron nikalna mushkil hai**, aasan nahi. Ar ki IE lagbhag 1521 hai, to Ar⁺ banane mein bahut energy lagti hai, isliye Ar aasani se ion nahi banata. K jaise kam IE wale metal aasani se cation banate hain.\n11. Na ki IE (lagbhag 496) Ca ki (lagbhag 590) se kam hai, isliye Na apna electron aasani se chhodta hai aur thande paani ke saath zyada tezi se react karne ki ummeed hai. (Asli mein bhi Na thande paani se tez react karta hai aur Ca dheere: Chapter 2 ki seedhi.) Ye bas ek anuman hai, prayog sirf teacher ka. **Jaanch:** 496 < 590 ✅.\n12. Aasani se chhodne ka order (kam IE pehle): **K (419), Na (496), Ca (590), Mg (738)**. K aur Na: dono Group 1, K mein ek shell zyada, electron door, isliye K aasani se chhodta hai. Na aur Mg: dono Period 3, Mg mein ek proton zyada aur shells wahi, isliye pull zyada aur IE zyada. **Jaanch:** 419 < 496 < 590 < 738 ✅.\n13. Seene se kas ke pakda khilona: wo chhota atom hai jo electron ko kas ke pakda hai (nucleus ki kheench zyada, doori kam): iski **IE zyada** hai. Haath ki lambaai par dheele se pakda khilona: wo bada atom hai jiska baahari electron door hai: iski **IE kam** hai. Ye wahi do ideas hain: doori aur nuclear pull.\n\n---\n\nHrishikesh ne ek chhoti si table apni copy mein banayi: group mein neeche, IE kam; period mein aage, IE zyada. \"Mama, ab khilone se hi pata chalta hai kaun sa atom electron deta hai aur kaun nahi!\" Mr. Tembe hanse: \"Bas yaad rakhna ki **keemat** badi, to chhodna mushkil. Ye galti sabse zyada log yahin karte hain.\"\n\n**Aage kya?** Topic 3 mein hum ulta sawaal poochhte hain: jab ek atom ko ek electron **milta** hai, to kya hota hai, kitni energy nikalti hai, aur kaun sa atom electron **lene** ke liye sabse zyada josh mein hai. Ek cricket match ki kahani ke saath: ek fielder jo har catch lene ko bechain, aur ek jo gend girata hai."
          }
