            "correctIndex": 3,
            "explanation": "Pitfall to avoid (Thinking that \"high ionisation energy\" means an atom loses electrons easily): This is completely backwards! Ionisation energy is the price you must pay to remove an electron. A high price means the electron is hard to remove, not easy! Think back to Lily hugging the rubber duck tightly against her chest: high effort required = hard to take away. Let's prove it with three elements: Na (496), Ne (2081), and K (419). Which atom loses an electron most easily? - Low IE = easy to remove. K has the lowest price (419), followed by Na (496), while Ne has a sky-high price (2081). - Ease of losing an electron: K > Na > Ne. - Because neon's IE is huge (2081), neon refuses to lose electrons — which is why neon is an unreactive noble gas! Two quick self-check questions whenever you feel confused: 1. Between K and Ne, which one readily forms a +1 cation? K⁺ (easily); Ne⁺ never forms in normal chemistry. 2. If \"high IE\" meant \"loses electrons easily,\" then neon would be the most reactive metal in Period 2 — when in reality it is an inert gas! Memory trick: Think of IE as a price tag in dollars — the higher the price tag, the harder it is to buy that electron away from the atom.",
