            "explanation": "Pitfall to avoid (\"A bond between two different atoms is always polar.\"): Truth: Whether a bond is polar depends on the electronegativity difference, not merely on the two atoms being different elements. Demo: P (2.1) and H (2.1) are different elements, yet their difference is 0.0—the P–H bonds in PH₃ (phosphine, a toxic gas—for study only) are completely non-polar. C (2.5) and H (2.1) have a difference of only 0.4—nearly non-polar. S (2.5) and H (2.1) also differ by just 0.4. Now increase the difference: H–Cl (0.9) and H–F (1.9) are clearly polar. Having \"two different atoms\" does not make a bond polar; a difference in pull does.",
