            "explanation": "Pitfall to avoid (\"No covalent compound ever dissolves in water.\"): Demo: Think back to our five grocery glasses. Sugar (covalent) dissolved completely, ethanol (covalent) mixes with water in all proportions, ammonia (covalent) is extremely soluble in water, and HCl (covalent) dissolves and ionizes in water. Meanwhile, cooking oil, wax, and CCl₄ (also covalent) do not dissolve in water. Whether a covalent substance dissolves in water depends on molecular polarity, not on the label \"covalent\": polar covalent molecules dissolve in polar water, while non-polar covalent molecules do not (\"like dissolves like\").",
            "explanationHinglish": "Bachke rehna (\"Saare covalent compounds paani mein nahi ghulte.\"): Demo: kirana jar test. Cheeni (covalent) ghul gayi, ethanol (covalent) paani mein poora mil jaata hai, ammonia (covalent) bahut zyada ghulti hai, aur HCl (covalent) ghulkar ions bhi bana deta hai. Dusri taraf tel, wax aur CCl₄ (covalent) paani mein nahi ghulte. Matlab ghulna bond ke naam se nahi, bond ke polar hone se tay hota hai: polar covalent aur ionic paani mein ghulte hain, non-polar nahi. Sahi rule: \"like dissolves like\"."
