          {
            "heading": "Key Vocabulary",
            "headingHinglish": "Naye shabd",
            "body": "| Term | Meaning | Example |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Master method** | The four-question checklist used to solve any \"what kind of bond and why\" problem | Part A |\n| **Rule of thumb** | A practical guideline that works reliably in most cases, while allowing for gradual transitions near the boundaries | Difference 0.5 to 1.7: polar covalent |\n| **Borderline bond** | A bond whose electronegativity difference sits near the boundary between two categories, so a single rigid label doesn't tell the whole story | Al–Cl, difference 1.5 |\n| **Donor** | The atom whose lone pair supplies both electrons for a coordinate bond | O in H₃O⁺ |\n| **Acceptor** | The atom or ion with an empty valence space that accepts a share in both electrons of a coordinate bond | H⁺ in H₃O⁺ |",
            "bodyHinglish": "| Shabd | Matlab (simple bhasha mein) | Example |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Master method** | Kisi bhi \"kaun sa bond aur kyun\" sawaal ko hal karne ke chaar sawaalon ki list | Part A |\n| **Thumb rule** | Aksar sahi rehne wala andaaza, jisme kuch kinare ke maamle apwaad hote hain | Farq 0.5 se 1.7: polar covalent |\n| **Borderline bond** | Wo bond jiska farq do bond-kism ki seema ke paas hai, to ek hi label poora sach nahi | Al–Cl, farq 1.5 |\n| **Donor** | Wo atom jiski lone pair bond ke dono electron deti hai | H₃O⁺ mein O |\n| **Acceptor** | Wo atom ya ion jise bond ke liye electron chahiye aur jiske paas khaali jagah hai | H₃O⁺ mein H⁺ |"
          },
          {
            "heading": "Part A: The Master Method: Four Questions",
            "headingHinglish": "Part A: Master method: chaar sawaal",
            "body": "In Grade 9 we used a 3-step method (metal vs. non-metal, bond type, electron count). This chapter deepened every step: now we also check whether a shared pair is pulled **equally or unequally**, whether **polyatomic ions** carry internal covalent bonds, and whether any bond formed as a **coordinate bond**. Ask these four questions in order:\n\n**Step 1: What is the electronegativity difference?** Subtract the smaller electronegativity from the larger one (whenever values are given). Rule of thumb: **0.4 or less** → non-polar covalent; **about 0.5 to 1.7** → polar covalent; **around 2.0 or higher** → ionic. Remember that these numbers are a **rule of thumb**, not a brick wall—bond character changes gradually.\n\n**Step 2: Metal or non-metal?** Two non-metals share electrons (covalent). A metal and a non-metal transfer electrons (ionic). This step works even when electronegativity numbers are not given, and serves as a cross-check on Step 1.\n\n**Step 3: Does the formula contain a polyatomic ion?** If a polyatomic ion (such as NH₄⁺ or OH⁻) is present, there is an **ionic bond outside** between the ions and **covalent bonds inside** the polyatomic ion (Topic 4).\n\n**Step 4: Did one atom supply both shared electrons?** If one atom donated a lone pair into another species' empty valence space, mark a **coordinate bond** with an arrow pointing from donor to acceptor (Topic 3).\n\n```\n   given formula\n         v\n   Step 1: EN difference?       <= 0.4: non-polar    0.5 to 1.7: polar    very large: ionic\n         v\n   Step 2: metal + non-metal?   yes: ionic           no: covalent\n         v\n   Step 3: polyatomic ion?      yes: ionic outside, covalent inside the ion\n         v\n   Step 4: lone-pair donation?  yes: coordinate bond, arrow D -> A\n         v\n   final answer: bond type(s) + reason + electron-count check\n```\n\n> **Figure 6.2** – Flowchart of the 4-step master method. Always finish your answer with the reason and an electron-count check.\n\n**One golden rule for every electron check:** Keep **total valence electrons** and **octet counts** clearly distinct. **Total valence electrons** = the sum of outer electrons brought by each atom (adjusted for any ionic charge), where each electron is drawn and counted **only once**. **Octet (or duplet) count** is checked around one atom at a time by counting its lone-pair electrons plus **both** electrons of every shared pair attached to it, because a shared pair counts toward both bonded atoms.",
            "bodyHinglish": "Class 9 mein 3-step tareeka tha (metal ya non-metal, type, electron ginti). Is chapter ne use kaafi gehra kar diya: ab bond ke andar **barabar ya ek taraf** ka sawaal bhi hai, aur **ion ke andar** ke bond aur **coordinate** bond bhi. Chaar sawaal, isi krama mein:\n\n**Step 1: Farq kitna?** Dono atomon ki electronegativity ka farq nikalo (agar value di ho). Thumb rule: lagbhag **0.4 ya usse kam**: non-polar covalent. Lagbhag **0.5 se 1.7**: polar covalent. **Usse bahut zyada**: ionic. Ye seema ek **thumb rule** hai, deewar nahi: badlaav dheere-dheere hota hai.\n\n**Step 2: Metal ya non-metal?** Dono non-metal hon to baantna (covalent). Ek metal aur ek non-metal ho to dena-lena (ionic). Ye step tab bhi chalta hai jab electronegativity ki value na ho, aur step 1 ki jaanch bhi karta hai.\n\n**Step 3: Formula mein ion hai?** Agar polyatomic ion (jaise NH₄⁺, OH⁻) hai to ion ke **bahar** ionic bond aur ion ke **andar** covalent bond (Topic 4).\n\n**Step 4: Kisi ne akele dono electron diye?** Agar ek atom ki lone pair aur doosre ka khaali jagah mil gaye, to coordinate bond, aur arrow donor se acceptor ki taraf (Topic 3).\n\n```\n   formula diya\n        v\n   Step 1: EN farq?        0.4 ya kam: non-polar    0.5 se 1.7: polar    bahut zyada: ionic\n        v\n   Step 2: metal + non-metal?   haan: ionic        nahi: covalent\n        v\n   Step 3: polyatomic ion?      haan: ion ke bahar ionic, andar covalent\n        v\n   Step 4: lone pair donation?  haan: coordinate bond, arrow D -> A\n        v\n   jawab: bond kism + kaaran + electron ginti ki jaanch\n```\n\n> **Figure 6.2** – Master method ka flow: chaar sawaal, upar se neeche. Har jawab ke saath ek kaaran aur ek ginti ki jaanch zaroor likho.\n\n**Ek niyam jo har puzzle mein lagega:** electron ginti do jagah hoti hai. **Kul electrons** = har atom ke valence electrons ka jod (ion ho to charge ke hisaab se ghatao ya badhao), aur har electron ek hi baar ginte hain. **Octet ki ginti** alag cheez hai: har atom ke lone pair ke electrons aur uske shared pairs ke **dono** electrons ginte hain, kyunki shared pair do atomon ke liye ginti mein aata hai."
          },
          {
            "heading": "Part B: Bond Bingo Rounds (Worked Puzzles)",
            "headingHinglish": "Part B: Bond Bingo ke rounds (hal kiye hue puzzles)",
            "body": "### Round 1. CaF₂: An Enormous Difference\n\n**Clue:** \"A metal and a non-metal with an enormous electronegativity difference.\"\n\n- Step 1: Ca = 1.0, F = 4.0. Difference = 4.0 − 1.0 = **3.0** (huge).\n- Step 2: Ca is a metal, F is a non-metal → electron transfer (ionic).\n- Calcium has 2 outer electrons and each fluorine atom needs 1 electron to complete its octet. So Ca transfers **1 electron to each of two F atoms**, forming **Ca²⁺ and 2 F⁻**.\n\n```\n   Before:        ..                          ..\n                : F .        x Ca x         . F :\n                  ..                          ..\n               (F: 7 e⁻)    (Ca: 2 e⁻)     (F: 7 e⁻)\n\n   After:         ..                         ..\n              [ : F .x ]⁻     Ca²⁺      [ x. F : ]⁻\n                  ..                         ..\n```\n\n> **Figure 6.3** – CaF₂: Ca (`x`) transfers its 2 outer electrons, one to each F atom. Each F⁻ ion now has 8 outer electrons (4 pairs), and Ca²⁺ also has 8 electrons in its new outer shell.\n\n**Check:** F⁻: 7 + 1 = 8 ✅. Ca²⁺: 20 − 2 = 18 electrons left, arranged as 2, 8, 8 ✅. Charge balance: (+2) + 2 × (−1) = 0 ✅. With a difference of 3.0, this is unmistakably ionic.\n\n### Round 2. H₂S: Two Non-metals with a Small Difference\n\n**Clue:** \"Two non-metals whose electronegativity difference is 0.4.\"\n\n- Step 2: Both S and H are non-metals → **electron sharing (covalent)**.\n- Step 1: S = 2.5, H = 2.1. Difference = 2.5 − 2.1 = **0.4**, sitting right on the rule-of-thumb border (nearly non-polar). Sulfur pulls the shared pairs **slightly** closer to itself, giving S a very slight δ⁻ and each H a very slight δ⁺.\n- Dot diagram: S has 6 outer electrons and each of the two H atoms has 1. Since S needs 2 more electrons for an octet, it **shares one pair with each H atom**.\n\n```\n            ..\n     H x  . S .  x H\n            ..\n```\n\n> **Figure 6.4** – Dot-and-cross diagram of H₂S: S has 2 lone pairs (`..` at top and bottom) and shares one pair with each H atom. (This diagram shows electron pairing; the bent 3D shape and exact angle of H₂S are covered in higher grades.)\n\n**Check:** Total valence electrons = 6 + 1 + 1 = **8**, and in the diagram there are 6 dots from S + 2 crosses from H = 8 ✅. Octet check: around S = 2 lone pairs (4 e⁻) + 2 shared pairs (4 e⁻) = 8 ✅; around each H = 2 ✅. (H₂S is a toxic gas with a rotten-egg odor—for study only.)\n\n### Round 3. H₃O⁺: Coordinate Bond in Action\n\n**Clue:** \"Formed when an acid dissolves in water; one bond has both electrons supplied by the same atom.\"\n\n- In water (H₂O), the oxygen atom has **2 lone pairs**. A bare H⁺ ion has zero electrons—just an empty valence space.\n- Step 4: Oxygen donates one of its lone pairs (**donor**) and H⁺ accepts a share in it (**acceptor**), forming a coordinate bond with the arrow pointing **from O to H⁺**.\n\n```\n          ..                               ..\n     H -- O -- H     +   H⁺    --->   H -- O -- H\n          ..                               v\n                                           H\n```\n\n> **Figure 6.5** – H₂O + H⁺ → H₃O⁺. The bottom lone pair on O becomes a coordinate bond (vertical arrow) to H⁺, with O supplying both shared electrons. Once formed, all three O–H bonds are identical (Topic 3).\n\n**Check (total outer electrons):** 6 (from O) + 3 (from three H) = 9, minus 1 for the **+1** charge = **8**. In the diagram, O has 4 pairs around it (1 lone pair + 3 bond pairs) = 8 electrons ✅. Equation balance: H₂O + H⁺ → H₃O⁺ has H: 2 + 1 = 3 ✅, O: 1 = 1 ✅, charge: 0 + (+1) = +1 ✅. This is the true species behind \"H⁺(aq)\" from Chapter 1 ([acids-bases-and-salts-part2.md](../acids-bases-and-salts/acids-bases-and-salts-part2.md)).\n\n### Round 4. NH₄Cl: All Three Bond Types in One Formula\n\n**Clue:** \"A single compound containing ionic, ordinary covalent, and coordinate bonds all together.\"\n\nStart with Step 3: NH₄Cl splits into **NH₄⁺** and **Cl⁻**.\n\n| Location | Bond Type | Count |\n|---|---|---|\n| Between NH₄⁺ and Cl⁻ | Ionic | 1 |\n| Inside NH₄⁺, three N–H links | Ordinary covalent (each H contributed 1 electron) | 3 |\n| Inside NH₄⁺, one N → H link | Coordinate (N's lone pair donated to H⁺) | 1 |\n\n**Check:** Inside NH₄⁺ there are 3 + 1 = 4 bonds (giving N an octet of 4 pairs = 8 electrons). Total outer electrons in NH₄⁺: 5 (from N) + 4 (from 4 H) = 9, minus 1 for the +1 charge = **8** ✅. In Cl⁻: 7 + 1 = 8 (4 lone pairs) ✅. Overall charge: (+1) + (−1) = 0 ✅.\n\n### Round 5. Ranking Six Bonds by Polarity\n\n**Question:** Arrange Cl–Cl, S–H, H–Cl, Al–Cl, Mg–Cl, and Ca–F in order from least polar to most polar/ionic, and state the zone for each.\n\nGiven values: Cl 3.0, S 2.5, H 2.1, Al 1.5, Mg 1.2, Ca 1.0, F 4.0.\n\n```\n   Cl-Cl   0.0   (no bar)\n   S-H     0.4   ####\n   H-Cl    0.9   #########\n   Al-Cl   1.5   ###############\n   Mg-Cl   1.8   ##################\n   Ca-F    3.0   ##############################\n```\n\n> **Figure 6.6** – Electronegativity differences across six bonds (each `#` = 0.1). The larger the difference, the more strongly the shared pair shifts to one side—until in Ca–F electron transfer is virtually complete.\n\n- **Cl–Cl (0.0):** non-polar covalent. **S–H (0.4):** right on the border, nearly non-polar. **H–Cl (0.9)** and **Al–Cl (1.5):** in the polar covalent zone. **Mg–Cl (1.8):** in the 1.7–2.0 borderline zone approaching ionic (Topic 1); since Mg is a metal (Step 2), MgCl₂ behaves as an ionic compound. **Ca–F (3.0):** strongly ionic.\n- Order: **Cl–Cl < S–H < H–Cl < Al–Cl < Mg–Cl < Ca–F**.\n\n**Check:** 3.0 − 3.0 = 0.0, 2.5 − 2.1 = 0.4, 3.0 − 2.1 = 0.9, 3.0 − 1.5 = 1.5, 3.0 − 1.2 = 1.8, 4.0 − 1.0 = 3.0, and 0.0 < 0.4 < 0.9 < 1.5 < 1.8 < 3.0 ✅.\n\n### Round 6. AlCl₃: When Step 1 and Step 2 Pull in Different Directions\n\n**Clue:** \"A metal combined with a non-metal, yet the electronegativity difference is only 1.5.\"\n\n- Step 2 says: Al is a metal and Cl is a non-metal → electron transfer (ionic).\n- Step 1 says: 3.0 − 1.5 = **1.5**, which falls inside the 0.5 to 1.7 polar covalent zone!\n\nHere our two indicators point in different directions. This is a classic **borderline bond**. The honest scientific answer is that the Al–Cl bond is neither purely ionic nor purely ordinary covalent—it has significant covalent character despite involving a metal. At the Grade 10 level, simply spotting that the two steps give mixed signals and stating both reasons is a complete, top-marks answer! (The deeper reason why small, highly charged metal ions like Al³⁺ pull electrons back toward themselves is covered in higher grades.)\n\n**Check:** 3.0 − 1.5 = 1.5, which sits 0.2 below the 1.7 cutoff ✅.\n\n### Round 7. Which Ones Dissolve in Water?\n\n**Clue:** \"Three separate glasses of water: add MgCl₂ to the first, ethanol to the second, and candle wax to the third.\"\n\n- **MgCl₂:** Ionic (Round 5, difference 1.8). Polar water molecules (Topic 2) orient their δ⁻ oxygen ends toward Mg²⁺ and their δ⁺ hydrogen ends toward Cl⁻, pulling the ions into solution. **Dissolves.**\n- **Ethanol:** Polar covalent molecule (with a polar O–H group); mixes completely with polar water. **Dissolves.**\n- **Candle wax:** Non-polar covalent; by \"like dissolves like\" (Topic 2), it **does not dissolve** in polar water.\n\nOne honest note: solubility rules are also rules of thumb—a few ionic crystals (such as CaF₂, the mineral fluorite) pack so tightly in their lattice that even water barely dissolves them (covered in higher grades).\n\n**Check:** Ionic and polar dissolve in water, non-polar does not: MgCl₂ ✅, ethanol ✅, wax ❌ (insoluble) ✅.\n\n### Round 8. Match the Substance to Its Melting or Boiling Point\n\n**Clue:** \"Four substances—MgO, NaCl, HCl, and candle wax—and four temperatures: (a) boils at about −85 °C, (b) melts at about 60 °C, (c) melts at about 801 °C, (d) melts at about 2850 °C.\"\n\n- HCl: small covalent molecules with weak attractions between molecules → **(a) −85 °C**.\n- Candle wax: larger non-polar covalent molecules, soft solid with a low melting point → **(b) 60 °C**.\n- NaCl: ionic lattice of singly charged Na⁺ and Cl⁻ ions → **(c) 801 °C**.\n- MgO: ionic lattice of doubly charged Mg²⁺ and O²⁻ ions with even stronger attraction → **(d) 2850 °C**.\n\n**Check:** −85 < 60 < 801 < 2850, with covalent substances at the low end, ionic compounds at the high end, and doubly charged MgO highest of all ✅.\n\n### Round 9. Aqueous HCl vs. Aqueous Sugar in a Conductivity Tester\n\n**Clue:** \"Two beakers with a conductivity tester: one holds HCl dissolved in water, the other holds sugar dissolved in water. One lights the bulb; the other does not.\"\n\n- **HCl in water:** The H–Cl bond is polar (difference 0.9). Polar water molecules pull on H and Cl strongly enough to split the covalent bond (**ionization**). The H⁺ attaches to a lone pair on water's oxygen atom to form **H₃O⁺**, leaving **Cl⁻**.\n- Equation: **HCl + H₂O → H₃O⁺ + Cl⁻** (the full version of HCl → H⁺ + Cl⁻ from our electrolysis chapter, [electrolysis.md](../electrolysis/electrolysis.md)).\n- Because the solution contains **free mobile ions**, the tester bulb **lights up**.\n- **Sugar in water:** Although sugar molecules have polar regions that let them dissolve in water, water **does not break** any bonds inside the sugar molecule—sugar dissolves as intact neutral molecules. With no free ions, the bulb **does not light up**.\n\n**Check:** H: 1 + 2 = 3 ✅, O: 1 = 1 ✅, Cl: 1 = 1 ✅, charge: 0 = (+1) + (−1) ✅. (HCl is a corrosive acid—for study only. At home, only ever test table salt and sugar solutions with adult supervision, and never taste any test solution.)\n\n```\n   Aqueous HCl solution                     Aqueous sugar solution\n\n   H₃O⁺  Cl⁻  H₃O⁺  Cl⁻                    sugar molecule  (intact, unsplit)\n   Cl⁻  H₃O⁺  Cl⁻  H₃O⁺                    sugar molecule  (intact, unsplit)\n   (free mobile ions carry charge)          (zero ions present)\n\n   bulb: GLOWS                              bulb: DOES NOT GLOW\n```\n\n> **Figure 6.7** – Side-by-side comparison of the two solutions: aqueous HCl contains free H₃O⁺ and Cl⁻ ions that carry electric current, whereas aqueous sugar contains only intact neutral molecules.\n\nOn Elena's bingo card, CaF₂, H₂S, MgCl₂, and AlCl₃ were now marked—each backed up by a rock-solid reason. Pointing to the remaining five squares (HI, KI, PH₃, CS₂, and SiH₄), Dr. Morales smiled: \"Those five are waiting for you in the Practice Questions!\"",
            "bodyHinglish": "### Round 1. CaF₂: bahut bada farq\n\n**Clue:** \"Metal aur non-metal. Farq bahut bada.\"\n\n- Step 1: Ca = 1.0, F = 4.0. Farq = 4.0 − 1.0 = **3.0**, bahut bada.\n- Step 2: Ca metal, F non-metal: dena-lena.\n- Ca ke baahari shell mein 2 electron hain, F ko 1 chahiye. To Ca **do F ko ek-ek** deta hai: **Ca²⁺ aur 2 F⁻**.\n\n```\n   Pehle:        ..                          ..\n               : F .        x Ca x         . F :\n                 ..                          ..\n              (F: 7 e)     (Ca: 2 e)       (F: 7 e)\n\n   Baad mein:    ..                         ..\n             [ : F .x ]⁻     Ca²⁺      [ x. F : ]⁻\n                 ..                         ..\n```\n\n> **Figure 6.3** – CaF₂: Ca (x) apne 2 baahari electron deta hai, har F ko ek-ek. Har F⁻ ke paas ab 8 electron (4 pairs), aur Ca²⁺ ki baahari shell mein bhi 8.\n\n**Jaanch:** F⁻: 7 + 1 = 8 ✅. Ca: 20 − 2 = 18 electron bache, jo 2, 8, 8 hain ✅. Charge: (+2) + 2 × (−1) = 0 ✅. Ye ionic hai, aur kinare ke paas nahi: 3.0 bahut bada hai.\n\n### Round 2. H₂S: dono non-metal, par farq chhota\n\n**Clue:** \"Dono non-metal. Farq 0.4.\"\n\n- Step 2: S aur H dono non-metal: **baantna**.\n- Step 1: S = 2.5, H = 2.1. Farq = 2.5 − 2.1 = **0.4**: thumb rule ki seema par, lagbhag non-polar. Itna zaroor ki S shared pair ko **thoda** zyada apni taraf rakhta hai, to S par δ⁻ aur H par δ⁺, par bahut halka.\n- Dot diagram: S ke 6 electron, do H ke 1-1. S ko 2 aur chahiye, to **do pairs share**.\n\n```\n            ..\n     H x  . S .  x H\n            ..\n```\n\n> **Figure 6.4** – H₂S ke dots: S ke paas 2 lone pairs (upar aur neeche ke \"..\"), aur dono H ke saath ek-ek shared pair. Ye sirf bonds ki ginti dikhata hai: molecule ka asli tedha shape aur angle aage ki class mein.\n\n**Jaanch:** kul electrons: 6 + 1 + 1 = **8**, aur figure mein: S ke 6 dots + 2 crosses = 8 ✅. Octet: S = 2 lone pairs (4 e) + 2 shared pairs (4 e) = 8 ✅. Har H = 2 ✅. (H₂S ek zehreeli gas hai jisme sadey ande jaisi badboo hoti hai: sirf padhne ke liye, koi prayog nahi.)\n\n### Round 3. H₃O⁺: coordinate bond\n\n**Clue:** \"Ek acid paani mein ghula hai. Ek bond ke dono electron ek hi atom ke.\"\n\n- Paani H₂O mein O ke paas **2 lone pairs** hain. H⁺ ke paas koi electron nahi, sirf khaali jagah.\n- Step 4: O lone pair deta hai (**donor**), H⁺ le leta hai (**acceptor**): coordinate bond, arrow **O se H⁺ ki taraf**.\n\n```\n          ..                               ..\n     H -- O -- H     +   H⁺    --->   H -- O -- H\n          ..                               v\n                                           H\n```\n\n> **Figure 6.5** – H₂O + H⁺ → H₃O⁺. Neeche wali lone pair arrow ban kar H⁺ ki taraf gayi: O ne dono electron diye. Baad mein teeno O–H bond ek jaise hain (Topic 3).\n\n**Jaanch (kul electrons):** O ke 6 + teen H ke 3 = 9, par ion ka charge **+1** hai, matlab ek electron kam: 9 − 1 = **8**. Figure mein O ke paas 4 pairs (1 lone pair + 3 bonds) = 8 electron ✅. Equation: H₂O + H⁺ → H₃O⁺: H 2 + 1 = 3 ✅, O 1 ✅, charge 0 + 1 = +1 ✅. Chapter 1 ke \"acid H⁺ deta hai\" ka asli matlab yahi H₃O⁺ hai ([acids-bases-and-salts-part2.md](../acids-bases-and-salts/acids-bases-and-salts-part2.md)).\n\n### Round 4. NH₄Cl: teeno bond ek compound mein\n\n**Clue:** \"Ek hi formula mein ionic, covalent aur coordinate teeno.\"\n\nStep 3 se shuru: NH₄Cl = **NH₄⁺** aur **Cl⁻**.\n\n| Jagah | Bond kism | Ginti |\n|---|---|---|\n| NH₄⁺ aur Cl⁻ ke beech | Ionic | 1 |\n| NH₄⁺ ke andar, 3 N–H | Covalent (har H ne apna electron diya) | 3 |\n| NH₄⁺ ke andar, 1 N→H | Coordinate (N ki lone pair, H⁺ khaali) | 1 |\n\n**Jaanch:** NH₄⁺ ke andar kul 3 + 1 = 4 bond (N ka octet: 4 pairs = 8 electron). Kul electrons: N 5 + 4 H = 9, charge +1 isliye 9 − 1 = **8** ✅. Cl⁻: 7 + 1 = 8 (4 lone pairs) ✅. Charge: (+1) + (−1) = 0 ✅.\n\n### Round 5. Bonds ko polarity ke krama mein lagao\n\n**Sawaal:** Cl–Cl, S–H, H–Cl, Al–Cl, Mg–Cl aur Ca–F ko kam se zyada polar ke krama mein likho, aur har ek ka zone batao.\n\nShared constants: Cl 3.0, S 2.5, H 2.1, Al 1.5, Mg 1.2, Ca 1.0, F 4.0.\n\n```\n   Cl-Cl   0.0   (koi bar nahi)\n   S-H     0.4   ####\n   H-Cl    0.9   #########\n   Al-Cl   1.5   ###############\n   Mg-Cl   1.8   ##################\n   Ca-F    3.0   ##############################\n```\n\n> **Figure 6.6** – Bonds ka electronegativity-farq (har # = 0.1). Farq jitna bada, shared pair utna ek taraf, aur Ca–F mein lagbhag poora ek taraf.\n\n- **Cl–Cl (0.0):** non-polar. **S–H (0.4):** kinare par, lagbhag non-polar. **H–Cl (0.9)** aur **Al–Cl (1.5):** polar covalent zone mein. **Mg–Cl (1.8):** 1.7 se 2 ki dhundhli patti mein (Topic 1), ionic ki taraf; Mg metal hai (step 2), to MgCl₂ ko ionic hi maana jaata hai. **Ca–F (3.0):** pakka ionic.\n- Krama: **Cl–Cl < S–H < H–Cl < Al–Cl < Mg–Cl < Ca–F**.\n\n**Jaanch:** 3.0 − 3.0 = 0.0, 2.5 − 2.1 = 0.4, 3.0 − 2.1 = 0.9, 3.0 − 1.5 = 1.5, 3.0 − 1.2 = 1.8, 4.0 − 1.0 = 3.0, aur 0.0 < 0.4 < 0.9 < 1.5 < 1.8 < 3.0 ✅.\n\n### Round 6. AlCl₃: jab do sanket takrayein\n\n**Clue:** \"Metal aur non-metal. Par farq sirf 1.5.\"\n\n- Step 2 kehta hai: Al metal, Cl non-metal, to dena-lena, ionic.\n- Step 1 kehta hai: 3.0 − 1.5 = **1.5**, yaani 0.5 se 1.7 ke polar covalent zone mein.\n\nDono sanket alag baat bol rahe hain. Ye ek **borderline bond** hai. Honest jawab: Al–Cl na pakka ionic hai na pakka covalent; thumb rule yahan do hisson mein baant jaata hai. Class 10 par itna kaafi hai ki aisa maamla pehchano, aur dono sanket likho. Aage ki class mein iska aur gehra karan aayega.\n\n**Jaanch:** 3.0 − 1.5 = 1.5, aur 1.5 zone ki beech mein hai, aur 1.7 se 0.2 neeche ✅.\n\n### Round 7. Paani mein kaun ghulega?\n\n**Clue:** \"Glass mein MgCl₂, ethanol aur candle wax ke tukde alag-alag daalo.\"\n\n- **MgCl₂:** ionic (Round 5, farq 1.8). Paani ke molecule polar hain (Topic 2): unke δ⁻ siro Mg²⁺ ki taraf aur δ⁺ siro Cl⁻ ki taraf mudte hain, aur ions alag ho jaate hain. **Ghulta hai.**\n- **Ethanol:** polar molecule hai (O–H wala hissa), paani ke saath **mil jaata hai**.\n- **Wax:** non-polar, like dissolves like (Topic 2) ke hisaab se paani mein **nahi ghulta**.\n\nEk honest line: ye bhi thumb rule hai. Kuch ionic compounds (jaise CaF₂, jo fluorite naam ka khanij hai) paani mein bahut kam ghulte hain, uska karan aage ki class mein.\n\n**Jaanch:** polar aur ionic paani mein, non-polar nahi: MgCl₂ ✅, ethanol ✅, wax ❌ (ghulta nahi) ✅.\n\n### Round 8. Properties ko substances se milao\n\n**Clue:** \"Chaar cheezein hain: MgO, NaCl, HCl, wax. Chaar properties: (a) lagbhag −85 °C par ubalta hai, (b) lagbhag 60 °C par pighalta hai, (c) lagbhag 801 °C par pighalta hai, (d) lagbhag 2850 °C par pighalta hai.\"\n\n- HCl: covalent molecules, kamzor khinchav, bahut kam ubaal-bindu: **(a)**.\n- Wax: bade covalent molecules, naram, low melting: **(b)**.\n- NaCl: ionic, Na⁺ aur Cl⁻ ke ek-ek charge: **(c)**.\n- MgO: ionic, Mg²⁺ aur O²⁻ ke **bade charge**, zyada mazboot pakad: **(d)** (NaCl se bhi zyada).\n\n**Jaanch:** −85 < 60 < 801 < 2850, aur covalent chhote melting wale, ionic bade wale, MgO sabse bada ✅.\n\n### Round 9. HCl ka ghol aur sugar ka ghol\n\n**Clue:** \"Conductivity tester do glassi mein: ek mein HCl ka ghol, ek mein sugar (cheeni) ka. Ek mein bulb jalta hai, ek mein nahi.\"\n\n- **HCl:** H–Cl bond polar hai (farq 0.9). Paani ke polar molecule Cl ko aur H ko khinchte hain, aur bond toot jaata hai: **ionisation**. Ek H paani ke O ki lone pair se jud kar **H₃O⁺** banta hai, aur Cl **Cl⁻** ban jaata hai.\n- Equation: **HCl + H₂O → H₃O⁺ + Cl⁻**. Ye wahi HCl → H⁺ + Cl⁻ hai jo electrolysis chapter mein tha ([electrolysis.md](../electrolysis/electrolysis.md)), bas asli roop mein.\n- Ghol mein **free ions** hain, to bulb **jalta hai**.\n- **Sugar:** molecule ke andar bond polar hain, par paani sugar ke molecule ko **todta nahi**; wo poore molecule ke roop mein ghulta hai. Koi ion nahi, to bulb **nahi jalta**.\n\n**Jaanch:** H 1 + 2 = 3 ✅, O 1 ✅, Cl 1 ✅, charge 0 = (+1) + (−1) ✅. (HCl ek tez acid hai: sirf padhne ke liye. Ghar par sirf namak ka ghol aur cheeni ka ghol, bade ke saath, kabhi chakhna nahi.)\n\n```\n   HCl ka ghol                              Sugar ka ghol\n\n   H₃O⁺  Cl⁻  H₃O⁺  Cl⁻                    sugar molecule  (poora, toota nahi)\n   Cl⁻  H₃O⁺  Cl⁻  H₃O⁺                    sugar molecule  (poora, toota nahi)\n   (ions ghoomte hain)                      (ion koi nahi)\n\n   bulb: jalta hai                          bulb: nahi jalta\n```\n\n> **Figure 6.7** – Do ghol ka mukabla: HCl ke ghol mein H₃O⁺ aur Cl⁻ ions (charge ko le jaane wale), sugar ke ghol mein sirf poore molecule. Isi liye pehla bijli chalata hai, doosra nahi.\n\nMugdha ke card par ab CaF₂, H₂S, MgCl₂ aur AlCl₃ ke cell kaam ho chuke the, **kaaran ke saath**. Baaki paanch cell (HI, KI, PH₃, CS₂, SiH₄) ke liye Mr. Mokashi ne kaha, \"Wo tumhare Practice mein hain, wahan khud karo.\""
          },
          {
            "heading": "Try It Yourself: Make Your Own Bond Bingo Card (On Paper)",
            "headingHinglish": "Khud karke dekho: apna Bond Bingo (paper par)",
            "body": "(Paper and pencil only; no chemicals.)\n\nDraw a 3 × 3 grid and fill the nine squares with any nine formulas from this chapter. Have a study partner call out clues such as *\"electronegativity difference is 0.0,\"* *\"one atom donated both electrons of a bond,\"* or *\"polar covalent gas that ionizes in water.\"* Every mark you place on a square must include a **one-line chemical reason**!",
            "bodyHinglish": "(Sirf kagaz aur pencil. Koi chemical nahi.)\n\nEk 3 x 3 ka card banao aur nau formulas likho jo is chapter mein aaye hain. Ek dost caller banega, aur clue sirf is tarah ke honge: \"farq 0 hai\", \"ek hi atom ne dono electron diye\", \"paani mein ions banate hain\". Har nishaan ke saath **ek line ka kaaran** likhna zaroori hai."
          },
          {
            "heading": "Common Pitfalls to Avoid",
            "headingHinglish": "Aam galtiyan (bachke rehna)",
            "body": "**Mistake 1: \"Double-counting shared electrons when drawing a dot diagram.\"**\nWhen finding the **total** number of dots to draw, count each valence electron **only once**; when checking **octets**, a shared pair counts toward both bonded atoms. Demo: In H₂S, total valence electrons = 6 + 1 + 1 = **8**. If a student draws 6 dots on S and then draws 2 extra electrons for each bond separately, the diagram ends up showing 6 + 2 + 2 = **10** electrons—2 more than actually exist! Always draw the exact total (8 electrons), with the 2 shared pairs sitting between S and the two H atoms so they count for both.\n\n**Mistake 2: \"Forgetting to adjust the electron count for an ion's charge.\"**\nDemo: In H₃O⁺, adding neutral valence electrons gives 6 (from O) + 3 (from three H) = 9 electrons—an odd number that cannot form pairs for an octet. Because the ion carries a **+1** charge, it has **one electron fewer**: 9 − 1 = **8** electrons (4 pairs). Conversely, for a negative ion you **add** electrons equal to the negative charge (e.g., Cl⁻: 7 + 1 = 8).\n\n**Mistake 3: \"Drawing the coordinate bond arrow backward.\"**\nThe coordinate arrow always points **from the donor to the acceptor**. Demo: In NH₃ + H⁺, drawing H⁺ → N would claim that H⁺ donated a pair of electrons to nitrogen—which is impossible because H⁺ has zero electrons! Since both electrons come from nitrogen's lone pair, the arrow must point **N → H⁺**.\n\n**Mistake 4: \"Putting δ⁻ on the atom with the smaller electronegativity.\"**\nIt is the exact opposite: whichever atom pulls the shared electron pair more strongly (larger electronegativity) becomes **δ⁻**. Demo: In H₂S, S = 2.5 and H = 2.1. Because 2.5 > 2.1, sulfur pulls the pair closer and carries **δ⁻**, while hydrogen carries **δ⁺**. Remember the playground seesaw from Topic 1: the balance point shifts toward the heavier (stronger-pulling) side.\n\n**Mistake 5: \"Thinking MgCl₂ contains a Cl–Cl covalent bond because the formula says Cl₂.\"**\nIn an ionic formula like MgCl₂, the subscript `2` simply means there are **two separate Cl⁻ ions** balancing one Mg²⁺ ion. Demo: MgCl₂ = Mg²⁺ + 2 Cl⁻, with charge balance (+2) + 2 × (−1) = 0 ✅. The two Cl⁻ ions are not bonded to each other (in fact, two negative ions repel each other!); each Cl⁻ ion is attracted to Mg²⁺. Elemental chlorine gas (Cl₂) has a Cl–Cl covalent bond, but MgCl₂ does not.",
            "bodyHinglish": "**Galti 1: \"Dot diagram mein electrons do baar gin liye.\"**\nKul electrons ek hi baar ginte hain, par octet ki ginti mein shared pair dono atomon ke liye aata hai. Ye dono ek ho jaate hain. Demo: H₂S mein kul electrons 6 + 1 + 1 = **8**. Agar koi har bond ke dono electron S ke liye bhi gine aur H ke liye bhi alag se dot bana de, to drawing mein 6 + 2 + 2 = **10** dots ho jaate hain, jo 8 se zyada hain: ye galat hai. Sahi tareeka: kul 8 dots, jinme 2 shared pairs ke electron dono taraf ginti mein aate hain.\n\n**Galti 2: \"Ion ka charge electron ginti mein bhool gaye.\"**\nDemo: H₃O⁺ ke liye O 6 + H 3 = 9 electron. Odd number aaya, aur 9 electron se octet nahi banta. Charge +1 yaani ek electron kam hai: 9 − 1 = **8**. Ab 4 pairs ban gaye. Isi tarah negative ion mein ek electron **jodna** padta hai (jaise Cl⁻: 7 + 1 = 8).\n\n**Galti 3: \"Coordinate bond ka arrow ulta bana diya.\"**\nArrow hamesha **donor se acceptor** ki taraf hota hai. Demo: NH₃ + H⁺ ke liye agar koi H⁺ → N banaye, to ye keh raha hai ki H⁺ ne electron diye. Par H⁺ ke paas koi electron hi nahi hai. Dono electron N ki lone pair se aaye, isliye sahi arrow **N → H⁺** hai.\n\n**Galti 4: \"δ⁻ us atom par hota hai jiski electronegativity kam hai.\"**\nUlta hai: shared pair ko jo zyada kheenchta hai, wo δ⁻ banta hai. Demo: H₂S mein S = 2.5 aur H = 2.1. Bada number S ka hai, to S par **δ⁻** aur H par δ⁺. Topic 1 ki seesaw yaad karo: jo taraf bhaari hai, kendra wahi taraf jhukta hai.\n\n**Galti 5: \"MgCl₂ mein Cl–Cl bond hai, kyunki Cl₂ likha hai.\"**\nFormula mein subscript 2 ka matlab sirf ye hai ki **do Cl⁻ ion** hain, ek Mg²⁺ ke saath. Demo: MgCl₂ = Mg²⁺ + 2 Cl⁻. Charge: (+2) + 2 × (−1) = 0 ✅. Do Cl⁻ ek doosre se jude nahi hain; har ek Mg²⁺ ke paas hai. Cl₂ (gas) mein Cl–Cl bond hota hai, par MgCl₂ mein nahi."
          },
          {
            "heading": "Quick Recap",
            "headingHinglish": "Quick Recap",
            "body": "- **4-step master method:** (1) electronegativity difference, (2) metal vs. non-metal, (3) polyatomic ion check, (4) lone-pair coordinate donation. Always include the reason and an electron-count check.\n- Rule-of-thumb cutoffs: ≤ 0.4 non-polar covalent, 0.5 to 1.7 polar covalent, ~2.0+ ionic. These are gradual zones, not rigid walls.\n- For **borderline** cases (such as Al–Cl at 1.5), state both indicators honestly rather than forcing a one-word label.\n- **δ⁻** always goes on the more electronegative atom. A **coordinate arrow** always points from **donor to acceptor**.\n- Total valence electrons = sum of outer electrons ∓ ionic charge (each electron drawn once). Octet checks count shared pairs for both bonded atoms.\n- Aqueous **HCl** conducts electricity because it ionizes into H₃O⁺ and Cl⁻; aqueous **sugar** does not conduct because it dissolves as intact neutral molecules.\n- In ionic formulas like MgCl₂, the subscript `2` counts separate Cl⁻ ions—there is no Cl–Cl bond.",
            "bodyHinglish": "- **4-step method:** (1) electronegativity ka farq, (2) metal ya non-metal, (3) polyatomic ion, (4) lone pair ka donation. Har jawab ke saath kaaran aur electron ginti.\n- Thumb rule: 0.4 ya kam non-polar, 0.5 se 1.7 polar, bahut zyada ionic. Ye deewar nahi, dheeme badalti seema hai.\n- **Borderline** maamle (jaise Al–Cl, 1.5) mein dono sanket likho, ek hi label thopo mat.\n- **δ⁻** usi par jo zyada kheenchta hai. **Coordinate arrow** donor se acceptor.\n- Kul electrons = valence ka jod ± charge, har electron ek baar. Octet ki ginti mein shared pair dono ke liye.\n- **HCl** ka ghol ionisation se H₃O⁺ aur Cl⁻ banata hai (bijli chalata hai); **sugar** poora molecule ban kar ghulta hai (bijli nahi).\n- MgCl₂ jaise formulas mein subscript ka matlab ion ki ginti hai, Cl–Cl bond nahi."
          },
          {
            "heading": "Practice Questions",
            "headingHinglish": "Practice Questions",
            "body": "(All questions are meant to be solved on paper. Use these values where needed: H 2.1, C 2.5, S 2.5, P 2.1, Cl 3.0, K 0.8, Br 2.8, and Be about 1.5 from Chapter 6. Remember that Group 17 halogens go Cl, Br, I from top to bottom.)\n\n1. CS₂ is a poisonous, flammable liquid (named only; nobody handles it). C has 4 and S has 6 valence electrons. (a) Find the C–S electronegativity difference and say whether the bond is polar. (b) Draw the dot diagram with two C=S double bonds and show that the total electron count matches.\n2. In H–I, iodine is more electronegative than hydrogen but less electronegative than bromine (2.8). Without a number for iodine, say whether the H–I difference is bigger or smaller than 0.7, and name the atom that carries δ⁻.\n3. KI is a white solid. Use the steps of the master method to say what kind of bond it has, even though no electronegativity value for iodine is given. What does the solid do on melting that a covalent solid like wax would not?\n4. SiH₄ (named only: poisonous and flammable gas, no practice with it) is like CH₄: Si has 4 valence electrons. Show that 8 electrons in total are needed, draw the dot diagram, and check the octet on Si and the duplet on H.\n5. (BeCl₂ is poisonous: named only.) In BeCl₂, find the electronegativity difference using Be about 1.5. Which atom carries δ⁻, and in which zone of the thumb rule is the bond?\n6. PH₃ (phosphine, a poisonous gas: named only) has a lone pair on P. It can join an H⁺ to form PH₄⁺ (for example, from HI). (a) Write the equation PH₃ + H⁺ → PH₄⁺ and check atoms and charge. (b) Which atom is the donor and which the acceptor, and in which direction does the arrow point? (c) Show by counting that P in PH₄⁺ has 8 electrons around it.\n7. The compound PH₄I is made of PH₄⁺ and I⁻. List every kind of bond in one formula unit and how many of each.\n8. In PH₃ the three P–H bonds are arranged in a pyramid, like NH₃. Find the P–H difference. Is the PH₃ molecule polar? Compare with NH₃ (N–H is 0.9).\n9. Predict, with reasons, which of KI and CS₂ dissolves in water. Then say which of the two solutions would light a conductivity tester bulb.\n10. HI is a corrosive, poisonous gas (named only) whose polar molecules split in water like HCl does: HI + H₂O → H₃O⁺ + I⁻. Check atoms and charge in the equation. Will the bulb of a tester glow in an HI solution? Explain.\n11. State True or False and correct the false ones: (a) KI contains covalent bonds inside. (b) The C=S bond in CS₂ is polar. (c) In BeCl₂ the Be atom carries δ⁻. (d) In PH₄⁺ the coordinate bond is weaker than the other three P–H bonds. (e) SiH₄ has a non-metal–non-metal covalent bond.\n12. A student draws SiH₄ with 10 dots and says, \"I counted the shared electrons for both atoms.\" Find the mistake and give the correct total.\n13. A student writes the arrow in PH₃ + H⁺ as H⁺ → PH₃. What is wrong, and what is the correct direction and the reason?\n14. Bond Bingo round. Clues: (a) an ionic solid that conducts when melted; (b) a gas whose central atom has one lone pair, three single bonds and all bonds with difference 0.0; (c) a gas whose polar molecules form H₃O⁺ and I⁻ in water; (d) a liquid with two C=S double bonds and no lone pair on the central atom. Match them to KI, HI, PH₃ and CS₂.\n\n### Answers\n\n1. (a) C = 2.5, S = 2.5, difference = **0.0**, so the C=S bond is **non-polar**. (b) In S=C=S: each S has 2 lone pairs (4 e⁻) and shares 2 pairs with C (4 e⁻) = 8; C shares 4 pairs in all (8 e⁻) = 8. Total valence electrons = 4 + 6 + 6 = **16**. In the diagram: 4 lone pairs (2 on each S) = 8 e⁻, plus 4 shared pairs = 8 e⁻, total = 16 ✅. Because each bond has a difference of 0.0, both bonds and the molecule are non-polar.\n2. Since iodine is more electronegative than H (2.1) but less electronegative than Br (2.8), I lies between 2.1 and 2.8. Therefore the H–I difference lies between 0 and 2.8 − 2.1 = 0.7, meaning it is **smaller than 0.7**. Because I is more electronegative than H, **iodine (I)** carries **δ⁻**. Check: for any value of I between 2.1 and 2.8, I − 2.1 < 0.7 ✅.\n3. Step 2: K is a Group 1 metal and I is a Group 17 non-metal, so KI has an **ionic bond** (K⁺ and I⁻) without needing iodine's exact electronegativity number. (Cross-check with Step 1: K = 0.8 is very low, guaranteeing a large difference.) When melted, KI's ions become **free to move**, so molten KI **conducts electricity**, whereas molten wax consists of neutral molecules and does not conduct.\n4. Total valence electrons: 4 (from Si) + 4 × 1 (from 4 H) = **8** electrons = 4 shared pairs. Si forms 4 single bonds, one with each H. Octet/duplet check: Si has 4 shared pairs × 2 = 8 (octet) ✅; each H has 1 shared pair = 2 (duplet) ✅. All 8 electrons are drawn once.\n5. Difference = 3.0 − 1.5 = **1.5**, with **Cl** carrying **δ⁻** (since 3.0 > 1.5). Because 1.5 lies between 0.5 and 1.7, it falls in the **polar covalent zone** (making BeCl₂ a classic borderline case like AlCl₃). Check: 3.0 − 1.5 = 1.5 ✅. (Why Be is stable with fewer than 8 electrons around it is covered in higher grades.)\n6. (a) PH₃ + H⁺ → PH₄⁺: H atoms: 3 + 1 = 4 ✅, P: 1 = 1 ✅, charge: 0 + (+1) = +1 ✅. (b) **Donor: P** (supplies its lone pair); **acceptor: H⁺** (empty valence space); arrow points **P → H⁺**. (c) Outer electrons: 5 (from P) + 4 (from 4 H) − 1 (for the +1 charge) = 8 electrons = 4 bond pairs × 2 = 8 around P ✅.\n7. In one formula unit of PH₄I: **1 ionic bond** (between PH₄⁺ and I⁻), **3 ordinary covalent P–H bonds** (inside PH₄⁺), and **1 coordinate P → H bond** (inside PH₄⁺). Check: 3 + 1 = 4 bonds inside PH₄⁺ ✅, and charge (+1) + (−1) = 0 ✅.\n8. P–H difference = 2.1 − 2.1 = **0.0**, so the P–H bonds themselves are non-polar (no δ⁺/δ⁻ bond arrows). Thus PH₃ is **nearly non-polar** (a tiny polarity remains from P's lone pair, covered in higher grades). In NH₃, each N–H bond has a difference of 3.0 − 2.1 = 0.9, so the three polar bond arrows in the pyramid add up to make NH₃ strongly polar—far more polar than PH₃. Check: 2.1 − 2.1 = 0.0 vs. 3.0 − 2.1 = 0.9 ✅.\n9. **KI** dissolves in water: it is ionic, and polar water molecules surround and separate K⁺ and I⁻ ions. **CS₂** is non-polar (Q1), so by \"like dissolves like\" it does not dissolve in polar water. Conductivity tester: the **KI solution** lights the bulb because it contains free K⁺ and I⁻ ions; CS₂ neither dissolves nor forms ions.\n10. Atom and charge check for HI + H₂O → H₃O⁺ + I⁻: H: 1 + 2 = 3 ✅, I: 1 = 1 ✅, O: 1 = 1 ✅, charge: 0 + 0 = (+1) + (−1) = 0 ✅. Because the solution contains free mobile H₃O⁺ and I⁻ ions, the tester bulb **will glow**—just like aqueous HCl.\n11. (a) **False** ❌: KI is made of monatomic K⁺ and I⁻ ions with only ionic bonding. (b) **False** ❌: the C–S difference is 2.5 − 2.5 = 0.0 (non-polar). (c) **False** ❌: Cl (3.0) is more electronegative than Be (1.5), so Cl carries δ⁻ and Be carries δ⁺. (d) **False** ❌: once PH₄⁺ forms, all four P–H bonds are identical. (e) **True** ✅ (silicon is often called a metalloid, and the Si–H bond is a shared-pair covalent bond).\n12. Total valence electrons in SiH₄ = 4 (from Si) + 4 × 1 (from 4 H) = **8**, not 10. The student confused the octet check (where a shared pair counts for both atoms) with the total number of valence electrons available to draw (where each electron is drawn only once).\n13. A coordinate bond arrow must point **from donor to acceptor**. H⁺ has zero electrons and cannot donate anything; phosphorus donates its lone pair, so the arrow must point **P → H⁺** (or **PH₃ → H⁺**).\n14. (a) **KI**: ionic solid whose ions move freely when melted. (b) **PH₃**: central P has 1 lone pair, 3 single bonds, and P–H difference = 0.0. (c) **HI**: polar gas that ionizes in water to give H₃O⁺ and I⁻. (d) **CS₂**: liquid with two C=S double bonds and no lone pair on the central C atom. Every clue matches a unique square ✅.\n\n---\n\nBy the final round, all nine squares on Elena's card were marked—every single one backed up by a clear chemical reason—and she called out, \"Bingo!\" Dr. Morales beamed. \"See? Not a single formula memorized by rote. Just four questions, applied step by step.\" At the top of her card, Elena wrote her takeaway: *\"Difference, metal vs. non-metal, polyatomic ion, lone pair—and always check the electron count.\"*",
            "bodyHinglish": "(Sab sawaal paper par hal karne ke liye hain. Use these values where needed: H 2.1, C 2.5, S 2.5, P 2.1, Cl 3.0, K 0.8, Br 2.8. Be about 1.5 (Chapter 6 table). Atoms of Group 17 go Cl, Br, I from top to bottom.)\n\n1. CS₂ is a poisonous, flammable liquid (named only; nobody handles it). C has 4 and S has 6 valence electrons. (a) Find the C–S electronegativity difference and say whether the bond is polar. (b) Draw the dot diagram with two C=S double bonds and show that the total electron count matches.\n2. In H–I, iodine is more electronegative than hydrogen but less electronegative than bromine (2.8). Without a number for iodine, say whether the H–I difference is bigger or smaller than 0.7, and name the atom that carries δ⁻.\n3. KI is a white solid. Use the steps of the master method to say what kind of bond it has, even though no electronegativity value for iodine is given. What does the solid do on melting that a covalent solid like wax would not?\n4. SiH₄ (named only: poisonous and flammable gas, no practice with it) is like CH₄: Si has 4 valence electrons. Show that 8 electrons in total are needed, draw the dot diagram, and check the octet on Si and the duplet on H.\n5. (BeCl₂ is poisonous: named only.) In BeCl₂, find the electronegativity difference using Be about 1.5. Which atom carries δ⁻, and in which zone of the thumb rule is the bond?\n6. PH₃ (phosphine, a poisonous gas: named only) has a lone pair on P. It can join an H⁺ to form PH₄⁺ (for example, from HI). (a) Write the equation PH₃ + H⁺ → PH₄⁺ and check atoms and charge. (b) Which atom is the donor and which the acceptor, and in which direction does the arrow point? (c) Show by counting that P in PH₄⁺ has 8 electrons around it.\n7. The compound PH₄I is made of PH₄⁺ and I⁻. List every kind of bond in one formula unit and how many of each.\n8. In PH₃ the three P–H bonds are arranged in a pyramid, like NH₃. Find the P–H difference. Is the PH₃ molecule polar? Compare with NH₃ (N–H is 0.9).\n9. Predict, with reasons, which of KI and CS₂ dissolves in water. Then say which of the two solutions would light a conductivity tester bulb.\n10. HI is a corrosive, poisonous gas (named only) whose polar molecules split in water like HCl does: HI + H₂O → H₃O⁺ + I⁻. Check atoms and charge in the equation. Will the bulb of a tester glow in an HI solution? Explain.\n11. State True or False and correct the false ones: (a) KI contains covalent bonds inside. (b) The C=S bond in CS₂ is polar. (c) In BeCl₂ the Be atom carries δ⁻. (d) In PH₄⁺ the coordinate bond is weaker than the other three P–H bonds. (e) SiH₄ has a non-metal–non-metal covalent bond.\n12. A student draws SiH₄ with 10 dots and says, \"I counted the shared electrons for both atoms.\" Find the mistake and give the correct total.\n13. A student writes the arrow in PH₃ + H⁺ as H⁺ → PH₃. What is wrong, and what is the correct direction and the reason?\n14. Bond Bingo round. Clues: (a) an ionic solid that conducts when melted; (b) a gas whose central atom has one lone pair, three single bonds and all bonds with difference 0.0; (c) a gas whose polar molecules form H₃O⁺ and I⁻ in water; (d) a liquid with two C=S double bonds and no lone pair on the central atom. Match them to KI, HI, PH₃ and CS₂.\n\n### Answers\n\n1. (a) C = 2.5, S = 2.5, farq = **0.0**, bond **non-polar**. (b) S=C=S: har S par 2 lone pairs (4 e) aur C ke saath 2 shared pairs (4 e) = 8; C ke paas 4 shared pairs (8 e) = 8. Kul valence: 4 + 6 + 6 = **16**. Figure mein: lone pairs 4 (2 har S par) = 8 e, bonds 4 pairs = 8 e, kul 16 ✅. Dono bond ka farq zero hai, isliye CS₂ ke bond non-polar.\n2. I, H se zyada electronegative hai (jaise I > 2.1) aur Br (2.8) se kam. To H–I farq 0 aur 0.7 ke beech hai: yaani **0.7 se chhota**. δ⁻ **iodine** par. Jaanch: I 2.1 se bada aur 2.8 se chhota, farq kabhi 0.7 se zyada nahi ✅.\n3. Step 2: K metal, I non-metal, to **ionic** (K⁺ aur I⁻); I ki value ki zaroorat nahi padi. Step 1 ki jaanch: K = 0.8 bahut kam hai, to bada farq hona hi hai. Melt hone par ions **free** ho jaate hain, to molten KI **bijli chalata hai**; wax molecules ke roop mein rehta hai aur bijli nahi chalata.\n4. Kul valence: 4 + 4 × 1 = **8** electron = 4 pairs. Si ke 4 bond, har ek mein 1 pair. Octet: Si ke paas 4 pairs × 2 = 8 ✅, har H ke paas 1 pair = 2 (duplet) ✅. Sab 8 electrons ek baar gine gaye.\n5. Farq = 3.0 − 1.5 = **1.5**, **Cl** par δ⁻. 1.5, 0.5 se 1.7 ke beech: **polar covalent zone**. Jaanch: 3.0 − 1.5 = 1.5 ✅. (Be ka octet yahan poora nahi hota: ye aage ki class ki baat hai.)\n6. (a) PH₃ + H⁺ → PH₄⁺: H 3 + 1 = 4 ✅, P 1 ✅, charge 0 + 1 = +1 ✅. (b) **Donor: P** (lone pair), **acceptor: H⁺** (khaali jagah), arrow **P → H⁺**. (c) P ke 5 + 4 H ke 4 = 9, charge +1 ke liye 9 − 1 = 8 electron = 4 bonds × 2 = 8 ✅.\n7. **Ionic:** PH₄⁺ aur I⁻ ke beech 1. **Covalent:** 3 P–H. **Coordinate:** 1 P→H. Jaanch: ion ke andar 3 + 1 = 4 bond ✅ aur charge (+1) + (−1) = 0 ✅.\n8. P–H farq = 2.1 − 2.1 = **0.0**, to bond non-polar. Is liye bonds par koi δ⁺ ya δ⁻ nahi aur koi bond-teer nahi, to PH₃ **bahut hi kam polar (lagbhag non-polar)** hai; P ki lone pair ki wajah se thodi si polarity rehti hai, uska hisaab aage ki class mein. NH₃ mein N–H = 3.0 − 2.1 = 0.9 hai, polar bonds aur pyramid shape, isliye NH₃ saaf polar hai, PH₃ se kahin zyada. Jaanch: 2.1 − 2.1 = 0.0, 3.0 − 2.1 = 0.9 ✅.\n9. **KI** ghulega: ionic hai, paani ke polar molecule K⁺ aur I⁻ ko alag kar dete hain. **CS₂** non-polar hai (Q1), like dissolves like ke hisaab se paani mein nahi ghulta. Tester: **KI ka ghol** bulb jalayega (free ions), CS₂ ke saath ions ka sawaal hi nahi.\n10. Atoms: H 1 + 2 = 3 ✅, I 1 ✅, O 1 ✅, charge 0 = (+1) + (−1) ✅. HI ke ghol mein H₃O⁺ aur I⁻ free hain, isliye bulb **jalega**. Ye HCl wali kahani ka doosra roop hai.\n11. (a) **False** ❌: KI ionic hai, K⁺ aur I⁻ ke beech sirf ionic bond. (b) **False** ❌: farq 0.0 hai. (c) **False** ❌: Cl δ⁻, Be δ⁺. (d) **False** ❌: PH₄⁺ mein chaaron bond ek jaise hain, bante hi farq pehchana nahi ja sakta. (e) **True** ✅ (Si ko kabhi metalloid bhi kehte hain, par Si–H bond baantne wala covalent bond hi hai).\n12. Kul valence electrons: 4 + 4 = **8**. 10 dots banana galat hai, kyunki ginti mein shared electrons ko do baar gin liya. Octet ki jaanch mein shared pair dono ke liye ginte hain, par drawing mein har electron ek baar hi banta hai.\n13. Arrow **donor se acceptor** ki taraf hota hai. H⁺ ke paas koi electron hai hi nahi, to wo donor nahi ho sakta. Dono electron P ki lone pair se aaye, isliye sahi arrow **P → H⁺**.\n14. (a) **KI**: ionic aur molten mein ions free. (b) **PH₃**: ek lone pair, 3 single bond, farq 0.0. (c) **HI**: polar molecule paani mein H₃O⁺ aur I⁻. (d) **CS₂**: 2 C=S double bond, C par lone pair nahi. Har clue ek hi ko milta hai: KI ke alawa koi ionic nahi, PH₃ ke alawa kisi par lone pair wala kendra nahi, HI akela jo ions deta hai, CS₂ akela double bond wala ✅.\n\n---\n\nMugdha ke card par aakhir tak saare nau cell kaaran ke saath bhar gaye aur use \"Bingo!\" bolne ka mauka mila. Mr. Mokashi ne kaha, \"Dekha, ratna nahi pada. Bas chaar sawaal poochhe aur jawab khud aa gaya.\" Mugdha ne apni copy mein likha: \"Farq, metal ya non-metal, ion, lone pair, aur aakhir mein ginti.\""
          },
          {
            "heading": "Chapter Summary",
            "headingHinglish": "Chapter ka saar",
            "body": "| Topic | What We Learned | One-Line Takeaway |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1. Polar Covalent Bond | A shared pair can be pulled unequally, creating partial charges δ⁺ and δ⁻ | The more electronegative atom becomes δ⁻, and bond character shifts gradually with electronegativity difference |\n| 2. Polar and Non-polar Molecules | Molecular polarity depends on both bond polarity and 3D shape | Symmetrical shapes cancel bond arrows; \"like dissolves like\" |\n| 3. Coordinate Bond | Both shared electrons come from one atom's lone pair (donor → acceptor) | Once NH₄⁺ or H₃O⁺ forms, all its covalent bonds are identical |\n| 4. One Compound, Three Bond Types | Polyatomic ions carry internal covalent/coordinate bonds while forming ionic lattices outside | Dissolving in water breaks only the outer ionic bond, leaving polyatomic ions intact |\n| 5. Comparing All Bonds | Bulk physical properties flow from particle structure and attractions | Lattice vs. discrete molecules explains melting points, brittleness, solubility, and conductivity |\n| 6. Putting It All Together | The 4-step master method and how to avoid common electron-counting traps | Check difference, metal/non-metal, polyatomic ions, lone-pair donation, and electron count |\n\nThat completes our two-year journey through Chemical Bonding! You can now look at any simple chemical formula and explain what kind of bonds hold it together, why they formed, and how the substance will behave in the real world.\n\n**What's next?** In Chapter 8 (Analytical Chemistry), we will step into the shoes of a chemical detective: how can you identify which metal ion is hiding inside an unknown salt solution? Just add drops of sodium hydroxide or ammonium hydroxide and watch the color of the precipitate that appears—blue, green, white, or reddish-brown!",
            "bodyHinglish": "| Topic | Kya seekha | Ek line |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1. Polar Covalent Bond | Shared pair barabar nahi bhi baanta jaata hai; farq se δ⁺ aur δ⁻ | Zyada kheenchne wala atom δ⁻ hota hai, aur farq se bond ka swabhav dheere-dheere badalta hai |\n| 2. Polar aur Non-polar Molecule | Molecule ki polarity bonds aur shape dono par | Symmetric shape mein arrows kat jaate hain; like dissolves like |\n| 3. Coordinate Bond | Ek hi atom ke dono electron (donor se acceptor) | NH₄⁺ aur H₃O⁺ mein banne ke baad saare bond ek jaise |\n| 4. Ek Compound, Teen Bond | Polyatomic ion aur ek formula mein teeno bond | Ion ke bahar ionic, andar covalent aur coordinate |\n| 5. Sab Bonds Ki Tulna | Saare bonds ke gun aur unke karan | Structure se melting point, solubility aur conduction samajh aate hain |\n| 6. Sab Kuch Mila Kar | 4-step method, mixed puzzles aur galtiyan | Farq, metal ya non-metal, ion, lone pair, phir ginti |\n\nChemical Bonding ka safar yahan poora hua: ab tum kisi bhi simple formula ko dekh kar bata sakte ho ki uske andar kaun sa bond hai, kyun, aur uske gun kya honge.\n\n**Aage kya?** Chapter 8 Analytical Chemistry mein hum dekhenge ki kisi anjaan namak mein kaun sa metal ion chhupa hai, ye kaise pata chale: sodium hydroxide milao aur jo thos banta hai uska rang dekho, neela, hara, safed ya laal-bhura."
          }
