            "explanation": "Pitfall to avoid (\"Double-counting shared electrons when drawing a dot diagram.\"): When finding the total number of dots to draw, count each valence electron only once; when checking octets, a shared pair counts toward both bonded atoms. Demo: In H₂S, total valence electrons = 6 + 1 + 1 = 8. If a student draws 6 dots on S and then draws 2 extra electrons for each bond separately, the diagram ends up showing 6 + 2 + 2 = 10 electrons—2 more than actually exist! Always draw the exact total (8 electrons), with the 2 shared pairs sitting between S and the two H atoms so they count for both.",
            "explanationHinglish": "Bachke rehna (\"Dot diagram mein electrons do baar gin liye.\"): Kul electrons ek hi baar ginte hain, par octet ki ginti mein shared pair dono atomon ke liye aata hai. Ye dono ek ho jaate hain. Demo: H₂S mein kul electrons 6 + 1 + 1 = 8. Agar koi har bond ke dono electron S ke liye bhi gine aur H ke liye bhi alag se dot bana de, to drawing mein 6 + 2 + 2 = 10 dots ho jaate hain, jo 8 se zyada hain: ye galat hai. Sahi tareeka: kul 8 dots, jinme 2 shared pairs ke electron dono taraf ginti mein aate hain."
