          {
            "heading": "Key Vocabulary",
            "headingHinglish": "Naye shabd",
            "body": "| Term | Meaning | Example |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Anion** | A negatively charged ion; the part of a salt that comes from an acid | Cl⁻, SO₄²⁻, CO₃²⁻ |\n| **Curdy precipitate** | A clumpy, cottage-cheese-like white solid that forms in solution | AgCl |\n| **Effervescence** | Rapid bubbling of a gas escaping from a liquid | CO₂ bubbling out when acid hits Na₂CO₃ |\n| **Brisk effervescence** | Immediate, vigorous fizzing of gas bubbles | Carbonate + dilute acid |\n| **Limewater** | A clear aqueous solution of Ca(OH)₂; turns milky when CO₂ gas is bubbled through it | Test for CO₂ (Chapter 1) |\n| **Acidify** | To add a few drops of dilute acid to a sample solution before adding the main test reagent | Adding dil. HNO₃ before AgNO₃ |\n| **Ammonia gas** | NH₃, a pungent gas that irritates the eyes, nose, and throat | Released from NH₄⁺ + OH⁻ on warming |\n| **Moist red litmus** | Damp red litmus paper, which turns blue in the presence of an alkaline gas like NH₃ | Test for NH₃ gas |\n| **Fume hood (fume cupboard)** | An enclosed lab cabinet with an exhaust fan that safely draws away irritating or toxic gases | Used for teacher demonstrations |",
            "bodyHinglish": "| Shabd | Matlab (simple bhasha mein) | Example |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Anion** | Negative charge wala ion; namak ka wo hissa jo acid se aaya | Cl⁻, SO₄²⁻, CO₃²⁻ |\n| **Curdy precipitate** | Dahi ki tarah phatta-phatta safed dhodha jo ghol mein bana | AgCl |\n| **Effervescence** | Ghol mein se gas ke bulbule tezi se uthna | Na₂CO₃ par acid daalne par CO₂ |\n| **Brisk effervescence** | Aisi bulbulahat jo turant aur tez ho | carbonate + dilute acid |\n| **Chune ka paani (lime water)** | Ca(OH)₂ ka saaf ghol; CO₂ milte hi dudhiya ho jaata hai | CO₂ ka test (Chapter 1) |\n| **Acidify karna** | Test se pehle ghol mein dilute acid daal dena | AgNO₃ se pehle dil. HNO₃ |\n| **Ammonia gas** | NH₃, tez gandh wali, gale aur aankh ko jalane wali gas | NH₄⁺ + OH⁻ se bani |\n| **Moist red litmus** | Geela laal litmus kaagaz, jo alkali (ya NH₃) se neela ho jaata hai | NH₃ ka test |\n| **Fume cupboard** | Hawa bahar kheenchne wala bandh almirah, jisme zehreeli gas wale kaam hote hain | lab ka teacher demo |"
          },
          {
            "heading": "Safety First (Read Carefully Before Proceeding)",
            "headingHinglish": "Safety pehle (ek baar dhyan se padho)",
            "body": "Every test in this topic is performed **in a school lab with the teacher** (wearing goggles and gloves, using very dilute solutions dispensed by the teacher) or observed as a **teacher demonstration**. Students never perform these tests alone or at home. Keep two special hazards in mind: **silver nitrate (AgNO₃) and barium chloride (BaCl₂) are toxic**, and AgNO₃ leaves dark black stains on skin, so students never handle them directly. **Ammonia gas (NH₃)** irritates the eyes, nose, and throat, so its test is performed in a fume hood (or studied on paper). Never taste any chemical or sniff directly at the mouth of a test tube. Leftover solutions containing silver or barium ions are never poured down the sink—the teacher collects them in a designated waste bottle.",
            "bodyHinglish": "Is topic ke saare tests **school lab mein teacher ke saath** hote hain (goggles, gloves, bahut dilute ghol, teacher khud dropper se daalta hai) ya **sirf dekhne ke liye (teacher demo)**. Koi student ye ghar par ya akele kabhi nahi karega. Aur do baatein: **silver nitrate (AgNO₃) aur barium chloride (BaCl₂) zehreele hain**, aur AgNO₃ skin par kaala daag chhodta hai, isliye inhe koi student nahi chhoota. **Ammonia gas** aankh, naak aur gale ko jalati hai: uska test fume cupboard mein ya sirf padhne ke liye. Koi chemical chakhna ya mooh ke paas sungna nahi. Bacha hua ghol student sink mein nahi daalta: teacher use alag bottle mein jama karta hai, kyunki silver aur barium ke ghol seedhe nali mein nahi bahaye jaate."
          },
          {
            "heading": "Part A: Chloride Ion (Cl⁻): Curdy White Precipitate with AgNO₃",
            "headingHinglish": "Part A: Chloride Cl⁻: AgNO₃ se curdy safed dhodha",
            "body": "In Chapter 1 we saw that mixing silver nitrate solution with sodium chloride solution immediately produces a white precipitate: AgNO₃ + NaCl → AgCl↓ + NaNO₃ ([acids-bases-and-salts-part5.md](../acids-bases-and-salts/acids-bases-and-salts-part5.md)). There we used it as a way to prepare an insoluble salt. Now we flip it into a detective test: **if an unknown solution contains chloride ions (Cl⁻), adding silver nitrate solution will produce that exact same curdy white precipitate**, making it our **proof for chloride**!\n\nOnly two ions do the actual work here, while Na⁺ (or whatever cation is present) and NO₃⁻ act as spectator ions:\n\nAg⁺ + Cl⁻ → AgCl↓ (white, curdy precipitate)\n\nSilver chloride (AgCl) has one more confirming signature: **it dissolves readily when excess dilute NH₄OH is added** (forming a colorless water-soluble complex ion, [Ag(NH₃)₂]⁺, studied in higher grades). That confirms the white precipitate really is silver chloride.\n\n### Why Do We Acidify with Dilute HNO₃ First?\n\nIn the official test for chloride, we **first add a few drops of dilute nitric acid (HNO₃)** before adding AgNO₃(aq) (or we check that the white precipitate is insoluble in dilute HNO₃). Why? Because **carbonate ions (CO₃²⁻)** also react with Ag⁺ to form a **whitish (pale yellowish-white) precipitate** of silver carbonate, Ag₂CO₃! If a solution contained carbonate instead of chloride, AgNO₃ alone could fool us into thinking chloride was present. Adding dilute HNO₃ destroys any carbonate ions (releasing CO₂ gas) and dissolves Ag₂CO₃, whereas true **AgCl is completely insoluble in dilute HNO₃**:\n\nNa₂CO₃ + 2AgNO₃ → Ag₂CO₃↓ + 2NaNO₃\nAg₂CO₃ + 2HNO₃ → 2AgNO₃ + H₂O + CO₂↑\n\nAnd why must we use **HNO₃** rather than dilute HCl to acidify the sample? Because **HCl already contains Cl⁻ ions!** Adding HCl would dump chloride ions into the tube and produce a white AgCl precipitate with AgNO₃ even if the original sample had zero chloride in it! In our gatehouse analogy, that would be like the security guard handing a delivery passkey to a stranger and then saying, \"Look, he has a passkey!\"\n\n```\n   In the test tube:              After adding AgNO₃:\n\n    :              :              :              :\n    : clear liquid :              : clear liquid :\n    :  (sample +   :              :    above     :\n    :  dil. HNO₃)  :              :              :\n    :              :              :  ########    :   <-- AgCl: white, curdy\n    :              :              :  ########    :       precipitate settled at bottom\n    :______________:              :______________:\n      before (clear)                 after\n```\n\n> **Figure 4.2** – The test for chloride: acidifying the sample solution with dilute HNO₃ and adding a few drops of AgNO₃ produces a curdy white precipitate of AgCl at the bottom of the tube.\n\n### Example 1. Testing for Cl⁻ in NaCl Solution\n\n**Question:** A colorless solution contains NaCl. The teacher adds a few drops of dilute HNO₃ followed by AgNO₃ solution. What is observed? Write the molecular and net ionic equations.\n\n**Step 1 (What was added):** Dilute HNO₃ (pre-check), then AgNO₃ solution.\n**Step 2 (What was seen):** A curdy white precipitate (insoluble in dilute HNO₃, soluble in dilute NH₄OH).\n**Step 3 (What it means):** Chloride ions (Cl⁻) are present.\n**Step 4 (Equations):**\nMolecular: NaCl + AgNO₃ → AgCl↓ + NaNO₃\nNet ionic: Ag⁺ + Cl⁻ → AgCl↓ (spectator ions: Na⁺ and NO₃⁻)\n\n**Check:** Atoms: Na 1 = 1, Cl 1 = 1, Ag 1 = 1, N 1 = 1, O 3 = 3 ✅. Ionic charge: left (+1) + (−1) = 0, right neutral AgCl = 0 ✅.\n\n### Example 2. MgCl₂ Has Two Chloride Ions per Formula Unit\n\n**Question:** A solution of MgCl₂ is acidified with dilute HNO₃ and treated with AgNO₃ solution. Write the balanced molecular equation and explain what happens to the Mg²⁺ ion.\n\nMgCl₂ + 2AgNO₃ → 2AgCl↓ + Mg(NO₃)₂\n\nBecause one formula unit of MgCl₂ releases **2 Cl⁻ ions**, it requires **2 Ag⁺ ions** and forms **2 formula units of AgCl**. Meanwhile, the Mg²⁺ ion remains dissolved in the water alongside the NO₃⁻ ions—it is a spectator ion and does not interfere with the chloride test at all.\n\n**Check:** Mg 1 = 1, Cl 2 = 2, Ag 2 = 2, N 2 = 2, O 6 = 6 ✅. For each chloride ion, the net ionic reaction is still Ag⁺ + Cl⁻ → AgCl↓ ✅.",
            "bodyHinglish": "Chapter 1 mein humne dekha tha ki silver nitrate aur NaCl milte hi safed precipitate banta hai: AgNO₃ + NaCl → AgCl↓ + NaNO₃ (Ye [acids-bases-and-salts-part5.md](../acids-bases-and-salts/acids-bases-and-salts-part5.md) mein hai). Wahan ye namak banane ka tareeka tha. Ab hum use ulta padhenge: **agar ghol mein chloride ion hai to silver nitrate daalne par wahi dhodha banega**, isliye ye dhodha **chloride ka saboot** hai.\n\nIsme saara kaam sirf do ions ka hai. Na⁺ (ya doosra cation) aur NO₃⁻ darshak (spectator) hain, wo kuch nahi badalte:\n\nAg⁺ + Cl⁻ → AgCl↓ (safed, curdy)\n\nIs AgCl ki ek pehchaan aur hai: ye **dilute NH₄OH mein ghul jaata hai** (ek complex ion banta hai, [Ag(NH₃)₂]⁺: complex ion, aage ki class mein). Isse pata chalta hai ki dhodha sach mein silver chloride hi tha.\n\n### Pehle acid kyun?\n\nTest mein **pehle dilute nitric acid (HNO₃)** daalte hain, phir AgNO₃. Kyun? Kyunki **carbonate** (CO₃²⁻) bhi silver ke saath ek **safed-sa (halka peela) precipitate** deta hai: Ag₂CO₃. Agar ghol mein carbonate hua to hum use galti se chloride samajh lenge. Dilute HNO₃ carbonate ko tod deta hai (CO₂ gas nikalti hai) aur Ag₂CO₃ ka dhodha bhi ghula deta hai, jabki AgCl ko wo nahi ghulata:\n\nNa₂CO₃ + 2AgNO₃ → Ag₂CO₃↓ + 2NaNO₃\nAg₂CO₃ + 2HNO₃ → 2AgNO₃ + H₂O + CO₂↑\n\nAur acid HNO₃ hi kyun, HCl kyun nahi? Kyunki HCl ke andar khud Cl⁻ hai! Wo daalte hi AgNO₃ se AgCl bana dega, chahe sample mein chloride tha ya nahi. Guard ke analogy mein ye aisa hai ki **check karne wala khud visitor ka naam bol de**.\n\n```\n   Test tube mein :               AgNO₃ daalne ke baad :\n\n    :              :              :              :\n    :   saaf ghol  :              :  ghol upar   :\n    :   (namak +   :              :  saaf hai    :\n    :    dil. HNO₃):              :              :\n    :              :              :  ########    :   <-- AgCl: safed,\n    :              :              :  ########    :       curdy, neeche baitha\n    :______________:              :______________:\n      pehle (saaf)                  baad mein\n```\n\n> **Figure 4.2** – Chloride ka test: namak ke ghol ko dilute HNO₃ se acidify karke AgNO₃ ki kuch boondein daalne par safed curdy AgCl neeche baith jaata hai. Upar ka ghol saaf rehta hai.\n\n### Example 1. NaCl mein Cl⁻ ka test\n\n**Sawaal:** Ek colourless ghol mein NaCl hai. Teacher ne dilute HNO₃ ki kuch boondein aur phir AgNO₃ daali. Kya dikhega? Equation likho.\n\n**Kadam 1 (kya daala):** dil. HNO₃ (pre-check), phir AgNO₃.\n**Kadam 2 (kya dikha):** safed curdy dhodha, jo NH₄OH daalne par ghul jaata hai.\n**Kadam 3 (kya matlab):** chloride ion maujood hai.\n**Kadam 4 (equation):**\nMolecular: NaCl + AgNO₃ → AgCl↓ + NaNO₃\nIonic: Ag⁺ + Cl⁻ → AgCl↓ (Na⁺ aur NO₃⁻ darshak)\n\n**Jaanch:** atoms: Na 1 = 1, Cl 1 = 1, Ag 1 = 1, N 1 = 1, O 3 = 3 ✅. Ionic equation mein charge: bayen taraf (+1) + (−1) = 0, dayen taraf AgCl neutral = 0 ✅.\n\n### Example 2. MgCl₂ mein do chloride\n\n**Sawaal:** MgCl₂ ke ek ghol ko acidify karke AgNO₃ milaya. Balanced equation likho aur batao ki Mg²⁺ ka kya hua.\n\nMgCl₂ + 2AgNO₃ → 2AgCl↓ + Mg(NO₃)₂\n\nMgCl₂ ke ek formula unit mein **2 Cl⁻** hain, isliye **2 Ag⁺** lagenge aur **2 AgCl** banenge. Mg²⁺ ghol mein hi rehta hai aur Mg(NO₃)₂ ka hissa ban jaata hai: wo darshak hai, isse test par koi asar nahi.\n\n**Jaanch:** Mg 1 = 1, Cl 2 = 2, Ag 2 = 2, N 2 = 2, O 6 = 6 ✅. Ionic equation har chloride ke liye wahi: Ag⁺ + Cl⁻ → AgCl↓, to 2 chloride ke liye 2 baar ✅."
          },
          {
            "heading": "Part B: Sulfate Ion (SO₄²⁻): White Precipitate with BaCl₂",
            "headingHinglish": "Part B: Sulphate SO₄²⁻: BaCl₂ se safed dhodha",
            "body": "The dedicated test for sulfate ions uses **barium chloride (BaCl₂) solution**. Because **barium sulfate (BaSO₄)** is insoluble in water (recall our solubility rules in Chapter 1, Topic 5), mixing Ba²⁺ ions with SO₄²⁻ ions immediately produces a heavy white precipitate:\n\nBa²⁺ + SO₄²⁻ → BaSO₄↓ (white precipitate)\n\n**Key signature of BaSO₄:** It is **insoluble in dilute mineral acids** (it does not dissolve in dilute HCl or dilute HNO₃). That property is crucial for our pre-check!\n\n### Why Do We Acidify with Dilute HCl First?\n\nOnce again, carbonate ions (CO₃²⁻) can set a trap: **barium carbonate (BaCO₃)** is *also* a white precipitate! If we added BaCl₂ to a solution containing carbonate, white BaCO₃ would precipitate out and mimic sulfate. To prevent that false alarm, we **first add dilute HCl** (or add dilute HCl to the white precipitate). Any carbonate present breaks down with brisk effervescence (releasing CO₂ gas), and BaCO₃ dissolves completely in dilute HCl—whereas **BaSO₄ stays completely undissolved**:\n\nNa₂CO₃ + BaCl₂ → BaCO₃↓ + 2NaCl\nBaCO₃ + 2HCl → BaCl₂ + H₂O + CO₂↑\n\nHere we acidify with dilute **HCl** (or dilute HNO₃), but **never with sulfuric acid (H₂SO₄)**—because H₂SO₄ already contains SO₄²⁻ ions and would immediately form a white BaSO₄ precipitate with BaCl₂ even if the sample contained no sulfate!\n\n```\n   Sample + BaCl₂:                Then add dilute HCl:\n\n    :              :              :              :\n    :  liquid      :              :  liquid      :\n    :              :              :              :\n    :  ########    :   ------>    :  ########    <-- BaSO₄: does NOT dissolve\n    :______________:              :______________:        (sulfate is present)\n\n    :              :              :              :\n    :  liquid      :              : clear liquid :\n    :  ########    :   ------>    : (bubbles of  :\n    :______________:              :  CO₂ rise)   :\n                                  :______________:   <-- BaCO₃: dissolves with fizzing\n                                                         (it was carbonate, not sulfate!)\n```\n\n> **Figure 4.3** – Top pair: a BaSO₄ precipitate remains undissolved after adding dilute HCl, confirming sulfate (SO₄²⁻). Bottom pair: a BaCO₃ precipitate dissolves with fizzing when dilute HCl is added, showing the white solid came from carbonate (CO₃²⁻) rather than sulfate.\n\n### Example 3. Testing for SO₄²⁻ in Na₂SO₄ Solution\n\n**Question:** To a solution of Na₂SO₄, the teacher adds a few drops of dilute HCl followed by BaCl₂ solution. What is observed? Write the equations and name the spectator ions.\n\n**Step 1:** Add dilute HCl (pre-check to rule out carbonate), then BaCl₂ solution.\n**Step 2:** A white precipitate forms that does not dissolve even when more dilute HCl is added.\n**Step 3:** Sulfate ions (SO₄²⁻) are present.\n**Step 4:** Molecular: Na₂SO₄ + BaCl₂ → BaSO₄↓ + 2NaCl. Net ionic: Ba²⁺ + SO₄²⁻ → BaSO₄↓. Spectator ions: Na⁺ and Cl⁻.\n\n**Check:** Na 2 = 2, S 1 = 1, O 4 = 4, Ba 1 = 1, Cl 2 = 2 ✅. Ionic charge: (+2) + (−2) = 0 ✅. (Barium salts are toxic—teacher demonstration only.)\n\n### Example 4. MgSO₄ and (NH₄)₂SO₄: Same Anion, Different Cations\n\n**Question:** Both MgSO₄ and (NH₄)₂SO₄ solutions are acidified with dilute HCl and treated with BaCl₂ solution. Will both give the exact same white precipitate? Write both molecular equations.\n\nYes! Both form the exact same white precipitate of **BaSO₄**, because **this test responds to the SO₄²⁻ anion regardless of which cation is paired with it**.\nMgSO₄ + BaCl₂ → BaSO₄↓ + MgCl₂\n(NH₄)₂SO₄ + BaCl₂ → BaSO₄↓ + 2NH₄Cl\n\n**Check:** First equation: Mg 1, S 1, O 4, Ba 1, Cl 2 on both sides ✅. Second equation: N 2, H 8, S 1, O 4, Ba 1, Cl 2 on both sides ✅. Both share the identical net ionic equation: Ba²⁺ + SO₄²⁻ → BaSO₄↓.",
            "bodyHinglish": "Sulphate ka fixed test **barium chloride (BaCl₂)** hai. Barium ke sulphate ka **BaSO₄** paani mein nahi ghulta (Chapter 1 ki solubility table mein sulphate ke exceptions mein BaSO₄ tha), isliye wo safed dhodha banakar neeche baith jaata hai:\n\nBa²⁺ + SO₄²⁻ → BaSO₄↓ (safed)\n\n**BaSO₄ ki khaas pehchaan:** wo **acid mein bhi nahi ghulta** (dilute HCl ya dilute HNO₃ mein bhi nahi). Yahi pehchaan agle hisse mein bahut kaam aayegi.\n\n### Pehle dilute HCl kyun?\n\nCarbonate yahan bhi dhokha de sakta hai: **barium carbonate (BaCO₃)** bhi safed hota hai. Isliye pehle ghol mein dilute **HCl** daalte hain. Agar carbonate tha, to wo tut jaata hai (CO₂ ke bulbule). Fir BaCl₂ daalne par agar safed dhodha banta hai, to wo **BaSO₄** hi ho sakta hai:\n\nNa₂CO₃ + BaCl₂ → BaCO₃↓ + 2NaCl\nBaCO₃ + 2HCl → BaCl₂ + H₂O + CO₂↑\n\nYahan acid HCl liya (HNO₃ nahi, koi dikkat nahi), par **sulphuric acid (H₂SO₄) kabhi nahi**, kyunki uske apne SO₄²⁻ se BaCl₂ turant safed BaSO₄ bana dega, chahe sample mein sulphate ho ya na ho.\n\n```\n   Sample + BaCl₂ :              Phir dilute HCl daalo :\n\n    :              :              :              :\n    :  ghol        :              :  ghol        :\n    :              :              :              :\n    :  ########    :   ------>    :  ########    <-- BaSO₄ : nahi ghula\n    :______________:              :______________:        (sulphate hai)\n\n    :              :              :              :\n    :  ghol        :              :  ghol saaf   :\n    :  ########    :   ------>    :  (bulbule    :\n    :______________:              :   uthe)      :\n                                  :______________:   <-- BaCO₃ : ghul gaya\n                                                         (carbonate tha)\n```\n\n> **Figure 4.3** – Upar wali jodi: BaSO₄ ka dhodha dilute HCl daalne ke baad bhi baitha rehta hai. Neeche wali jodi: BaCO₃ ka dhodha dilute HCl se ghul jaata hai aur CO₂ ke bulbule nikalte hain. Isi se dono ko alag pehchante hain.\n\n### Example 3. Na₂SO₄ mein SO₄²⁻\n\n**Sawaal:** Na₂SO₄ ke ghol mein teacher ne dilute HCl ki kuch boondein aur phir BaCl₂ ghol daala. Kya dikhega? Equation aur spectator ions batao.\n\n**Kadam 1:** dil. HCl (carbonate hata do), phir BaCl₂.\n**Kadam 2:** safed dhodha, jo aur HCl daalne par bhi nahi ghulta.\n**Kadam 3:** sulphate ion maujood hai.\n**Kadam 4:** Na₂SO₄ + BaCl₂ → BaSO₄↓ + 2NaCl. Ionic: Ba²⁺ + SO₄²⁻ → BaSO₄↓. Darshak: Na⁺ aur Cl⁻.\n\n**Jaanch:** Na 2 = 2, S 1 = 1, O 4 = 4, Ba 1 = 1, Cl 2 = 2 ✅. Ionic charge: (+2) + (−2) = 0 ✅. (Barium zehreela hai: sirf teacher demo.)\n\n### Example 4. MgSO₄ aur (NH₄)₂SO₄: ek hi anion, do alag cation\n\n**Sawaal:** MgSO₄ aur (NH₄)₂SO₄ dono par acidify karke BaCl₂ daala gaya. Kya dono mein ek jaisa dhodha banega? Equations likho.\n\nHaan, dono mein safed BaSO₄, kyunki **dhodha anion ka hota hai, cation ka nahi**.\nMgSO₄ + BaCl₂ → BaSO₄↓ + MgCl₂\n(NH₄)₂SO₄ + BaCl₂ → BaSO₄↓ + 2NH₄Cl\n\n**Jaanch:** pehli mein Mg 1, S 1, O 4, Ba 1, Cl 2 dono taraf ✅. Doosri mein N 2, H 8, S 1, O 4, Ba 1, Cl 2 dono taraf ✅. Dono mein ionic equation wahi: Ba²⁺ + SO₄²⁻ → BaSO₄↓."
          },
          {
            "heading": "Part C: Carbonate Ion (CO₃²⁻): Add Dilute Acid, Watch for Bubbles and Milky Limewater",
            "headingHinglish": "Part C: Carbonate CO₃²⁻: acid daalo, bulbule dekho",
            "body": "The test for carbonate is our most direct anion test because instead of forming a precipitate in the sample tube, **it releases a gas**! Recall from Chapter 1 that whenever an acid meets a metal carbonate, carbon dioxide gas (CO₂) fizzes out rapidly. That reaction serves as our dedicated test: **add dilute acid (such as dilute HCl or HNO₃), observe brisk effervescence (vigorous bubbling), and pass the escaping gas through clear limewater—the limewater turns milky!**\n\nNa₂CO₃ + 2HCl → 2NaCl + H₂O + CO₂↑\nCaCO₃ + 2HCl → CaCl₂ + H₂O + CO₂↑\nCa(OH)₂ + CO₂ → CaCO₃↓ + H₂O (limewater turns milky due to fine white CaCO₃↓)\n\nIn net ionic form, every soluble carbonate follows the same equation: **CO₃²⁻ + 2H⁺ → H₂O + CO₂↑**\n\n```\n   Teacher demonstration: carbonate + dilute acid\n\n                 delivery tube (carries CO₂ gas across)\n        .--------------------------.\n        :                          :\n     [stopper]                     :\n     :    :                   :    :    :\n     :~~~~:                   :~~~~:~~~~:   <-- clear limewater, Ca(OH)₂(aq)\n     :o o :                   :    o    :       (turns milky as CO₂ bubbles through)\n     :_o__:                   :_________:\n   test tube                  second tube\n   (carbonate +               (limewater)\n    dilute acid)\n```\n\n> **Figure 4.4** – Teacher demonstration of the carbonate test: adding dilute acid to a carbonate produces brisk bubbles of CO₂ gas. A stopper fitted with a delivery tube channels the gas into a tube of clear limewater, turning it milky. No heating is required for this test.\n\n**Safety and good lab technique:** During the demonstration, the teacher makes sure to **lift the delivery tube out of the limewater as soon as the bubbling slows down**, so that cold limewater cannot get sucked backward (\"suck-back\") into the reaction tube. Because carbonates fizz vigorously with dilute acid at room temperature, no burner is needed.\n\n### Example 5. Complete Carbonate Test on Na₂CO₃ Solution\n\n**Question:** Dilute HCl is added to a solution of Na₂CO₃. What is observed, how is the gas identified, and what are the balanced equations?\n\n**Step 1:** Add dilute HCl.\n**Step 2:** Immediate brisk effervescence; passing the colorless, odorless gas through clear limewater turns the limewater milky.\n**Step 3:** The gas is CO₂, proving that carbonate ions (CO₃²⁻) are present.\n**Step 4:** Na₂CO₃ + 2HCl → 2NaCl + H₂O + CO₂↑ and Ca(OH)₂ + CO₂ → CaCO₃↓ + H₂O.\n\n**Check:** First equation: Na 2 = 2, C 1 = 1, O: 3 = 1 + 2 = 3 ✅ (1 in H₂O, 2 in CO₂), H 2 = 2, Cl 2 = 2 ✅. Limewater equation: Ca 1 = 1, O: 2 + 2 = 4 = 3 + 1 ✅, H 2 = 2, C 1 = 1 ✅.\n\n### Example 6. Testing an Insoluble Carbonate: Solid CaCO₃ (Limestone / Chalk)\n\n**Question:** Solid calcium carbonate (CaCO₃) does not dissolve in water. How can we test it for carbonate?\n\nTeacher demonstration: place a small pinch of the solid directly in a test tube and add dilute HCl. It fizzes briskly as CO₂ gas is released, turning limewater milky—while the solid dissolves to leave Ca²⁺ ions in the resulting CaCl₂ solution: CaCO₃ + 2HCl → CaCl₂ + H₂O + CO₂↑.\n\n**Check:** Ca 1 = 1, C 1 = 1, O: 3 = 1 + 2 = 3, H 2 = 2, Cl 2 = 2 ✅.",
            "bodyHinglish": "Carbonate ka test sabse seedha hai kyunki **dhodha nahi, gas** dikhti hai. Chapter 1 mein humne dekha tha ki acid kisi metal carbonate par girta hai to tez bulbulahat ke saath CO₂ nikalti hai. Wahi yahan **test** ban jaata hai: **dilute acid daalo, tez bulbulahat (brisk effervescence) dikhe, aur gas ko chune ke paani mein guzaro: wo dudhiya ho jaaye.**\n\nNa₂CO₃ + 2HCl → 2NaCl + H₂O + CO₂↑\nCaCO₃ + 2HCl → CaCl₂ + H₂O + CO₂↑\nCa(OH)₂ + CO₂ → CaCO₃↓ + H₂O (chune ka paani dudhiya, CaCO₃ ke kaaran)\n\nIonic roop mein dono carbonate ke liye ek hi baat: CO₃²⁻ + 2H⁺ → H₂O + CO₂↑\n\n```\n   Teacher demo : carbonate + dilute acid\n\n                 delivery tube (CO₂ isse jaati hai)\n        .--------------------------.\n        :                          :\n     [cork]                        :\n     :    :                   :    :    :\n     :~~~~:                   :~~~~:~~~~:   <-- chune ka saaf paani\n     :o o :                   :    o    :       (CO₂ ke bulbule)\n     :_o__:                   :_________:\n   test tube                  boiling tube\n   (carbonate +\n    dilute acid)\n```\n\n> **Figure 4.4** – Carbonate ka teacher demo: test tube mein carbonate par dilute acid daalne se CO₂ ke bulbule nikalte hain. Test tube ke mooh par ek cork hai jisme se delivery tube nikalti hai; uska doosra sira chune ke saaf paani mein dooba hai, aur CO₂ use dudhiya kar deti hai. Is demo mein kuch garam nahi kiya jaata.\n\n**Suraksha aur sahi tareeka:** demo mein teacher dhyan rakhta hai ki jab bulbule nikalna band ho jaayein ya pehle hi, **delivery tube chune ke paani mein se bahar nikaal liya jaata hai**, warna chune ka paani wapas test tube mein khinch (suck-back) sakta hai. Ye test aam taur par thande mein hota hai, garam nahi karna padta.\n\n### Example 5. Na₂CO₃ ka poora test\n\n**Sawaal:** Na₂CO₃ ke ghol mein dilute HCl daala gaya. Kya dikhega, kis gas ko kaise pehchanoge, aur kya equation hai?\n\n**Kadam 1:** dilute HCl daalo.\n**Kadam 2:** turant tez bulbulahat. Gas ko chune ke paani mein guzaaro: paani dudhiya.\n**Kadam 3:** ye CO₂ hai, to ghol mein carbonate hai.\n**Kadam 4:** Na₂CO₃ + 2HCl → 2NaCl + H₂O + CO₂↑ aur Ca(OH)₂ + CO₂ → CaCO₃↓ + H₂O.\n\n**Jaanch:** Na 2 = 2, C 1 = 1, O 3 = 1 + 2 = 3 ✅ (H₂O mein 1, CO₂ mein 2), H 2 = 2, Cl 2 = 2 ✅. Chune ke paani wali equation: Ca 1, O 2 + 2 = 4 = 3 + 1 ✅, H 2 = 2, C 1 = 1 ✅.\n\n### Example 6. Thos CaCO₃ (chuna-patthar)\n\n**Sawaal:** Safed thos chuna-patthar paani mein nahi ghulta. Usme carbonate kaise pehchanoge?\n\nTeacher demo: teacher thos ko test tube mein lekar dilute HCl daalta hai: bulbule uthte hain, CO₂ nikalegi aur chune ka paani dudhiya hoga. Equation: CaCO₃ + 2HCl → CaCl₂ + H₂O + CO₂↑. Yahan Ca²⁺ (cation) aur CO₃²⁻ (anion) dono ek hi thos mein hain: acid ke baad jo ghol bana, usme Ca²⁺ ghula hua hai.\n\n**Jaanch:** Ca 1 = 1, C 1 = 1, O 3 = 1 + 2 = 3, H 2 = 2, Cl 2 = 2 ✅."
          },
          {
            "heading": "Part D: Ammonium Ion (NH₄⁺): Warm Gently with NaOH Solution",
            "headingHinglish": "Part D: Ammonium NH₄⁺: NaOH ke saath halka garam karo",
            "body": "Now let's look at one special cation: the **ammonium ion, NH₄⁺**. Unlike our seven metal cations in Topics 2 and 3, NH₄⁺ does **not** form an insoluble hydroxide precipitate when NaOH is added—instead, **it releases a gas**! Recall from Chapter 7 (Chemical Bonding II) that NH₄⁺ is a polyatomic ion (one N bonded to four H atoms with a +1 charge). When an ammonium salt is **warmed gently with NaOH solution**, the OH⁻ ion pulls an H⁺ off NH₄⁺ to form water and release **ammonia gas (NH₃)**:\n\nNH₄Cl + NaOH → NaCl + NH₃↑ + H₂O\nNet ionic: NH₄⁺ + OH⁻ → NH₃↑ + H₂O\n\nAmmonia gas (NH₃) has two classic signatures:\n1. It has a **sharp, pungent odor**.\n2. Because it is an alkaline gas, it turns **moist red litmus paper blue** when held near the mouth of the test tube.\n\n(Students never sniff the gas directly; if a teacher demonstrates checking the odor, they use a gentle hand-\"waft\" inside a fume hood. We cover this test here for study.)\n\n```\n   Teacher demonstration (in a fume hood): ammonium salt + NaOH, warmed gently\n\n        moist red litmus held near mouth:   red --> turns BLUE\n               ^\n               :   NH₃ gas rises out of the open tube\n               :\n      :                :\n      :    solution    :   <-- ammonium salt + NaOH\n      : (warmed gently):\n      :________________:\n              ^\n         gentle warming (by teacher)\n```\n\n> **Figure 4.5** – Teacher demonstration for ammonium ions: warming an ammonium salt with NaOH solution releases NH₃ gas, which rises and turns a strip of moist red litmus paper held near the mouth of the open test tube blue.\n\n### Example 7. Testing for NH₄⁺ in NH₄Cl\n\n**Question:** A solution of NH₄Cl is warmed gently with NaOH solution. What is observed? Write the balanced molecular and net ionic equations.\n\n**Step 1:** Add NaOH solution and warm gently (performed by the teacher in a fume hood).\n**Step 2:** A pungent gas evolves that turns moist red litmus paper held at the mouth of the tube blue.\n**Step 3:** Ammonium ions (NH₄⁺) are present.\n**Step 4:** Molecular: NH₄Cl + NaOH → NaCl + NH₃↑ + H₂O. Net ionic: NH₄⁺ + OH⁻ → NH₃↑ + H₂O. Spectator ions: Na⁺ and Cl⁻.\n\n**Check:** N 1 = 1, H: 4 + 1 = 5 = 3 + 2 ✅, Cl 1 = 1, Na 1 = 1, O 1 = 1 ✅. Ionic charge: (+1) + (−1) = 0 = 0 ✅.\n\n### Example 8. (NH₄)₂SO₄: One Salt, Two Separate Ion Tests\n\n**Question:** How would a chemist identify both ions in ammonium sulfate, (NH₄)₂SO₄?\n\nThis salt contains the **cation NH₄⁺** and the **anion SO₄²⁻**, and **each requires its own separate test on a fresh portion of the sample**:\n- **For NH₄⁺:** Warm gently with NaOH solution to release NH₃ gas (turns moist red litmus blue):\n  (NH₄)₂SO₄ + 2NaOH → Na₂SO₄ + 2NH₃↑ + 2H₂O\n- **For SO₄²⁻:** Acidify a second portion with dilute HCl and add BaCl₂ solution to get a white precipitate of BaSO₄ (insoluble in acid):\n  (NH₄)₂SO₄ + BaCl₂ → BaSO₄↓ + 2NH₄Cl\n\n**Check:** First equation: N 2 = 2, H: 8 + 2 = 10 = 6 + 4 ✅, S 1 = 1, O: 4 + 2 = 6 = 4 + 2 ✅, Na 2 = 2 ✅. Second equation: N 2, H 8, S 1, O 4, Ba 1, Cl 2 on both sides ✅. One test identifies one ion—so identifying the whole salt takes **two separate tests**.",
            "bodyHinglish": "Ab ek positive ion. NH₄⁺ ka test **cation wale tests se alag** hai: isme dhodha nahi banta, balki **gas** nikalti hai. Chapter 7 (chemical bonding II) mein humne dekha tha ki NH₄⁺ ek polyatomic ion hai (N aur 4 H). Jab ammonium namak ko **NaOH** ke saath halka garam karte hain, OH⁻ NH₄⁺ se ek H⁺ le leta hai aur **ammonia gas** (NH₃) bana deta hai:\n\nNH₄Cl + NaOH → NaCl + NH₃↑ + H₂O\nIonic: NH₄⁺ + OH⁻ → NH₃↑ + H₂O\n\nNH₃ ki do pehchaan: (1) **tez, chubhne wali gandh**; (2) ye ek alkaline gas hai, to **geela (moist) laal litmus kaagaz neela** kar deti hai. Gandh sungna student ka kaam nahi: teacher agar sunghata hai to hand se halki hawa test tube ke mooh se apni taraf lata hai (\"waft\"), seedha nahi sungta. Hum yahan ise **sirf padh rahe hain**.\n\n```\n   Teacher demo (fume cupboard mein) : ammonium namak + NaOH, halka garam\n\n        moist red litmus (mooh ke paas):   laal --> neela\n               ^\n               :   NH₃ gas upar uthti hai\n               :\n      :                :\n      :     ghol       :   <-- ammonium namak + NaOH\n      :   (halka garam):\n      :________________:\n              ^\n         thodi aanch (teacher)\n```\n\n> **Figure 4.5** – Ammonium ka teacher demo: ammonium namak aur NaOH ke ghol ko halke se garam karne par NH₃ gas uthti hai. Moist red litmus kaagaz test tube ke mooh ke paas rakhne par neela ho jaata hai. Test tube khula rehta hai, band nahi.\n\n### Example 7. NH₄Cl mein NH₄⁺\n\n**Sawaal:** NH₄Cl ke ghol mein NaOH daalkar halka garam kiya. Kya hoga? Balanced equation aur ionic equation likho.\n\n**Kadam 1:** NaOH daalo aur halka garam karo (teacher).\n**Kadam 2:** tez gandh wali gas, moist red litmus neela.\n**Kadam 3:** NH₄⁺ maujood hai.\n**Kadam 4:** NH₄Cl + NaOH → NaCl + NH₃↑ + H₂O. Ionic: NH₄⁺ + OH⁻ → NH₃↑ + H₂O. Darshak: Na⁺ aur Cl⁻.\n\n**Jaanch:** N 1 = 1, H 4 + 1 = 5 = 3 + 2 ✅, Cl 1 = 1, Na 1 = 1, O 1 = 1 ✅. Ionic charge: (+1) + (−1) = 0 = 0 ✅ (dono taraf neutral).\n\n### Example 8. (NH₄)₂SO₄: ek namak, do pehchaan\n\n**Sawaal:** Ammonium sulphate (NH₄)₂SO₄ mein ek hi namak se kaun-kaun se ion alag-alag tests se milenge?\n\nIs namak mein **cation NH₄⁺** aur **anion SO₄²⁻** dono hain, aur **dono ka test alag** hai:\n- NH₄⁺ ke liye NaOH ke saath halka garam: (NH₄)₂SO₄ + 2NaOH → Na₂SO₄ + 2NH₃↑ + 2H₂O.\n- SO₄²⁻ ke liye acidify karke BaCl₂: (NH₄)₂SO₄ + BaCl₂ → BaSO₄↓ + 2NH₄Cl.\n\n**Jaanch:** pehli equation: N 2 = 2, H 8 + 2 = 10 = 6 + 4 ✅, S 1 = 1, O 4 + 2 = 6 = 4 + 2 ✅, Na 2 = 2 ✅. Doosri: N 2, H 8, S 1, O 4, Ba 1, Cl 2 dono taraf ✅. Ek test se ek hi ion milta hai: isliye dono ion ke liye **do alag test** zaroori hain."
          },
          {
            "heading": "Part E: Calculating the Mass of a Precipitate",
            "headingHinglish": "Part E: Precipitate ka mass: ek chhota hisaab",
            "body": "Can we predict the exact mass of precipitate formed in an anion test? Yes—using the exact same 4-step mole method from Chapter 5! (Atomic masses: Mg = 24, S = 32, O = 16, Cl = 35.5, Ba = 137.)\n\n### Example 9. How Much BaSO₄ Forms from MgSO₄?\n\n**Question:** In a teacher demonstration, a solution containing 1.20 g of MgSO₄ is treated with enough BaCl₂ solution to precipitate all the sulfate ions. What mass of BaSO₄ precipitate forms?\n\n**Step 1 (Balanced equation):** MgSO₄ + BaCl₂ → BaSO₄↓ + MgCl₂. Mole ratio is 1 : 1.\n**Step 2 (Molar masses):** MgSO₄ = 24 + 32 + 4 × 16 = 24 + 32 + 64 = **120 g/mol**. BaSO₄ = 137 + 32 + 64 = **233 g/mol**.\n**Step 3 (Moles of MgSO₄):** 1.20 ÷ 120 = **0.01 mol**.\n**Step 4 (From the 1 : 1 mole ratio):** Moles of BaSO₄ formed = 0.01 mol, so mass of BaSO₄ = 0.01 × 233 = **2.33 g**.\n\n**Check (conservation of mass):** Mass of BaCl₂ needed = 0.01 × (137 + 71 = 208) = 2.08 g. Total reactants = 1.20 + 2.08 = 3.28 g; total products = 2.33 g BaSO₄ + (0.01 × 95 = 0.95 g MgCl₂) = 3.28 g ✅.",
            "bodyHinglish": "Kya dhodhe ka mass pehle se bataya ja sakta hai? Haan, Chapter 5 (mole concept) ki wahi tareeka: pehle moles, phir mass. (Mass: Mg 24, S 32, O 16, Cl 35.5, Ba 137.)\n\n### Example 9. MgSO₄ se kitna BaSO₄?\n\n**Sawaal:** Teacher ne 1.20 g MgSO₄ ke ghol mein BaCl₂ itna daala ki sulphate poora dhodha ban jaaye. Kitna BaSO₄ banega?\n\n**Kadam 1 (equation):** MgSO₄ + BaCl₂ → BaSO₄↓ + MgCl₂. Ratio 1 : 1.\n**Kadam 2 (molar mass):** MgSO₄ = 24 + 32 + 4 × 16 = 24 + 32 + 64 = **120** g/mol. BaSO₄ = 137 + 32 + 64 = **233** g/mol.\n**Kadam 3 (moles):** MgSO₄ = 1.20 ÷ 120 = **0.01 mol**.\n**Kadam 4 (ratio se):** BaSO₄ ke moles bhi 0.01 mol, to mass = 0.01 × 233 = **2.33 g**.\n\n**Jaanch:** BaCl₂ chahiye: 0.01 × 208 = 2.08 g (BaCl₂ = 137 + 71 = 208). Mass sangrakshan: reactants 1.20 + 2.08 = 3.28 g; products BaSO₄ 2.33 g + MgCl₂ (0.01 × 95 = 0.95 g) = 3.28 g ✅."
          },
          {
            "heading": "Part F: All Four Tests at a Glance",
            "headingHinglish": "Part F: Chaaron tests ek nazar mein",
            "body": "```\n   Unknown salt solution (colorless):\n\n   Chloride (Cl⁻)?    dil. HNO₃, then AgNO₃     --> curdy white ppt (dissolves in dil. NH₄OH)\n   Sulfate (SO₄²⁻)?   dil. HCl, then BaCl₂      --> white ppt (does NOT dissolve in dil. acid)\n   Carbonate (CO₃²⁻)? dilute acid               --> brisk bubbles of CO₂ (turns limewater milky)\n   Ammonium (NH₄⁺)?   NaOH + gentle warming     --> pungent NH₃ gas (turns moist red litmus blue)\n\n   None of the three anions above?              --> could be another anion, such as nitrate (NO₃⁻)\n```\n\n> **Figure 4.6** – Summary of the four tests: the reagent added, what is observed, and what it proves. (The specific test for nitrate, NO₃⁻, is covered in higher grades.)\n\n| Ion | Reagent (and Acid Pre-check) | Observation | Net Ionic Equation |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Chloride, Cl⁻ | Acidify with dil. HNO₃, then add AgNO₃ | Curdy white precipitate; dissolves in dilute NH₄OH | Ag⁺ + Cl⁻ → AgCl↓ |\n| Sulfate, SO₄²⁻ | Acidify with dil. HCl, then add BaCl₂ | White precipitate; insoluble in dilute acid | Ba²⁺ + SO₄²⁻ → BaSO₄↓ |\n| Carbonate, CO₃²⁻ | Add dilute acid (HCl or HNO₃) | Brisk effervescence of CO₂; turns limewater milky | CO₃²⁻ + 2H⁺ → H₂O + CO₂↑ |\n| Ammonium, NH₄⁺ | Add NaOH and warm gently | Pungent NH₃ gas; turns moist red litmus blue | NH₄⁺ + OH⁻ → NH₃↑ + H₂O |\n\nOne helpful tip for elimination puzzles: if a soluble salt tests negative for chloride, sulfate, and carbonate (no precipitate with acidified AgNO₃ or BaCl₂, and no bubbles with dilute acid), and you are told the shelf contains only chlorides, sulfates, carbonates, and nitrates, then by elimination the anion must be **nitrate (NO₃⁻)**!",
            "bodyHinglish": "```\n   Namak ka ghol (colourless) :\n\n   Chloride?   dil. HNO₃, phir AgNO₃    --> safed curdy dhodha, NH₄OH mein ghulta hai\n   Sulphate?   dil. HCl, phir BaCl₂     --> safed dhodha, acid mein NAHI ghulta\n   Carbonate?  dilute acid              --> tez bulbule, CO₂, chune ka paani dudhiya\n   Ammonium?   NaOH + halka garam       --> tez gandh, moist red litmus neela\n\n   Teeno mein se koi nahi?              --> ek aur anion ho sakta hai, jaise nitrate NO₃⁻\n```\n\n> **Figure 4.6** – Chaar tests ka saar: har ion ka reagent, kya dikhta hai, aur kya matlab nikalta hai. Nitrate (NO₃⁻) ke liye alag test hai, aage ki class mein.\n\n| Ion | Reagent (pehle acid?) | Kya dikhta hai | Equation (ionic) |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Chloride Cl⁻ | pehle dil. HNO₃, phir AgNO₃ | safed curdy dhodha, NH₄OH mein ghulta hai | Ag⁺ + Cl⁻ → AgCl↓ |\n| Sulphate SO₄²⁻ | pehle dil. HCl, phir BaCl₂ | safed dhodha, acid mein nahi ghulta | Ba²⁺ + SO₄²⁻ → BaSO₄↓ |\n| Carbonate CO₃²⁻ | dilute acid | tez bulbule, CO₂, chune ka paani dudhiya | CO₃²⁻ + 2H⁺ → H₂O + CO₂↑ |\n| Ammonium NH₄⁺ | NaOH, halka garam | NH₃: tez gandh, moist red litmus neela | NH₄⁺ + OH⁻ → NH₃↑ + H₂O |\n\nEk aur sachchi baat: agar kisi namak mein upar ke teeno anion nahi milte (na dhodha, na bulbule), to anion **nitrate (NO₃⁻)** ya koi aur ho sakta hai. Nitrate ka apna test hota hai, jo aage ki class mein aayega."
          },
          {
            "heading": "Try It Yourself: The Gatehouse Matching Game (On Paper)",
            "headingHinglish": "Khud karke dekho: gate-check ki paper game",
            "body": "(No chemicals needed—just paper and pencil with a family member or study partner.)\n\nWrite down four types of visitors at an apartment gate: \"Courier,\" \"Family Guest,\" \"Resident,\" and \"Service Technician,\" each paired with a specific check (Passkey, Intercom Call, Windshield Decal, Work ID Badge). Then switch to our four chemical ions and quiz each other with clues: *\"My ion gives brisk bubbles with dilute acid and turns limewater milky!\"* (Answer: carbonate, CO₃²⁻.) Whenever a test requires an acid pre-check (such as adding dilute HCl before BaCl₂ for sulfate, or dilute HNO₃ before AgNO₃ for chloride), make sure the player states the pre-check too!",
            "bodyHinglish": "(Koi chemical nahi: sirf paper aur pencil, adult ke saath.)\n\nEk kaagaz par chaar visitor likho: \"delivery\", \"mehmaan\", \"resident\", \"dukandaar\". Har ek ke liye ek fixed check likho (OTP, intercom, sticker, ID card). Phir ek dost ko bolo: \"Mere paas ek visitor hai jiski pehchaan sticker se hoti hai.\" Dost bataye ki wo kaun hai. Ab chaar ions ke liye wahi khel khelo: \"Mera ion tez bulbule deta hai, chune ka paani dudhiya karta hai.\" (Jawab: carbonate.) Dhyan do ki jahan \"pre-check\" ki zaroorat ho (jaise sulphate ke liye dil. HCl), wahan pehle wo likho."
          },
          {
            "heading": "Common Pitfalls to Avoid",
            "headingHinglish": "Aam galtiyan (bachke rehna)",
            "body": "**Mistake: \"Adding BaCl₂ alone gave a white precipitate, so the solution definitely contains sulfate (SO₄²⁻).\"**\nThis looks convincing because BaSO₄ is a famous white precipitate. However, **barium carbonate (BaCO₃) is also a white precipitate!** Let's see how a detective avoids this trap in three steps (teacher demonstration only, as barium salts are toxic):\n\n1. **The teacher takes a solution of Na₂CO₃ (which contains zero sulfate) and adds BaCl₂ directly:** Na₂CO₃ + BaCl₂ → BaCO₃↓ + 2NaCl. A heavy white precipitate appears! Stopping here would lead to the false conclusion \"sulfate is present.\"\n2. **Now the teacher adds dilute HCl to that white precipitate:** BaCO₃ + 2HCl → BaCl₂ + H₂O + CO₂↑. The white precipitate fizzes and **dissolves completely**, proving it was BaCO₃ (carbonate), not sulfate!\n3. **Next, the teacher repeats the experiment on Na₂SO₄ solution:** BaCl₂ gives a white precipitate, and when dilute HCl is added, **the precipitate does not dissolve at all**. That proves it is genuine **BaSO₄**.\n\n| Sample Solution | Upon Adding BaCl₂ Alone | Upon Adding Dilute HCl | True Conclusion |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Na₂CO₃ | White precipitate (BaCO₃) | Dissolves with fizzing (CO₂ bubbles) | Carbonate (CO₃²⁻) |\n| Na₂SO₄ | White precipitate (BaSO₄) | Does not dissolve | Sulfate (SO₄²⁻) |\n\n**Rule:** A white precipitate with BaCl₂ is **not** proof of sulfate unless you also acidify with **dilute HCl** (either before adding BaCl₂ to remove any carbonate, or afterward to confirm the white precipitate does not dissolve in dilute acid). And never use H₂SO₄ to acidify—it brings its own SO₄²⁻ ions!",
            "bodyHinglish": "**Galti: \"BaCl₂ daalne par safed dhodha bana, to sulphate pakka ho gaya.\"**\nYe sach lagta hai kyunki BaSO₄ safed hota hai. Par **BaCO₃ bhi safed** hota hai. Demo teen kadam mein (teacher demo, sirf dekhne ke liye: barium zehreela hai):\n\n1. **Teacher ek ghol leta hai jisme Na₂CO₃ hai.** Usme BaCl₂ daala: Na₂CO₃ + BaCl₂ → BaCO₃↓ + 2NaCl. Safed dhodha ban gaya! Agar yahin ruk jaate to hum bolte \"sulphate\", jo galat hota (isme sulphate hai hi nahi).\n2. **Ab us dhodhe par dilute HCl daalo.** BaCO₃ + 2HCl → BaCl₂ + H₂O + CO₂↑. Dhodha ghul gaya aur bulbule uthe: ye carbonate ka saboot hai, sulphate ka nahi.\n3. **Ab Na₂SO₄ wale ghol mein wahi karo.** BaCl₂ se dhodha, par dilute HCl daalne par **dhodha nahi ghulta**. Isse pata chalta hai ki wo BaSO₄ tha.\n\n| Ghol | BaCl₂ daalne par | Dilute HCl daalne par | Matlab |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Na₂CO₃ | safed dhodha | ghul gaya, bulbule | carbonate |\n| Na₂SO₄ | safed dhodha | nahi ghula | sulphate |\n\n**Niyam:** BaCl₂ ka safed dhodha **sirf shuruaat** hai. Pehle dilute HCl se carbonate hataao (ya dhodha ban jaane par use dilute HCl se test karo). Agar dhodha **acid mein bhi nahi ghulta**, tab hi sulphate. (Aur H₂SO₄ ka istemaal acid ke roop mein nahi: wo khud SO₄²⁻ de deta hai.)"
          },
          {
            "heading": "Quick Recap",
            "headingHinglish": "Quick Recap",
            "body": "- Every salt has a cation and an anion: Topics 2 and 3 covered cations, and Topic 4 covers anions plus ammonium.\n- **Chloride (Cl⁻):** Acidify with dilute HNO₃, then add AgNO₃ → curdy white AgCl↓ (insoluble in HNO₃, soluble in dilute NH₄OH). Ag⁺ + Cl⁻ → AgCl↓. Never acidify with HCl, because HCl contains Cl⁻.\n- **Sulfate (SO₄²⁻):** Acidify with dilute HCl, then add BaCl₂ → white BaSO₄↓ (insoluble in dilute HCl). Ba²⁺ + SO₄²⁻ → BaSO₄↓. Never acidify with H₂SO₄, because H₂SO₄ contains SO₄²⁻.\n- **Carbonate (CO₃²⁻):** Add dilute acid → brisk effervescence of CO₂ gas, which turns clear limewater milky. CO₃²⁻ + 2H⁺ → H₂O + CO₂↑.\n- **Ammonium (NH₄⁺):** Warm gently with NaOH solution → pungent NH₃ gas that turns moist red litmus paper blue. NH₄⁺ + OH⁻ → NH₃↑ + H₂O.\n- Anion precipitates depend only on the anion: both MgSO₄ and (NH₄)₂SO₄ give the exact same BaSO₄ precipitate with BaCl₂.\n- Silver and barium compounds are toxic, and ammonia gas is irritating: all tests are teacher demonstrations or supervised school-lab procedures.\n- Precipitate masses follow Chapter 5 mole ratios: 1.20 g of MgSO₄ (0.01 mol) yields 2.33 g of BaSO₄ (0.01 mol).\n- **Avoid the carbonate trap:** Always use the dilute acid pre-check before (or with) AgNO₃ and BaCl₂ so carbonate ions cannot mimic chloride or sulfate.",
            "bodyHinglish": "- Har namak mein cation aur anion hote hain: Topic 2 aur 3 mein cation, ab anion aur ammonium.\n- **Chloride Cl⁻:** dilute HNO₃, phir AgNO₃: safed curdy AgCl, jo NH₄OH mein ghulta hai. Ag⁺ + Cl⁻ → AgCl↓. HCl nahi, kyunki use khud Cl⁻ hai.\n- **Sulphate SO₄²⁻:** dilute HCl, phir BaCl₂: safed BaSO₄, jo acid mein nahi ghulta. Ba²⁺ + SO₄²⁻ → BaSO₄↓. H₂SO₄ nahi, kyunki use khud SO₄²⁻ hai.\n- **Carbonate CO₃²⁻:** dilute acid se tez bulbule (CO₂), jo chune ke paani ko dudhiya karti hai. CO₃²⁻ + 2H⁺ → H₂O + CO₂↑.\n- **Ammonium NH₄⁺:** NaOH ke saath halka garam: NH₃ gas, tez gandh, moist red litmus neela. NH₄⁺ + OH⁻ → NH₃↑ + H₂O.\n- Dhodha anion ka hota hai: MgSO₄ aur (NH₄)₂SO₄ dono BaCl₂ se BaSO₄ dete hain.\n- Silver aur barium ke compounds zehreele hain, ammonia gas bhi: sab test teacher ke saath ya sirf padhne ke liye.\n- Precipitate ka mass mole se: 1.20 g MgSO₄ ke liye 2.33 g BaSO₄.\n- **Galti se bacho:** BaCl₂ ka safed dhodha carbonate ka bhi ho sakta hai, isliye acid se check zaroori."
          },
          {
            "heading": "Practice Questions",
            "headingHinglish": "Practice Questions",
            "body": "(All questions are meant to be solved on paper. Atomic masses: H 1, C 12, N 14, O 16, Na 23, Mg 24, S 32, Cl 35.5, K 39, Ag 108, Ba 137.)\n\n1. A teacher tests a colourless solution of potassium chloride, KCl, by adding dilute nitric acid and then silver nitrate solution. State what is seen, write the balanced equation and the ionic equation, and name the spectator ions.\n2. Potassium sulphate, K₂SO₄, solution is acidified with dilute HCl and barium chloride solution is added. State what is seen, write the balanced equation and the ionic equation. What would be seen if dilute HCl is added to the precipitate?\n3. Sodium carbonate solution is treated with dilute nitric acid. State what is seen, how the gas is tested, and write the balanced equation and the ionic equation. Which spectator ions are present?\n4. Ammonium nitrate solution, NH₄NO₃, is warmed with NaOH solution in a teacher demo. Write the balanced equation and the ionic equation, and state what happens to a moist red litmus paper held near the mouth of the test tube. Why is the paper held near the mouth and not dipped in the liquid?\n5. A teacher adds excess barium chloride to a solution containing 5.22 g of potassium sulphate, so that all the sulphate forms a precipitate. Find the moles of K₂SO₄ and the mass of BaSO₄ formed.\n6. A solution contains 5.35 g of ammonium chloride, NH₄Cl. Excess acidified silver nitrate is added in a teacher demo. Find the mass of silver chloride formed and the mass of AgNO₃ used up. Which two ions of NH₄Cl are present, and which test would show the other one?\n7. Three colourless solutions are labelled P, Q and R. They are MgCl₂, K₂SO₄ and Na₂CO₃ in some order. Dilute HCl produces brisk bubbles only in P. After the dilute HCl treatment, BaCl₂ produces a white precipitate only in Q. Identify P, Q and R, and name the test that confirms R.\n8. State True or False and correct the false ones: (a) Dilute HCl is a good acid to add before the silver nitrate test for chloride. (b) BaSO₄ dissolves in dilute HCl. (c) The white precipitate AgCl dissolves in dilute NH₄OH. (d) NH₃ turns moist red litmus blue. (e) MgSO₄ gives no precipitate with BaCl₂.\n9. Why is dilute H₂SO₄ not used to acidify a solution before the barium chloride test for sulphate? What would the test falsely show?\n10. A student adds BaCl₂ to a colourless solution and gets a white precipitate. The precipitate dissolves completely in dilute HCl with bubbles. Which ion was present, which ion was NOT present, and what is the gas?\n11. Write the ionic equation for each of the four tests of this topic, and for each say which common ions in the unreacted solution are spectators. Use the salts NaCl, MgSO₄, Na₂CO₃ and NH₄Cl.\n12. Describe the safe way a teacher carries out the ammonia test and the carbonate test: which gas is dangerous, where is the test done, how is the gas smelt or tested, what precautions are taken with the delivery tube, and why can a student never do these at home?\n\n### Answers\n\n1. Dilute HNO₃ is added first (to rule out carbonate), followed by AgNO₃: a **curdy white precipitate of AgCl** forms, which dissolves in dilute NH₄OH. Molecular equation: KCl + AgNO₃ → AgCl↓ + KNO₃. Net ionic: Ag⁺ + Cl⁻ → AgCl↓. Spectator ions: **K⁺ and NO₃⁻**. Check: K 1, Cl 1, Ag 1, N 1, O 3 on both sides ✅.\n2. A **white precipitate of BaSO₄** forms. Molecular: K₂SO₄ + BaCl₂ → BaSO₄↓ + 2KCl. Net ionic: Ba²⁺ + SO₄²⁻ → BaSO₄↓. If dilute HCl is added to the precipitate, **the precipitate remains undissolved** (BaSO₄ is insoluble in dilute acids). Check: K 2, S 1, O 4, Ba 1, Cl 2 on both sides ✅; ionic charge (+2) + (−2) = 0 ✅.\n3. **Immediate brisk effervescence (bubbles)**; when the escaping gas is passed through clear limewater, the **limewater turns milky**, confirming the gas is CO₂. Molecular: Na₂CO₃ + 2HNO₃ → 2NaNO₃ + H₂O + CO₂↑. Net ionic: CO₃²⁻ + 2H⁺ → H₂O + CO₂↑. Spectator ions: **Na⁺ and NO₃⁻**. Check: Na 2, C 1, O: 3 + 6 = 9 = 6 + 1 + 2, H 2, N 2 ✅.\n4. Molecular: NH₄NO₃ + NaOH → NaNO₃ + NH₃↑ + H₂O. Net ionic: NH₄⁺ + OH⁻ → NH₃↑ + H₂O. The moist red litmus paper held near the mouth of the tube **turns blue**. It must be held near the mouth (in the rising gas) rather than dipped into the liquid because the liquid already contains NaOH (an alkali), which would turn red litmus blue on contact even if no ammonia gas were produced! Check: N: 2 = 1 + 1, H: 4 + 1 = 5 = 3 + 2, O: 3 + 1 = 4 = 3 + 1, Na 1 = 1 ✅.\n5. Molar mass of K₂SO₄ = 2 × 39 + 32 + 64 = **174 g/mol**. Moles of K₂SO₄ = 5.22 ÷ 174 = **0.03 mol**. From K₂SO₄ + BaCl₂ → BaSO₄↓ + 2KCl (1 : 1 ratio), moles of BaSO₄ = 0.03 mol. Molar mass of BaSO₄ = 233 g/mol, so mass = 0.03 × 233 = **6.99 g**. Check: BaCl₂ needed = 0.03 × 208 = 6.24 g; total reactants = 5.22 + 6.24 = 11.46 g; total products = 6.99 + (0.06 × 74.5 = 4.47 g KCl) = 11.46 g ✅.\n6. Molar mass of NH₄Cl = 14 + 4 + 35.5 = **53.5 g/mol**. Moles = 5.35 ÷ 53.5 = **0.1 mol**. From NH₄Cl + AgNO₃ → AgCl↓ + NH₄NO₃ (1 : 1 : 1 ratio): molar mass of AgCl = 108 + 35.5 = 143.5 g/mol, so mass = 0.1 × 143.5 = **14.35 g**. Molar mass of AgNO₃ = 108 + 14 + 48 = 170 g/mol, so mass = 0.1 × 170 = **17.0 g**. The two ions in NH₄Cl are **NH₄⁺ and Cl⁻**. To test for the other ion (NH₄⁺), warm a fresh portion gently with NaOH solution to release NH₃ gas, which turns moist red litmus blue. Check: 14.35 + (0.1 × 80 = 8.0 g NH₄NO₃) = 22.35 g = 5.35 + 17.0 ✅.\n7. Brisk bubbles with dilute HCl occur only in **P**, so **P = Na₂CO₃** (carbonate). After dilute HCl treatment, BaCl₂ gives a white precipitate only in **Q**, so **Q = K₂SO₄** (sulfate). That leaves **R = MgCl₂** (chloride). To confirm R: acidify with dilute HNO₃ and add AgNO₃ solution to get a curdy white precipitate of AgCl (soluble in dilute NH₄OH). Check: each solution matches one unique anion ✅.\n8. (a) **False** ❌: HCl contains Cl⁻ ions of its own and would give a false white AgCl precipitate with AgNO₃; **dilute HNO₃** must be used instead. (b) **False** ❌: BaSO₄ is insoluble in dilute HCl. (c) **True** ✅. (d) **True** ✅. (e) **False** ❌: MgSO₄ contains SO₄²⁻ ions and gives a white precipitate of BaSO₄ with BaCl₂.\n9. Dilute H₂SO₄ contains SO₄²⁻ ions of its own. Adding H₂SO₄ before BaCl₂ would introduce sulfate ions into the sample and immediately precipitate white BaSO₄, **falsely indicating that sulfate was present** in the original sample even if there was none. Therefore, dilute **HCl** is used instead.\n10. Because the white precipitate dissolved completely in dilute HCl with fizzing, it was **BaCO₃**, proving that **carbonate (CO₃²⁻)** was present and **sulfate (SO₄²⁻) was NOT present** (since BaSO₄ does not dissolve in dilute HCl). The gas released is **CO₂** (which turns limewater milky). Equation: BaCO₃ + 2HCl → BaCl₂ + H₂O + CO₂↑ ✅ (Ba 1, C 1, O 3, H 2, Cl 2 on both sides).\n11. Chloride (NaCl + AgNO₃): **Ag⁺ + Cl⁻ → AgCl↓**; spectators: Na⁺ and NO₃⁻. Sulfate (MgSO₄ + BaCl₂): **Ba²⁺ + SO₄²⁻ → BaSO₄↓**; spectators: Mg²⁺ and Cl⁻. Carbonate (Na₂CO₃ + 2HCl): **CO₃²⁻ + 2H⁺ → H₂O + CO₂↑**; spectators: Na⁺ and Cl⁻. Ammonium (NH₄Cl + NaOH): **NH₄⁺ + OH⁻ → NH₃↑ + H₂O**; spectators: Cl⁻ and Na⁺. Check: charges balance on both sides in all four equations ✅.\n12. **Irritating/hazardous gas:** ammonia (NH₃), which irritates the eyes, nose, and throat. The ammonia test is performed by the teacher in a **fume hood**, holding moist red litmus paper near the mouth of the open test tube and never sniffing the tube directly (using at most a gentle hand-waft). In the carbonate test, the teacher keeps the reaction unheated and **lifts the delivery tube out of the limewater before bubbling stops** so cold limewater cannot suck back into the reaction tube. Students must never try these at home because home kitchens lack a fume hood, lab PPE, teacher supervision, and safe chemical waste disposal, and reagents like NaOH, AgNO₃, and BaCl₂ are corrosive or toxic.\n\n---\n\nBack home, Sophia set up the four-visitor gatehouse game on paper for her younger brother and said, \"Uncle Martin showed me that one single check can't identify every ion—each ion has its own dedicated check, plus a pre-check so carbonate doesn't fool us!\" Uncle Martin smiled. \"Now you have the full detective kit: cation checks from Topics 2 and 3, and anion checks from Topic 4.\"\n\n**What's next?** Suppose a rainstorm washes the paper labels off four bottles in the school chemistry storeroom. How do we combine our cation tests and anion tests in a clean step-by-step sequence to identify the complete salt in every bottle? Let's find out in Topic 5!",
            "bodyHinglish": "(Sab sawaal paper par hal karne ke liye hain. Mass: H 1, C 12, N 14, O 16, Na 23, Mg 24, S 32, Cl 35.5, K 39, Ag 108, Ba 137.)\n\n1. A teacher tests a colourless solution of potassium chloride, KCl, by adding dilute nitric acid and then silver nitrate solution. State what is seen, write the balanced equation and the ionic equation, and name the spectator ions.\n2. Potassium sulphate, K₂SO₄, solution is acidified with dilute HCl and barium chloride solution is added. State what is seen, write the balanced equation and the ionic equation. What would be seen if dilute HCl is added to the precipitate?\n3. Sodium carbonate solution is treated with dilute nitric acid. State what is seen, how the gas is tested, and write the balanced equation and the ionic equation. Which spectator ions are present?\n4. Ammonium nitrate solution, NH₄NO₃, is warmed with NaOH solution in a teacher demo. Write the balanced equation and the ionic equation, and state what happens to a moist red litmus paper held near the mouth of the test tube. Why is the paper held near the mouth and not dipped in the liquid?\n5. A teacher adds excess barium chloride to a solution containing 5.22 g of potassium sulphate, so that all the sulphate forms a precipitate. Find the moles of K₂SO₄ and the mass of BaSO₄ formed.\n6. A solution contains 5.35 g of ammonium chloride, NH₄Cl. Excess acidified silver nitrate is added in a teacher demo. Find the mass of silver chloride formed and the mass of AgNO₃ used up. Which two ions of NH₄Cl are present, and which test would show the other one?\n7. Three colourless solutions are labelled P, Q and R. They are MgCl₂, K₂SO₄ and Na₂CO₃ in some order. Dilute HCl produces brisk bubbles only in P. After the dilute HCl treatment, BaCl₂ produces a white precipitate only in Q. Identify P, Q and R, and name the test that confirms R.\n8. State True or False and correct the false ones: (a) Dilute HCl is a good acid to add before the silver nitrate test for chloride. (b) BaSO₄ dissolves in dilute HCl. (c) The white precipitate AgCl dissolves in dilute NH₄OH. (d) NH₃ turns moist red litmus blue. (e) MgSO₄ gives no precipitate with BaCl₂.\n9. Why is dilute H₂SO₄ not used to acidify a solution before the barium chloride test for sulphate? What would the test falsely show?\n10. A student adds BaCl₂ to a colourless solution and gets a white precipitate. The precipitate dissolves completely in dilute HCl with bubbles. Which ion was present, which ion was NOT present, and what is the gas?\n11. Write the ionic equation for each of the four tests of this topic, and for each say which common ions in the unreacted solution are spectators. Use the salts NaCl, MgSO₄, Na₂CO₃ and NH₄Cl.\n12. Describe the safe way a teacher carries out the ammonia test and the carbonate test: which gas is dangerous, where is the test done, how is the gas smelt or tested, what precautions are taken with the delivery tube, and why can a student never do these at home?\n\n### Answers\n\n1. Dilute HNO₃ pehle (carbonate ho to hata de), phir AgNO₃: **safed curdy dhodha AgCl**, jo dilute NH₄OH mein ghulta hai. Equation: KCl + AgNO₃ → AgCl↓ + KNO₃. Ionic: Ag⁺ + Cl⁻ → AgCl↓. Darshak: **K⁺ aur NO₃⁻**. Jaanch: K 1, Cl 1, Ag 1, N 1, O 3 dono taraf ✅.\n2. **Safed dhodha BaSO₄** banega. K₂SO₄ + BaCl₂ → BaSO₄↓ + 2KCl. Ionic: Ba²⁺ + SO₄²⁻ → BaSO₄↓. Dilute HCl daalne par dhodha **nahi ghulega** (BaSO₄ acid mein nahi ghulta). Jaanch: K 2, S 1, O 4, Ba 1, Cl 2 dono taraf ✅, ionic charge (+2) + (−2) = 0 ✅.\n3. **Turant tez bulbule**; gas ko chune ke saaf paani mein guzaaro: **dudhiya** ho jaata hai, ye CO₂ hai. Equation: Na₂CO₃ + 2HNO₃ → 2NaNO₃ + H₂O + CO₂↑. Ionic: CO₃²⁻ + 2H⁺ → H₂O + CO₂↑. Darshak: **Na⁺ aur NO₃⁻**. Jaanch: Na 2, C 1, O 3 + 6 = 9 = 6 + 1 + 2, H 2, N 2 ✅.\n4. NH₄NO₃ + NaOH → NaNO₃ + NH₃↑ + H₂O. Ionic: NH₄⁺ + OH⁻ → NH₃↑ + H₂O. Moist red litmus **neela** ho jaata hai. Mooh ke paas isliye rakhte hain kyunki gas upar uthti hai, aur litmus ko ghol mein dubo diya to NaOH (alkali) se hi neela ho jaata, jisse ammonia ka saboot nahi milta. Jaanch: N 2 = 1 + 1, H 4 + 1 = 5 = 3 + 2, O 3 + 1 = 4 = 3 + 1, Na 1 = 1 ✅.\n5. K₂SO₄ ka molar mass = 2 × 39 + 32 + 64 = **174** g/mol. Moles = 5.22 ÷ 174 = **0.03 mol**. K₂SO₄ + BaCl₂ → BaSO₄↓ + 2KCl (ratio 1 : 1), to BaSO₄ = 0.03 mol. BaSO₄ = 233 g/mol, mass = 0.03 × 233 = **6.99 g**. Jaanch: BaCl₂ chahiye 0.03 × 208 = 6.24 g; reactants 5.22 + 6.24 = 11.46 g; products 6.99 + (0.06 × 74.5 = 4.47 g KCl) = 11.46 g ✅.\n6. NH₄Cl = 14 + 4 + 35.5 = **53.5** g/mol. Moles = 5.35 ÷ 53.5 = **0.1 mol**. NH₄Cl + AgNO₃ → AgCl↓ + NH₄NO₃ (ratio 1 : 1 : 1). AgCl = 108 + 35.5 = 143.5, mass = 0.1 × 143.5 = **14.35 g**. AgNO₃ = 108 + 14 + 48 = 170, mass = 0.1 × 170 = **17 g**. Dono ion: **NH₄⁺ aur Cl⁻**. Doosra ion (NH₄⁺) NaOH ke saath halka garam karke NH₃ gas se (moist red litmus neela). Jaanch: Cl⁻ ka AgNO₃ test ✅ aur 14.35 + (0.1 × 80 = 8 g NH₄NO₃) = 22.35 g = 5.35 + 17 ✅.\n7. Sirf **P** mein tez bulbule: **P = Na₂CO₃** (carbonate). HCl daalne ke baad BaCl₂ sirf **Q** mein safed dhodha deta hai, to **Q = K₂SO₄**. Bacha **R = MgCl₂**. R ka confirm test: dilute HNO₃ phir AgNO₃: safed curdy AgCl (jo NH₄OH mein ghulta hai). Jaanch: teeno anion alag alag, har ek ke liye ek hi match ✅.\n8. (a) **False** ❌: HCl mein khud Cl⁻ hai, to AgNO₃ se AgCl bana dega; **dilute HNO₃** lena chahiye. (b) **False** ❌: BaSO₄ acid mein nahi ghulta. (c) **True** ✅. (d) **True** ✅. (e) **False** ❌: MgSO₄ mein SO₄²⁻ hai, to BaCl₂ se safed BaSO₄ banega.\n9. H₂SO₄ mein khud SO₄²⁻ hai. Wo BaCl₂ se turant BaSO₄ bana dega, chahe original ghol mein sulphate ho ya na ho. To test **galat se sulphate dikha dega**. Isliye pehle dilute **HCl** daalte hain.\n10. Ghula hua dhodha **BaCO₃** tha, to ghol mein **carbonate (CO₃²⁻)** tha. **Sulphate maujood nahi tha**, kyunki BaSO₄ dilute HCl mein nahi ghulta. Gas **CO₂** hai (chune ka paani dudhiya). Equation: BaCO₃ + 2HCl → BaCl₂ + H₂O + CO₂↑ ✅ (Ba 1, C 1, O 3, H 2, Cl 2 dono taraf).\n11. Chloride (NaCl): Ag⁺ + Cl⁻ → AgCl↓, darshak Na⁺ aur NO₃⁻. Sulphate (MgSO₄): Ba²⁺ + SO₄²⁻ → BaSO₄↓, darshak Mg²⁺ aur Cl⁻. Carbonate (Na₂CO₃): CO₃²⁻ + 2H⁺ → H₂O + CO₂↑, darshak Na⁺ aur acid ka anion (jaise Cl⁻). Ammonium (NH₄Cl): NH₄⁺ + OH⁻ → NH₃↑ + H₂O, darshak Cl⁻ aur Na⁺. Jaanch: har ionic equation ka charge dono taraf barabar: (+1) + (−1) = 0, (+2) + (−2) = 0, (−2) + 2(+1) = 0, (+1) + (−1) = 0 ✅.\n12. **Dangerous gas:** ammonia (aankh, naak, gale ko jalati hai). Ammonia test **fume cupboard** mein hota hai, teacher moist red litmus test tube ke mooh ke paas rakhta hai aur sungne ke liye sirf \"waft\" (haath se halki hawa) karta hai, seedha nahi sungta. Carbonate test mein teacher test tube **khula** rakhta hai, aur gas ka kaam hote hi ya bulbule rukne se pehle **delivery tube chune ke paani se bahar nikaal leta hai**, taaki suck-back na ho. Student ghar par nahi kar sakta kyunki ghar par fume cupboard, goggles, gloves aur teacher nahi hote, aur NaOH corrosive hai.\n\n---\n\nPrajakta ne ghar jaakar apne chhote bhai ko gate-check ka khel samjhaya aur boli, \"Chacha ne bataya ki ek hi test se saare ion nahi milte, har ek ka apna fixed check hota hai.\" Mr. Phansalkar ne muskura kar kaha, \"Shabaash! Bas ek baat yaad rakhna: jahan dhokha ho sakta hai, wahan pehle pre-check.\"\n\n**Aage kya?** Ab humare paas cation ke tests (Topic 2 aur 3) aur anion ke tests (aaj) dono hain. Agar ek anjaan namak ki botal ka label dhul gaya ho, to in sab tests ko ek ke baad ek kaise lagayein ki botal ka naam pata chal jaaye? Topic 5 mein."
          }
