            "explanation": "Pitfall to avoid (\"Trying to identify the entire salt from the color of the solution alone.\"): Demo: Suppose Bottle A holds a blue solution. Saying \"It's blue, so it must be copper(II) sulfate!\" is a classic rookie mistake: the blue color tells us only that Cu²⁺ is likely present, while the anion could equally well be sulfate (CuSO₄), chloride (CuCl₂), or nitrate (Cu(NO₃)₂)—all three form blue solutions! Even worse, four of our seven cations (Ca²⁺, Zn²⁺, Pb²⁺, Al³⁺) plus NH₄⁺ form colorless solutions that look identical to plain water. Always follow the full chain: color clue + cation test + anion test.",
