            "correctIndex": 0,
            "explanation": "Pitfall to avoid (\"Sprinkling liquid test reagents directly onto a dry solid salt instead of dissolving the salt in water first.\"): Demo: Suppose a student tries to test for sulfate by dropping BaCl₂ onto dry crystals of a salt without dissolving the salt in water first. In a dry crystal, the ions are locked tightly inside a rigid 3D lattice rather than moving freely through solution, so the two reagents cannot mix at the ionic level to form a clean precipitate. Once you dissolve the salt in distilled water, its cations and anions break free and move throughout the liquid, allowing Ba²⁺ + SO₄²⁻ → BaSO₄↓ to happen instantaneously! (The one common exception where we add dilute acid directly to a solid is testing an insoluble carbonate like CaCO₃ for CO₂ bubbles.) Dry solid salt Salt dissolved in water (ions locked in crystal lattice) (free mobile ions ready to react) + - + - + - + - + - + - + - + - + - + - + - + - - + > Figure 5.5 – In a dry solid crystal, ions are locked in a rigid lattice; dissolving the salt in water frees the ions so they can collide and react immediately with test reagents.",
            "explanationHinglish": "Bachke rehna (\"Anion ka test thos namak par hi kar lo, ghulne ki kya zaroorat?\"): Demo: ek student sochta hai ki thos namak par seedha BaCl₂ chhidak de. Par thos mein ions lattice mein jakde hote hain, aazad nahi, aur dono thos ek doosre ko chhu bhi nahi paate jaise ghol mein paas aate hain. Isliye precipitate nahi banta aur student galti se kehta hai \"sulphate nahi hai\". Ghol mein wahi namak ions mein toot kar aazad ho jata hai, aur Ba²⁺ + SO₄²⁻ → BaSO₄↓ turant ho jata hai. Thos namak Paani mein ghola hua namak (ions lattice mein jakde) (ions aazad, ghoomte hain) + - + - + - + - + - + - + - + - + - + - + - + - - + > Figure 5.5 – Thos mein ions regular jaali mein jakde hain, ghol mein ions aazad hain. Isliye pehle ghulate hain, phir tests karte hain."
