          {
            "heading": "Mystery Box Game Day",
            "headingHinglish": "Mystery Box game day",
            "body": "Friday afternoon was **\"Mystery Box Game Day\"** for the school Science Club. Mr. Grant, the club advisor, stood in the corner of the hall with his nephew Ethan. Ethan's team had been handed a shuffled stack of twenty **Clue Cards**—each card printed with a single lab observation such as *\"a white precipitate settled at the bottom,\"* *\"dissolved completely when excess drops were added,\"* or *\"released a pungent gas that turned moist red litmus blue.\"* Across the table sat six small Mystery Boxes, each labeled with the name of a salt. The challenge: sort the clue cards into the right boxes to prove which salt was which.\n\nAt first, Ethan's teammates grabbed handfuls of cards at random and got completely tangled up—two cards seemed to say the same thing, one card didn't seem to fit anywhere, and another looked as if it could belong to three different boxes! Ethan frowned. \"Uncle Grant, this is a total maze. Where do we even start?\"\n\nMr. Grant smiled and tapped the table. \"Sort your cards into three neat piles first: **'Color Observed,'** **'Cation Test,'** and **'Anion Test.'** Once you read the clues in that exact order, every card will drop straight into its rightful box. A good detective never reads clues in a jumble—first inspect the scene, then lock down the positive ion, then lock down the negative ion, and finally write the full name.\"\n\nEthan's team sorted the deck into those three piles—and solved all six Mystery Boxes in record time. \"Order first, answer second!\" said Ethan. \"Exactly,\" replied Mr. Grant. \"And that is our 4-step master method for today.\"",
            "bodyHinglish": "School mein \"Mystery Box Game Day\" tha. Mr. Gunjal, jo school ke science club ke incharge the, apne bhatije Nachiket ke saath hall ke ek kone mein khade the. Nachiket ki team ko ek bada kaardon ka dher mila tha: har card par ek **suraag** (clue) likha tha, jaise \"neeche safed dhodha baitha\", \"boondein aur daalne par ghayab\", \"tez gandh wali gas, laal litmus neela\". Doosri taraf 6 chhote dibbe the, har dibbe par ek ion ka naam. Kaam: har clue card ko sahi ion ke dibbe mein daalna.\n\nNachiket ki team ne sab card ek saath uthaye aur uljh gayi. Do cards ek hi ion ke the, ek card ka koi dibba hi nahi mil raha tha, aur ek card to dusre dibbe ke saath bhi fit lag raha tha. Nachiket ne kaha, \"Chacha, ye to bilkul bhool-bhulaiya hai. Kahan se shuru karein?\"\n\nMr. Gunjal ne cards ko teen dher mein baant diya: **\"Rang dekha\"**, **\"Cation ka test\"**, **\"Anion ka test\"**. \"Pehle ye dekho ki kaun sa clue kis kisam ka hai. Jab sahi kram mein cards laga doge, to har ion khud-ba-khud apne dibbe mein chala jaayega. Jaasoos bhi pehle jagah ko dekhta hai, phir ek-ek suraag ko ek-ek rule se jodta hai, aur ant mein poora naam likhta hai.\"\n\nNachiket ki team ne wahi kiya aur 6 mein se 6 dibbe sahi nikle. \"Chacha, matlab pehle kram, phir jawab.\" Mr. Gunjal muskuraye. \"Aur yahi kram aaj ke chaar-kadam ke master method mein hai.\""
          },
          {
            "heading": "Key Vocabulary",
            "headingHinglish": "Naye shabd",
            "body": "| Term | Meaning | Example |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Master method** | The 4-step sequence used to solve any unknown-solution or unknown-salt problem, always in the same order | Inspect color → test cation → test anion → write formula & equations |\n| **Observation table** | A structured record pairing each reagent added with what was visibly observed | Added NaOH drops → white precipitate formed |\n| **Reagent** | The known chemical added to carry out a test | NaOH, NH₄OH, AgNO₃, BaCl₂ |\n| **Net ionic equation** | The equation showing only the ions that actually react to form the precipitate, gas, or water—omitting spectator ions | Cu²⁺ + 2OH⁻ → Cu(OH)₂↓ |\n| **Spectator ion** | An ion present in the solution that remains unchanged before and after the reaction | Na⁺ and NO₃⁻ in Cu(NO₃)₂ + NaOH |\n| **State symbol** | The symbol in parentheses showing physical state: `(s)`, `(l)`, `(g)`, or `(aq)` | Cu(OH)₂(s), NaNO₃(aq) |\n| **Dissolving of a precipitate** | When an insoluble precipitate reacts with excess reagent to form a clear, soluble species | Zn(OH)₂ dissolving in excess NaOH |",
            "bodyHinglish": "| Shabd | Matlab (simple bhasha mein) | Example |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Master method** | Kisi bhi anjaan ghol ko pehchanne ke 4 kadam, hamesha isi kram mein | Dekho, cation, anion, likho |\n| **Observation table** | Har reagent ke saamne likha hua \"kya dikha\" | NaOH daala: safed dhodha |\n| **Reagent** | Wo ghol ya cheez jo hum test ke liye daalte hain | NaOH, NH₄OH, AgNO₃, BaCl₂ |\n| **Ionic equation** | Sirf wo ions jo asal mein badalte hain, spectator ions chhod kar | Cu²⁺ + 2OH⁻ → Cu(OH)₂↓ |\n| **Spectator ion** | Wo ion jo ghol mein rehta hai par reaction mein badalta nahi | NaOH aur Cu(NO₃)₂ mein Na⁺ aur NO₃⁻ |\n| **State symbol** | Bracket mein likha hua (s), (l), (g), (aq) | Cu(OH)₂(s), NaNO₃(aq) |\n| **Dhodha ghulna** | Precipitate ka excess reagent mein ghul kar ghayab ho jaana | Zn(OH)₂ ka excess NaOH mein ghulna |"
          },
          {
            "heading": "Part A: The 4-Step Master Method",
            "headingHinglish": "Part A: Chaar-kadam ka master method",
            "body": "Whenever you are given an unknown salt or solution problem, follow these four steps in order:\n\n1. **Inspect the color.** Note the color of the solid or its aqueous solution: **blue** points toward Cu²⁺, **pale green** toward Fe²⁺, **yellow-brown** toward Fe³⁺, and **colorless** toward Ca²⁺, Zn²⁺, Pb²⁺, Al³⁺, or NH₄⁺ (as well as Na⁺ and K⁺). Remember that color is only an opening clue, never final proof (Topic 5).\n2. **Test the cation.** In one small portion of the solution, add **a few drops of NaOH**, then **excess NaOH**. In a second clean test tube, add **a few drops of NH₄OH**, then **excess NH₄OH**. Record the precipitate color and whether it dissolves in excess (Topics 2 and 3). If cold NaOH gives no precipitate, warm gently to test for NH₄⁺.\n3. **Test the anion.** In fresh portions of the solution, run the anion tests from Topic 4: dilute HNO₃ + AgNO₃ for Cl⁻; dilute HCl + BaCl₂ for SO₄²⁻; dilute acid + limewater for CO₃²⁻.\n4. **Write the name, formula, and balanced equations.** Use charge balance (criss-cross) to write the salt's formula, then write the net ionic and molecular equations for each positive test.\n\n```\n   Step 1           Step 2              Step 3             Step 4\n   LOOK      --->   CATION test  --->   ANION test  --->   NAME & FORMULA\n   (color)          (NaOH, NH₄OH:       (AgNO₃, BaCl₂,     (charge balance,\n                    drops & excess)     dilute acid)       balanced equations)\n\n   At every step, ask the three questions: What was added? What was seen? What does it mean?\n```\n\n> **Figure 6.1** – The 4-step master method. At every stage, apply our three detective questions from Topic 1: What was added? What was seen? What does it mean?\n\nOne essential lab rule: **always run cation and anion tests on separate small portions** of the sample solution in clean test tubes—dumping multiple test reagents into the same tube mixes up precipitates and ruins the clues!\n\n### How to Sketch a Precipitate Test Tube\n\nIn diagrams, a precipitate sits **at the bottom** of the test tube with clear liquid above it, while a precipitate that dissolves in excess reagent leaves a completely clear tube:\n\n```\n    After a few drops of NaOH                  After excess NaOH\n\n         :  clear liquid :                         :  clear liquid :\n         :  (colorless)  :                         :  (colorless)  :\n         :               :                         :               :\n         :   white ppt   :                         :    no ppt     :\n         :_______________:                         :_______________:\n              (a)                                       (b)\n```\n\n> **Figure 6.2** – Testing a Zn²⁺, Pb²⁺, or Al³⁺ solution with NaOH: (a) a few drops produce a white precipitate at the bottom; (b) adding excess NaOH dissolves the precipitate completely, leaving a clear, colorless solution.\n\nRecall from Topic 2 that Zn(OH)₂, Pb(OH)₂, and Al(OH)₃ dissolve in excess NaOH because they are **amphoteric hydroxides** (Chapter 2, Part E).",
            "bodyHinglish": "Anjaan ghol ya thos namak milne par ye chaar kadam lo, hamesha isi kram mein.\n\n1. **Dekho.** Thos ka rang aur ghol ka rang: neela matlab Cu²⁺ ka ishara, halka hara matlab Fe²⁺, peela-bhura matlab Fe³⁺, rangheen matlab baaki ions (Ca²⁺, Zn²⁺, Pb²⁺, Al³⁺, Na⁺, K⁺, NH₄⁺ ke ghol rangheen hote hain). Rang sirf ishara hai, saboot nahi (Topic 5).\n2. **Cation ka test.** Ghol ke thode hisse mein pehle NaOH ki **kuch boondein**, phir **excess**. Alag saaf test tube mein NH₄OH ki kuch boondein, phir excess. Dhodhe ka rang aur excess mein kya hua, likho (Topic 2 aur Topic 3 ke table).\n3. **Anion ka test.** Alag hisse mein anion ke test: Cl⁻ ke liye dilute HNO₃ phir AgNO₃, SO₄²⁻ ke liye dilute HCl phir BaCl₂, CO₃²⁻ ke liye dilute acid aur chuna paani, NH₄⁺ ke liye NaOH ke saath halka garam (Topic 4).\n4. **Naam aur formula likho.** Cation aur anion ka charge dekh kar criss-cross se formula, phir har test ki balanced equation (pehle ionic, phir molecular).\n\n```\n   Kadam 1          Kadam 2             Kadam 3            Kadam 4\n   DEKHO     --->   CATION ka    --->   ANION ka    --->    NAAM aur FORMULA\n   (rang)           test (NaOH,         test (AgNO₃,        (charge balance,\n                     NH₄OH)              BaCl₂, acid)        equations)\n\n   Har kadam par teen sawaal: kya daala? kya dikha? kya matlab?\n```\n\n> **Figure 6.1** – Master method: chaar kadam ek ke baad ek. Har kadam par wahi teen sawaal puchho jo Topic 1 mein seekhe the: kya daala, kya dikha, uska matlab kya.\n\nEk zaroori niyam: **cation aur anion ke test alag-alag hisson mein** kiye jaate hain, kyunki ek test tube mein sab milane se rang aur dhodhe aapas mein ulajh jaate hain.\n\n### Dhodhe ka figure: kaise likhein\n\nTest tube mein dhodha **neeche** baithta hai, upar saaf ghol rehta hai. Aisa figure hum bahut baar banayenge.\n\n```\n    NaOH ki boondein daalne ke baad          Excess NaOH daalne ke baad\n\n         :   saaf ghol   :                         :   saaf ghol   :\n         :   (rangheen)  :                         :   (rangheen)  :\n         :               :                         :               :\n         :  safed dhodha :                         :  koi dhodha   :\n         :_______________:                         :_______________:\n              (a)                                       (b)\n```\n\n> **Figure 6.2** – Zn²⁺, Pb²⁺ ya Al³⁺ ke ghol mein NaOH: (a) boondein: neeche safed dhodha, (b) excess: dhodha ghul gaya, saaf rangheen ghol bacha.\n\nYe wahi picture hai jo Topic 2 mein dikha tha: Zn(OH)₂, Pb(OH)₂ aur Al(OH)₃ excess NaOH mein ghulte hain (amphoteric swabhav, Chapter 2 ka Part E)."
          },
          {
            "heading": "Part B: Master Cation Reference Table",
            "headingHinglish": "Part B: Cation-ke-liye observation table",
            "body": "Here is the complete 7-cation reference table from Topics 2 and 3 (using fresh, dilute solutions—first adding 2–3 drops, then excess):\n\n| Ion | With a Few Drops of NaOH | With Excess NaOH | With a Few Drops of NH₄OH | With Excess NH₄OH |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Ca²⁺ | Slight white ppt, Ca(OH)₂ | Insoluble (does not dissolve) | No ppt (or very faint haze) | No change |\n| Fe²⁺ | Dirty green ppt, Fe(OH)₂ | Insoluble | Dirty green ppt | Insoluble |\n| Fe³⁺ | Reddish-brown ppt, Fe(OH)₃ | Insoluble | Reddish-brown ppt | Insoluble |\n| Cu²⁺ | Pale blue ppt, Cu(OH)₂ | Insoluble | Pale blue ppt | Dissolves → deep (inky) blue solution |\n| Zn²⁺ | White ppt, Zn(OH)₂ | Dissolves → colorless solution (sodium zincate, Na₂ZnO₂) | White ppt | Dissolves → colorless solution |\n| Pb²⁺ | White ppt, Pb(OH)₂ | Dissolves → colorless solution (sodium plumbite, Na₂PbO₂) | White ppt | Insoluble |\n| Al³⁺ | White gelatinous ppt, Al(OH)₃ | Dissolves → colorless solution (sodium aluminate, NaAlO₂) | White gelatinous ppt | Insoluble |\n\n(The deep inky-blue copper species [Cu(NH₃)₄]²⁺ and colorless zinc species [Zn(NH₃)₄]²⁺ in excess NH₄OH are **complex ions**, covered in detail in higher grades.)\n\nThis gives us a super-compact **\"Who Dissolves in Excess?\"** map:\n\n```\n   Dissolve in EXCESS NaOH     :  Zn²⁺   Pb²⁺   Al³⁺\n   Do NOT dissolve in excess NaOH :  Ca²⁺   Fe²⁺   Fe³⁺   Cu²⁺\n\n   Dissolve in EXCESS NH₄OH    :  Cu²⁺ (deep inky blue)   Zn²⁺ (colorless)\n   Do NOT dissolve in excess NH₄OH:  Ca²⁺   Fe²⁺   Fe³⁺   Pb²⁺   Al³⁺\n```\n\n> **Figure 6.3** – The \"Who Dissolves in Excess?\" map. Notice that **Zn²⁺** is the only cation whose precipitate dissolves in *both* excess NaOH and excess NH₄OH, while **Cu²⁺** is the only one whose precipitate stays insoluble in excess NaOH yet dissolves in excess NH₄OH!\n\nNow let's put this map to work on eight mixed puzzles.",
            "bodyHinglish": "Sab 7 cations ke natije, shared table ke hisaab se. (Taazaa, patle ghol; ek-do boondein, phir excess.)\n\n| Ion | NaOH ki boondein | Excess NaOH | NH₄OH ki boondein | Excess NH₄OH |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Ca²⁺ | safed dhodha (halka) | nahi ghulta | dhodha nahi ya bahut halka | koi badlaav nahi |\n| Fe²⁺ | ganda hara dhodha Fe(OH)₂ | nahi ghulta | ganda hara dhodha | nahi ghulta |\n| Fe³⁺ | laal-bhura dhodha Fe(OH)₃ | nahi ghulta | laal-bhura dhodha | nahi ghulta |\n| Cu²⁺ | halka neela dhodha Cu(OH)₂ | nahi ghulta | halka neela dhodha | ghulta hai, gehra (siyahi jaisa) neela ghol |\n| Zn²⁺ | safed dhodha Zn(OH)₂ | ghulta hai, rangheen ghol (sodium zincate Na₂ZnO₂) | safed dhodha | ghulta hai, rangheen ghol |\n| Pb²⁺ | safed dhodha Pb(OH)₂ | ghulta hai, rangheen ghol (sodium plumbite Na₂PbO₂) | safed dhodha | nahi ghulta |\n| Al³⁺ | safed gelatinous dhodha Al(OH)₃ | ghulta hai, rangheen ghol (sodium aluminate NaAlO₂) | safed gelatinous dhodha | nahi ghulta |\n\n(Gehre neele ghol aur zincate jaise ions ke naam aur formula **complex ion** hain, aage ki class mein. Aaj hume sirf natija yaad rakhna hai.)\n\nIsse ek chhota \"kaun ghulta hai\" nakshaa bhi banta hai.\n\n```\n   Excess NaOH mein ghulte hain :  Zn²⁺   Pb²⁺   Al³⁺\n   Excess NaOH mein NAHI ghulte :  Ca²⁺   Fe²⁺   Fe³⁺   Cu²⁺\n\n   Excess NH₄OH mein ghulte hain : Cu²⁺ (gehra neela)   Zn²⁺ (rangheen)\n   Excess NH₄OH mein NAHI ghulte : Ca²⁺   Fe²⁺   Fe³⁺   Pb²⁺   Al³⁺\n```\n\n> **Figure 6.3** – Dono reagents ke excess ka nakshaa. Ek ion jo dono mein ghulta hai (Zn²⁺) aur ek jo sirf NH₄OH mein ghulta hai (Cu²⁺) hi alag pehchaan dete hain.\n\nAb asli kaam: puzzles hal karna."
          },
          {
            "heading": "Part C: Mixed Detective Puzzles (Worked Examples)",
            "headingHinglish": "Part C: Mile-jule puzzles (worked)",
            "body": "**Safety reminder for all puzzles:** Compounds of lead, barium, and silver are toxic (and AgNO₃ stains skin black); copper salts are harmful if swallowed; even dilute NaOH is corrosive to eyes and skin; and ammonia gas is pungent and irritating. **Students never perform any of these tests alone or at home.** All heavy-metal waste is collected by the teacher in designated waste containers—never poured down the drain.\n\n### Puzzle 1. A White Precipitate That Dissolves in One Alkali and Not the Other\n\nA colorless solution **P** gives a **white precipitate** with a few drops of NaOH, and the precipitate **dissolves** in excess NaOH. In a second tube, a few drops of NH₄OH give a **white precipitate** that **does not dissolve** in excess NH₄OH.\n\n**Step 1:** Colorless solution rules out Cu²⁺, Fe²⁺, and Fe³⁺.\n**Step 2:** A white precipitate that dissolves in excess NaOH narrows P to Zn²⁺, Pb²⁺, or Al³⁺. Because the precipitate does **not** dissolve in excess NH₄OH, Zn²⁺ is ruled out (Zn(OH)₂ dissolves in excess NH₄OH). That leaves **Pb²⁺ or Al³⁺**.\n**Step 3:** Since NaOH and NH₄OH alone cannot separate Pb²⁺ from Al³⁺ (Topic 3), we check one extra clue: adding a few drops of **dilute HCl** to a fresh portion of P produces a **white precipitate** (teacher demonstration). That identifies **Pb²⁺** (forming sparingly soluble lead(II) chloride, PbCl₂↓, from Chapter 1).\n**Step 4:** Cation is **Pb²⁺**. Equations: Pb²⁺ + 2OH⁻ → Pb(OH)₂↓ and Pb(OH)₂ + 2NaOH → Na₂PbO₂ + 2H₂O.\n\n**Check:** In Pb²⁺ + 2OH⁻ → Pb(OH)₂↓: Pb 1 = 1, O 2 = 2, H 2 = 2, charge (+2) + 2 × (−1) = 0 ✅. In the second equation: left has Pb 1, Na 2, O: 2 + 2 = 4, H: 2 + 2 = 4; right has Na₂PbO₂ (Na 2, Pb 1, O 2) + 2H₂O (O 2, H 4) → O 4, H 4 ✅.\n\n### Puzzle 2. Pale Blue to Deep Inky Blue (Using a Flowchart)\n\nSolution **Q** is **blue**. Trace its reactions in the flowchart below:\n\n```\n   Solution Q (blue)\n        :\n        v\n   Few drops of NaOH  --->  pale blue precipitate\n        :\n        v\n   Excess NaOH        --->  precipitate stays unchanged (insoluble)\n   \n   Second portion:\n   Few drops of NH₄OH --->  pale blue precipitate\n        :\n        v\n   Excess NH₄OH       --->  precipitate dissolves -> DEEP (inky) blue solution\n```\n\n> **Figure 6.4** – Flowchart of results for Solution Q with NaOH and NH₄OH. Dissolving in excess NH₄OH to form a deep inky-blue solution is the unmistakable signature of Cu²⁺.\n\n**Step 1:** Blue solution points toward Cu²⁺.\n**Step 2:** Pale blue precipitate with both alkalis, insoluble in excess NaOH, and dissolving in excess NH₄OH to give a **deep inky-blue solution** confirms **Cu²⁺** beyond a shadow of a doubt.\n**Step 3:** The bottle sits on a shelf of chlorides, sulfates, and nitrates, and tests with acidified AgNO₃ and acidified BaCl₂ both give **no precipitate** (ruling out Cl⁻ and SO₄²⁻). By elimination, the anion is **NO₃⁻**.\n**Step 4:** Pairing Cu²⁺ (+2) with two NO₃⁻ (−1) ions gives **Cu(NO₃)₂**. Equation: Cu(NO₃)₂ + 2NaOH → Cu(OH)₂↓ + 2NaNO₃.\n\n**Check:** Cu 1 = 1, N 2 = 2, O: left 6 (in nitrate) + 2 (in NaOH) = 8, right 2 (in Cu(OH)₂) + 6 (in 2NaNO₃) = 8 ✅. Na 2 = 2 ✅, H 2 = 2 ✅.\n\n### Puzzle 3. A Pale Green Solution Whose Precipitate Turns Brown in Air\n\nSolution **R** is **pale green**. Adding a few drops of NaOH gives a **dirty green precipitate** that **does not dissolve** in excess NaOH. With NH₄OH it also gives a **dirty green precipitate** that **does not dissolve** in excess NH₄OH. When left standing exposed to air, the top edge of the green precipitate slowly turns **reddish-brown**.\n\n**Step 1:** Pale green solution points toward Fe²⁺.\n**Step 2:** Dirty green precipitate insoluble in excess of both alkalis confirms **Fe²⁺** (whereas Fe³⁺ would have given a reddish-brown precipitate right from the first drop).\n**Steps 3 & 4:** The browning at the top occurs because oxygen in the air slowly oxidizes iron(II) hydroxide toward iron(III) hydroxide (Topic 2). Net ionic equation: **Fe²⁺ + 2OH⁻ → Fe(OH)₂↓**.\n\n**Check:** Fe 1 = 1, O 2 = 2, H 2 = 2, charge (+2) + 2(−1) = 0 ✅.\n\n### Puzzle 4. A Slight White Precipitate That Never Dissolves\n\nColorless solution **S** gives a **slight white precipitate** with a few drops of NaOH, which **does not dissolve** in excess NaOH. Adding NH₄OH to a fresh portion gives **no precipitate** (or at most a faint haze).\n\n**Step 2:** Among our four cations that form white precipitates (Ca²⁺, Zn²⁺, Pb²⁺, Al³⁺), **only Ca²⁺** forms a white precipitate that does not dissolve in excess NaOH—and giving no precipitate with weak NH₄OH clinches **Ca²⁺**!\n**Step 4:** Net ionic equation: **Ca²⁺ + 2OH⁻ → Ca(OH)₂↓**.\n\n**Check:** Ca 1 = 1, O 2 = 2, H 2 = 2, charge +2 − 2 = 0 ✅.\n\n### Puzzle 5. Anion Detective: Curdy White Precipitate\n\nColorless solution **T** is acidified with a few drops of dilute HNO₃, and then AgNO₃ solution is added: a **curdy white precipitate** forms immediately. When dilute NH₄OH is added, the precipitate **dissolves completely**. (AgNO₃ is toxic and stains skin—teacher demonstration only.)\n\n**Step 3:** A curdy white precipitate with acidified AgNO₃ that dissolves in dilute NH₄OH is the dedicated proof for **chloride, Cl⁻**.\n**Step 4:** Net ionic equation: **Ag⁺ + Cl⁻ → AgCl↓**.\n\n**Check:** Ag 1 = 1, Cl 1 = 1, charge (+1) + (−1) = 0 ✅.\n\n### Puzzle 6. A Solid Salt That Releases Two Different Gases in Two Tests!\n\nWhite solid **U** is tested in two separate portions by the teacher:\n- **(i)** Adding dilute acid to the first portion produces **brisk effervescence**, and passing the gas through clear limewater turns the limewater **milky**.\n- **(ii)** Warming the second portion gently with NaOH solution in a fume hood releases a **pungent gas** that turns **moist red litmus paper blue**.\n\n**Step 3:** Test (i) proves the gas is CO₂, identifying the anion as **carbonate, CO₃²⁻**. Test (ii) proves the gas is NH₃, identifying the cation as **ammonium, NH₄⁺**.\n**Step 4:** Combining NH₄⁺ (+1) and CO₃²⁻ (−2) by charge balance gives **(NH₄)₂CO₃**, ammonium carbonate! Reaction with warm NaOH: **(NH₄)₂CO₃ + 2NaOH → Na₂CO₃ + 2H₂O + 2NH₃↑**.\n\n**Check:** N 2 = 2 ✅; H: left 8 + 2 = 10, right 4 (in 2H₂O) + 6 (in 2NH₃) = 10 ✅; C 1 = 1 ✅; O: 3 + 2 = 5 = 3 + 2 ✅; Na 2 = 2 ✅.\n\n### Puzzle 7. Putting Cation and Anion Flowcharts Together\n\nTo a portion of yellow-brown solution **V**, the teacher **first adds dilute HCl**: no bubbles form. Next, the teacher adds **BaCl₂ solution**: a **white precipitate** forms that **does not dissolve** in dilute acid. To a second portion of V, the teacher adds NaOH: a **reddish-brown precipitate** forms that **does not dissolve** in excess NaOH. (Barium salts are toxic—teacher demonstration only.)\n\n```\n   Portion 1 of V :  dilute HCl  --->  no bubbles (not carbonate)\n                         :\n                         v\n                     add BaCl₂   --->  white ppt, insoluble in acid\n                         :\n                         v\n                     anion = SO₄²⁻\n\n   Portion 2 of V :  add NaOH    --->  reddish-brown ppt, insoluble in excess\n                         :\n                         v\n                     cation = Fe³⁺\n```\n\n> **Figure 6.5** – Flowchart for Solution V: the anion and cation are identified cleanly in two separate portions.\n\nNet ionic equations: **Ba²⁺ + SO₄²⁻ → BaSO₄↓** and **Fe³⁺ + 3OH⁻ → Fe(OH)₃↓**. Thus Solution V contains **Fe³⁺ and SO₄²⁻** ions (iron(III) sulfate, Fe₂(SO₄)₃).\n\n**Check:** Ba 1 = 1, S 1 = 1, O 4 = 4, charge (+2) + (−2) = 0 ✅. Fe 1 = 1, O 3 = 3, H 3 = 3, charge (+3) + 3(−1) = 0 ✅.\n\n### Puzzle 8. Two Colorless Bottles: Which Is Zn²⁺ and Which Is Al³⁺?\n\nTwo colorless solutions **X** and **Y** (known to contain no lead) both give a **white precipitate** with a few drops of NaOH, and **both precipitates dissolve** in excess NaOH. With a few drops of NH₄OH, both again give a **white precipitate**. When **excess NH₄OH** is added, the precipitate in **X dissolves completely**, whereas the precipitate in **Y remains undissolved**.\n\n**Step 1:** Both are colorless.\n**Step 2:** Dissolving in excess NaOH narrows both to Zn²⁺, Pb²⁺, or Al³⁺; since lead is absent, they must be Zn²⁺ and Al³⁺.\n**Step 3:** In excess NH₄OH, Zn(OH)₂ dissolves while Al(OH)₃ does not. Therefore, **X = Zn²⁺** and **Y = Al³⁺**!\n**Step 4:** For X: Zn²⁺ + 2OH⁻ → Zn(OH)₂↓ and Zn(OH)₂ + 2NaOH → Na₂ZnO₂ + 2H₂O. For Y: Al³⁺ + 3OH⁻ → Al(OH)₃↓ and Al(OH)₃ + NaOH → NaAlO₂ + 2H₂O.\n\n**Check:** Zn(OH)₂ + 2NaOH → Na₂ZnO₂ + 2H₂O: Zn 1, Na 2, O 4 = 2 + 2, H 4 = 4 ✅. Al(OH)₃ + NaOH → NaAlO₂ + 2H₂O: Al 1, Na 1, O 4 = 2 + 2, H 4 = 4 ✅.",
            "bodyHinglish": "**Poison line (aaj ke puzzles ke liye):** lead ke compound zehreele hain, barium ke compound zehreele hain, silver nitrate ki boondein bhi chamdi par kaale daag chhodti hain, tamba ke namak khaane par nuksaan karte hain, NaOH ka patla ghol bhi aankh aur chamdi ko jalata hai, aur ammonia gas ki tez gandh jalan karti hai. **Is chapter mein koi bhi test student akele ya ghar par nahi karta.** Heavy metals ka bacha hua ghol teacher alag dibbe mein jama karta hai, naali mein kabhi nahi.\n\n### Puzzle 1. Safed dhodha jo ghul gaya (observation table se)\n\nEk rangheen ghol P mein NaOH ki kuch boondein: **safed dhodha**. Aur NaOH daalne par dhodha **ghul gaya**. Doosre test tube mein NH₄OH ki boondein: **safed dhodha**, excess daalne par **nahi ghula**.\n\n**Kadam 1:** ghol rangheen, to Cu²⁺, Fe²⁺, Fe³⁺ nahi.\n**Kadam 2:** NaOH mein safed dhodha jo excess mein ghule: Zn²⁺, Pb²⁺, Al³⁺ (teeno). NH₄OH ke excess mein **nahi** ghula: Zn²⁺ nahi (Zn ghulta hai). Bacha: **Pb²⁺ ya Al³⁺**.\n**Kadam 3:** in dono ko NaOH aur NH₄OH se alag nahi kar sakte (Topic 3). Isliye ek aur suraag chahiye: ghol P mein **dilute HCl** ki boondein daalne par safed dhodha aaya (sirf teacher demo). Ye Pb²⁺ ka suraag hai (lead chloride thande paani mein kam ghulta hai, Chapter 1).\n**Kadam 4:** ion **Pb²⁺**. Equations: Pb²⁺ + 2OH⁻ → Pb(OH)₂↓ aur Pb(OH)₂ + 2NaOH → Na₂PbO₂ + 2H₂O.\n\n**Jaanch:** Pb²⁺ + 2OH⁻: Pb 1 = 1, O 2 = 2, H 2 = 2, charge (+2) + 2 × (−1) = 0 ✅. Doosri equation: left mein Pb 1, Na 2, O 2 + 2 = 4, H 2 + 2 = 4; right mein Na₂PbO₂ (Na 2, Pb 1, O 2) aur 2H₂O (O 2, H 4): O 4, H 4 ✅.\n\n### Puzzle 2. Neela ghol, dhodha, aur gehra neela (flowchart se)\n\nEk **neela** ghol Q hai. Kaun si cheezein dikhti hain, flowchart mein lagao.\n\n```\n   Ghol Q (neela)\n        :\n        v\n   NaOH ki boondein  --->  halka neela dhodha\n        :\n        v\n   Excess NaOH       --->  dhodha jaisa ka taisa (nahi ghula)\n   \n   Alag hissa :\n   NH₄OH ki boondein --->  halka neela dhodha\n        :\n        v\n   Excess NH₄OH      --->  dhodha ghula, GEHRA (siyahi jaisa) neela ghol\n```\n\n> **Figure 6.4** – Flowchart: Q mein NaOH aur NH₄OH ke natije. NH₄OH ke excess mein gehra neela ghol Cu²⁺ ki khaas pehchaan hai.\n\n**Kadam 1:** ghol neela: Cu²⁺ ka ishara (sirf ishara).\n**Kadam 2:** halka neela dhodha dono se, excess NaOH mein nahi ghula, excess NH₄OH mein **gehra neela ghol** bana: Cu²⁺ ke siwa shared table mein koi nahi.\n**Kadam 3:** label par likha hai \"nitrate\". Anion ke tests (AgNO₃, BaCl₂) par kuch nahi aaya: ye Cl⁻ aur SO₄²⁻ ke tests ka natija **nahi** hai, label ke hisaab se NO₃⁻ (uski pakki pehchaan aage ki class mein).\n**Kadam 4:** Cu²⁺ aur NO₃⁻: charge (+2) aur 2 × (−1): formula **Cu(NO₃)₂**. Equation: Cu(NO₃)₂ + 2NaOH → Cu(OH)₂↓ + 2NaNO₃.\n\n**Jaanch:** Cu 1 = 1, N 2 = 2, O: left 6 (nitrate) + 2 (NaOH) = 8, right 2 (Cu(OH)₂) + 6 (2NaNO₃) = 8 ✅. Na 2 = 2 ✅, H 2 = 2 ✅.\n\n### Puzzle 3. Halka hara ghol jo bhura ho gaya\n\nGhol R **halka hara** hai. NaOH ki boondein daalne par **ganda hara dhodha**, excess mein **nahi ghula**. NH₄OH ke saath bhi **ganda hara dhodha**, excess mein **nahi ghula**. Kuch der khula chhodne par upar ka hissa **bhura** hone laga.\n\n**Kadam 1:** halka hara ghol: Fe²⁺ ka ishara.\n**Kadam 2:** ganda hara dhodha, dono ke excess mein nahi ghula: **Fe²⁺**. (Fe³⁺ ka dhodha laal-bhura hota, hara nahi.)\n**Kadam 3 aur 4:** bhura hona: hawa Fe²⁺ ko dheere-dheere Fe³⁺ mein badal deti hai (Topic 2, ek line). Ionic equation: Fe²⁺ + 2OH⁻ → Fe(OH)₂↓. Jis anion ke saath ye aaya, use alag test se dekhenge.\n\n**Jaanch:** Fe 1 = 1, O 2 = 2, H 2 = 2, charge (+2) + 2(−1) = 0 ✅.\n\n### Puzzle 4. Halka safed dhodha jo ghula hi nahi\n\nRangheen ghol S mein NaOH ki boondein: **halka safed dhodha** (bahut kam). Excess NaOH mein **nahi ghula**. NH₄OH ki boondein: **koi dhodha nahi ya bahut halka**.\n\n**Kadam 2:** NaOH mein safed dhodha jo excess mein nahi ghula: Ca²⁺ (Zn²⁺, Pb²⁺, Al³⁺ to ghul jaate). NH₄OH par dhodha nahi: Ca²⁺ ki pehchaan paki.\n**Kadam 4:** Ca²⁺ + 2OH⁻ → Ca(OH)₂↓.\n\n**Jaanch:** Ca 1 = 1, O 2 = 2, H 2 = 2, charge +2 − 2 = 0 ✅.\n\n### Puzzle 5. Anion ka puzzle: kaun sa ion safed curdy dhodha deta hai?\n\nEk rangheen ghol T. Pehle dilute HNO₃ ki thodi boondein, phir AgNO₃ ghol: **safed, curdy (dahi jaisa) dhodha**. Is dhodhe mein NH₄OH daalne par dhodha **ghul gaya**. (AgNO₃ zehreela hai aur chamdi par kaale daag chhodta hai: sirf teacher demo.)\n\n**Kadam 3:** safed curdy dhodha jo NH₄OH mein ghule: **Cl⁻**.\n**Kadam 4:** Ionic equation: Ag⁺ + Cl⁻ → AgCl↓.\n\n**Jaanch:** Ag 1 = 1, Cl 1 = 1, charge (+1) + (−1) = 0 ✅.\n\n### Puzzle 6. Thos namak: gas ke do suraag\n\nThos U (safed). **(i)** Teacher ne dilute acid daala: tez bulbule, gas jo chuna paani ko **doodhiya** karti hai. **(ii)** Doosre hisse ko NaOH ke saath halka garam kiya: **tez gandh wali gas**, jo gili laal litmus ko **neela** karti hai. (Ammonia gas ki gandh tez aur jalan wali hai: sirf fume cupboard mein teacher, student sirf padhta hai.)\n\n**Kadam 3:** (i) CO₂ aur doodhiya chuna paani: **CO₃²⁻**. (ii) NH₃ gas, gili laal litmus neela: **NH₄⁺**.\n**Kadam 4:** NH₄⁺ (+1) aur CO₃²⁻ (−2): criss-cross se **(NH₄)₂CO₃**. Equation: (NH₄)₂CO₃ + 2NaOH → Na₂CO₃ + 2H₂O + 2NH₃.\n\n**Jaanch:** N 2 = 2 ✅. H: left 8 + 2 = 10, right 4 (2H₂O) + 6 (2NH₃) = 10 ✅. C 1 = 1 ✅, O 3 + 2 = 5 = 3 + 2 ✅, Na 2 = 2 ✅.\n\n### Puzzle 7. BaCl₂ ke saath safed dhodha: kaun sa anion?\n\nGhol V mein **dilute HCl pehle** daala: koi bulbule nahi. Phir BaCl₂: **safed dhodha** jo acid mein **nahi ghula**. Cation ke liye: V peela-bhura hai, NaOH ki boondein: **laal-bhura dhodha**, excess mein **nahi ghula**. (Barium ke compound zehreele hain: sirf teacher demo.)\n\n```\n   Ghol V :  dilute HCl  --->  koi bulbule nahi\n                  :\n                  v\n             BaCl₂ daala --->  safed dhodha, acid mein NAHI ghula\n                  :\n                  v\n             anion = SO₄²⁻\n\n   Alag hissa :  NaOH  --->  laal-bhura dhodha, excess mein nahi ghula\n                  :\n                  v\n             cation = Fe³⁺\n```\n\n> **Figure 6.5** – Puzzle 7 ka flowchart: anion aur cation alag-alag hisson mein nikale gaye.\n\nIonic equations: Ba²⁺ + SO₄²⁻ → BaSO₄↓ aur Fe³⁺ + 3OH⁻ → Fe(OH)₃↓. Ghol mein **Fe³⁺ aur SO₄²⁻** ions hain.\n\n**Jaanch:** Ba 1 = 1, S 1 = 1, O 4 = 4, charge (+2) + (−2) = 0 ✅. Fe 1 = 1, O 3 = 3, H 3 = 3, charge (+3) + 3(−1) = 0 ✅.\n\n### Puzzle 8. Do bottles, do pehchaan (Zn²⁺ ya Al³⁺)\n\nDo rangheen bottles X aur Y hain. Dono mein NaOH ki boondein daalne par **safed dhodha**, excess mein **dono ghul jaate hain**. NH₄OH ki boondein dono mein **safed dhodha**. Excess NH₄OH: X ka dhodha **ghul gaya**, Y ka dhodha **nahi ghula**. Ye maloom hai ki dono mein se **koi lead nahi** hai.\n\n**Kadam 1:** dono rangheen, kuch bhi kahna mushkil.\n**Kadam 2:** NaOH ke excess mein ghule: Zn²⁺, Pb²⁺, Al³⁺. Lead nahi (diya hua). Bacha Zn²⁺ ya Al³⁺.\n**Kadam 3:** NH₄OH ke excess mein ghule to **Zn²⁺**, na ghule to **Al³⁺**. X = **Zn²⁺**, Y = **Al³⁺**.\n**Kadam 4:** X: Zn²⁺ + 2OH⁻ → Zn(OH)₂↓, Zn(OH)₂ + 2NaOH → Na₂ZnO₂ + 2H₂O. Y: Al³⁺ + 3OH⁻ → Al(OH)₃↓, Al(OH)₃ + NaOH → NaAlO₂ + 2H₂O.\n\n**Jaanch:** Zn(OH)₂ + 2NaOH → Na₂ZnO₂ + 2H₂O: Zn 1, Na 2, O 4 = 2 + 2, H 4 = 4 ✅. Al(OH)₃ + NaOH → NaAlO₂ + 2H₂O: Al 1, Na 1, O 4 = 2 + 2, H 4 = 4 ✅."
          },
          {
            "heading": "Part D: How Much Precipitate Forms? (Applying the Chapter 5 Mole Method)",
            "headingHinglish": "Part D: Kitna dhodha banega? (Chapter 5 ka mole method)",
            "body": "### Example 1. Calculating Precipitate and Dissolved Salt Masses from Cu(NO₃)₂ + NaOH\n\n**Question:** Suppose **18.75 g** of Cu(NO₃)₂ in solution reacts with the exact required amount of NaOH. What mass of Cu(OH)₂ precipitate settles at the bottom, and what mass of NaNO₃ remains dissolved in the solution above it? (Cu = 63.5, N = 14, O = 16, H = 1, Na = 23.)\n\n**Balanced equation:** Cu(NO₃)₂ + 2NaOH → Cu(OH)₂↓ + 2NaNO₃\n\n**Step 1 (Molar masses):**\n- Cu(NO₃)₂ = 63.5 + 2 × (14 + 3 × 16) = 63.5 + 2 × 62 = **187.5 g/mol**.\n- Cu(OH)₂ = 63.5 + 2 × 17 = **97.5 g/mol**.\n- NaNO₃ = 23 + 14 + 48 = **85 g/mol**.\n\n**Step 2 (Moles of Cu(NO₃)₂):** 18.75 ÷ 187.5 = **0.1 mol**.\n**Step 3 (Mole ratios):** 1 mol of Cu(NO₃)₂ produces 1 mol of Cu(OH)₂ and 2 mol of NaNO₃. So 0.1 mol produces **0.1 mol of Cu(OH)₂** and **0.2 mol of NaNO₃**.\n**Step 4 (Masses):**\n- Mass of Cu(OH)₂ precipitate = 0.1 × 97.5 = **9.75 g**.\n- Mass of dissolved NaNO₃ = 0.2 × 85 = **17.0 g**.\n\n```\n       :  clear solution (17.0 g NaNO₃)  :\n       :                                 :\n       :   Cu(OH)₂ precipitate: 9.75 g   :\n       :_________________________________:\n```\n\n> **Figure 6.6** – Inside the test tube after complete reaction: 9.75 g of pale blue Cu(OH)₂ precipitate sits at the bottom, while 17.0 g of NaNO₃ stays dissolved in the clear liquid above.\n\n**Check (conservation of mass):** Mass of NaOH reacted = 0.2 mol × 40 = 8.0 g. Total reactants = 18.75 + 8.0 = 26.75 g; total products = 9.75 + 17.0 = 26.75 g ✅.",
            "bodyHinglish": "### Example 1. Cu(NO₃)₂ aur NaOH se dhodhe ka mass\n\n**Sawaal:** **18.75 g** Cu(NO₃)₂ ke ghol mein NaOH ki bilkul zaroori matra daali gayi. Kitna Cu(OH)₂ ka dhodha banega, aur ghol mein kitna NaNO₃ rahega? (Cu = 63.5, N = 14, O = 16, H = 1, Na = 23.)\n\n**Equation:** Cu(NO₃)₂ + 2NaOH → Cu(OH)₂↓ + 2NaNO₃\n\n**Kadam 1 (molar mass):** Cu(NO₃)₂ = 63.5 + 2 × (14 + 3 × 16) = 63.5 + 2 × 62 = **187.5 g/mol**. Cu(OH)₂ = 63.5 + 2 × 17 = **97.5 g/mol**. NaNO₃ = 23 + 14 + 48 = **85 g/mol**.\n**Kadam 2 (mole):** 18.75 ÷ 187.5 = **0.1 mol**.\n**Kadam 3 (ratio):** 1 mol Cu(NO₃)₂ se 1 mol Cu(OH)₂ aur 2 mol NaNO₃. To 0.1 mol se 0.1 mol Cu(OH)₂ aur 0.2 mol NaNO₃.\n**Kadam 4 (mass):** Cu(OH)₂ = 0.1 × 97.5 = **9.75 g**. NaNO₃ = 0.2 × 85 = **17 g**.\n\n```\n       :  saaf ghol (NaNO₃ ghula)  :\n       :                           :\n       :   Cu(OH)₂ dhodha : 9.75 g :\n       :___________________________:\n```\n\n> **Figure 6.6** – Test tube: Cu(OH)₂ ka halka neela dhodha neeche baitha hai, upar saaf ghol mein NaNO₃ ghula hai.\n\n**Jaanch:** NaOH ka mass = 0.2 mol × 40 = 8 g. Left: 18.75 + 8 = 26.75 g. Right: 9.75 + 17 = 26.75 g ✅ (mass conservation).\n\n(Ye numerical sirf \"kitna dhodha\" jaanne ke liye hai; asli lab mein aisa tola-bhar kaam teacher hi karta hai.)"
          },
          {
            "heading": "Common Pitfalls to Avoid",
            "headingHinglish": "Aam galtiyan (bachke rehna)",
            "body": "**Mistake 1: \"Squeezing in a huge squirt of NaOH all at once, and missing the precipitate stage completely!\"**\nDemo: Suppose a student tests a Zn²⁺ or Al³⁺ solution by dumping a full dropper of NaOH into the test tube all at once. Because NaOH is immediately in **excess**, any white Zn(OH)₂ or Al(OH)₃ precipitate that forms dissolves almost instantaneously, leaving a **clear, colorless solution**! The student then writes down *\"No precipitate formed—no metal ion present,\"* completely missing Zn²⁺, Pb²⁺, or Al³⁺!\n\n```\n   Wrong technique                        Correct technique\n   (dumping excess NaOH all at once)      (a few drops first, then excess)\n\n   solution ---> clear colorless liquid   few drops ---> white precipitate seen!\n   (precipitate stage missed completely!) excess    ---> precipitate dissolves\n   \n   Result: Zn²⁺, Pb²⁺, Al³⁺ missed!       Result: amphoteric ion spotted cleanly!\n```\n\n> **Figure 6.7** – Adding a large squirt of NaOH all at once skips right past the initial precipitate stage for Zn²⁺, Pb²⁺, and Al³⁺. Always add **just 2–3 drops first**, observe the precipitate, and only then add excess!\n\n**Mistake 2: \"Writing 'dissolves in excess' in your notes without recording WHICH alkali (NaOH or NH₄OH) was used!\"**\nDemo: Suppose an Al³⁺ solution forms a white precipitate that dissolves in excess **NaOH**. If a student carelessly jots down *\"white ppt, dissolves in excess\"* without naming the reagent, and later reads that note as *\"dissolves in excess NH₄OH,\"* they will misidentify the ion as **Zn²⁺**! Remember: Al³⁺ dissolves in excess **NaOH**, but does **not** dissolve in excess **NH₄OH**.\n\nAlways write the reagent name right next to \"excess\":\n\n| Observation Situation | Vague / Confusing Note | Clear, Accurate Note |\n|---|---|---|\n| White ppt that dissolved in excess | \"dissolved in excess\" | \"dissolved in excess **NaOH**\" (or \"in excess **NH₄OH**\") |\n| Al³⁺ + excess NH₄OH | \"dissolves\" ❌ | \"**insoluble** in excess NH₄OH\" ✅ |\n| Al³⁺ + excess NaOH | \"insoluble\" ❌ | \"**dissolves** in excess NaOH\" ✅ |\n\n**Mistake 3: \"Leaving spectator ions inside a net ionic equation.\"**\nDemo: For Cu(NO₃)₂ + 2NaOH → Cu(OH)₂↓ + 2NaNO₃, writing Cu²⁺ + 2NO₃⁻ + 2Na⁺ + 2OH⁻ → Cu(OH)₂↓ + 2Na⁺ + 2NO₃⁻ gives the *complete ionic equation*, not the **net ionic equation**. Whenever a question asks for the net ionic (or simply ionic) equation of a precipitation test, cancel the spectator ions (2Na⁺ and 2NO₃⁻) that appear identically on both sides to leave the clean reaction: **Cu²⁺ + 2OH⁻ → Cu(OH)₂↓**.\n\n**Check:** 2Na⁺ and 2NO₃⁻ cancel on both sides ✅, leaving Cu 1 = 1, O 2 = 2, H 2 = 2, charge +2 + 2(−1) = 0 ✅.\n\n**Mistake 4: \"Labeling a precipitate as `(aq)` instead of `(s)` (or `↓`).\"**\nDemo: Writing Cu²⁺(aq) + 2OH⁻(aq) → Cu(OH)₂(aq) contradicts your own test-tube drawing! The state symbol `(aq)` means *dissolved in water*, whereas a precipitate is an **insoluble solid** that settles out of the water. Always mark a precipitate with **`(s)`** or a downward arrow **`↓`** (or both: `Cu(OH)₂(s)↓`), dissolved ions and soluble salts with `(aq)`, gases with `(g)` or `↑`, and liquid water with `(l)`.",
            "bodyHinglish": "**Galti 1: \"Excess NaOH ek saath dher sa daal diya, dhodha dikha hi nahi.\"**\nDemo: Zn²⁺ ya Al³⁺ ke ghol mein ek student ne ek saath poori dropper-bhar NaOH daal di. Test tube mein **saaf rangheen ghol** hi dikha. Usne likh diya: \"koi dhodha nahi, to koi ion nahi!\" Galat. Dhodha bana tha, par itni jaldi excess aaya ki turant ghul gaya.\n\n```\n   Galat tareeka                         Sahi tareeka\n   (ek saath dher NaOH)                  (boond-boond, phir excess)\n\n   ghol  --->  saaf ghol                 boondein  --->  safed dhodha dikha\n   (dhodha dikha hi nahi)                excess    --->  dhodha ghul gaya\n   \n   nateeja: Zn²⁺, Pb²⁺, Al³⁺ chhoot gaye  nateeja: teeno ka pata chala\n```\n\n> **Figure 6.7** – Excess ek saath daalne se Zn²⁺, Pb²⁺, Al³⁺ ka dhodha-stage chhoot jaata hai. Hamesha pehle **kuch boondein**, dekho, phir excess.\n\nNiyam: pehle teacher kuch boondein hi deta hai, dhodha dekhne ke baad hi excess. Excess ka matlab \"dhodha ghulne tak thoda-thoda aur\".\n\n**Galti 2: \"NaOH aur NH₄OH ke natije aapas mein badal diye.\"**\nDemo: ek Al³⁺ ke ghol mein safed dhodha aaya aur excess daalne par ghul gaya. Student ne notebook mein sirf likha \"excess mein ghula\", aur bina yaad kiye ki wo NaOH tha ya NH₄OH, use \"NH₄OH mein ghula\" maan liya. Natija: usne ghol ko Zn²⁺ likh diya (kyunki \"Zn NH₄OH mein ghulta hai\"). Asal mein wo NaOH ka natija tha: Al³⁺ **NaOH** ke excess mein ghulta hai, **NH₄OH** ke excess mein **nahi**.\n\nSudhaar ka tareeka: table mein har natije ke saamne **reagent ka naam** likho.\n\n| Ghol ka natija | Galat likha | Sahi likha |\n|---|---|---|\n| Safed dhodha, excess mein ghula | \"ghula\" | \"ghula NaOH mein\" ya \"ghula NH₄OH mein\" |\n| Al³⁺ ke saath excess NH₄OH | ghula | **nahi ghula** |\n| Al³⁺ ke saath excess NaOH | nahi ghula | **ghula** |\n\nAadat: har kholi mein reagent ka naam likho, taaki Zn²⁺ aur Al³⁺ ya Cu²⁺ jaise ions ulajhein nahi.\n\n**Galti 3: \"Ionic equation mein spectator ions bhi likh diye.\"**\nDemo: Cu(NO₃)₂ aur NaOH ki molecular equation Cu(NO₃)₂ + 2NaOH → Cu(OH)₂↓ + 2NaNO₃ hai. Ek student ne ionic equation likhi: Cu²⁺ + 2NO₃⁻ + 2Na⁺ + 2OH⁻ → Cu(OH)₂ + 2Na⁺ + 2NO₃⁻. Ye **poori ionic equation** hai aur galat nahi, par jo **net ionic equation** maanga jaata hai wo chhoti hai. Dono taraf 2NO₃⁻ aur 2Na⁺ hain: ye spectator hain, kaat do. Bacha: Cu²⁺ + 2OH⁻ → Cu(OH)₂↓.\n\nSaath hi, spectator ion hamesha **dono taraf ek jaise** hote hain. Agar koi ion sirf ek taraf ho, to wo spectator nahi hai.\n\n**Jaanch:** Na⁺ 2 = 2, NO₃⁻ 2 = 2 kat gaye ✅. Bache: Cu 1 = 1, O 2 = 2, H 2 = 2, charge +2 + 2(−1) = 0 ✅.\n\n**Galti 4: \"Dhodhe ko (aq) likh diya, ya state symbol hi nahi likha.\"**\nDemo: ek student ne likha: Cu²⁺(aq) + 2OH⁻(aq) → Cu(OH)₂(aq). Phir usne test tube ki tasveer banayi: dhodha neeche baitha. Tasveer aur equation mein jhagda! (aq) ka matlab ghol mein ghula hua, jabki dhodha **ghulta nahi**, wo neeche baithta hai. Sahi: Cu²⁺(aq) + 2OH⁻(aq) → Cu(OH)₂(s)↓.\n\nNiyam: ghulansheel salt aur ions (aq), dhodha (s), gas (g), paani (l). Dhodhe ke aage ↓ bhi laga sakte ho."
          },
          {
            "heading": "Quick Recap",
            "headingHinglish": "Quick Recap",
            "body": "- **4-step master method:** (1) Inspect color → (2) Test cation with NaOH and NH₄OH (a few drops first, then excess) → (3) Test anion in fresh portions (acid pre-check + AgNO₃ or BaCl₂; dilute acid for carbonate) → (4) Write the salt formula by charge balance and balanced equations.\n- **Cation summary:** Ca²⁺ slight white (insoluble in excess NaOH; no ppt with NH₄OH); Fe²⁺ dirty green (insoluble in excess of both); Fe³⁺ reddish-brown (insoluble in excess of both); Cu²⁺ pale blue (insoluble in excess NaOH; dissolves in excess NH₄OH to give a deep inky-blue solution); Zn²⁺ white (dissolves in excess of **both** NaOH and NH₄OH to give colorless solutions); Pb²⁺ and Al³⁺ white / gelatinous white (dissolve in excess NaOH, insoluble in excess NH₄OH; distinguish Pb²⁺ with KI → yellow PbI₂↓ or dil. HCl → white PbCl₂↓).\n- **Anion & ammonium summary:** Cl⁻ → curdy white AgCl↓ with dil. HNO₃ + AgNO₃ (dissolves in NH₄OH); SO₄²⁻ → white BaSO₄↓ with dil. HCl + BaCl₂ (insoluble in acid); CO₃²⁻ → brisk CO₂↑ bubbles with dilute acid (turns limewater milky); NH₄⁺ → pungent NH₃↑ gas on warming with NaOH (turns moist red litmus blue).\n- **Equations:** Cancel spectator ions to write net ionic equations; mark precipitates with `(s)` or `↓` and dissolved species with `(aq)`.\n- **Stoichiometry:** 18.75 g of Cu(NO₃)₂ (0.1 mol) reacts with 8.0 g of NaOH (0.2 mol) to yield 9.75 g of Cu(OH)₂↓ and 17.0 g of NaNO₃.\n- **Four pitfalls to avoid:** Never dump excess reagent in all at once; always specify *which* reagent is in excess; cancel spectator ions in net ionic equations; never label a solid precipitate `(aq)`.",
            "bodyHinglish": "- **Master method:** dekho (rang), cation ka test (NaOH phir NH₄OH: boondein aur excess), anion ka test (alag hisse mein), naam aur formula (charge balance) aur equations.\n- **Cation table:** Ca²⁺ halka safed (nahi ghulta); Fe²⁺ ganda hara; Fe³⁺ laal-bhura; Cu²⁺ halka neela (NH₄OH excess mein gehra neela); Zn²⁺ safed (NaOH aur NH₄OH dono ke excess mein ghulta); Pb²⁺ aur Al³⁺ safed (NaOH excess mein ghulte, NH₄OH excess mein nahi).\n- **Anion:** Cl⁻ safed curdy AgCl (NH₄OH mein ghulta); SO₄²⁻ safed BaSO₄ (acid mein nahi ghulta); CO₃²⁻ bulbule aur chuna paani doodhiya; NH₄⁺ garam NaOH se tez gandh wali gas.\n- **Equations:** ionic pehle, spectator ions kaat kar; dhodha (s)↓, ghulansheel (aq).\n- **Kitna dhodha:** molar mass, mole, ratio, mass (Cu(NO₃)₂ 18.75 g se Cu(OH)₂ 9.75 g).\n- **Galtiyan:** excess ek saath mat daalo; reagent ka naam har natije ke saath likho; spectator ions kaato; dhodhe ko (s) likho.\n- Har test **school lab mein teacher ke saath** ya **teacher demo**; lead, barium, silver zehreele; ammonia fume cupboard mein; bacha hua ghol teacher ke hisse."
          },
          {
            "heading": "Practice Questions",
            "headingHinglish": "Practice Questions",
            "body": "(All questions are meant to be solved on paper. Atomic masses: H 1, N 14, O 16, Na 23, Al 27, Fe 56.)\n\n1. Solution A is yellow-brown. With a few drops of NaOH it gives a reddish-brown precipitate that does not dissolve in excess NaOH. With a few drops of NH₄OH it gives a reddish-brown precipitate that does not dissolve in excess NH₄OH. Name the cation and write the ionic equation for the precipitate.\n2. Solution B is colourless. With a few drops of NaOH it gives a white precipitate that dissolves in excess NaOH. With a few drops of NH₄OH it gives a white precipitate that also dissolves in excess NH₄OH. Which of the seven ions is present? Write the equation for the dissolving of the precipitate in NaOH.\n3. A teacher uses 4.26 g of Al(NO₃)₃ in solution and adds exactly the right amount of NaOH. Write the balanced equation, and find the mass of Al(OH)₃ precipitate and the mass of NaNO₃ formed. Check that mass is conserved.\n4. How many grams of Fe(NO₃)₂ are needed to give 4.5 g of Fe(OH)₂ precipitate with NaOH? Write the equation first, then find the mass of NaOH that reacts.\n5. Solution C gives a slight white precipitate with a few drops of NaOH, which stays unchanged in excess NaOH. NH₄OH gives no precipitate. Separately, the silver nitrate test (after dilute HNO₃) and the barium chloride test (after dilute HCl) give no precipitate, and dilute acid gives no gas. The bottle is known to hold a chloride, a sulphate, a carbonate or a nitrate. Name the cation and the anion, and write the formula of the salt.\n6. Solid D, with warm NaOH, gives a pungent gas that turns moist red litmus blue. A solution of D, with dilute HNO₃ and then silver nitrate, gives a white curdy precipitate that dissolves in NH₄OH. Name the two ions in D, and write the ionic equation for the gas-forming test.\n7. Two colourless solutions E and F both give a white precipitate with a few drops of NaOH. In excess NaOH, E's precipitate dissolves but F's does not. With NH₄OH, E gives a white precipitate that stays in excess NH₄OH, while F gives no precipitate. It is known that neither solution contains aluminium. Name each ion and explain your logic.\n8. A student adds a large dropper-full of NaOH to a colourless solution and sees only a clear solution. Which of the seven ions could be present? Why can copper not be one of them? What should the student do differently?\n9. One bottle contains a pale green solution and another a yellow-brown solution. Both give precipitates with NaOH that do not dissolve in excess. Name the ions, write the two ionic equations, and say which solution may slowly change colour on standing in air.\n10. Draw a flowchart, using only NaOH and NH₄OH, to separate Zn²⁺, Al³⁺ and Ca²⁺ in three colourless solutions.\n11. State True or False and correct the false ones: (a) A white precipitate with NaOH proves the ion is Zn²⁺. (b) In the net ionic equation for Fe²⁺ with NaOH, Na⁺ is a spectator ion. (c) Cu(OH)₂ is written with (aq) because it forms in a solution. (d) Excess NH₄OH dissolves the precipitates of both Cu²⁺ and Zn²⁺.\n12. Complete this table from memory for Pb²⁺, Cu²⁺, Fe²⁺ and Ca²⁺: colour of precipitate with NaOH drops, effect of excess NaOH, effect of excess NH₄OH.\n13. A student writes the formula of the salt of Fe²⁺ with the nitrate ion as FeNO₃. Use charges to write the correct formula and say what is wrong with the student's formula.\n\n### Answers\n\n1. Yellow-brown solution giving a reddish-brown precipitate insoluble in excess of both NaOH and NH₄OH identifies **Fe³⁺**. Net ionic equation: **Fe³⁺ + 3OH⁻ → Fe(OH)₃↓**. Check: Fe 1, O 3, H 3, charge (+3) + 3(−1) = 0 ✅.\n2. Colorless solution giving a white precipitate that dissolves in **both** excess NaOH and excess NH₄OH uniquely identifies **Zn²⁺** (since Pb²⁺ and Al³⁺ precipitates do not dissolve in excess NH₄OH). Equation for dissolving in excess NaOH: **Zn(OH)₂ + 2NaOH → Na₂ZnO₂ + 2H₂O**. Check: Zn 1, Na 2, O: 4 = 2 + 2, H 4 = 4 ✅.\n3. Balanced equation: **Al(NO₃)₃ + 3NaOH → Al(OH)₃↓ + 3NaNO₃**. Molar masses: Al(NO₃)₃ = 27 + 3 × 62 = 213 g/mol; Al(OH)₃ = 27 + 3 × 17 = 78 g/mol; NaNO₃ = 85 g/mol. Moles of Al(NO₃)₃ = 4.26 ÷ 213 = **0.02 mol**. Mass of Al(OH)₃ = 0.02 × 78 = **1.56 g**. Mass of NaNO₃ = 0.06 × 85 = **5.10 g**. Mass of NaOH reacted = 0.06 × 40 = 2.40 g. Check: 4.26 + 2.40 = 6.66 g and 1.56 + 5.10 = 6.66 g ✅. Atom check: Al 1 = 1, N 3 = 3, O: 9 + 3 = 12 = 3 + 9, H 3 = 3, Na 3 = 3 ✅.\n4. Balanced equation: **Fe(NO₃)₂ + 2NaOH → Fe(OH)₂↓ + 2NaNO₃**. Molar mass of Fe(OH)₂ = 56 + 34 = 90 g/mol, so moles = 4.5 ÷ 90 = **0.05 mol**. From the 1 : 1 mole ratio, 0.05 mol of Fe(NO₃)₂ is required. Molar mass of Fe(NO₃)₂ = 56 + 124 = 180 g/mol, so mass = 0.05 × 180 = **9.0 g**. Mass of NaOH = 2 × 0.05 = 0.10 mol × 40 = **4.0 g**. Check: mass of NaNO₃ = 0.10 × 85 = 8.5 g; left = 9.0 + 4.0 = 13.0 g, right = 4.5 + 8.5 = 13.0 g ✅.\n5. A slight white precipitate with NaOH that stays insoluble in excess NaOH, paired with no precipitate in NH₄OH, identifies the cation as **Ca²⁺**. Negative tests with acidified AgNO₃ (not Cl⁻), acidified BaCl₂ (not SO₄²⁻), and dilute acid (not CO₃²⁻) leave **nitrate, NO₃⁻**, by elimination. Pairing Ca²⁺ (+2) with two NO₃⁻ (−1) ions gives the formula **Ca(NO₃)₂**. Check: (+2) + 2 × (−1) = 0 ✅.\n6. Releasing a pungent gas with warm NaOH that turns moist red litmus blue identifies **NH₄⁺**. Giving a curdy white precipitate with acidified AgNO₃ that dissolves in NH₄OH identifies **Cl⁻**. Net ionic equation for the gas test: **NH₄⁺ + OH⁻ → NH₃↑ + H₂O**. Check: N 1 = 1, H: 4 + 1 = 5 = 3 + 2, O 1 = 1, charge (+1) + (−1) = 0 = 0 ✅.\n7. **In E:** The white precipitate dissolves in excess NaOH (narrowing it to Zn²⁺, Pb²⁺, or Al³⁺) but stays undissolved in excess NH₄OH (ruling out Zn²⁺). Since aluminum is absent, **E = Pb²⁺** (lead compounds are toxic—teacher demo only). **In F:** The white precipitate does not dissolve in excess NaOH and gives no precipitate with NH₄OH, proving **F = Ca²⁺**. Check: E matches the Pb²⁺ row and F matches the Ca²⁺ row of our table ✅.\n8. Dumping a large squirt of NaOH all at once can immediately dissolve an amphoteric white precipitate, so the solution could contain **Zn²⁺, Pb²⁺, or Al³⁺** (or Ca²⁺ only if no precipitate formed yet, though Ca²⁺ gives a slight white ppt in excess NaOH). Copper (**Cu²⁺**) cannot be present because a Cu²⁺ solution is blue (not colorless) and its pale blue Cu(OH)₂ precipitate **does not dissolve** in excess NaOH. The student should start with a fresh portion and add **just 2–3 drops of NaOH first**, observe whether a white precipitate forms, and only then add excess NaOH.\n9. Pale green solution: **Fe²⁺**, net ionic equation **Fe²⁺ + 2OH⁻ → Fe(OH)₂↓** (dirty green precipitate). Yellow-brown solution: **Fe³⁺**, net ionic equation **Fe³⁺ + 3OH⁻ → Fe(OH)₃↓** (reddish-brown precipitate). The pale green **Fe²⁺** solution (and its dirty green precipitate) slowly turns brown on standing in air as oxygen oxidizes Fe²⁺ to Fe³⁺. Check: both equations balance atoms and charge ✅.\n10. Add a few drops of NaOH to all three: all three form white precipitates (slight white for Ca²⁺). Now add **excess NaOH**: the precipitates in the Zn²⁺ and Al³⁺ tubes dissolve, whereas the precipitate in the **Ca²⁺** tube stays undissolved—identifying **Ca²⁺**. Next, take fresh portions of the remaining two solutions and add **excess NH₄OH**: the precipitate that dissolves is **Zn²⁺**, and the precipitate that stays undissolved is **Al³⁺**. Flowchart:\n\n```\n   Few drops of NaOH  --->  white precipitate in all three (slight in Ca²⁺)\n          :\n          v\n   Excess NaOH        --->  dissolves     : Zn²⁺ or Al³⁺\n          :                 stays intact  : Ca²⁺\n          v\n   Excess NH₄OH       --->  dissolves     : Zn²⁺\n                            stays intact  : Al³⁺\n```\n\n   Check: all three ions are cleanly separated ✅.\n11. (a) **False** ❌: Ca²⁺, Zn²⁺, Pb²⁺, and Al³⁺ all form white precipitates with NaOH; you must test with excess NaOH and excess NH₄OH to identify Zn²⁺. (b) **True** ✅: Na⁺ remains unchanged in solution on both sides. (c) **False** ❌: Cu(OH)₂ is an insoluble solid precipitate, so its state symbol is **`(s)`**, not `(aq)`. (d) **True** ✅: both Cu(OH)₂ (forming a deep inky-blue solution) and Zn(OH)₂ (forming a colorless solution) dissolve in excess NH₄OH.\n12. **Pb²⁺:** white precipitate; dissolves in excess NaOH (colorless); insoluble in excess NH₄OH. **Cu²⁺:** pale blue precipitate; insoluble in excess NaOH; dissolves in excess NH₄OH (deep inky-blue solution). **Fe²⁺:** dirty green precipitate; insoluble in excess NaOH; insoluble in excess NH₄OH. **Ca²⁺:** slight white precipitate; insoluble in excess NaOH; no precipitate (no change) with NH₄OH.\n13. Because Fe²⁺ has a **+2** charge and NO₃⁻ has a **−1** charge, two NO₃⁻ ions are needed to balance one Fe²⁺ ion, giving the correct formula **Fe(NO₃)₂**. The student's formula `FeNO₃` has a net charge of (+2) + (−1) = +1, so its charges are not balanced! Parentheses around `NO₃` are required to show two complete nitrate groups. Check: Fe 1, N 2, O 6; charge +2 + 2(−1) = 0 ✅.",
            "bodyHinglish": "(Sab sawaal paper par hal karne ke liye hain. Atomic masses: H 1, N 14, O 16, Na 23, Al 27, Fe 56.)\n\n1. Solution A is yellow-brown. With a few drops of NaOH it gives a reddish-brown precipitate that does not dissolve in excess NaOH. With a few drops of NH₄OH it gives a reddish-brown precipitate that does not dissolve in excess NH₄OH. Name the cation and write the ionic equation for the precipitate.\n2. Solution B is colourless. With a few drops of NaOH it gives a white precipitate that dissolves in excess NaOH. With a few drops of NH₄OH it gives a white precipitate that also dissolves in excess NH₄OH. Which of the seven ions is present? Write the equation for the dissolving of the precipitate in NaOH.\n3. A teacher uses 4.26 g of Al(NO₃)₃ in solution and adds exactly the right amount of NaOH. Write the balanced equation, and find the mass of Al(OH)₃ precipitate and the mass of NaNO₃ formed. Check that mass is conserved.\n4. How many grams of Fe(NO₃)₂ are needed to give 4.5 g of Fe(OH)₂ precipitate with NaOH? Write the equation first, then find the mass of NaOH that reacts.\n5. Solution C gives a slight white precipitate with a few drops of NaOH, which stays unchanged in excess NaOH. NH₄OH gives no precipitate. Separately, the silver nitrate test (after dilute HNO₃) and the barium chloride test (after dilute HCl) give no precipitate, and dilute acid gives no gas. The bottle is known to hold a chloride, a sulphate, a carbonate or a nitrate. Name the cation and the anion, and write the formula of the salt.\n6. Solid D, with warm NaOH, gives a pungent gas that turns moist red litmus blue. A solution of D, with dilute HNO₃ and then silver nitrate, gives a white curdy precipitate that dissolves in NH₄OH. Name the two ions in D, and write the ionic equation for the gas-forming test.\n7. Two colourless solutions E and F both give a white precipitate with a few drops of NaOH. In excess NaOH, E's precipitate dissolves but F's does not. With NH₄OH, E gives a white precipitate that stays in excess NH₄OH, while F gives no precipitate. It is known that neither solution contains aluminium. Name each ion and explain your logic.\n8. A student adds a large dropper-full of NaOH to a colourless solution and sees only a clear solution. Which of the seven ions could be present? Why can copper not be one of them? What should the student do differently?\n9. One bottle contains a pale green solution and another a yellow-brown solution. Both give precipitates with NaOH that do not dissolve in excess. Name the ions, write the two ionic equations, and say which solution may slowly change colour on standing in air.\n10. Draw a flowchart, using only NaOH and NH₄OH, to separate Zn²⁺, Al³⁺ and Ca²⁺ in three colourless solutions.\n11. State True or False and correct the false ones: (a) A white precipitate with NaOH proves the ion is Zn²⁺. (b) In the net ionic equation for Fe²⁺ with NaOH, Na⁺ is a spectator ion. (c) Cu(OH)₂ is written with (aq) because it forms in a solution. (d) Excess NH₄OH dissolves the precipitates of both Cu²⁺ and Zn²⁺.\n12. Complete this table from memory for Pb²⁺, Cu²⁺, Fe²⁺ and Ca²⁺: colour of precipitate with NaOH drops, effect of excess NaOH, effect of excess NH₄OH.\n13. A student writes the formula of the salt of Fe²⁺ with the nitrate ion as FeNO₃. Use charges to write the correct formula and say what is wrong with the student's formula.\n\n### Answers\n\n1. Peele-bhure ghol aur laal-bhura dhodha jo dono excess mein nahi ghula: **Fe³⁺**. Ionic equation: Fe³⁺ + 3OH⁻ → Fe(OH)₃↓. Jaanch: Fe 1, O 3, H 3, charge (+3) + 3(−1) = 0 ✅.\n2. Rangheen ghol, safed dhodha jo NaOH aur NH₄OH dono ke excess mein ghula: shared table mein sirf **Zn²⁺** aisa karta hai (Pb²⁺ aur Al³⁺ NH₄OH ke excess mein nahi ghulte). Equation: Zn(OH)₂ + 2NaOH → Na₂ZnO₂ + 2H₂O. Jaanch: Zn 1, Na 2, O 4 = 2 + 2, H 4 = 4 ✅.\n3. Equation: Al(NO₃)₃ + 3NaOH → Al(OH)₃↓ + 3NaNO₃. Molar mass: Al(NO₃)₃ = 27 + 3 × 62 = 213, Al(OH)₃ = 27 + 3 × 17 = 78, NaNO₃ = 85. Mole = 4.26 ÷ 213 = **0.02 mol**. Al(OH)₃ = 0.02 × 78 = **1.56 g**. NaNO₃ = 0.06 × 85 = **5.1 g**. NaOH = 0.06 × 40 = 2.4 g. Jaanch: 4.26 + 2.4 = 6.66 g, 1.56 + 5.1 = 6.66 g ✅. Atom ginti: Al 1 = 1, N 3 = 3, O 9 + 3 = 12 = 3 + 9, H 3 = 3, Na 3 = 3 ✅.\n4. Equation: Fe(NO₃)₂ + 2NaOH → Fe(OH)₂↓ + 2NaNO₃. Fe(OH)₂ = 56 + 34 = 90. 4.5 ÷ 90 = **0.05 mol**. 1 mol Fe(NO₃)₂ se 1 mol Fe(OH)₂, to 0.05 mol Fe(NO₃)₂. Fe(NO₃)₂ = 56 + 124 = 180, to **0.05 × 180 = 9.0 g**. NaOH: 2 × 0.05 = 0.1 mol, 0.1 × 40 = **4.0 g**. Jaanch: NaNO₃ = 0.1 × 85 = 8.5 g. Left: 9.0 + 4.0 = 13.0 g, right: 4.5 + 8.5 = 13.0 g ✅.\n5. NaOH se halka safed dhodha jo excess mein nahi ghula aur NH₄OH se dhodha nahi: cation **Ca²⁺**. Anion: AgNO₃ se kuch nahi (Cl⁻ nahi), BaCl₂ se kuch nahi (SO₄²⁻ nahi), acid se gas nahi (CO₃²⁻ nahi); diye gaye chaar mein se bacha **NO₃⁻** (iska apna test aage ki class mein). Formula: Ca²⁺ aur 2 NO₃⁻, to **Ca(NO₃)₂**. Jaanch: (+2) + 2 × (−1) = 0 ✅.\n6. Garam NaOH se tez gandh, gili laal litmus neela: **NH₄⁺**. AgNO₃ se safed curdy dhodha jo NH₄OH mein ghula: **Cl⁻**. Equation: NH₄⁺ + OH⁻ → NH₃ + H₂O. Jaanch: N 1 = 1, H 4 + 1 = 5 = 3 + 2, O 1 = 1, charge (+1) + (−1) = 0 = 0 ✅.\n7. **E:** NaOH ke excess mein ghula, to Zn²⁺, Pb²⁺ ya Al³⁺. NH₄OH ke excess mein nahi ghula, to Zn²⁺ nahi. Aluminium nahi (diya hua), to **E = Pb²⁺** (lead zehreela: sirf teacher demo). **F:** safed dhodha jo excess NaOH mein nahi ghula aur NH₄OH se dhodha nahi: **F = Ca²⁺**. Jaanch: E ke natije table ki Pb²⁺ row se, F ke Ca²⁺ row se milte hain ✅.\n8. Ek saath dher NaOH daalne se dhodha ghul gaya ho sakta hai, to wo ion jo excess NaOH mein ghulte hain: **Zn²⁺, Pb²⁺, Al³⁺**. Cu²⁺ ka dhodha excess NaOH mein **nahi ghulta**, to wo saaf ghol nahi rehta (aur Cu²⁺ ka ghol neela hota). Student ko **kuch boondein** daalni chahiye, dhodha dekhna chahiye, phir excess.\n9. Halka hara ghol: **Fe²⁺**, equation Fe²⁺ + 2OH⁻ → Fe(OH)₂↓. Peela-bhura: **Fe³⁺**, equation Fe³⁺ + 3OH⁻ → Fe(OH)₃↓. Hara wala (Fe²⁺) khule mein dheere-dheere bhura hone lagta hai kyunki hawa use Fe³⁺ mein badalti hai. Jaanch: Fe(OH)₂: Fe 1, O 2, H 2, charge 0 ✅; Fe(OH)₃: Fe 1, O 3, H 3, charge 0 ✅.\n10. Teeno mein NaOH ki boondein: Zn²⁺ aur Al³⁺ mein safed dhodha, Ca²⁺ mein halka safed. Excess NaOH: Zn²⁺ aur Al³⁺ ghulte hain, Ca²⁺ nahi: Ca²⁺ alag. Ab Zn²⁺ aur Al³⁺ ke naye hisse mein NH₄OH excess tak: ghule to Zn²⁺, na ghule to Al³⁺. Flowchart:\n\n```\n   NaOH ki boondein  --->  teeno mein safed dhodha (Ca²⁺ mein halka)\n          :\n          v\n   Excess NaOH       --->  ghula : Zn²⁺ ya Al³⁺\n          :                nahi ghula : Ca²⁺\n          v\n   NH₄OH excess      --->  ghula : Zn²⁺\n                           nahi ghula : Al³⁺\n```\n\n   Jaanch: teeno ions alag-alag pehchane gaye ✅.\n11. (a) **False** ❌: safed dhodha Ca²⁺, Zn²⁺, Pb²⁺, Al³⁺ sab dete hain; excess aur NH₄OH ke test se hi pata chalta hai. (b) **True** ✅: Na⁺ dono taraf ek jaisa, badalta nahi. (c) **False** ❌: Cu(OH)₂ dhodha hai, to (s). (d) **True** ✅: dono ke dhodhe excess NH₄OH mein ghulte hain (Cu: gehra neela, Zn: rangheen).\n12. Pb²⁺: safed dhodha, excess NaOH mein ghulta, excess NH₄OH mein nahi. Cu²⁺: halka neela dhodha, excess NaOH mein nahi ghulta, excess NH₄OH mein ghulta (gehra neela). Fe²⁺: ganda hara dhodha, excess NaOH mein nahi ghulta, excess NH₄OH mein nahi ghulta. Ca²⁺: halka safed dhodha, excess NaOH mein nahi ghulta, excess NH₄OH mein koi badlaav nahi.\n13. Fe²⁺ ka charge +2 aur NO₃⁻ ka −1: do NO₃⁻ chahiye, to **Fe(NO₃)₂**. FeNO₃ mein charge (+2) + (−1) = +1, neutral nahi, isliye galat. Bracket zaroori hai kyunki 2 poore NO₃ jhund chahiye. Jaanch: Fe 1, N 2, O 6; charge +2 + 2(−1) = 0 ✅."
          },
          {
            "heading": "Chapter Summary: Analytical Chemistry",
            "headingHinglish": "Chapter ka saar: Analytical Chemistry",
            "body": "| Topic | What We Learned | One-Line Takeaway |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Chemical tests, precipitates, tracking ions, and spectator ions | For every test, ask three questions: What was added? What was seen? What does it mean? |\n| 2 | Testing 7 cations with NaOH (drops and excess) | The white precipitates of Zn²⁺, Pb²⁺, and Al³⁺ dissolve in excess NaOH (amphoteric hydroxides) |\n| 3 | Testing the same 7 cations with NH₄OH (drops and excess) | In excess NH₄OH, only Cu²⁺ (deep inky blue) and Zn²⁺ (colorless) precipitates dissolve |\n| 4 | Testing anions (Cl⁻, SO₄²⁻, CO₃²⁻) and ammonium (NH₄⁺) | Acidify before adding AgNO₃ or BaCl₂ so carbonate cannot give a false precipitate |\n| 5 | Complete plan for identifying an unknown salt | Follow the 5-step order: inspect color → dissolve → test cation → test anion → write formula |\n| 6 | 4-step master method, mixed puzzles, stoichiometry, and 4 lab pitfalls | Add reagent drop by drop first, record which alkali is in excess, cancel spectators, and label precipitates `(s)` |\n\n---\n\nEthan's team took first place on Mystery Box Game Day! As Mr. Grant bundled the Clue Cards back together with a rubber band, he smiled at Ethan: \"See how every mystery unravels once you follow the right sequence? Look, cation, anion, formula.\" Ethan tucked the prize ribbon into his notebook and grinned. \"Next year, Uncle Grant, I'm designing the Mystery Boxes myself!\"\n\n**What's next?** In Chapter 9 (Study of Compounds), we will explore four powerhouse industrial chemicals—hydrogen chloride (HCl), ammonia (NH₃), nitric acid (HNO₃), and sulfuric acid (H₂SO₄)—seeing how they are manufactured on a massive scale and why their chemical properties make them so reactive and useful (all explored safely through study and teacher demonstrations).",
            "bodyHinglish": "| Topic | Kya seekha | Ek line |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Test, precipitate, ions ka hisaab, spectator ions | Har test mein teen sawaal: kya daala, kya dikha, matlab kya |\n| 2 | NaOH ke saath 7 cations ka rang aur excess mein behaviour | Zn²⁺, Pb²⁺, Al³⁺ ke dhodhe excess NaOH mein ghulte hain (amphoteric) |\n| 3 | NH₄OH ke saath wahi 7 cations, deep blue aur rangheen ghol | NH₄OH excess mein sirf Cu²⁺ (gehra neela) aur Zn²⁺ (rangheen) ghulte hain |\n| 4 | Anion aur ammonium ke test: Cl⁻, SO₄²⁻, CO₃²⁻, NH₄⁺ | Acid pehle, phir AgNO₃ ya BaCl₂; carbonate ko pehle hataana zaroori hai |\n| 5 | Anjaan namak ki khoj: sab tests saath | Rang dekho, ghulao, cation, anion, formula: dhyan se kram |\n| 6 | Master method, mile-jule puzzles, mass ka numerical, 4 aam galtiyan | Boond-boond daalo, reagent ka naam likho, spectator kaato, (s) likho |\n\n---\n\nNachiket ki team ne Mystery Box game day mein pehla inaam jeeta. Mr. Gunjal ne dher saare cards ko wapas ek rubber band mein bandhte hue kaha, \"Dekha, kram pakad liya to koi bhi bhool-bhulaiya sulajh jaati hai: dekho, cation, anion, likho.\" Nachiket ne cards ka dibba uthaya aur bola, \"Chacha, agle saal main apna Mystery Box khud banaunga!\"\n\n**Aage kya?** Ab agle Chapter 9 (Study of Compounds) mein hum dekhenge ki HCl, ammonia, nitric acid aur sulphuric acid jaise chemicals, jo factories lakhon tonne mein banati hain, kaise banaye jaate hain aur ye itne tez gandh wale, jalane wale aur daag chhodne wale kyun hote hain (sab kuch sirf padhne aur dekhne ke liye, student kabhi nahi banata)."
          }
