            "correctIndex": 3,
            "explanation": "Pitfall to avoid (\"Squeezing in a huge squirt of NaOH all at once, and missing the precipitate stage completely!\"): Demo: Suppose a student tests a Zn²⁺ or Al³⁺ solution by dumping a full dropper of NaOH into the test tube all at once. Because NaOH is immediately in excess, any white Zn(OH)₂ or Al(OH)₃ precipitate that forms dissolves almost instantaneously, leaving a clear, colorless solution! The student then writes down *\"No precipitate formed—no metal ion present,\"* completely missing Zn²⁺, Pb²⁺, or Al³⁺! Wrong technique Correct technique (dumping excess NaOH all at once) (a few drops first, then excess) solution ---> clear colorless liquid few drops ---> white precipitate seen! (precipitate stage missed completely!) excess ---> precipitate dissolves Result: Zn²⁺, Pb²⁺, Al³⁺ missed! Result: amphoteric ion spotted cleanly! > Figure 6.7 – Adding a large squirt of NaOH all at once skips right past the initial precipitate stage for Zn²⁺, Pb²⁺, and Al³⁺. Always add just 2–3 drops first, observe the precipitate, and only then add excess!",
