          {
            "heading": "Part A: HCl Gas and Hydrochloric Acid: Two Forms of One Substance",
            "headingHinglish": "Part A: HCl gas aur hydrochloric acid: ek cheez ke do roop",
            "body": "**Hydrogen chloride** is a gas whose molecule consists of one H atom and one Cl atom joined by a covalent bond: H–Cl. When this gas dissolves in water, it forms **hydrochloric acid**. In Class 9, we saw the synthesis H₂ + Cl₂ → 2HCl ([hydrogen-part4.md](../../../class9/chemistry/hydrogen/hydrogen-part4.md): \"dissolves in water to form hydrochloric acid\"). Let's examine both forms side by side.\n\n**Properties of the gas (for reading only):**\n- **Colourless**, with a **sharp, choking (pungent)** odour (even a teacher never sniffs it directly).\n- **Denser than air:** its vapour density is 18.25, compared to about 14.5 for air (Chapter 5, Topic 2).\n- **Extremely soluble in water:** about 450 litres of HCl gas can dissolve in just 1 litre of water.\n- **Misty fumes in moist air:** when HCl gas escapes into open air, it dissolves in atmospheric moisture to form microscopic droplets of hydrochloric acid, which appear as a white mist. That mist is not smoke—it is tiny liquid droplets of acid.\n- **Turns moist blue litmus red** (but has no effect on dry litmus—we will see why in Common Pitfalls).\n\n**Properties of the aqueous solution:** As we saw in Chapter 1, hydrochloric acid is a **strong acid**: in water, it ionises almost completely into H₃O⁺ and Cl⁻ ions. Because of these H₃O⁺ ions, it shows all the classic reactions of an acid: releasing H₂ with active metals (Chapter 2), releasing CO₂ with carbonates and bicarbonates (Chapter 1), and forming salt and water with bases. We have already covered those reactions, so we won't repeat them here. Among the metal chlorides formed is **ZnCl₂**, which we need to know by name only.\n\nCommercial **concentrated** hydrochloric acid is about 36 percent HCl by mass and gives off misty white fumes when the bottle is opened.\n\n### Example 1. How much denser is HCl than air?\n\n**Problem:** Calculate the vapour density of HCl and find the ratio of its density to that of air. (H = 1, Cl = 35.5; average molar mass of air = 29.)\n\n**Step 1:** Molar mass of HCl = 1 + 35.5 = **36.5** g/mol.\n**Step 2:** Vapour density = molar mass ÷ 2 = 36.5 ÷ 2 = **18.25**. Vapour density of air = 29 ÷ 2 = **14.5** (both appeared in the VD table in Chapter 5; the next step is new).\n**Step 3:** Ratio of masses of equal volumes at the same T and P = 36.5 ÷ 29 ≈ **1.26**. Thus, HCl gas is about 1.26 times as dense as air.\n\n**Check:** 18.25 ÷ 14.5 = 1.26 as well ✅ (both routes give the exact same answer).",
            "bodyHinglish": "**Hydrogen chloride** ek gas hai jiske molecule mein ek H aur ek Cl covalent bond se jude hain: H–Cl. Jab ye gas paani mein ghulti hai to **hydrochloric acid** banta hai. Class 9 mein H₂ + Cl₂ → 2HCl aaya tha ([hydrogen-part4.md](../../../class9/chemistry/hydrogen/hydrogen-part4.md): \"paani mein ghul kar hydrochloric acid\"). Ab dono roop ek-ek karke dekhte hain.\n\n**Gas roop ke gun (sirf padhne ke liye):**\n- **Bina rang ki**, **tez, dam ghutane wali** gandh (teacher bhi use seedha nahi sunghta).\n- **Hawa se bhaari:** vapour density 18.25, hawa ka lagbhag 14.5 (Chapter 5, Topic 2).\n- **Bahut zyada ghulansheel:** ek litre paani mein lagbhag 450 litre HCl gas ghul jaati hai.\n- **Nami se dhuan-sa:** khuli hawa mein HCl gas hawa ki nami se milkar acid ki chhoti-chhoti boondein bana deti hai, jo safed kohre ki tarah dikhti hain. Wo gas ka dhuan nahi, acid ki boondein hain.\n- **Geele blue litmus ko laal** karti hai (sookhe ko nahi: Aam galtiyan mein dekhenge).\n\n**Ghol roop ke gun:** Chapter 1 mein dekha tha ki HCl ek **strong acid** hai: paani mein lagbhag poori tarah H₃O⁺ aur Cl⁻ mein tut jaati hai. Isliye wo saare acid wale kaam karti hai: metals ke saath H₂ (Chapter 2), carbonates ke saath CO₂ (Chapter 1), bases ke saath namak aur paani. Ye pehle ho chuka hai, yahan dobara nahi. Metals ke saath bani chlorides mein ek **ZnCl₂** hai: iska hum sirf naam yaad rakhte hain.\n\nBazaar ka **concentrated** hydrochloric acid lagbhag 36 percent HCl ka ghol hota hai aur khuli botal mein dhuan-sa chhodta hai.\n\n### Example 1. HCl hawa se kitni bhaari?\n\n**Sawaal:** HCl ka vapour density nikalo aur hawa se uska anupaat batao. (H 1, Cl 35.5; hawa ka average molar mass 29.)\n\n**Kadam 1:** HCl ka molar mass = 1 + 35.5 = **36.5** g/mol.\n**Kadam 2:** Vapour density = molar mass ÷ 2 = 36.5 ÷ 2 = **18.25**. Hawa ka = 29 ÷ 2 = **14.5** (dono Chapter 5 ki VD table mein the; naya kadam agla hai).\n**Kadam 3:** Barabar T aur P par barabar volume ke vazan ka anupaat = 36.5 ÷ 29 = lagbhag **1.26**. HCl hawa se lagbhag 1.26 guna bhaari hai.\n\n**Jaanch:** 18.25 ÷ 14.5 = 1.26 bhi aata hai ✅ (dono raaste ek hi jawab)."
          },
          {
            "heading": "Part B: Preparing HCl Gas in the Lab (For Reading Only)",
            "headingHinglish": "Part B: Lab mein HCl gas kaise banti hai (sirf padhne ke liye)",
            "body": "**Raw materials:** common salt (**NaCl**) and **concentrated H₂SO₄**. Concentrated H₂SO₄ is a **non-volatile acid**: it has a very high boiling point, so it does not boil away when heated. When it is warmed with NaCl, the much more volatile **HCl** escapes as a gas.\n\n**With gentle heating (below about 200 °C):** NaCl + H₂SO₄ (concentrated) → NaHSO₄ + HCl\n\n**At higher temperatures, a second step occurs:** NaHSO₄ + NaCl → Na₂SO₄ + HCl\n\nAdding the two steps gives the **overall reaction:** 2NaCl + H₂SO₄ → Na₂SO₄ + 2HCl\n\nIn the laboratory, gentle heating is normally used so that mainly the first step takes place. (In the Chapter 1 teacher demo, dry HCl gas was also made from NaCl and conc. H₂SO₄, where we wrote the overall equation directly.)\n\n```\n          \\       /     <-- thistle funnel (conc. H₂SO₄ is poured in here)\n           \\     /\n            \\   /\n             : :\n       ===cork===: :===cork===.________________  delivery tube: HCl gas travels\n       :         : :          :                   through here to the next vessel\n       :         : :          :\n      /          : :           \\\n     :           : :            :\n     :   ~~~~~~~~: :~~~~~~~~~   :  <-- NaCl + conc. H₂SO₄ (funnel stem dips below acid level)\n     :      (NaCl + acid)       :\n      \\                        /\n       \\______________________/\n            round-bottom flask\n                  ^\n                  :  gentle heat (burner)\n```\n\n> **Figure 1.1** – HCl generator: solid NaCl sits in the flask while conc. H₂SO₄ is added through the thistle funnel (the bottom tip of the funnel stem stays submerged below the liquid surface so gas cannot escape back up the funnel). A delivery tube passes through the cork to carry the gas forward under gentle heating. Because the delivery tube is open at the far end, the apparatus is never sealed.\n\n```\ndelivery tube       short tube: dry gas exits\nfrom flask          here\n      :      +-------------------------------------+\n      :      :                                     :\n   ===:=cork=:===                                  :            <-- open mouth of jar:\n   :  :         :                                  :                lighter air escapes here\n   :  :         :                       :          :          :\n   :  :         :                       :          :          :\n   :~~:~~~~~~~~~:                       :          : tube goes:\n   :  :         :                       :HCl gas   : to bottom:\n   :  :conc.    :                       :fills jar : of jar   :\n   :  :H₂SO₄    :                       :from the  :          :\n   :  :         :                       :bottom up :          :\n   \\____________/                       :          :          :\n                                        :          :          :\n  wash bottle                           :                     :\n  (for drying the gas)                  :                     :\n                                        \\_____________________/\n                                     gas jar (standing upright)\n```\n\n> **Figure 1.2** – Drying and collecting the gas. The wash bottle holds conc. H₂SO₄: moist gas enters through the **long tube** (submerged in the acid), bubbles through the acid to dry, and exits through the **short tube** (ending just below the cork). From there, the delivery tube reaches the **bottom** of the upright gas jar: since HCl is denser than air, it fills the jar from the bottom up while pushing lighter air out the open mouth. The entire setup stays inside a fume cupboard.\n\n**Why each design choice matters:**\n- **Why dry the gas?** Gas leaving the flask carries traces of moisture. **Concentrated H₂SO₄** absorbs water vapour without reacting with HCl, making it the ideal drying agent in the wash bottle. (In Chapter 1, the teacher also used a guard tube packed with **anhydrous CaCl₂**—since it also does not react with HCl, it works too.)\n- **Why not use dilute H₂SO₄?** Dilute acid contains plenty of water, and because HCl is extraordinarily soluble in water, it would simply stay dissolved instead of escaping as a gas.\n- **Why extend the delivery tube to the bottom of the jar?** So that the heavy HCl gas settles at the bottom and smoothly pushes the lighter air upward and out.\n\n### Example 2. Atom count across the three equations\n\n**Problem:** Verify that all three equations are balanced, and show that the overall reaction is the sum of the two steps.\n\nStep 1: NaCl + H₂SO₄ → NaHSO₄ + HCl. Step 2: NaHSO₄ + NaCl → Na₂SO₄ + HCl.\nAdd both sides: NaCl + H₂SO₄ + NaHSO₄ + NaCl → NaHSO₄ + HCl + Na₂SO₄ + HCl. Since NaHSO₄ appears once on each side as an intermediate, it cancels out, leaving **2NaCl + H₂SO₄ → Na₂SO₄ + 2HCl**.\n\n**Check:** Step 1: Na 1 = 1, Cl 1 = 1, H 2 = 1 + 1, S 1 = 1, O 4 = 4 ✅. Step 2: Na 2 = 2, H 1 = 1, S 1 = 1, O 4 = 4, Cl 1 = 1 ✅. Overall: Na 2 = 2, Cl 2 = 2, H 2 = 2, S 1 = 1, O 4 = 4 ✅.\n\n### Example 3. How much HCl from 58.5 g of NaCl?\n\n**Problem:** In a demonstration, a teacher gently warms 58.5 g of NaCl with the exact stoichiometric amount of conc. H₂SO₄ required for the first step. Find the mass and STP volume of HCl produced, and the mass of H₂SO₄ consumed. (Na = 23, S = 32, O = 16.)\n\n**Step 1 (moles):** Molar mass of NaCl = 23 + 35.5 = 58.5 g/mol, so moles = 58.5 ÷ 58.5 = **1 mol**.\n**Step 2 (mole ratio):** From NaCl : H₂SO₄ : HCl = 1 : 1 : 1, we get HCl = **1 mol** and H₂SO₄ = **1 mol**.\n**Step 3 (mass):** Mass of HCl = 1 × 36.5 = **36.5 g**. Molar mass of H₂SO₄ = 2 + 32 + 64 = 98 g/mol, so mass = **98 g**.\n**Step 4 (volume):** At STP, 1 mol of any gas occupies 22.4 L, so volume of HCl = **22.4 L** (Chapter 5, Topic 5).\n\n**Check:** Conservation of mass: reactants = 58.5 + 98 = 156.5 g; products = NaHSO₄ (23 + 1 + 32 + 64 = 120 g) + HCl (36.5 g) = 156.5 g ✅.",
            "bodyHinglish": "**Kachcha maal:** namak **NaCl** aur **concentrated H₂SO₄**. Concentrated H₂SO₄ **non-volatile** hai: uska ubal-bindu bahut ooncha hai, to garam hone par bhi wo gas ban kar nahi udta. Isliye jab wo NaCl se milta hai to jo gas ban kar nikalti hai wo **HCl** hi hai.\n\n**Halki garmi par (lagbhag 200 °C se neeche):** NaCl + H₂SO₄ (concentrated) → NaHSO₄ + HCl\n\n**Zyada garmi par doosra kadam:** NaHSO₄ + NaCl → Na₂SO₄ + HCl\n\nDono kadam jodo to **kul reaction:** 2NaCl + H₂SO₄ → Na₂SO₄ + 2HCl\n\nLab mein aam taur par halki garmi mein pehla kadam hi chalta hai. Chapter 1 ke teacher demo mein bhi sookhi HCl gas isi tarah NaCl aur conc. H₂SO₄ se bani thi (wahan seedha kul equation likhi thi).\n\n```\n          \\       /     <-- thistle funnel (conc. H₂SO₄ isse daalte hain)\n           \\     /\n            \\   /\n             : :\n       ===cork===: :===cork===.________________  delivery tube: HCl gas yahan se\n       :         : :          :                   agle bartan ki taraf jaati hai\n       :         : :          :\n      /          : :           \\\n     :           : :            :\n     :   ~~~~~~~~: :~~~~~~~~~   :  <-- NaCl + conc. H₂SO₄ (funnel ki nali acid ke andar)\n     :      (NaCl + acid)       :\n      \\                        /\n       \\______________________/\n            round-bottom flask\n                  ^\n                  :  halki aanch (burner)\n```\n\n> **Figure 1.1** – HCl ka generator: flask mein thos NaCl hai; thistle funnel se conc. H₂SO₄ daalte hain (funnel ki nali ka neeche ka sira acid ke andar dooba rehta hai, taaki gas funnel se bahar na nikle). Cork mein se delivery tube nikalti hai jo agle bartan tak jaati hai. Halki aanch lagti hai. Delivery tube aage khuli hai, isliye apparatus band nahi hai.\n\n```\nflask se aayi       chhoti nali: sukhi gas bahar\ndelivery tube       jaati hai\n      :      +-------------------------------------+\n      :      :                                     :\n   ===:=cork=:===                                  :            <-- jar ka muh khula:\n   :  :         :                                  :                hawa yahan se nikalti hai\n   :  :         :                       :          :          :\n   :  :         :                       :          :          :\n   :~~:~~~~~~~~~:                       :          : nali jar :\n   :  :         :                       :HCl gas   : ke tale  :\n   :  :conc.    :                       :jar mein  : tak      :\n   :  :H₂SO₄    :                       :neeche se :          :\n   :  :         :                       :bharti hai:          :\n   \\____________/                       :          :          :\n                                        :          :          :\n  wash bottle                           :                     :\n  (gas sukhaane ke liye)                :                     :\n                                        \\_____________________/\n                                     gas jar (seedha khada)\n```\n\n> **Figure 1.2** – Gas ko sukhana aur ikattha karna. Wash bottle mein conc. H₂SO₄ hai: **lambi nali** (acid ke andar dooba sira) se geeli gas andar aati hai aur acid ke bulbulon se guzarkar sookhti hai; **chhoti nali** (cork ke bas neeche) se sukhi gas bahar jaati hai. Wahan se nali jar ke **tale tak** jaati hai: HCl bhaari hai, to jar mein neeche se bharti hai aur halki hawa upar ke khule muh se nikal jaati hai. Poora set-up fume cupboard mein.\n\n**Har cheez kyun?**\n- **Sukhana kyun?** Flask se nikalne wali gas mein nami hoti hai. **Concentrated H₂SO₄** paani ko kheench leta hai aur HCl se koi reaction nahi karta: isliye wash bottle mein wahi lete hain. (Chapter 1 mein teacher ne **anhydrous CaCl₂** wali guard tube se bhi sukhaya tha: wo bhi HCl ke saath reaction nahi karta, to wo bhi chalta hai.)\n- **Dilute H₂SO₄ kyun nahi?** Usme paani hota hai, aur HCl itni ghulansheel hai ki turant ghul jaati, gas nikalti hi nahi.\n- **Delivery tube jar ke tale tak kyun?** Taaki bhaari HCl neeche jam kar halki hawa ko upar se dhakel de.\n\n### Example 2. Teen equations ka atom count\n\n**Sawaal:** Check karo ki teeno equations balanced hain, aur dikhao ki kul reaction dono kadamon ka jod hai.\n\nKadam 1: NaCl + H₂SO₄ → NaHSO₄ + HCl. Kadam 2: NaHSO₄ + NaCl → Na₂SO₄ + HCl.\nDono ko jodo: NaCl + H₂SO₄ + NaHSO₄ + NaCl → NaHSO₄ + HCl + Na₂SO₄ + HCl. Dono taraf NaHSO₄ ek-ek baar hai, wo kat jaata hai (beech ka padarth). Bachta hai **2NaCl + H₂SO₄ → Na₂SO₄ + 2HCl**.\n\n**Jaanch:** Kadam 1: Na 1 = 1, Cl 1 = 1, H 2 = 1 + 1, S 1 = 1, O 4 = 4 ✅. Kadam 2: Na 2 = 2, H 1 = 1, S 1 = 1, O 4 = 4, Cl 1 = 1 ✅. Kul: Na 2 = 2, Cl 2 = 2, H 2 = 2, S 1 = 1, O 4 = 4 ✅.\n\n### Example 3. 58.5 g NaCl se kitni HCl?\n\n**Sawaal:** Teacher ne 58.5 g NaCl par conc. H₂SO₄ ki theek utni matra daali jo pehle kadam ke liye chahiye (halki garmi). Kitni HCl banegi (mass aur STP par volume) aur kitna H₂SO₄ lagega? (Na 23, S 32, O 16.)\n\n**Kadam 1 (moles):** NaCl = 23 + 35.5 = 58.5 g/mol, to moles = 58.5 ÷ 58.5 = **1 mol**.\n**Kadam 2 (ratio):** NaCl : H₂SO₄ : HCl = 1 : 1 : 1, to HCl = **1 mol**, H₂SO₄ = **1 mol**.\n**Kadam 3 (mass):** HCl = 1 × 36.5 = **36.5 g**. H₂SO₄ = 2 + 32 + 64 = 98, to **98 g**.\n**Kadam 4 (volume):** STP par 1 mol = 22.4 L, to HCl = **22.4 L** (Chapter 5, Topic 5).\n\n**Jaanch:** mass sangrakshan: reactants 58.5 + 98 = 156.5 g; products NaHSO₄ (23 + 1 + 32 + 64 = 120 g) + HCl 36.5 g = 156.5 g ✅."
          },
          {
            "heading": "Part C: How to Collect and Dissolve It",
            "headingHinglish": "Part C: Kaise ikattha karein aur kaise ghulayein",
            "body": "The method used to collect any gas in the lab depends on two physical properties: **how dense** it is compared to air, and **how soluble** it is in water.\n\n| Property of the Gas | Collection Method | Applies to HCl? |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Denser** than air | Upward displacement of air: upright jar, delivery tube to the bottom | **Yes**: HCl is about 1.26 times as dense as air |\n| **Lighter** than air | Downward displacement of air: inverted jar, delivery tube to the top | No |\n| **Very slightly** soluble in water | Over water (jar inverted in a water trough) | **No**: HCl is extremely soluble in water |\n\nIf you tried to collect HCl over water, the gas would dissolve into the water almost instantly and the jar would never fill. That is why HCl is always collected by **upward displacement of air**, and never over water.\n\n### Making Hydrochloric Acid: The Inverted Funnel Arrangement\n\nTo dissolve HCl gas in water and make hydrochloric acid, you must never dip a bare delivery tube straight into water. Because HCl dissolves so fast, the pressure inside the tube drops suddenly and water gets sucked backward up the tube into the hot generating flask (**suck-back**), which can crack the hot glass. To prevent this, an **inverted funnel** is attached to the end of the delivery tube:\n\n```\n               :   :   <-- HCl gas arrives through rubber tubing\n               :   :\n              _:___:_\n              \\     /\n               \\   /         <-- inverted funnel: narrow stem on top, wide rim below\n                \\ /\n   ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~  <-- water surface: the funnel rim just touches the water\n   :                            :\n   :     water (in beaker)      :\n   :    HCl dissolves here      :\n   :____________________________:\n              beaker\n```\n\n> **Figure 1.3** – Dissolving HCl in water: the wide rim of the inverted funnel is positioned so that it just touches the surface of the water. If water begins to rise inside the funnel, the water level in the beaker drops slightly, leaving an air gap below the rim. Air enters the funnel and the water falls right back down, completely preventing suck-back.",
            "bodyHinglish": "Gas ikattha karne ka tareeka gas ke do gun se tay hota hai: wo hawa se **kitni bhaari** hai aur paani mein **kitni ghulansheel** hai.\n\n| Gas ka gun | Collection ka tareeka | HCl par lagu? |\n|---|---|---|\n| Hawa se **bhaari** | Upward displacement of air: jar seedha khada, nali jar ke tale tak | **Haan**: HCl hawa se lagbhag 1.26 guna bhaari |\n| Hawa se **halki** | Downward displacement of air: jar ulta, nali upar tak | Nahi |\n| Paani mein **bahut kam** ghulansheel | Paani ke upar (jar paani mein ulta) | **Nahi**: HCl bahut ghulansheel hai |\n\nHCl ko paani ke upar ikattha karne ki koshish karte to wo paani mein ghul jaati aur jar kabhi nahi bharta. Isliye HCl hamesha **hawa ke upward displacement** se, paani ke upar kabhi nahi.\n\n### Hydrochloric acid banana: ulta funnel\n\nHCl ka ghol banane ke liye nali ko seedha paani mein nahi dubote, kyunki gas itni tezi se ghulti hai ki paani nali mein ulta khinch kar garam flask tak pahunch sakta hai (**suck-back**). Isliye ek **ulta funnel** lagate hain:\n\n```\n               :   :   <-- rubber tube se HCl gas aati hai\n               :   :\n              _:___:_\n              \\     /\n               \\   /         <-- ulta funnel: patli nali upar, chauda mooh neeche\n                \\ /\n   ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~  <-- paani ki satah: funnel ka kinara bas paani ko chhoota hai\n   :                            :\n   :     paani (beaker mein)    :\n   :    HCl yahan ghulti hai    :\n   :____________________________:\n              beaker\n```\n\n> **Figure 1.3** – HCl ko paani mein ghulana: ulta funnel ka chauda kinara paani ki satah ko bas chhoota hai. Funnel ke andar paani thoda upar chadh sakta hai, par tab beaker ki satah neeche utarti hai, funnel ka kinara paani se bahar aa jaata hai, hawa andar aa jaati hai aur paani wapas gir jaata hai: suck-back nahi hota."
          },
          {
            "heading": "Part D: The Fountain Experiment (Teacher Demo Only)",
            "headingHinglish": "Part D: Fountain experiment (sirf dekhne ke liye, teacher demo)",
            "body": "A classic teacher demonstration vividly proves just how soluble HCl gas is in water. The teacher fills a **completely dry**, thick-walled round-bottom flask with dry HCl gas (behind a safety screen) and clamps it upside down. Its rubber stopper carries two tubes: a long glass tube ending in a fine jet near the top inside the flask and dipping into a beaker of blue litmus solution below, and a short dropper filled with a little water.\n\n```\n            _____________\n          /               \\\n         :          ^      :    <-- jet: upper tip of the long tube (near top of flask)\n         :          :      :        where the fountain sprays out\n         : dry      :      :\n         : HCl gas  :      :\n          \\         :     /\n            \\_      :   _/\n              :     : :\n              : :   : :         <-- inverted flask: bulb on top, neck pointing down\n              : :   : :\n          ======:===:======     <-- stopper (===) with two holes\n                :   :\n              (     )\n              (water)           <-- dropper: squeezing injects a few drops of water\n              (     )\n             rubber bulb\n                    :\n      ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~:~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~\n      :             :     <-- lower end of long tube submerged in solution\n      :             :                         :\n      :   water + blue litmus                 :\n      :                                       :\n      :                                       :\n      :_______________________________________:\n                      beaker\n```\n\n> **Figure 1.4** – The HCl fountain demonstration. The inverted flask holds dry HCl gas. When the teacher squeezes the dropper, a few drops of water enter the flask and dissolve a large volume of HCl gas almost instantly. The pressure inside the flask plummets, so external atmospheric pressure pushes the blue litmus solution up the long tube, spraying into the flask as a dramatic **red** fountain.\n\n**What you observe:** the moment the dropper is squeezed, liquid rushes up the long tube and bursts out of the jet as a fountain, turning **blue litmus red** because aqueous HCl is an acid. The flask must be **completely dry** at the start—if it were damp, the gas would dissolve prematurely before the demonstration even began, and no sharp pressure drop would occur.\n\n### Example 4. How little water is enough?\n\n**Problem:** A dry 500 mL flask is filled with HCl gas. Assuming 1 mL of water can dissolve 450 mL of HCl gas, estimate the minimum volume of water needed to dissolve all the gas in the flask.\n\n**Step 1:** 450 mL of gas requires 1 mL of water.\n**Step 2:** Water needed for 500 mL of gas = 500 ÷ 450 ≈ **1.1 mL**.\n**Step 3:** That means just a squeeze of the dropper (about 1 mL) is enough to absorb nearly the entire flask of gas, causing an immediate drop in pressure.\n\n**Check:** 1.1 × 450 = 495 mL, which is right around 500 mL ✅.",
            "bodyHinglish": "Ek mazedaar demo se pata chalta hai ki HCl kitni zyada ghulansheel hai. Teacher ek **bilkul sookha** round-bottom flask HCl gas se bharta hai (mote kaanch ka, safety screen ke peeche). Flask ko ulta karke clamp mein lagate hain: gardan neeche, cork mein do nali. Ek lambi nali ka sira flask ke andar upar tak hai aur doosra sira beaker ke neele litmus wale paani mein. Doosri nali ke saath ek chhota dropper hai jisme thoda paani bhara hai.\n\n```\n            _____________\n          /               \\\n         :          ^      :    <-- jet: lambi nali ka upar ka sira (flask ke top ke paas)\n         :          :      :        yahin se paani ka fuwwara phootta hai\n         : sukhi    :      :\n         : HCl gas  :      :\n          \\         :     /\n            \\_      :   _/\n              :     : :\n              : :   : :         <-- flask ulta lagaya hai: bulb upar, gardan neeche\n              : :   : :\n          ======:===:======     <-- cork (===): isme se do nali guzarti hain\n                :   :\n              (     )\n              (paani)           <-- dropper: dabane par kuch boondein paani flask mein\n              (     )\n             rubber bulb\n                    :\n      ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~:~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~\n      :             :     <-- lambi nali ka neeche ka sira paani mein dooba\n      :             :                         :\n      :   paani + neela litmus                :\n      :                                       :\n      :                                       :\n      :_______________________________________:\n                      beaker\n```\n\n> **Figure 1.4** – HCl ka fountain demo. Ulte flask mein sukhi HCl gas hai. Dropper dabane par kuch boondein paani andar jaati hain aur gas turant ghulne lagti hai. Flask ke andar ka dabao gir jaata hai, to bahar ki hawa ka dabao beaker ke paani ko lambi nali se upar dhakelta hai aur wo jet se fuwwara banke phoot padta hai. Neela litmus laal ho jaata hai.\n\n**Kya dikhta hai:** dropper dabate hi paani lambi nali mein tezi se chadhta hai, jet se fuwwara banke flask mein phootta hai aur **neela litmus laal ho jaata hai**, kyunki ghola hua HCl acid hai. Flask **sookha** isliye hona chahiye ki pehle se nami hoti to gas pehle hi ghul chuki hoti aur dabao nahi girta.\n\n### Example 4. Kitna paani kaafi hai?\n\n**Sawaal:** 500 mL ke sookhe flask mein HCl gas bhari hai. Maan lo 1 mL paani mein 450 mL HCl ghulti hai. Lagbhag kitna paani flask ki saari gas ghulane ke liye kaafi hai?\n\n**Kadam 1:** 450 mL gas ke liye 1 mL paani chahiye.\n**Kadam 2:** 500 mL gas ke liye paani = 500 ÷ 450 = lagbhag **1.1 mL**.\n**Kadam 3:** Matlab dropper ki thodi si boondein kaafi hain: isi wajah se turant dabao gir jaata hai.\n\n**Jaanch:** 1.1 × 450 = 495 mL, jo 500 mL ke kareeb hai ✅."
          },
          {
            "heading": "Part E: Identifying HCl and the White Fumes with Ammonia",
            "headingHinglish": "Part E: HCl ki pehchaan aur ammonia ke saath safed dhuan",
            "body": "There are three straightforward ways to identify HCl (all performed as teacher demos or studied for reading):\n\n1. **Moist blue litmus turns red.**\n2. **Dense white fumes with ammonia:** when a glass rod dipped in conc. HCl is held near a glass rod dipped in conc. aqueous ammonia, thick white fumes appear in the air between them: NH₃ + HCl → NH₄Cl (tiny suspended solid particles of ammonium chloride).\n3. **Curdy white precipitate with silver nitrate:** aqueous HCl contains Cl⁻ ions, which react with AgNO₃ solution to form AgCl: HCl + AgNO₃ → AgCl↓ + HNO₃ (silver nitrate is toxic and stains skin, so this is a teacher demo only; see Chapter 8, Topic 4, where we covered acidifying with dilute HNO₃ and dissolving the precipitate in NH₄OH).\n\n```\n      glass rod (conc. HCl)             glass rod (conc. ammonia)\n                \\                                /\n                 \\       . o   .  o  .          /\n                  \\    . white fumes   o  .    /\n                   \\   (solid NH₄Cl specks)   /\n```\n\n> **Figure 1.5** – Bringing two moistened glass rods close together produces dense white fumes in the air between them: gaseous HCl and gaseous NH₃ combine to form microscopic solid particles of NH₄Cl. This is strictly a teacher demo, as both vapours are irritating.\n\n### Example 5. Stoichiometry of HCl and NH₃\n\n**Problem:** 18.25 g of HCl reacts completely with ammonia gas (NH₃ + HCl → NH₄Cl). Calculate the mass of NH₃ consumed and the mass of NH₄Cl formed. (N = 14, H = 1, Cl = 35.5.)\n\n**Step 1:** Moles of HCl = 18.25 ÷ 36.5 = **0.5 mol**. The mole ratio is 1 : 1 : 1.\n**Step 2:** Mass of NH₃ = 0.5 × 17 = **8.5 g**. Mass of NH₄Cl (molar mass = 14 + 4 + 35.5 = 53.5 g/mol) = 0.5 × 53.5 = **26.75 g**.\n\n**Check:** 18.25 + 8.5 = 26.75 g ✅ (conservation of mass). Atom count: N 1, H 4, Cl 1 on both sides ✅.",
            "bodyHinglish": "HCl ke teen aasaan tests hain (sab teacher ke saath ya sirf padhne ke liye):\n\n1. **Geela blue litmus laal** hota hai.\n2. **Ammonia ke saath safed ghana dhuan:** jab conc. HCl ki geeli kaanch ki rod aur conc. ammonia ki geeli rod ek-doosri ke paas laate hain, beech ki hawa mein safed dhuan ban jaata hai: NH₃ + HCl → NH₄Cl (thos ammonium chloride ke chhote kan).\n3. **Silver nitrate ke saath safed curdy dhodha:** HCl ke ghol mein Cl⁻ hai, to AgNO₃ se AgCl banta hai. HCl + AgNO₃ → AgCl↓ + HNO₃ (silver nitrate zehreela hai: sirf teacher demo; Chapter 8, Topic 4; wahan dilute HNO₃ se pehle acidify karna aur NH₄OH mein ghulne ki baat hai: yahan dobara nahi).\n\n```\n      geeli rod (conc. HCl)             geeli rod (conc. ammonia)\n                \\                                /\n                 \\       . o   .  o  .          /\n                  \\    . safed dhuan  o  .     /\n                   \\     (NH₄Cl ke kan)       /\n```\n\n> **Figure 1.5** – Do geeli rods ke sire paas aane par beech mein safed dhuan banta hai: gas HCl aur gas NH₃ milkar thos NH₄Cl ke chhote kan banate hain. Ye teacher demo hai: dono bahut tez gandh wale hain.\n\n### Example 5. HCl aur NH₃ ka hisaab\n\n**Sawaal:** 18.25 g HCl poori tarah ammonia se milti hai (NH₃ + HCl → NH₄Cl). Kitna NH₃ lagega aur kitna NH₄Cl banega? (N 14, H 1, Cl 35.5.)\n\n**Kadam 1:** HCl = 18.25 ÷ 36.5 = **0.5 mol**. Ratio 1 : 1 : 1.\n**Kadam 2:** NH₃ = 0.5 × 17 = **8.5 g**. NH₄Cl (14 + 4 + 35.5 = 53.5) = 0.5 × 53.5 = **26.75 g**.\n\n**Jaanch:** 18.25 + 8.5 = 26.75 g ✅ (mass sangrakshan). Atoms: N 1, H 4, Cl 1 dono taraf ✅."
          },
          {
            "heading": "Part F: How HCl Is Made in Industry",
            "headingHinglish": "Part F: Factory mein HCl kaise banti hai",
            "body": "In industry, the cleanest and most direct way to manufacture HCl is **direct combination of hydrogen and chlorine**. In Class 9, we saw H₂ + Cl₂ → 2HCl. And in Chapter 4 (Electrolysis, Topic 5), we learned that the chlor-alkali electrolysis of brine produces **both Cl₂ and H₂** at the same plant. Those two gases are fed into a special burner and reacted directly: H₂ + Cl₂ → 2HCl. The resulting HCl gas is then absorbed in water to yield concentrated hydrochloric acid. This process is remarkably economical because both raw materials come straight from the same electrolysis plant.\n\n```\n   Brine (concentrated NaCl solution)\n        :\n        v\n   Electrolysis (chlor-alkali plant, Chapter 4)\n        :\n        +--> Cl₂ (at anode) ---------+\n        :                            v\n        +--> H₂ (at cathode) --> burned in reactor: H₂ + Cl₂ → 2HCl (gas)\n        :                            :\n        +--> NaOH (separate stream)  v\n                            absorbed in water (absorption tower)\n                                     :\n                                     v\n                      concentrated hydrochloric acid (~36%)\n```\n\n> **Figure 1.6** – Industrial route to HCl: electrolysis of brine yields Cl₂ and H₂ alongside NaOH; burning H₂ in Cl₂ produces HCl gas, which is absorbed in water to make concentrated hydrochloric acid.\n\n### Example 6. By volume and by mass\n\n**Problem:** (a) If 10 L of H₂ and 10 L of Cl₂ react completely at the same temperature and pressure, what volume of HCl gas is formed? (b) What mass of HCl is formed when 2 g of H₂ burns completely in chlorine? (H = 1, Cl = 35.5.)\n\n**Step 1 (volume):** In H₂ + Cl₂ → 2HCl, the mole ratio is 1 : 1 : 2. At the same T and P, gas volumes are in the same ratio as moles (Chapter 5, Topic 5), so 10 L + 10 L → **20 L of HCl** (total gas volume stays 20 L).\n**Step 2 (mass):** 2 g of H₂ = 1 mol. This requires 1 mol of Cl₂ = 71 g, producing 2 mol of HCl = 2 × 36.5 = **73 g**.\n\n**Check:** 2 + 71 = 73 g ✅.",
            "bodyHinglish": "Industry mein HCl ka sabse seedha tareeka: **hydrogen aur chlorine ko seedha jodna.** Class 9 mein H₂ + Cl₂ → 2HCl dekha tha. Chapter 4 (Electrolysis, Topic 5) mein chlor-alkali process se brine ke electrolysis se **Cl₂ aur H₂ dono** bante hain. Wahi dono gas ek burner mein jalaye jaate hain: H₂ + Cl₂ → 2HCl. Banne wali HCl gas ko paani mein ghulakar gaadha hydrochloric acid milta hai. Ye safal tareeka isliye hai ki dono kachche maal ek hi plant se mil jaate hain.\n\n```\n   Brine (namak ka gaadha ghol)\n        :\n        v\n   Electrolysis (chlor-alkali, Chapter 4)\n        :\n        +--> Cl₂ (anode par) -------+\n        :                           v\n        +--> H₂ (cathode par) --> burner mein jalana: H₂ + Cl₂ → 2HCl (gas)\n        :                           :\n        +--> NaOH (alag se)         v\n                            paani mein ghulana (absorption)\n                                    :\n                                    v\n                      gaadha hydrochloric acid (lagbhag 36 percent)\n```\n\n> **Figure 1.6** – HCl ka industrial rasta: brine ke electrolysis se Cl₂ aur H₂ bante hain (NaOH alag), dono burner mein milkar HCl gas dete hain, aur gas ko paani mein ghulakar gaadha acid banta hai.\n\n### Example 6. Volume aur mass dono se\n\n**Sawaal:** 10 L H₂ aur 10 L Cl₂ (barabar T aur P par) se kitni HCl banegi (volume)? Aur 2 g H₂ jalne par kitni HCl (mass)? (H 1, Cl 35.5.)\n\n**Kadam 1 (volume):** H₂ + Cl₂ → 2HCl mein ratio 1 : 1 : 2. Barabar T aur P par gas ke volume ka ratio mole ratio hota hai (Chapter 5, Topic 5), to 10 L + 10 L → **20 L HCl** (volume badla nahi: 20 L bachti hai).\n**Kadam 2 (mass):** 2 g H₂ = 1 mol. Chahiye 1 mol Cl₂ = 71 g. HCl = 2 mol = 2 × 36.5 = **73 g**.\n\n**Jaanch:** 2 + 71 = 73 g ✅."
          },
          {
            "heading": "Part G: Where HCl Is Used",
            "headingHinglish": "Part G: HCl kahan kaam aati hai",
            "body": "| Application | How It Works |\n|---|---|\n| **Pickling of metals** | Dissolves oxide scale off iron or steel surfaces before electroplating or galvanising (recall from Chapter 4 that electroplating requires a clean metal surface) |\n| **Household descalers & toilet cleaners (dilute)** | Dissolves carbonate limescale deposits; bottles carry adult-only safety warnings |\n| **Making metal chlorides** | Reacting the acid with bases or carbonates produces salts such as CaCl₂ and ZnCl₂ (names only) |\n| **In the human stomach** | Gastric juice contains about 0.5 percent HCl (pH ~1.5 to 2), which activates digestive enzymes and kills harmful bacteria in food |\n\n### Example 7. How dilute is stomach acid?\n\n**Problem:** Gastric juice contains about 0.5 percent HCl by mass. How many grams of HCl are present in 250 g of gastric juice? Roughly how many times more concentrated is commercial concentrated HCl (36 percent) than stomach acid?\n\n**Step 1:** 0.5 percent means 0.5 g per 100 g, so in 250 g there is 250 × 0.5 ÷ 100 = **1.25 g of HCl**.\n**Step 2:** Concentration ratio = 36 ÷ 0.5 = **72 times** as concentrated.\n\n**Check:** 1.25 ÷ 250 × 100 = 0.5 percent ✅. That explains why the cleaner on Mia's bathroom shelf is far more hazardous than stomach juice.",
            "bodyHinglish": "| Upyog | Kaise |\n|---|---|\n| **Pickling (safai)** | Plating ya galvanising se pehle lohe ya steel ki sataah par jami parat ko acid se ghulakar saaf karte hain (Chapter 4 ki electroplating ke liye saaf dhaatu chahiye) |\n| **Toilet cleaner (dilute)** | Chune ki jamav (carbonate) ko ghulata hai; bottle par \"sirf bade\" likha hota hai |\n| **Chlorides banana** | Acid + base ya carbonate se namak: jaise CaCl₂, ZnCl₂ (sirf naam) |\n| **Pet mein** | Lagbhag 0.5 percent HCl (pH lagbhag 1.5 se 2) khaana pachane aur kitanuon ko maarne mein madad karta hai |\n\n### Example 7. Pet ka acid kitna halka hai?\n\n**Sawaal:** Pet ke ras mein lagbhag 0.5 percent HCl hota hai. 250 g ras mein kitni HCl hogi? Aur bazaar ka gaadha acid (lagbhag 36 percent) pet ke ras se lagbhag kitna guna gaadha hai?\n\n**Kadam 1:** 0.5 percent matlab 100 g mein 0.5 g, to 250 g mein 250 × 0.5 ÷ 100 = **1.25 g HCl**.\n**Kadam 2:** Gaadhapan ka anupaat = 36 ÷ 0.5 = **72 guna** (lagbhag).\n\n**Jaanch:** 1.25 ÷ 250 × 100 = 0.5 percent ✅. Isliye Vaishnavi ki bottle ka acid pet ke acid se kai guna tez hota hai."
          },
          {
            "heading": "Try It Yourself (No Chemicals)",
            "headingHinglish": "Khud karke dekho (koi chemical nahi)",
            "body": "With an adult present, read the labels on household cleaning bottles **without opening any bottle**. Spot which ones carry corrosive or toxic hazard symbols and which ones state \"Do not mix with bleach or other cleaners.\" Sketch these hazard symbols onto index cards with a friend and sort household products into \"Adults only with protective care\" and \"Everyday safe items.\" Never open, sniff, or mix any bottle.",
            "bodyHinglish": "Adult ke saath ghar mein jitni safai wali bottles hain unke labels **band bottle ko bina kholey** padho. Dekho kin par khopdi-haddi ya corrosive wale nishaan hain aur kin par \"kisi aur ke saath na milayein\" likha hai. Kaagaz ke chhote cards par ye nishaan khud banao aur dost ke saath \"sirf bade\" aur \"sab ke liye\" ke dher mein baanto. Bottle ko kholna, sunghna ya milana nahi hai."
          },
          {
            "heading": "Common Pitfalls to Avoid",
            "headingHinglish": "Aam galtiyan (bachke rehna)",
            "body": "**Mistake: \"Since HCl is an acid, dry HCl gas will also turn dry blue litmus paper red.\"**\nIt is easy to fall into this trap because we call HCl an acid. However, acidic behaviour (caused by H₃O⁺ ions) only appears **in the presence of water**. Let's revisit the Chapter 1 teacher demo through the lens of Chapter 7 bonding (teacher demo, for observation only):\n\n1. **Dry HCl gas + dry blue litmus paper:** no colour change at all. Dry HCl gas consists of covalent H–Cl molecules with no free H⁺ or H₃O⁺ ions.\n2. **The same gas + a drop of water on the litmus paper:** the paper turns red immediately. Polar water molecules pull apart the polar covalent H–Cl bond: HCl + H₂O → H₃O⁺ + Cl⁻ (Chapter 7, Topic 2). Once H₃O⁺ ions form, acidic properties appear.\n3. **White mist of HCl in open air:** this happens for the exact same reason—the dry gas pulls moisture from the air to form tiny liquid droplets of aqueous hydrochloric acid.\n\n```\n   Case 1: dry HCl gas + dry blue litmus     :  stays blue\n   Case 2: dry HCl gas + moist blue litmus   :  turns red (water forms H₃O⁺)\n   Case 3: aqueous HCl + dry blue litmus     :  turns red (H₃O⁺ is already present)\n```\n\n> **Figure 1.7** – Dry HCl gas by itself does not turn dry blue litmus red; acidic behaviour requires water to produce H₃O⁺ ions.\n\nRule to remember: **HCl only acts as an acid when water is present.** In your stomach, in a toilet cleaner, and inside the fountain flask, water is always there.",
            "bodyHinglish": "**Galti: \"HCl gas apne aap mein acidic hai, to sookhi HCl gas bhi neele litmus ko laal karegi.\"**\nYe sach lagta hai kyunki HCl ka naam hi acid hai. Par acid wala asar (H₃O⁺) **paani ke saath** aata hai. Chapter 1 ke teacher demo ko ab Chapter 7 ki nazar se dekhte hain (teacher demo, sirf dekhne ke liye):\n\n1. **Sookhi HCl gas, sookha neela litmus:** koi badlav nahi. Sookhi gas mein H–Cl molecules hain: bond covalent hai, koi khula H⁺ nahi.\n2. **Wahi gas, par litmus par paani ki ek boond:** litmus turant laal. Paani ke molecule polar hain aur HCl ke polar bond ko tod dete hain: HCl + H₂O → H₃O⁺ + Cl⁻ (Chapter 7, Topic 2). Ab H₃O⁺ hai, to asar dikha.\n3. **Khuli hawa mein HCl ka safed kohra:** ye bhi isi wajah se: gas ne hawa ki nami se paani liya aur acid ki boondein ban gayeen.\n\n```\n   Case 1: sookhi HCl gas + sookha neela litmus  :  neela hi rehta hai\n   Case 2: sookhi HCl gas + geela neela litmus   :  laal (paani ne H₃O⁺ banaya)\n   Case 3: HCl ka ghol + sookha neela litmus     :  laal (ghol mein H₃O⁺ pehle se hai)\n```\n\n> **Figure 1.7** – Sookhi HCl gas akeli litmus ko laal nahi karti; ghol mein ya paani ke saath hi acid wala asar aata hai.\n\nNiyam: **HCl ka asar paani ke saath hi dikhta hai.** Isliye pet mein, toilet cleaner mein aur fountain demo ke ghol mein, sab jagah paani maujood hai."
          },
          {
            "heading": "Quick Recap",
            "headingHinglish": "Quick Recap",
            "body": "- **HCl gas** is colourless, pungent, about 1.26 times as dense as air (VD 18.25 vs 14.5 for air), and extremely soluble in water; it forms misty white fumes in moist air.\n- **Hydrochloric acid** is an aqueous solution of HCl gas; it is a strong acid (~36 percent in commercial concentrated acid, ~0.5 percent in stomach acid).\n- **Lab preparation:** NaCl + H₂SO₄ (conc.) → NaHSO₄ + HCl (gentle heat, below ~200 °C); at higher temperatures, NaHSO₄ + NaCl → Na₂SO₄ + HCl; overall 2NaCl + H₂SO₄ → Na₂SO₄ + 2HCl.\n- The gas is dried using **conc. H₂SO₄** and collected by **upward displacement of air** (never over water).\n- To dissolve HCl in water, use an **inverted funnel** arrangement to prevent suck-back.\n- **Fountain experiment:** squeezing a few drops of water into a dry flask of HCl dissolves the gas, drops the internal pressure, and drives blue litmus solution up the jet as a red fountain.\n- **Tests:** turns moist blue litmus red; gives dense white fumes of NH₄Cl with NH₃; gives a curdy white precipitate of AgCl with acidified AgNO₃ (Chapter 8).\n- **Industry:** H₂ + Cl₂ → 2HCl (using H₂ and Cl₂ from the chlor-alkali process). **Uses:** pickling metals, descaling/cleaning (dilute), making chlorides, and digestion in the stomach.\n- **Watch out:** dry HCl gas does not turn dry blue litmus red—water is required for ionisation.\n- All preparations and tests are strictly teacher demos or for reading; never mix acidic cleaners with bleach.",
            "bodyHinglish": "- **HCl gas** bina rang ki, tez gandh wali, hawa se lagbhag 1.26 guna bhaari (vapour density 18.25 vs hawa ka 14.5) aur bahut ghulansheel hai; nami se safed kohra banati hai.\n- **Hydrochloric acid** HCl gas ka paani mein ghol hai; strong acid; bazaar ka gaadha acid lagbhag 36 percent; pet mein lagbhag 0.5 percent.\n- **Lab:** NaCl + H₂SO₄ (conc.) → NaHSO₄ + HCl (halki garmi, lagbhag 200 °C se neeche); zyada garmi par NaHSO₄ + NaCl → Na₂SO₄ + HCl; kul 2NaCl + H₂SO₄ → Na₂SO₄ + 2HCl.\n- Gas ko **conc. H₂SO₄** se sukhate hain aur **upward displacement of air** se ikattha karte hain (paani ke upar kabhi nahi).\n- Acid banane ke liye **ulta funnel**, taaki suck-back na ho.\n- **Fountain demo:** sookhe flask mein dropper se thoda paani; gas ghulti hai, dabao girta hai, paani fuwwara banke chadhta hai, litmus laal.\n- **Tests:** geela blue litmus laal; NH₃ ke saath safed dhuan (NH₄Cl); AgNO₃ ke saath safed curdy dhodha (Chapter 8).\n- **Industry:** H₂ + Cl₂ → 2HCl (chlor-alkali ki H₂ aur Cl₂). **Upyog:** pickling, toilet cleaner (dilute), chlorides, pet ka acid.\n- **Galti se bacho:** sookhi HCl gas litmus ko laal nahi karti: paani chahiye.\n- Sab kuch teacher ke saath ya sirf padhne ke liye; toilet cleaner kabhi bleach ke saath nahi."
          },
          {
            "heading": "Practice Questions",
            "headingHinglish": "Practice Questions",
            "body": "(Atomic masses: H = 1, N = 14, O = 16, Na = 23, S = 32, Cl = 35.5, K = 39, Ca = 40, Ag = 108. 1 mol of gas = 22.4 L at STP.)\n\n1. Potassium chloride, KCl, is warmed gently with the exact amount of concentrated H₂SO₄ needed for the first step (KCl + H₂SO₄ → KHSO₄ + HCl). If 44.7 g of KCl is used, find the mass of HCl formed, its volume at STP, and the mass of H₂SO₄ needed.\n2. For 23.4 g of NaCl and the exact amount of H₂SO₄ for the overall reaction 2NaCl + H₂SO₄ → Na₂SO₄ + 2HCl, find the mass and the volume at STP of HCl, and the mass of Na₂SO₄ formed. Check by conservation of mass.\n3. How much NaCl and how much H₂SO₄ (in grams, by the overall equation) are needed to prepare 15.68 L of HCl at STP?\n4. A gas jar holds 3.36 L of HCl at STP. Find the mass of HCl in it. Using 22.4 L per mole, find the density of HCl in g per L and compare it with that of air (molar mass 29). Why does this decide the method of collection?\n5. 67.2 L of H₂ at STP is burnt completely in chlorine. Find the volume of Cl₂ needed, the volume of HCl formed, and the masses of all three gases.\n6. A teacher adds acidified AgNO₃ to a solution containing 3.33 g of calcium chloride, CaCl₂ (CaCl₂ + 2AgNO₃ → 2AgCl + Ca(NO₃)₂). Find the mass of AgCl and the mass of AgNO₃ used. Why does calcium not affect the test?\n7. Gastric juice contains about 0.5 percent HCl by mass. How much HCl is in 800 g of it? If a cleaner contained 10 times that percentage, what would its percentage be?\n8. A dry 250 mL flask is filled with HCl in a fountain demo. If 1 mL of water dissolves about 450 mL of HCl, estimate the volume of water that can dissolve all the gas. Why must the flask be dry, and why does the litmus turn red?\n9. State True or False and correct the false ones: (a) Dry HCl gas turns dry blue litmus red. (b) HCl gas is collected by upward displacement of air. (c) HCl is collected over water. (d) Concentrated H₂SO₄ is used to dry HCl. (e) Dilute H₂SO₄ is used to make HCl from NaCl. (f) Dry HCl gas turns moist blue litmus red.\n10. NaCl, KCl, CaCl₂, and ZnCl₂ (name only) are four chlorides. How many chloride ions are present in one formula unit of each? What common observation do their solutions give with acidified silver nitrate, and why does the cation not matter?\n11. A student draws a set-up for HCl in which the gas is collected in an inverted jar with the tube reaching its top, and the delivery tube is dipped deep in water at the end. Name two errors and the correct method for each.\n12. Why is it said that the hydrogen and chlorine for industrial HCl are conveniently available from one plant? Name the process and the third product.\n\n### Answers\n\n1. KCl = 39 + 35.5 = 74.5 g/mol, moles = 44.7 ÷ 74.5 = **0.6 mol**. Ratio 1 : 1 : 1, so HCl = 0.6 mol = 0.6 × 36.5 = **21.9 g**, volume = 0.6 × 22.4 = **13.44 L**. H₂SO₄ = 0.6 × 98 = **58.8 g**. Check: reactants = 44.7 + 58.8 = 103.5 g; products = KHSO₄ (39 + 1 + 32 + 64 = 136; 0.6 × 136 = 81.6 g) + HCl (21.9 g) = 103.5 g ✅.\n2. NaCl = 23.4 ÷ 58.5 = **0.4 mol**. From 2NaCl : H₂SO₄ : Na₂SO₄ : 2HCl, HCl = 0.4 mol = 0.4 × 36.5 = **14.6 g**, volume = 0.4 × 22.4 = **8.96 L**. Na₂SO₄ = 0.2 mol × 142 = **28.4 g**. Check: H₂SO₄ = 0.2 × 98 = 19.6 g; reactants = 23.4 + 19.6 = 43.0 g; products = 14.6 + 28.4 = 43.0 g ✅.\n3. HCl = 15.68 ÷ 22.4 = **0.7 mol**. Since 2NaCl : 2HCl = 1 : 1, NaCl = 0.7 mol = 0.7 × 58.5 = **40.95 g**. Since H₂SO₄ : 2HCl = 1 : 2, H₂SO₄ = 0.35 mol = 0.35 × 98 = **34.3 g**. Check: reactants = 40.95 + 34.3 = 75.25 g; products = HCl (0.7 × 36.5 = 25.55 g) + Na₂SO₄ (0.35 × 142 = 49.7 g) = 75.25 g ✅.\n4. Moles = 3.36 ÷ 22.4 = **0.15 mol**, mass = 0.15 × 36.5 = **5.475 g**. Density of HCl = 36.5 ÷ 22.4 ≈ **1.63 g/L**; density of air = 29 ÷ 22.4 ≈ **1.29 g/L**. Because HCl is denser than air, it is collected in an upright jar with the tube reaching the bottom: **upward displacement of air**. Check: 1.63 ÷ 1.29 = 1.26 (matching Example 1) ✅.\n5. H₂ = 67.2 ÷ 22.4 = **3 mol**. From H₂ + Cl₂ → 2HCl: Cl₂ = 3 mol = **67.2 L**, HCl = 6 mol = **134.4 L**. Masses: H₂ = 3 × 2 = **6 g**, Cl₂ = 3 × 71 = **213 g**, HCl = 6 × 36.5 = **219 g**. Check: 6 + 213 = 219 g ✅.\n6. CaCl₂ = 40 + 71 = 111 g/mol, moles = 3.33 ÷ 111 = **0.03 mol**. 1 mol CaCl₂ gives 2 mol AgCl, so AgCl = 0.06 mol = 0.06 × 143.5 = **8.61 g**. AgNO₃ = 0.06 mol × 170 = **10.2 g**. Calcium (Ca²⁺) is a **spectator ion**: the precipitate forms purely from Ag⁺ + Cl⁻ → AgCl↓ (Chapter 8). Check: reactants = 3.33 + 10.2 = 13.53 g; products = 8.61 + Ca(NO₃)₂ (0.03 × 164 = 4.92 g) = 13.53 g ✅. (Silver nitrate is toxic: teacher demo only.)\n7. 800 × 0.5 ÷ 100 = **4 g** of HCl. Ten times that concentration = **5 percent**. Check: 4 ÷ 800 × 100 = 0.5 ✅.\n8. Water needed = 250 ÷ 450 ≈ **0.56 mL**. The flask must be **dry** so the gas does not dissolve prematurely before the demo starts, which would prevent the sudden pressure drop needed to drive the fountain. The litmus turns red because dissolved HCl ionises in water to produce H₃O⁺ ions (acidic). Check: 0.56 × 450 = 252 mL ≈ 250 mL ✅.\n9. (a) **False** ❌: dry HCl does not turn dry blue litmus red; water is needed for ionisation. (b) **True** ✅. (c) **False** ❌: HCl is extremely soluble in water, so it is collected by upward displacement of air. (d) **True** ✅. (e) **False** ❌: dilute acid contains water in which HCl dissolves; conc. H₂SO₄ is required. (f) **True** ✅: moisture on the litmus provides the water needed to form H₃O⁺.\n10. NaCl has 1, KCl has 1, CaCl₂ has 2, and ZnCl₂ has 2 chloride ions per formula unit. All four solutions give a **curdy white precipitate of AgCl** with acidified silver nitrate because the test detects the Cl⁻ ion (Ag⁺ + Cl⁻ → AgCl↓), while the cations (Na⁺, K⁺, Ca²⁺, Zn²⁺) are spectator ions (Chapter 8, Topic 4). Check: Cl count from formulas: NaCl 1, KCl 1, CaCl₂ 2, ZnCl₂ 2 ✅.\n11. **Error 1:** Since HCl is denser than air, the jar must stand upright with the tube reaching the bottom; an inverted jar with a top tube is used for gases lighter than air. **Error 2:** Dipping a bare delivery tube deep into water causes suck-back; the correct method is to attach an inverted funnel whose rim just touches the water surface. (Nor can HCl be collected over water, since it is far too soluble.)\n12. In the chlor-alkali process (electrolysis of brine), **both Cl₂ (at the anode) and H₂ (at the cathode)** are produced side by side in the same plant, with **NaOH** as the third product. Thus, both raw materials for H₂ + Cl₂ → 2HCl are immediately available on-site.\n\n---\n\nMia glanced back at the high shelf one more time and said, \"Uncle, it makes complete sense now: it's the same HCl in both places, just super concentrated in the bottle and very dilute—and safely behind a mucus barrier—inside my stomach.\" Dr. Chen nodded. \"Exactly. And whenever a label says 'For adult use only,' we leave it untouched.\"\n\n**What's next?** HCl was a dense, pungent acidic gas. In Topic 2, we meet a gas with the exact opposite personality—lighter than air, alkaline in water, and the starting point for agricultural fertilisers worldwide: **ammonia**.",
            "bodyHinglish": "(Mass: H 1, N 14, O 16, Na 23, S 32, Cl 35.5, K 39, Ca 40, Ag 108. 1 mol gas = 22.4 L at STP.)\n\n1. Potassium chloride, KCl, is warmed gently with the exact amount of concentrated H₂SO₄ needed for the first step (KCl + H₂SO₄ → KHSO₄ + HCl). If 44.7 g of KCl is used, find the mass of HCl formed, its volume at STP and the mass of H₂SO₄ needed.\n2. For 23.4 g of NaCl and the exact amount of H₂SO₄ for the overall reaction 2NaCl + H₂SO₄ → Na₂SO₄ + 2HCl, find the mass and the volume at STP of HCl, and the mass of Na₂SO₄ formed. Check by conservation of mass.\n3. How much NaCl and how much H₂SO₄ (in grams, by the overall equation) are needed to prepare 15.68 L of HCl at STP?\n4. A gas jar holds 3.36 L of HCl at STP. Find the mass of HCl in it. Using 22.4 L per mole, find the density of HCl in g per L and compare it with that of air (molar mass 29). Why does this decide the method of collection?\n5. 67.2 L of H₂ at STP is burnt completely in chlorine. Find the volume of Cl₂ needed, the volume of HCl formed and the masses of all three gases.\n6. A teacher adds acidified AgNO₃ to a solution containing 3.33 g of calcium chloride, CaCl₂ (CaCl₂ + 2AgNO₃ → 2AgCl + Ca(NO₃)₂). Find the mass of AgCl and the mass of AgNO₃ used. Why does calcium not affect the test?\n7. Gastric juice contains about 0.5 percent HCl by mass. How much HCl is in 800 g of it? If a cleaner contained 10 times that percentage, what would its percentage be?\n8. A dry 250 mL flask is filled with HCl in a fountain demo. If 1 mL of water dissolves about 450 mL of HCl, estimate the volume of water that can dissolve all the gas. Why must the flask be dry, and why does the litmus turn red?\n9. State True or False and correct the false ones: (a) Dry HCl gas turns dry blue litmus red. (b) HCl gas is collected by upward displacement of air. (c) HCl is collected over water. (d) Concentrated H₂SO₄ is used to dry HCl. (e) Dilute H₂SO₄ is used to make HCl from NaCl. (f) Dry HCl gas turns moist blue litmus red.\n10. NaCl, KCl, CaCl₂ and ZnCl₂ (name only) are four chlorides. How many chloride ions are present in one formula unit of each? What common observation do their solutions give with acidified silver nitrate, and why does the cation not matter?\n11. A student draws a set-up for HCl in which the gas is collected in an inverted jar with the tube reaching its top, and the delivery tube is dipped deep in water at the end. Name two errors and the correct method for each.\n12. Why is it said that the hydrogen and chlorine for industrial HCl are conveniently available from one plant? Name the process and the third product.\n\n### Answers\n\n1. KCl = 39 + 35.5 = 74.5 g/mol, moles = 44.7 ÷ 74.5 = **0.6 mol**. Ratio 1 : 1 : 1, to HCl = 0.6 mol = 0.6 × 36.5 = **21.9 g**, volume = 0.6 × 22.4 = **13.44 L**. H₂SO₄ = 0.6 × 98 = **58.8 g**. Jaanch: reactants 44.7 + 58.8 = 103.5 g; products KHSO₄ (39 + 1 + 32 + 64 = 136; 0.6 × 136 = 81.6 g) + HCl 21.9 g = 103.5 g ✅.\n2. NaCl = 23.4 ÷ 58.5 = **0.4 mol**. 2NaCl : H₂SO₄ : Na₂SO₄ : 2HCl, to HCl = 0.4 mol = 0.4 × 36.5 = **14.6 g**, volume = 0.4 × 22.4 = **8.96 L**. Na₂SO₄ = 0.2 mol × 142 = **28.4 g**. Jaanch: H₂SO₄ = 0.2 × 98 = 19.6 g; reactants 23.4 + 19.6 = 43.0 g; products 14.6 + 28.4 = 43.0 g ✅.\n3. HCl = 15.68 ÷ 22.4 = **0.7 mol**. 2NaCl : 2HCl = 1 : 1, to NaCl = 0.7 mol = 0.7 × 58.5 = **40.95 g**. H₂SO₄ : 2HCl = 1 : 2, to H₂SO₄ = 0.35 mol = 0.35 × 98 = **34.3 g**. Jaanch: reactants 40.95 + 34.3 = 75.25 g; products HCl 0.7 × 36.5 = 25.55 g + Na₂SO₄ 0.35 × 142 = 49.7 g = 75.25 g ✅.\n4. Moles = 3.36 ÷ 22.4 = **0.15 mol**, mass = 0.15 × 36.5 = **5.475 g**. Density HCl = 36.5 ÷ 22.4 = lagbhag **1.63 g/L**; hawa = 29 ÷ 22.4 = lagbhag **1.29 g/L**. HCl bhaari hai, isliye jar seedha khada, nali neeche tak: **upward displacement of air**. Jaanch: 1.63 ÷ 1.29 = 1.26 (Example 1 jaisa) ✅.\n5. H₂ = 67.2 ÷ 22.4 = **3 mol**. H₂ + Cl₂ → 2HCl: Cl₂ = 3 mol = **67.2 L**, HCl = 6 mol = **134.4 L**. Mass: H₂ = 3 × 2 = **6 g**, Cl₂ = 3 × 71 = **213 g**, HCl = 6 × 36.5 = **219 g**. Jaanch: 6 + 213 = 219 g ✅.\n6. CaCl₂ = 40 + 71 = 111 g/mol, moles = 3.33 ÷ 111 = **0.03 mol**. 1 CaCl₂ se 2 AgCl, to AgCl = 0.06 mol = 0.06 × 143.5 = **8.61 g**. AgNO₃ = 0.06 mol × 170 = **10.2 g**. Calcium Ca²⁺ **darshak** hai: dhodha sirf Ag⁺ + Cl⁻ → AgCl↓ se banta hai (Chapter 8). Jaanch: reactants 3.33 + 10.2 = 13.53 g; products 8.61 + Ca(NO₃)₂ (0.03 × 164 = 4.92 g) = 13.53 g ✅. (Silver nitrate zehreela hai: teacher demo.)\n7. 800 × 0.5 ÷ 100 = **4 g** HCl. 10 guna = **5 percent**. Jaanch: 4 ÷ 800 × 100 = 0.5 ✅.\n8. Paani = 250 ÷ 450 = lagbhag **0.56 mL**. Flask **sookha** isliye ki nami hoti to gas pehle hi ghul jaati aur dabao nahi girta, fuwwara nahi banta. Litmus laal isliye ki ghola hua HCl paani mein H₃O⁺ deta hai (acid). Jaanch: 0.56 × 450 = 252 mL ≈ 250 mL ✅.\n9. (a) **False** ❌: sookhi HCl sookhe litmus ko laal nahi karti; paani chahiye. (b) **True** ✅. (c) **False** ❌: HCl bahut ghulansheel hai, upward displacement of air se. (d) **True** ✅. (e) **False** ❌: dilute acid mein paani hai, HCl ghul jaati hai; conc. H₂SO₄ chahiye. (f) **True** ✅: geele litmus par paani mil gaya.\n10. NaCl 1, KCl 1, CaCl₂ 2, ZnCl₂ 2 chloride ions. Chaaron ke ghol acidified AgNO₃ se **safed curdy AgCl** dete hain, kyunki test Cl⁻ ka hai: Ag⁺ + Cl⁻ → AgCl↓, cation (Na⁺, K⁺, Ca²⁺, Zn²⁺) darshak hai (Chapter 8, Topic 4). Jaanch: formulas se Cl ki ginti: NaCl 1, KCl 1, CaCl₂ 2, ZnCl₂ 2 ✅.\n11. **Galti 1:** HCl bhaari hai, to jar seedha khada hona chahiye aur nali jar ke tale tak; ulte jar mein upar tak nali lagana halki gas ka tareeka hai. **Galti 2:** nali ko gehra paani mein dalna suck-back dega; sahi tareeka ulta funnel jiska kinara bas paani ko chhoota ho. (HCl ko paani ke upar bhi nahi ikattha karte: bahut ghulansheel.)\n12. Chlor-alkali process (brine ka electrolysis) mein **Cl₂ (anode par) aur H₂ (cathode par)** dono ek hi plant se milte hain, aur teesra product **NaOH** hai. To H₂ + Cl₂ → 2HCl ke liye alag se kachcha maal laana nahi padta.\n\n---\n\nVaishnavi ne bottle ko ek baar phir dekha aur boli, \"Mama, ab samajh aaya: bottle mein bhi wahi HCl, par bahut gaadhi, aur pet mein bahut halki aur ek parat ke peeche surakshit.\" Mr. Dabholkar ne kaha, \"Bilkul. Aur jis bottle par 'sirf bade' likha ho, usse door hi rehna.\"\n\n**Aage kya?** HCl ek bhaari, tez gandh wali acidic gas thi. Topic 2 mein ek aisi gas milti hai jo ulta swabhav rakhti hai: hawa se halki, kshaariya, aur jisse khet ki khaad bhi banti hai: **ammonia**."
          }
