            "correctIndex": 3,
            "explanation": "Pitfall to avoid (\"Every acidic gas can be dried with concentrated H₂SO₄.\"): While acidic gases do not undergo an acid-base reaction with conc. H₂SO₄, remember from Topic 5 that concentrated H₂SO₄ is also an oxidising agent! Let's compare what happens when three acidic gases pass through a conc. H₂SO₄ wash bottle (for reading only): | Acidic Gas | What Happens Inside a Conc. H₂SO₄ Wash Bottle | |---|---| | HCl | Only moisture is absorbed; dry HCl gas exits cleanly | | SO₂ | Only moisture is absorbed; dry SO₂ gas exits cleanly | | H₂S | Conc. H₂SO₄ oxidises H₂S to yellow solid sulphur; the gas is destroyed! | Rule to remember: Before choosing conc. H₂SO₄ as a drying agent, check both acid-base reactivity AND redox reactivity. Easily oxidised gases like H₂S, HBr, and HI cannot be dried with concentrated H₂SO₄ because it oxidises them.",
