            "heading": "Introduction",
            "headingHinglish": "Introduction",
            "body": "At the end of Chapter 9, we posed a fascinating question: table sugar, laundry soap, plastic bags, and gasoline look nothing alike, yet all of them are built around a single element—**carbon**. In Chapter 7, you studied covalent bonds and electronegativity, and back in Class 9 you saw that a carbon atom has 4 valence electrons. This opening topic of Chapter 10 starts with one fundamental question: **what makes this one element so extraordinarily versatile?** Today we will explore carbon's \"four hands\" (tetravalency), why it can link up into millions of compounds (catenation), and how pure carbon itself can appear in forms as dramatically different as a sparkling diamond, soft pencil graphite, and black charcoal.",
            "bodyHinglish": "Chapter 9 ke aakhir mein ek sawaal chhoda tha: cheeni, sabun, plastic aur petrol, ye sab alag-alag cheezein hain, par inke peeche ek hi tatva hai, **carbon**. Chapter 7 mein tumne covalent bond aur electronegativity dekhi thi, aur Class 9 mein ye bhi ki carbon ke atom mein 4 valence electron hote hain. Is chapter ka pehla topic ek simple sawaal se shuru hota hai: **ek hi tatva itna khaas kyun hai?** Aaj hum carbon ke \"chaar haath\", uske lakhon compounds ki wajah, aur ek hi tatva ke bilkul alag dikhne wale roopon (heera, graphite, koyla) ko samjhenge."
