          {
            "heading": "Part A: Carbon's \"Four Hands\": Tetravalency",
            "headingHinglish": "Part A: Carbon ke \"chaar haath\": tetravalency",
            "body": "In Class 9, we learned that carbon (atomic number Z = 6) has the electronic configuration **2, 4**. It has 4 valence electrons in its outer shell and needs **4 more electrons** to complete a stable octet of 8. An atom can achieve an octet either by gaining/losing electrons (forming ionic bonds) or by **sharing** electrons (forming covalent bonds). For carbon, the ionic route is practically impossible:\n\n- **Losing 4 electrons to form a C⁴⁺ cation:** the tiny carbon nucleus has only 6 protons holding onto its electrons. Stripping away 4 electrons one after another against that strong attraction requires an enormous amount of energy—far more than ordinary chemical reactions can supply.\n- **Gaining 4 electrons to form a C⁴⁻ anion:** a nucleus with only 6 protons would find it extremely difficult to hold onto 10 electrons, because of the strong mutual repulsion among the 10 negatively charged electrons crowded into a small atom.\n\nTherefore, carbon takes the **sharing route**: it shares its 4 valence electrons with other atoms to form **4 covalent bonds**. This property of having a valency of 4 is called **tetravalency**. With hydrogen, for instance, carbon forms methane, CH₄ (Class 9). Because the electronegativities of carbon (**2.5**) and hydrogen (**2.1**) differ by only 0.4 (Chapter 6), the C–H bond is **virtually non-polar** (Chapter 7).\n\n```\n   Carbon atom (Z = 6)                   Methane (CH₄): 4 covalent bonds\n\n   nucleus: 6 protons                          H\n   shell 1: 2 electrons                        I\n   shell 2: 4 electrons                   H -- C -- H\n   (configuration 2, 4)                        I\n   needs 4 more -> forms 4 bonds               H\n```\n\n> **Figure 1.1** – Left: carbon's electronic configuration (2, 4), with 4 valence electrons needing 4 more for an octet. Right: in methane (CH₄), carbon forms 4 covalent bonds (valency 4) and each hydrogen forms 1 bond (valency 1), completing every atom's outer shell.\n\n### Example 1. Counting bonds to find a molecular formula\n\n**Problem:** Three carbon atoms are linked in a straight chain by single bonds, and all remaining valencies are satisfied by hydrogen atoms. How many hydrogen atoms are present, and how many covalent bonds are in the molecule altogether?\n\n**Step 1 (valency budget):** Three carbon atoms have a total valency of 3 × 4 = **12**. The 2 C–C single bonds between them use up 2 × 2 = 4 valency units (1 from each carbon per bond). That leaves 12 − 4 = **8** unused valency units, which are filled by **8 hydrogen atoms** (each with valency 1), giving the formula **C₃H₈**.\n**Step 2 (total bonds):** 2 C–C bonds + 8 C–H bonds = **10 covalent bonds**. Alternatively, (total valency of all atoms = 12 + 8) ÷ 2 = 20 ÷ 2 = **10 bonds**.\n\n```\n        H   H   H\n        I   I   I\n    H - C - C - C - H\n        I   I   I\n        H   H   H\n```\n\n> **Figure 1.2** – A three-carbon straight chain (propane, C₃H₈): every carbon atom has exactly 4 bonds and every hydrogen atom has 1 bond. The middle carbon is bonded to 2 H atoms and 2 C atoms; each end carbon is bonded to 3 H atoms and 1 C atom.\n\n**Check:** Each carbon has 4 bonds ✅; total H atoms = 3 + 2 + 3 = 8 ✅; total bonds = 10 ✅.",
            "bodyHinglish": "Class 9 mein carbon ka electron arrangement dekha tha: **2, 4**. Bahari shell mein 4 electron hain, aur wo shell tab poori hoti jab 8 electron ho: yaani **4 aur chahiye**. Ek tatva ke atom ke paas do hi tareeke hote hain: electron lena ya dena (ionic bond), ya electron **share** karna (covalent bond). Carbon ke liye ionic raasta lagbhag band hai:\n\n- **4 electron dena** (C⁴⁺ banane ke liye): nucleus mein sirf 6 proton hain, jo bahari electron ko bahut kas kar pakadte hain. Ek-ek karke 4 electron nikalne mein itni zyada energy chahiye ki ye practically nahi hota.\n- **4 electron lena** (C⁴⁻ banane ke liye): 6 proton ko ab 10 electron sambhalne padenge, aur electron aapas mein ek dusre ko dhakelte hain. Ye bhi bahut mushkil hai.\n\nIsliye carbon ne **share karne wala** raasta chuna: wo apne chaar electron chaar aur atomon ke saath share karke **4 covalent bond** banata hai. Ye hi uski **tetravalency** hai. Hydrogen ke saath: methane CH₄ (Class 9 mein banaya tha). Carbon aur hydrogen ki electronegativity **2.5** aur **2.1** hai (Chapter 6), farq sirf 0.4, to C–H bond lagbhag **non-polar** hai (Chapter 7 ka rule).\n\n```\n   Carbon ka parmanu (Z = 6)            Methane CH₄: 4 bond\n\n   nucleus: 6 proton                           H\n   shell 1: 2 electron                         I\n   shell 2: 4 electron                    H -- C -- H\n   (arrangement 2, 4)                          I\n   4 aur chahiye -> 4 bond                     H\n```\n\n> **Figure 1.1** – Baayen, carbon ka electron arrangement 2, 4: bahari shell mein 4 electron, 4 aur chahiye. Daayen, methane mein carbon ke 4 bond (valency 4: chaaron lakeer gini gayi) aur har hydrogen ka 1 bond (valency 1). Har atom ki shell poori ho gayi.\n\n### Example 1. Bond gin kar formula banana\n\n**Sawaal:** Ek chain mein 3 carbon sidhe jude hain, baaki sab valency hydrogen se poori hoti hai. Hydrogen kitne honge, aur kul bond kitne?\n\n**Kadam 1 (valency ka bajat):** 3 carbon ki kul valency = 3 × 4 = **12**. Beech wale 2 C–C bond mein 2 × 2 = 4 valency lagi (har bond mein dono atomon ki 1-1 valency). Bachi valency = 12 − 4 = **8**, aur har hydrogen ki valency 1 hai, to **8 hydrogen**: C₃H₈.\n**Kadam 2 (kul bond):** 2 C–C + 8 C–H = **10 bond**. Doosre tareeke se: (kul valency 12 + 8) ÷ 2 = 20 ÷ 2 = 10.\n\n```\n        H   H   H\n        I   I   I\n    H - C - C - C - H\n        I   I   I\n        H   H   H\n```\n\n> **Figure 1.2** – Teen carbon ki sidhi chain (C₃H₈): har carbon ke chaar bond aur har hydrogen ka ek bond gine ja sakte hain. Beech wale carbon ke upar-neeche 2 H aur do taraf C hai; kinaaron ke carbon ke paas 3 H aur 1 C hai.\n\n**Jaanch:** har carbon: upar-neeche 2 H (ya 3 H kinaare par) + C se judna = 4 ✅; H ki ginti: 3 + 2 + 3 → upar 3, neeche 3, aur dono sire par 1-1 = 8 ✅; bond 10 ✅."
          },
          {
            "heading": "Part B: Small Size and Strong Bonds: Catenation and Millions of Compounds",
            "headingHinglish": "Part B: Chhota size, mazboot bond: catenation aur lakhon compounds",
            "body": "Many other elements have valencies of 2, 3, or 4 (such as oxygen, nitrogen, or silicon), yet none of them builds vast families of long-chain molecules like carbon does. Why is carbon the champion of chain-building? Two reasons work together:\n\n1. **A carbon atom is small:** when two carbon atoms share electrons, their nuclei sit close to the shared electron pair, holding it tightly. As a result, the **C–C single bond is exceptionally strong**, making long carbon chains stable instead of falling apart.\n2. **Carbon has four hands (valency 4):** even after a carbon atom uses two of its bonds to link with neighbours on the left and right in a chain, it still has **two hands left over** to bond with hydrogen, oxygen, nitrogen, chlorine, or another carbon branch!\n\nThis remarkable self-linking ability—atoms of the same element bonding to one another to form **straight chains, branched chains, and closed rings**—is called **catenation**. Silicon (sitting directly below carbon in Group 14) also has 4 valence electrons and can form short chains of a few atoms, but because silicon atoms are larger, the Si–Si bond is much weaker and silicon chains are unstable and reactive. Carbon chains, by contrast, can run to hundreds or thousands of carbon atoms!\n\n```\n   Straight chain                 Branched chain                 Closed ring\n   (4 carbons, C₄H₁₀)             (4 carbons, C₄H₁₀)             (5 carbons, C₅H₁₀)\n\n    H   H   H   H                   H                             CH₂\n    I   I   I   I                   I                            /    \\\nH - C - C - C - C - H           H - C - H                    CH₂        CH₂\n    I   I   I   I                   I                          I          I\n    H   H   H   H           H       I       H                CH₂ ------ CH₂\n                            I       I       I\n                        H - C ----- C ----- C - H\n                            I       I       I\n                            H       H       H\n```\n\n> **Figure 1.3** – Three ways carbon atoms can link up via catenation. Left: a 4-carbon straight chain, C₄H₁₀ (10 H atoms). Middle: a 4-carbon branched chain with the exact same formula, C₄H₁₀ (three CH₃ groups attached to a central CH, giving 3 + 3 + 3 + 1 = 10 H). Right: a 5-carbon ring, C₅H₁₀ (each CH₂ has 2 H bonds and 2 ring C–C bonds = valency 4; 10 H total).\n\nNotice a striking detail in Figure 1.3: the straight chain and the branched chain share the **exact same molecular formula** (C₄H₁₀) yet have **different structures**—we will explore these **isomers** in Topic 3!\n\n### Example 2. Comparing hydrogen counts in a chain vs a ring\n\n**Problem:** A 5-carbon straight-chain alkane has 12 hydrogen atoms (C₅H₁₂). If those 5 carbon atoms are joined into a closed 5-membered ring (with single bonds), how many hydrogen atoms does the ring have, and how does the total number of covalent bonds compare?\n\n**Step 1:** To close an open chain into a ring, the two end carbons must join to form one new C–C bond. To free up a hand on each end carbon, **one H atom is removed from each end** (2 H atoms in total). Thus, the 5-carbon ring has 12 − 2 = **10 H atoms**: **C₅H₁₀**.\n**Step 2 (total bonds):** Open chain: (5 × 4 + 12) ÷ 2 = **16 bonds** (4 C–C + 12 C–H). Closed ring: (5 × 4 + 10) ÷ 2 = **15 bonds** (5 C–C + 10 C–H).\n\n**Check:** Ring bonds = 5 C–C + 10 C–H = 15 ✅; chain bonds = 4 C–C + 12 C–H = 16 ✅. Closing the ring adds 1 C–C bond and removes 2 C–H bonds, reducing the total bond count by 1.\n\n### How Many Carbon Compounds Exist—and Why?\n\nCombine catenation with four more possibilities: (a) chains can be of **virtually any length**, (b) branches can attach at **many positions**, (c) carbon atoms can join by **single, double, or triple bonds** (C–C, C=C, C≡C; Topic 5), and (d) carbon forms strong covalent bonds with other elements such as **H, O, N, S, Cl, and Br**. Put all of these together—like LEGO bricks that can click together end-to-end, sideways, in loops, and with different-coloured pieces—and the result is astonishing: **more than ten million (10,000,000+) carbon compounds** are known today, far outnumbering the compounds of all other elements combined!\n\n### Organic vs Inorganic Compounds\n\nEarly chemists believed that carbon compounds like sugar, urea, and oils could only be made inside **living organisms** (plants and animals) through a mysterious \"vital force,\" which is why they named them **organic compounds**. In 1828, the German chemist **Friedrich Wöhler** synthesized **urea** (an organic compound found in urine) in his lab from an inorganic salt, disproving the vital-force myth forever. The name **organic chemistry**, however, stuck as the branch of chemistry devoted to carbon compounds.\n\n| Feature | Organic Compounds | Inorganic Compounds (salts, mineral acids, metals) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Main elements | Carbon and hydrogen (often with O, N, Cl, Br, S) | Any of the elements, often including metals |\n| Type of bonding | Almost always covalent | Often ionic (though some are covalent) |\n| Combustibility | Most burn in air | Most do not burn |\n| Melting & boiling points | Usually relatively low (e.g., wax, sugar) | Usually high for ionic solids (e.g., NaCl) |\n| Solubility in water | Most are insoluble in water (with exceptions like sugar and ethanol) | Many ionic salts dissolve in water |\n| Electrical conductivity | Usually non-conductors as liquids or solutions | Ionic salts conduct when molten or dissolved in water |\n\n*(Note: A few simple carbon-containing substances—specifically **CO, CO₂, carbonates, and bicarbonates**—are traditionally classified as **inorganic** because their properties closely resemble mineral compounds.)*",
            "bodyHinglish": "Kai tatva (jaise oxygen, nitrogen) 2 ya 3 valency wale hain, par unke atom lambi chain nahi banate. Carbon kyun banata hai? Do karan:\n\n1. **Carbon ka atom chhota hai**, to jab do carbon atom aapas mein jude to unka bond bahut paas se bana, aur wo **mazboot** hota hai. Mazboot C–C bond ko todna mushkil, isliye chain **sthir** (stable) rehti hai.\n2. **Chaar valency** hain, isliye ek carbon do taraf se chain mein judta hai aur phir bhi usse do aur haath bache hote hain: dusre carbon ke liye, ya hydrogen, oxygen, nitrogen, chlorine ke liye.\n\nIsi gun ko **catenation** kehte hain: carbon ke atomon ka aapas mein **chain, shakha aur ring** banana. Silicon (carbon ke neeche wala tatva) kuch chain banata hai, par uske atom bade hain, bond kamzor, isliye uski chain chhoti hi reh jaati hai. Carbon ki chain bahut lambi ho sakti hai: sau se zyada carbon tak.\n\n```\n   Seedhi chain                  Shakha (branch) wali           Ring\n   (4 carbon, C₄H₁₀)             (4 carbon, C₄H₁₀)              (5 carbon, C₅H₁₀)\n\n    H   H   H   H                   H                             CH₂\n    I   I   I   I                   I                            /    \\\nH - C - C - C - C - H           H - C - H                    CH₂        CH₂\n    I   I   I   I                   I                          I          I\n    H   H   H   H           H       I       H                CH₂ ------ CH₂\n                            I       I       I\n                        H - C ----- C ----- C - H\n                            I       I       I\n                            H       H       H\n```\n\n> **Figure 1.3** – Carbon ki teen tarah ki banawat. Baayen, sidhi chain C₄H₁₀ (10 H: upar-neeche 8 aur sire par 2). Beech mein, shakha wali chain, wahi formula C₄H₁₀ (teen CH₃ ek beech wale carbon se jude, 3 + 3 + 3 + 1 = 10 H). Daayen, 5 carbon ki ring (har CH₂ mein 2 H, 2 ring bond: valency 4; kul 10 H). Har carbon ki valency 4 gini gayi.\n\nDhyan do: Figure 1.3 ki pehli aur doosri chain ka **formula ek hi** (C₄H₁₀) hai par **shakal alag**: iska poora khel Topic 3 mein.\n\n### Example 2. Chain aur ring mein H ka farq\n\n**Sawaal:** 5 carbon ki sidhi chain mein 12 H hote hain (C₅H₁₂). Wahi 5 carbon ring mein jod do to H kitne? Bond kitne kam ya zyada?\n\n**Kadam 1:** Ring banane ke liye dono sire ke carbon ek dusre se jude. Ek naya C–C bond banane ke liye har sire ke carbon ne **ek hydrogen chhoda**. Isliye ring mein 12 − 2 = **10 H**: C₅H₁₀.\n**Kadam 2 (bond):** chain: (5 × 4 + 12) ÷ 2 = **16 bond** (4 C–C + 12 C–H). Ring: (5 × 4 + 10) ÷ 2 = **15 bond** (5 C–C + 10 C–H).\n\n**Jaanch:** ring mein 5 C–C + 10 C–H = 15 ✅; chain mein 4 + 12 = 16 ✅. Ring mein ek C–C bond zyada, par 2 C–H kam: kul ek kam bond.\n\n### Kitne compounds? Aur kyun?\n\nCatenation ke saath teen cheezein aur jodo: (a) chain **kisi bhi lambai** ki ho sakti hai, (b) shakha **kahin bhi** lag sakti hai, (c) carbon, carbon ke saath **ek, do ya teen bond** bhi bana sakta hai (C–C, C=C, C≡C: Topic 5), (d) carbon ke baaki haath **hydrogen, oxygen, nitrogen, chlorine, bromine** se judte hain, aur C–H, C–O, C–Cl bond bhi mazboot hain. Ye saare vikalp milakar itne alag-alag compound ban sakte hain ki **ek karod se zyada carbon ke compounds** jaane ja chuke hain (lagbhag), jabki baaki saare tatvon ke milakar bahut kam. Ye ek Lego block jaisa hai: ek hi tarah ka block (carbon), par chain, shakha, ring, pul aur rang (alag atom) ke saath jode jaate hain.\n\n### Organic aur inorganic\n\nPehle log sochte the ki carbon wale compound sirf **jeevit cheezon** (paudhe, jaanvar) hi bana sakte hain, isliye unhe \"organic\" kaha gaya. 1828 mein Friedrich Wöhler ne lab mein ek aise compound (urea) ko bana diya, jo pehle sirf jeevon mein mila tha. Tab se ye \"jeevit shakti\" wali baat khatam ho gayi, par **naam bacha reh gaya**.\n\n| Gun | Organic compound | Inorganic compound (namak, acid, dhaatu) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Mukhya tatva | carbon aur hydrogen (saath O, N, Cl, Br) | alag-alag, aksar dhaatu |\n| Bond | aksar covalent | aksar ionic (par covalent bhi) |\n| Jalna | aksar jalte hain | aksar nahi jalte |\n| Melting point | aksar kam (cheeni, wax) | aksar ooncha (NaCl) |\n| Paani mein | aksar nahi ghulte, ya kam | namak aksar ghulte hain |\n| Bijli | solid ya liquid mein aksar nahi chalate | ghol ya pighle roop mein aksar chalate hain |\n\n(Table mein \"aksar\" isliye kyunki apwaad hote hain: cheeni organic hai aur paani mein khoob ghulti hai.) Ek line: kuch carbon wale yaugik, jaise **CO, CO₂ aur carbonate**, organic nahi gine jaate: wo inorganic maane jaate hain."
          },
          {
            "heading": "Part C: One Element, Different Forms: Allotropes",
            "headingHinglish": "Part C: Ek hi tatva, alag roop: allotrope",
            "body": "Even when carbon bonds **only to itself** as a pure element, its atoms can connect in different three-dimensional patterns, creating materials with completely different physical properties. Different structural forms of the same element are called **allotropes**. Let's compare the three crystalline allotropes of carbon.\n\n### 1. Diamond\n\nIn diamond, **every carbon atom is bonded to 4 other carbon atoms** by strong single covalent bonds arranged symmetrically in three-dimensional space (forming a rigid 3D tetrahedral network—what we called a **giant covalent lattice** in Chapter 7). The entire diamond crystal is essentially one giant molecule locked together in every direction.\n\n```\n                       C(+3)\n                       I\n           C(+3) ----- C ----- C(+3)\n                       I\n                       C(+3)\n```\n\n> **Figure 1.4** – A small portion of the diamond lattice flattened onto the page: the central carbon atom forms 4 single covalent bonds to 4 neighbouring carbons (valency 4). Each outer carbon also forms 4 bonds—one shown here and \"(+3)\" continuing outward into the 3D network.\n\nThis rigid 3D network explains all of diamond's famous properties:\n- **Hardest known natural substance:** every atom is locked in place by 4 strong covalent bonds in three dimensions.\n- **Non-conductor of electricity:** all 4 valence electrons of every carbon atom are localized in C–C single bonds; there are no free mobile electrons to carry current.\n- **Extremely high melting point** (over 3500 °C): melting requires breaking a vast network of strong covalent bonds.\n- **Colourless and brilliant** when cut (it bends and reflects light strongly, making it prized in jewellery).\n- **Uses:** jewellery, glass-cutting tools, rock-drilling bits, and diamond dust for polishing hard gemstones.\n\n### 2. Graphite\n\nIn graphite, **every carbon atom is bonded to only 3 neighbouring carbon atoms** in the same plane, forming flat **hexagonal (six-membered) rings** fused together like chicken wire into **flat two-dimensional sheets (layers)**. These flat sheets are stacked one on top of another.\n\n```\n                 \\             /\n                  C ========= C\n                 /             \\\n       ------- C                 C -------\n                 \\\\           //\n                  C --------- C\n                 /             \\\n```\n\n> **Figure 1.5** – One hexagon inside a single sheet of graphite: each of the 6 carbon atoms is bonded to 3 neighbours (2 in the ring and 1 extending outward in the sheet). To satisfy carbon's valency of 4, one bond per carbon is drawn as a double bond (=): 2 + 1 + 1 = 4. In reality, those fourth valence electrons are not fixed to one bond—they are delocalised and free to move across the entire sheet!\n\nBecause each carbon atom uses only 3 of its 4 valence electrons for single bonds within the sheet, its **fourth valence electron is free to move** along the layer. That makes graphite an **excellent conductor of electricity** (unlike diamond!). Furthermore, while the covalent bonds *within* each flat sheet are very strong, the forces *between* stacked sheets are **very weak**:\n\n```\n   Layer 1     o-o-o-o-o-o-o-o-o-o-o-o     <-- strong covalent bonds within each sheet\n               . . . . . . . . . . . .     <-- weak attraction between stacked sheets\n   Layer 2     o-o-o-o-o-o-o-o-o-o-o-o\n               . . . . . . . . . . . .\n   Layer 3     o-o-o-o-o-o-o-o-o-o-o-o\n\n   When rubbed or pressed, one sheet slides easily over the next  ----->\n```\n\n> **Figure 1.6** – Side view of stacked graphite layers: strong covalent bonds (`o-o`) hold carbon atoms together inside each flat sheet, but only weak forces (`.`) hold one sheet to the next, allowing the layers to slide smoothly over one another.\n\nThis layered structure explains graphite's properties: **soft, smooth, and slippery; greyish-black with a metallic sheen; a good conductor of electricity; and a very high melting point.**\n- **Uses:** **pencil \"leads\"** (a mixture of graphite and clay—despite the old name \"pencil lead,\" it contains **zero lead (Pb)**!), **electrodes** in electrolysis cells and dry-cell batteries (Chapter 4), and a **dry lubricant** for heavy machinery running at high temperatures where oil would burn.\n- Why are pencils graded \"H\" and \"B\"? Adding more clay makes a harder lead (**H**) that leaves a light line; adding more graphite makes a softer, blacker lead (**B**) whose layers slide onto paper effortlessly.\n\n### Example 3. Comparing packing density in diamond and graphite\n\n**Problem:** You have 0.35 g of pure carbon as diamond and 0.35 g of pure carbon as graphite. Given that the density of diamond is about 3.5 g/cm³ and the density of graphite is about 2.2 g/cm³, calculate the volume of each sample and find the ratio of their volumes.\n\n**Step 1 (volume = mass ÷ density):** Diamond: 0.35 ÷ 3.5 = **0.10 cm³**. Graphite: 0.35 ÷ 2.2 = **0.159 cm³** (≈ **0.16 cm³**).\n**Step 2 (ratio):** Graphite occupies 3.5 ÷ 2.2 ≈ **1.59 times** as much volume as the same mass of diamond.\n\n**Check:** 3.5 × 0.10 = 0.35 g ✅; 2.2 × 0.159 = 0.35 g ✅. Because graphite's flat sheets are spaced relatively far apart, its atoms are packed less densely than in diamond's tight 3D network.\n\n### 3. Fullerene (C₆₀) and Modern Carbon Nanomaterials\n\nIn 1985, scientists discovered a third crystalline allotrope of carbon: **buckminsterfullerene, C₆₀**. Unlike diamond and graphite (which are giant networks), C₆₀ consists of discrete **60-carbon molecules** shaped exactly like a **soccer ball (football)**, made of **12 pentagons and 20 hexagons**! Each carbon atom sits at a vertex bonded to 3 neighbours. Here is a neat geometry check: 12 pentagons have 12 × 5 = 60 corners, and 20 hexagons have 20 × 6 = 120 corners; since every carbon atom is shared by 3 faces (1 pentagon and 2 hexagons), the total number of carbon atoms is (60 + 120) ÷ 3 = **60** ✅!\n\nTwo related modern carbon materials worth knowing by name: **graphene** is a **single, one-atom-thick sheet** peeled off graphite, and a **carbon nanotube** is a graphene sheet rolled into a microscopic cylinder—both are extraordinarily strong and conduct electricity.\n\n### Example 4. Stoichiometry of C₆₀\n\n**Problem:** What is the molar mass of C₆₀? How many moles of C₆₀ molecules and how many moles of carbon atoms are present in 7.2 g of C₆₀? What mass of CO₂ is formed if 7.2 g of C₆₀ is burned completely (C₆₀ + 60O₂ → 60CO₂)? (C = 12, O = 16.)\n\n**Step 1:** Molar mass of C₆₀ = 60 × 12 = **720** g/mol.\n**Step 2:** Moles of C₆₀ = 7.2 ÷ 720 = **0.01 mol**. Moles of carbon atoms = 0.01 × 60 = **0.6 mol**.\n**Step 3:** From the equation, 1 mol of C₆₀ gives 60 mol of CO₂, so 0.01 × 60 = **0.6 mol of CO₂** = 0.6 × 44 = **26.4 g**.\n\n**Check:** Atom count: C 60 = 60 ✅, O 120 = 60 × 2 ✅. Conservation of mass: 7.2 g carbon + O₂ (0.6 × 32 = 19.2 g) = 26.4 g ✅.\n\n### Comparing All Three Crystalline Allotropes\n\n| Property | Diamond | Graphite | Fullerene (C₆₀) |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Neighbours per C atom | 4 | 3 | 3 |\n| Structure | 3D giant covalent network | Stacked flat hexagonal sheets | Discrete 60-atom soccer-ball cage |\n| Hardness | Hardest natural substance | Soft and slippery | Molecular solid |\n| Electrical conductivity | Non-conductor | Good conductor | (Studied in higher classes) |\n| Appearance | Colourless, transparent crystal | Greyish-black, opaque | Dark powder |\n| Key uses | Jewellery, cutting glass, rock drills | Pencil leads, electrodes, dry lubricant | Materials research |",
            "bodyHinglish": "Carbon ke atomon ke jude hone ka tareeka kai ho sakta hai, aur har tareeke se alag **gun** wala padarth banta hai. Ek hi tatva ke aise alag roopon ko **allotrope** kehte hain.\n\n### Heera (diamond)\n\nHeere mein **har carbon ke atom ke 4 padosi carbon** hote hain (chaaron valency carbon se hi puri). Ye 4 padosi ek poore **teen-aayami (3D) jaal** mein faile hote hain: poora crystal ek bada molecule jaisa hai (Chapter 7 mein \"giant covalent\" kaha tha). Har bond mazboot aur har taraf se jakda hua.\n\n```\n                       C(+3)\n                       I\n           C(+3) ----- C ----- C(+3)\n                       I\n                       C(+3)\n```\n\n> **Figure 1.4** – Heere ka ek tukda, chapte (2D) roop mein dikhaya gaya: beech ke carbon ke 4 bond (valency 4 gini gayi). Har bahri carbon ke bhi 4 bond hain: ek dikhaya, \"(+3)\" bataata hai ki baaki 3 bond aage jaal mein jaate hain. Asli heere mein ye 4 bond teen aayam mein alag-alag disha mein fail kar poora jaal banate hain.\n\nIsse heere ke gun nikalte hain:\n- **Sabse kadak prakritik padarth**: har atom 4 mazboot bond se jakda hai.\n- **Bijli ka kuchalak (non-conductor)**: har carbon ke saare 4 electron bond mein bandhe hain, koi electron chalne ke liye khula nahi.\n- **Bahut ooncha melting point**: poora jaal todna padega.\n- **Bina rang ka, chamakta** (light ko khoob modta hai: isi liye jewellery mein).\n- **Upyog:** jewellery, glass kaatna, rock drilling ke bits, polishing ka powder.\n\n### Graphite\n\nGraphite mein **har carbon ke sirf 3 padosi** carbon hote hain, aur wo chhote-chhote **chhe-kone (hexagon)** banate hain. Ye hexagon milkar ek **chapti parat (layer)** banate hain, aur aisi kai parat ek ke upar ek rakhi hoti hain.\n\n```\n                 \\             /\n                  C ========= C\n                 /             \\\n       ------- C                 C -------\n                 \\\\           //\n                  C --------- C\n                 /             \\\n```\n\n> **Figure 1.5** – Graphite ki ek parat ka ek hexagon: chhe carbon, har carbon ke 3 padosi (2 hexagon mein, 1 bahar parat mein). Valency 4 ginne ke liye har carbon ka ek bond dohra (=) dikhaya gaya: dohra + ek ikhra + bahar ka ikhra = 4. Asli graphite mein ye \"dohre\" bond ke electron kisi ek jagah nahi bandhe, poori parat mein ghoom sakte hain.\n\nGraphite ki **chauthi valency** wala electron kisi ek bond mein nahi bandha, poori parat mein ghoom sakta hai. Isliye graphite **bijli ka achha conductor** hai (heera nahi). Aur parat ke andar bond mazboot, par **parton ke beech** sirf **kamzor** aakarshan hai:\n\n```\n   parat 1     o-o-o-o-o-o-o-o-o-o-o-o     <-- parat ke andar mazboot bond\n               . . . . . . . . . . . .     <-- parton ke beech kamzor jod\n   parat 2     o-o-o-o-o-o-o-o-o-o-o-o\n               . . . . . . . . . . . .\n   parat 3     o-o-o-o-o-o-o-o-o-o-o-o\n\n   dabane ya ragadne par ek parat dusri par phisal jaati hai  ----->\n```\n\n> **Figure 1.6** – Graphite ko side se dekhne par: parat ke andar atom mazboot bond (o-o) se jude hain, par parat aur parat ke beech kamzor jod (.) hai. Isliye parat aasani se phisal jaati hai.\n\nIsse graphite ke gun: **narm, chikna, phisalne wala; kaala, halka chamak; bijli ka conductor; ooncha melting point.**\n- **Upyog:** pencil ki nok (asli mein graphite aur chikni mitti ka mishran: \"lead\" naam galat hai, isme lead (Pb) hota hi nahi), **electrode** (Chapter 4 mein carbon electrode dekhe the), **lubricant** (mashin ke parts ko phisalne wala), dry cell ka carbon rod.\n- Pencil mein \"H\" ka matlab zyada mitti (kadak, halki lakeer), \"B\" ka matlab zyada graphite (narm, gehri lakeer): ye bhi parat ke phisalne ka kamaal hai.\n\n### Example 3. Heere aur graphite mein kaun zyada ghana\n\n**Sawaal:** 0.35 g carbon heere ke roop mein hai, aur utna hi 0.35 g graphite ke roop mein. Heera ka density lagbhag 3.5 g per cm³ aur graphite ka lagbhag 2.2 g per cm³. Dono ka volume kitna, aur kaun zyada jagah leta hai?\n\n**Kadam 1 (volume = mass ÷ density):** Heera: 0.35 ÷ 3.5 = **0.10 cm³**. Graphite: 0.35 ÷ 2.2 = **0.159 cm³**, yaani lagbhag **0.16 cm³**.\n**Kadam 2:** graphite lagbhag 3.5 ÷ 2.2 = 1.59 guna jagah leta hai.\n\n**Jaanch:** 3.5 × 0.10 = 0.35 ✅; 2.2 × 0.159 = 0.35 ✅. Matlab heere mein atom zyada kasoke baithe hain, aur graphite mein parton ke beech khaali jagah bachti hai.\n\n### Fullerene (C₆₀) aur nayi khoj\n\n1985 mein carbon ka ek aur roop mila: **C₆₀ fullerene**. Ek molecule mein **60 carbon atom**, jo **football** jaisi shakal banate hain: **12 panchkon (pentagon) aur 20 hexagon**. Har carbon ke 3 padosi, aur ye ek **alag molecule** hai (heere ke jaal jaisa bada nahi). Ek mazedaar jaanch: 12 × 5 = 60 aur 20 × 6 = 120; har carbon 3 shaklon (1 pentagon + 2 hexagon) ka kinaara hai, to carbon = (60 + 120) ÷ 3 = **60** ✅.\n\nEk line mein aur: **graphene** graphite ki **sirf ek parat** hai (ek atom moti), aur **nanotube** us parat ko nali ki tarah lapetne se bani hai: ye abhi vigyan ki nayi khoj hain, bahut hi mazboot aur bijli conduct karne wale.\n\n### Example 4. Ek molecule C₆₀ ka hisaab\n\n**Sawaal:** C₆₀ ka molar mass kya hai? 7.2 g C₆₀ mein kitne mol C₆₀ aur kitne mol carbon atom? Poora jalaya (C₆₀ + 60O₂ → 60CO₂) to kitna CO₂? (C 12, O 16.)\n\n**Kadam 1:** molar mass = 60 × 12 = **720** g/mol.\n**Kadam 2:** 7.2 ÷ 720 = **0.01 mol** C₆₀. Carbon atom = 0.01 × 60 = **0.6 mol**.\n**Kadam 3:** reaction ke ratio se 1 mol C₆₀ se 60 mol CO₂: 0.01 × 60 = **0.6 mol CO₂** = 0.6 × 44 = **26.4 g** (sirf padhne ke liye).\n\n**Jaanch:** atoms: C 60 = 60 ✅, O 120 = 60 × 2 ✅. Mass: 7.2 g carbon + O₂ (0.6 × 32 = 19.2 g) = 26.4 g ✅.\n\n### Teeno roop ek table mein\n\n| Gun | Heera | Graphite | C₆₀ |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Har C ke padosi | 4 | 3 | 3 |\n| Banawat | 3D giant jaal | chapti paratein | football molecule |\n| Kadapan | sabse kadak | narm, phisalne wala | alag molecule |\n| Bijli | nahi chalata | chalata hai | (yahan sirf naam) |\n| Rang | bina rang ka | kaala | gehra rang ka powder |\n| Upyog | gehna, cutting, drilling | pencil, electrode, lubricant | khoj (naya) |"
          },
          {
            "heading": "Part D: Non-Crystalline Forms: Amorphous Carbon",
            "headingHinglish": "Part D: Bina regular banawat wale roop: amorphous carbon",
            "body": "Several familiar forms of carbon lack a regular, long-range crystalline network; they are called **amorphous** forms of carbon (actually consisting of tiny, disordered microscopic fragments of graphite-like sheets):\n\n| Form | How It Forms / Is Made | Key Uses |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Coal** | Formed underground over millions of years from buried plant matter | Fuel for power plants and industry |\n| **Coke** | Made by heating coal strongly in the absence of air; hard, porous, nearly pure carbon | Reducing agent and fuel in blast furnaces (Chapter 3) |\n| **Charcoal** | Made by heating wood (or plant material) in a limited supply of air | Fuel; **activated charcoal** adsorbs coloured impurities and odours from water and gases |\n| **Lamp-black (soot)** | Fine black powder collected from the incomplete combustion of oil or hydrocarbon fuels | Black printing ink, paints, shoe polish, and reinforcing rubber in car tyres |\n\nWhen any form of carbon burns in a plentiful supply of oxygen, it forms **carbon dioxide (CO₂)**:\n\nC + O₂ → CO₂\n\nWhen oxygen is limited, however, incomplete combustion produces deadly **carbon monoxide (CO)**—which is why braziers and charcoal grills must never be used indoors.",
            "bodyHinglish": "Carbon ke kuch roop ki koi lambi, regular banawat nahi hoti: unhe **amorphous** kehte hain. Unme carbon ke chhote-chhote tukde (graphite jaise bahut chhote tukde) beqaayda jude hote hain.\n\n| Roop | Kaise banta hai | Upyog |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Koyla (coal)** | Zameen ke neeche dabe paudhe lakhon saalon mein (lagbhag) | eendhan, bijli ghar |\n| **Coke** | Koyle ko hawa ke bina garam karne se: kadak, shuddh carbon | blast furnace (Chapter 3), eendhan |\n| **Charcoal** | Lakdi ko kam hawa mein garam karne se | eendhan, paani aur gas ke rang-gandh ko sokhne wala (activated charcoal) |\n| **Lamp-black (soot)** | Diya ya tel ke adhoore jalne ka kaala dhuan | ink, paint, shoe polish, tyre |\n\nKoyla poora shuddh carbon nahi hota (isme aur cheezein bhi hoti hain), coke aur charcoal zyada shuddh hote hain. Jab inhe oxygen ki kaafi hawa mein jalate hain, to saari carbon **CO₂** ban jaati hai:\n\nC + O₂ → CO₂\n\nAur oxygen kam ho to **CO** (zehreeli gas): isiliye Suraksha ka box."
          },
          {
            "heading": "Part E: The Proof: All One Element",
            "headingHinglish": "Part E: Saboot: ek hi tatva",
            "body": "How did chemists prove beyond doubt that colourless diamond, black graphite, and charcoal are all made of the **exact same element**? By **burning equal masses of pure samples in oxygen**! In the 1770s, **Antoine Lavoisier** used a giant magnifying lens to focus sunlight onto a diamond inside a sealed jar of oxygen; the diamond vanished and produced **only carbon dioxide gas**. Subsequent experiments showed that burning **equal masses of pure diamond, pure graphite, and pure charcoal** in excess oxygen yields **only CO₂, and in the exact same mass**. If they contained different elements, they would produce different oxides or different masses of CO₂.\n\n```\n   Teacher demo (for reading only): burning a pure carbon sample in a stream of oxygen\n\n   oxygen     hard-glass combustion tube (open at both ends)    lime water\n   cylinder    :   sample                heated zone           (Ca(OH)₂ solution)\n    [  ]----->   =====[ carbon ]=======>=====\\\n    [  ]                 ^                     \\\n                  strong burner flame           \\__ delivery tube dips into\n                                                    lime water\n                                              +-------------------+\n                                              :  ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~ :\n                                              :   (turns milky)   :\n                                              +-------------------+\n```\n\n> **Figure 1.7** – Teacher demo showing that carbon burns in oxygen to form CO₂ (for reading only): dry oxygen flows through an open hard-glass combustion tube where the carbon sample is heated strongly. The exiting gas bubbles through lime water, turning it milky and proving CO₂ was formed. Before turning off the burner, the delivery tube is lifted out of the lime water to prevent suck-back.\n\n### Example 5. Equal masses of diamond and graphite give equal masses of CO₂\n\n**Problem:** A 0.12 g sample of pure diamond (0.6 carat; 1 carat = 0.2 g) and a 0.12 g sample of pure graphite are burned separately in excess oxygen (C + O₂ → CO₂). Calculate the mass and STP volume of CO₂ formed, and the mass of oxygen consumed in each case. (C = 12, O = 16; molar volume = 22.4 L at STP.)\n\n**Step 1:** Moles of carbon = 0.12 ÷ 12 = **0.01 mol** (identical for both samples, since both are pure carbon).\n**Step 2:** From the 1 : 1 : 1 mole ratio, CO₂ = 0.01 mol × 44 = **0.44 g** (volume = 0.01 × 22.4 = **0.224 L** = 224 mL), and O₂ consumed = 0.01 × 32 = **0.32 g** for each sample.\n\n**Check:** Atom count: C 1 = 1, O 2 = 2 ✅. Mass balance: 0.12 + 0.32 = 0.44 g ✅. **Both give the exact same 0.44 g of CO₂**—conclusive proof that diamond and graphite are allotropes of the same element! Furthermore, under extreme temperature and pressure, graphite can even be converted into synthetic diamond in the lab.",
            "bodyHinglish": "Ab Gayatri ke sawaal ka jawab: kaise pata chale ki heera aur graphite dono carbon hi hain? Saboot **jalane se** milta hai. 1770 ke dashak mein **Lavoisier** ne badi lens se dhoop ko heere par kendrit karke use band bartan ki hawa mein jalaya (heera gaayab ho gaya), aur baad mein vaigyanikon ne ye bhi dekha ki **shuddh heera, shuddh graphite aur shuddh charcoal** teeno **sirf CO₂ gas** dete hain, aur jab **mass barabar** ho to **CO₂ ki mass bhi barabar** hoti hai. Agar teeno alag tatva hote, to alag-alag product milte.\n\n```\n   Teacher demo (sirf padhne ke liye): nal se oxygen ke saath\n                                          garam karne par CO₂\n\n   oxygen    kaanch ki nali (dono sire khule)             chuna ka paani\n   cylinder   :   namoona              garam nali              (lime water)\n    [  ]----->   =====[ carbon ]=======>=====\\\n    [  ]                 ^                     \\\n                  teacher ki garam lau          \\__ delivery tube ka sira\n                                                    chuna paani mein dooba\n                                              +-------------------+\n                                              :  ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~ :\n                                              :   (doodhiya hota)  :\n                                              +-------------------+\n```\n\n> **Figure 1.7** – Carbon ke jalne ka teacher demo (sirf padhne ke liye): oxygen khuli kaanch ki nali mein jaati hai, jahan teacher ek garam lau se namoone ko garam karta hai. Nikli gas delivery tube se chuna paani (lime water) mein jaati hai, jo doodhiya ho jaata hai: CO₂ ki pehchaan. System band nahi hai: ek sira oxygen ke liye khula, doosra paani ke andar. Heating rokne se pehle delivery tube ko chuna paani se bahar nikaal lete hain taaki paani peeche na chadh aaye (suck-back).\n\n### Example 5. Heere aur graphite, barabar mass, barabar CO₂\n\n**Sawaal:** 0.12 g shuddh heera (lagbhag 0.6 carat; 1 carat = 0.2 g) aur 0.12 g shuddh graphite alag-alag jalaye gaye (C + O₂ → CO₂). Har ek se kitna CO₂ (mass aur STP par volume) aur kitni oxygen chahiye? (C 12, O 16; molar volume 22.4 L.)\n\n**Kadam 1:** carbon = 0.12 ÷ 12 = **0.01 mol** (dono mein, kyunki dono sirf carbon).\n**Kadam 2:** ratio 1 : 1 : 1. CO₂ = 0.01 mol × 44 = **0.44 g**; volume = 0.01 × 22.4 = **0.224 L** (224 mL). Oxygen = 0.01 × 32 = **0.32 g**.\n\n**Jaanch:** atoms: C 1 = 1, O 2 = 2 ✅. Mass: 0.12 + 0.32 = 0.44 g ✅. **Dono ke liye ek hi jawab**: yahi saboot hai ki dono ek hi tatva hain.\n\nAur ek aur baat: bahut zyada pressure aur garmi par graphite ko heere mein badla bhi ja sakta hai (kuch lab mein banaye heere aise bante hain). **Tatva ek hi, atomon ka jaal badal gaya.**"
          },
          {
            "heading": "Try It Yourself: The Pencil-Lead LED Tester",
            "headingHinglish": "Khud karke dekho: pencil ka LED tester",
            "body": "> **At home with an adult present.** Use **only two 1.5 V AA battery cells** (3 V total)—never touch wall sockets or mains electricity under any circumstances.\n\n**Safety:** Use the same low-voltage LED tester from Chapter 4: two AA cells in a holder, an LED, and a **220 ohm resistor connected in series** (to protect the LED). Ask an **adult to sharpen a wooden pencil at both ends**, and keep the pointed tips away from your face.\n\n**Materials:** 2 AA cells in a battery holder, 1 LED, 1 resistor (220 ohm), connecting wires, a wooden pencil sharpened at both ends, a plastic ruler, a stainless-steel spoon, and a rubber eraser.\n\n```\n         LED           220 ohm resistor\n    +----|>|----+---[\\/\\/\\/\\/]---+\n    :                            :\n   (+)                           :\n  2 AA cells                     :\n   (-)                           :\n    :                            :\n    +------+              +------+\n           :              :\n           B              A        <- test leads touched to both graphite tips\n           v              v\n         <|=================|>     <- pencil sharpened at both ends (wood in the middle)\n```\n\n> **Figure 1.8** – Pencil-lead conductivity tester: the circuit loop with two AA cells, a 220 ohm resistor, and an LED is completed by touching test leads A and B to the exposed **graphite tips at both ends** of the pencil.\n\n**Procedure:** (1) Touch leads A and B to the **two graphite tips** of the pencil and watch the LED. (2) Touch both leads to the **wooden barrel** of the pencil. (3) Test the plastic ruler, steel spoon, and rubber eraser the same way, and record which materials light the LED.\n\n**What you will observe:** Touching the two graphite tips **lights the LED** (completing the circuit through the graphite core from end to end!), whereas touching the wooden barrel, plastic ruler, or rubber eraser leaves the LED dark. That glow is direct proof of the delocalised fourth valence electrons moving along graphite's layers (Figure 1.5)!",
            "bodyHinglish": "> **Ghar par adult ke saath** (koi bada paas mein rahe). Sirf 2 AA cells (kul 3 V), ghar ki bijli (mains), socket ya plug ka kabhi, kisi bhi roop mein, kaam nahi.\n\n**Safety:** tester wahi hai jo Chapter 4 mein banaya tha: 2 AA cells, ek chhoti LED aur **uske saath series mein 220 ohm ka resistor** (resistor LED ko bachata hai). Pencil ko **adult dono taraf se chheele**, aur nok se aankh-chehra door rakho. Kuch chakhna nahi; cells ko kholna ya jalana nahi.\n\n**Samaan:** 2 AA cells (holder ke saath), LED, 220 ohm resistor, do taar, ek lakdi wali pencil (dono taraf chheeli hui), ek plastic ka ruler, ek steel ka chamach, ek rubber (eraser).\n\n```\n         LED           resistor 220 ohm\n    +----|>|----+---[\\/\\/\\/\\/]---+\n    :                            :\n   (+)                           :\n  2 AA cells                     :\n   (-)                           :\n    :                            :\n    +------+              +------+\n           :              :\n           B              A        <- taar ke sire, jo pencil ki nok par lagte hain\n           v              v\n         <|=================|>     <- pencil: dono taraf graphite nok khuli, beech mein lakdi\n```\n\n> **Figure 1.8** – Pencil ka tester: LED, 220 ohm resistor, 2 AA cells aur taar ka loop A aur B par khula hai. A aur B ko pencil ki **dono graphite nok** se chhuao; pencil ka graphite loop poora karta hai.\n\n**Tareeka:** (1) taar ke sire pencil ki **dono nok** par lagao: LED ko dekho. (2) taar ko pencil ke **lakdi wale hisse** par ek hi taraf lagao: LED dekho. (3) ye hi ruler, steel ke chamach aur rubber par karo: kaun LED jalata hai? Natija likho.\n\n**Kya dekhoge:** nok-se-nok jodne par LED **jalegi** (kabhi halki, kyunki graphite taar jaisa achha conductor nahi), lakdi, ruler aur rubber se nahi, steel ke chamach se jalegi. Matlab pencil ke andar ka graphite bijli chalata hai: ye wahi chauthi valency ka electron hai jo Figure 1.5 mein ghoom raha tha. (Heera ka tukda hota to LED nahi jalti: par ghar mein wo nahi karte.)\n\nAur do aur safe activities: **balls aur sticks (ya building blocks) se heere ke 4-4 jod ka aur graphite ke 3-3 jod ka model**, aur **kaagaz par Figure 1.3 jaisi chain aur ring banana, valency ginte hue**."
          },
          {
            "heading": "Common Pitfalls to Avoid",
            "headingHinglish": "Aam galtiyan (bachke rehna)",
            "body": "**Mistake: \"Diamond and graphite must be different elements because one is a clear, super-hard crystal and the other is a soft, black conductor.\"**\nIt is easy to assume such radically different materials must be made of different elements. However, an element is defined by the **atomic number (type of atoms)** it contains, not by its outward appearance. Three lines of proof settle it (for reading only):\n\n1. **Identical combustion product and mass:** 0.12 g of pure diamond and 0.12 g of pure graphite burned in oxygen produce **only CO₂**, and **0.44 g of CO₂** in both cases (Example 5).\n2. **Same atoms:** 12 g of diamond and 12 g of graphite each contain exactly **1 mole of carbon atoms** (Z = 6).\n3. **Interconversion:** subjecting graphite to extreme heat and pressure rearranges its 3-neighbour sheets into diamond's 4-neighbour 3D network without adding or removing any other element.\n\n| Question | Diamond | Graphite |\n|---|---|---|\n| Which element is it made of? | Pure carbon (Z = 6) | Pure carbon (Z = 6) |\n| Product of complete combustion | CO₂ only | CO₂ only |\n| Neighbours bonded to each C atom | 4 (3D network) | 3 (flat hexagonal layers) |\n| Conducts electricity? | No (no free electrons) | Yes (1 delocalised electron per C) |\n\n**Rule to remember:** **Allotropes are different structural arrangements of the exact same element.**",
            "bodyHinglish": "**Galti: \"Heera aur graphite alag tatva hain, kyunki ek bina rang ka kadak aur doosra kaala narm.\"**\nYe galat sach jaisa lagta hai kyunki gun itne alag hain. Par tatva uske **atoms ki kism** se tay hota hai, shakal se nahi. Teen jaanch (sirf padhne ke liye):\n\n1. **Jalane par:** 0.12 g heera aur 0.12 g graphite, dono jale to **sirf CO₂**, aur dono se **0.44 g** (Example 5). Alag tatva hote to alag product aur alag mass aati.\n2. **Mass se atom ginti:** 12 g heera aur 12 g graphite dono mein **1 mol carbon atom** (12 ÷ 12 = 1). Atom ek hi kism ke, bas jude alag tareeke se.\n3. **Ek se doosra:** pressure aur garmi se graphite ko heere mein badla ja sakta hai. Agar alag tatva hote, to ek tatva dusre mein nahi badal sakta tha.\n\n| Sawaal | Heera | Graphite |\n|---|---|---|\n| Kis tatva ka bana | carbon | carbon |\n| Jalane par product | CO₂ | CO₂ |\n| Har C ke padosi | 4 | 3 |\n| Bijli | nahi | haan |\n\n**Niyam:** **allotrope = ek hi tatva, atomon ke jude hone ka alag tareeka.** Gun alag hone se tatva alag nahi hota."
          },
          {
            "heading": "Quick Recap",
            "headingHinglish": "Quick Recap",
            "body": "- Carbon has electronic configuration **2, 4** (4 valence electrons, **tetravalent**); because forming C⁴⁺ or C⁴⁻ ions requires too much energy, carbon bonds by sharing electrons (**covalent bonding**). The C–H bond is virtually non-polar (electronegativities 2.5 and 2.1).\n- Small atomic size, strong C–C bonds, and a valency of 4 give carbon unrivalled **catenation**—forming **straight chains, branched chains, and rings**, along with single, double, and triple bonds and bonds to H, O, N, Cl, and Br, yielding **millions of organic compounds**.\n- **Organic compounds** are carbon compounds (mostly covalent, combustible, low-melting), though CO, CO₂, and carbonates are classed as inorganic.\n- **Crystalline allotropes:** **diamond** (each C bonded to 4 C in a 3D giant network; hardest natural substance, insulator, used in jewellery and cutting tools), **graphite** (each C bonded to 3 C in flat hexagonal layers with 1 free electron; soft, slippery conductor, used in pencils, electrodes, and lubricants), and **fullerene C₆₀** (60-carbon soccer-ball molecule of 12 pentagons and 20 hexagons).\n- **Amorphous forms:** coal, coke, charcoal, and lamp-black.\n- Burning equal masses of pure diamond and graphite gives the exact same mass of CO₂ (C + O₂ → CO₂), proving both are pure carbon. Burning carbon in limited air forms deadly **CO**—never burn charcoal indoors.\n- The 3 V pencil-lead LED test shows that graphite conducts electricity.",
            "bodyHinglish": "- Carbon ka arrangement **2, 4**: 4 valence electron, **tetravalent**; electron dena ya lena bahut mushkil, isliye wo **covalent** bond banata hai. C–H lagbhag non-polar (2.5 aur 2.1).\n- Chhota size, mazboot C–C bond aur chaar valency se **catenation**: **chain, shakha aur ring**. Saath mein multiple bond aur H, O, N, Cl, Br ke jod: isliye lakhon (ek karod se zyada) compound.\n- **Organic** compound = carbon wale (aksar covalent, jalne wale, kam melting point); CO, CO₂, carbonate organic nahi gine jaate.\n- **Allotrope**: **heera** (har C ke 4 padosi, sabse kadak, non-conductor, jewellery aur cutting), **graphite** (har C ke 3 padosi, parat wala, conductor, pencil, electrode, lubricant), **C₆₀** (60 carbon, football; 12 pentagon + 20 hexagon). Graphene aur nanotube: ek line mein.\n- **Amorphous**: koyla, coke, charcoal, lamp-black.\n- Jalne par C + O₂ → CO₂; heera aur graphite barabar mass par barabar CO₂ dete hain: **saboot ki dono ek hi tatva**. Kam oxygen mein CO: zehreeli, angithi khule mein.\n- Pencil tester (2 AA, 220 ohm, adult ke saath): graphite bijli chalata hai.\n- **Galti:** heera aur graphite alag tatva nahi, ek hi tatva ke allotrope hain."
          },
          {
            "heading": "Practice Questions",
            "headingHinglish": "Practice Questions",
            "body": "(All questions are for written calculation. Atomic masses: C = 12, O = 16. Molar volume = 22.4 L at STP. Approximate densities: diamond = 3.5 g/cm³, graphite = 2.2 g/cm³.)\n\n1. A carbon atom has electron arrangement 2, 4. Explain in 3 to 4 lines why carbon does not form C⁴⁺ or C⁴⁻ ions easily and so forms covalent bonds.\n2. In graphite, each carbon atom is joined to 3 other carbon atoms. How many valencies of each atom are left over after counting one bond to each neighbour? What does this leftover valency do for the properties of graphite?\n3. 0.6 g of lamp-black (assume it is pure carbon) is burnt completely: C + O₂ → CO₂. Find the mass of CO₂ and its volume at STP.\n4. 0.3 g of diamond and 0.3 g of graphite are burnt separately in excess oxygen. Find the mass of CO₂ from each. What does this show about the two substances?\n5. A sample of C₆₀ has a mass of 14.4 g. Find the number of moles of C₆₀, the number of moles of carbon atoms, and the mass of CO₂ obtained on burning it completely (C₆₀ + 60O₂ → 60CO₂).\n6. A 0.7 g piece of diamond and a 0.7 g piece of graphite are compared. Find the volume of each and say which one has its atoms packed closer together, and by what factor (ratio).\n7. A sample of coke is 90 percent carbon by mass (the rest is non-combustible ash). Find the mass of CO₂ formed when 5.0 g of this coke is burnt completely.\n8. In C₆₀, each carbon is joined to 3 other carbons and has no hydrogen. (a) How many carbon-carbon links are there in total? (b) Every carbon needs one more unit of valency: how many double bonds are there? (c) How many single bonds?\n9. State True or False and correct the false ones: (a) Diamond is a good conductor of electricity. (b) The \"lead\" of a pencil is graphite and clay, not the metal lead. (c) Charcoal, coke, and lamp-black are different elements. (d) C₆₀ has 60 carbon atoms arranged like a football. (e) Graphite and diamond give different products when burnt in excess oxygen.\n10. Why is graphite used as a lubricant, while diamond is used on tools that cut glass? Use the arrangement of atoms in each.\n11. A student connects a 2 AA cell LED tester (with the 220 ohm resistor, adult nearby) with two wires touching a pencil: the LED glows when the wires touch the two sharpened ends but not when both wires touch the wooden part. Explain. Would a diamond piece in the circuit light the LED?\n12. A black solid X, when burnt in oxygen, gives only a gas that turns lime water milky. 0.24 g of X gives 0.88 g of this gas. Show whether X could be pure carbon, and say whether this test can tell if X is graphite, charcoal, or lamp-black.\n13. Why is a closed room unsafe for a burning charcoal brazier? Name the gas, and give two reasons why it is dangerous.\n14. Why is lamp-black called amorphous but diamond crystalline? Give one use of lamp-black.\n\n### Answers\n\n1. Forming C⁴⁺ requires removing 4 electrons against the strong pull of 6 protons in a small atom, which takes far too much energy. Forming C⁴⁻ requires 6 protons to hold 10 mutually repelling electrons, which is also energetically unfavourable. Therefore, carbon **shares** its 4 valence electrons to form **covalent bonds**.\n2. Each carbon atom has a valency of 4; forming one bond to each of its 3 neighbours uses 3 valencies, leaving **1 valency (1 valence electron) left over**. Because this fourth electron is delocalised and free to move along the sheet, graphite is a **good conductor of electricity**.\n3. Moles of C = 0.6 ÷ 12 = **0.05 mol**. From the 1 : 1 ratio, CO₂ = 0.05 mol × 44 = **2.2 g**; volume at STP = 0.05 × 22.4 = **1.12 L**. Check: C 1 = 1, O 2 = 2 ✅; O₂ used = 0.05 × 32 = 1.6 g, and 0.6 + 1.6 = 2.2 g ✅.\n4. In both cases, moles of C = 0.3 ÷ 12 = **0.025 mol**, producing 0.025 × 44 = **1.1 g of CO₂** (and nothing else). Producing the exact same mass of CO₂ proves both are pure carbon—**allotropes of the same element**. Check: 0.3 + (0.025 × 32 = 0.8) = 1.1 g ✅.\n5. Molar mass of C₆₀ = 60 × 12 = **720** g/mol. Moles of C₆₀ = 14.4 ÷ 720 = **0.02 mol**. Moles of C atoms = 0.02 × 60 = **1.2 mol**. Mass of CO₂ = 1.2 mol × 44 = **52.8 g**. Check: C 60 = 60, O 120 = 120 ✅; mass: 14.4 + O₂ (1.2 × 32 = 38.4) = 52.8 g ✅.\n6. Volume of diamond = 0.7 ÷ 3.5 = **0.20 cm³**. Volume of graphite = 0.7 ÷ 2.2 = **0.318 ≈ 0.32 cm³**. **Diamond** has its atoms packed closer together, by a factor of 3.5 ÷ 2.2 ≈ **1.59 times**. Check: 3.5 × 0.20 = 0.7 g ✅.\n7. Mass of pure carbon = 5.0 × 0.90 = **4.5 g**. Moles of C = 4.5 ÷ 12 = **0.375 mol**. Mass of CO₂ = 0.375 × 44 = **16.5 g**. Check: 0.375 × 44 = 16.5 g ✅.\n8. (a) Each of the 60 carbons connects to 3 neighbours: 60 × 3 ÷ 2 = **90 carbon-carbon links** (since each link is shared by 2 atoms). (b) Each carbon needs 1 more valency unit, and each double bond supplies 1 extra unit to 2 carbons: 60 ÷ 2 = **30 double bonds**. (c) Single bonds = 90 − 30 = **60 single bonds**. Check: each carbon has 1 double bond (2) + 2 single bonds (2) = valency 4 ✅.\n9. (a) **False** ❌: diamond is a non-conductor because all 4 valence electrons are locked in covalent bonds. (b) **True** ✅: pencil lead is a mixture of graphite and clay, containing zero metallic lead (Pb). (c) **False** ❌: all three are amorphous forms of the same element, carbon. (d) **True** ✅. (e) **False** ❌: both give only CO₂ when burned in excess oxygen.\n10. In graphite, flat sheets of carbon atoms are held together only by weak forces between layers, allowing the layers to slide easily over one another as a lubricant. In diamond, every carbon atom is locked to 4 neighbours in a rigid 3D covalent network, making it hard enough to cut glass.\n11. The pencil core is made of graphite, which has mobile delocalised electrons that conduct electricity and complete the circuit. Wood is an electrical insulator, so the circuit stays open. A diamond piece would **not** light the LED because all its valence electrons are localized in C–C single bonds.\n12. Moles of CO₂ = 0.88 ÷ 44 = **0.02 mol**, which contains 0.02 × 12 = **0.24 g of carbon**—matching the entire 0.24 g mass of X. Thus **X is pure carbon**. However, combustion alone cannot distinguish whether X is graphite, charcoal, or lamp-black, since equal masses of any pure carbon form give the same mass of CO₂.\n13. In a closed room, oxygen is depleted and incomplete combustion of carbon produces **carbon monoxide (CO)**. It is dangerous because (1) it is colourless and odourless, giving no warning, and (2) it binds to haemoglobin in the blood, blocking oxygen transport and causing fatal poisoning.\n14. Lamp-black lacks a regular long-range repeating atomic arrangement (amorphous), whereas diamond has a regular repeating 3D lattice of atoms (crystalline). Use of lamp-black: black printing ink, pigments, shoe polish, or tyre rubber reinforcement.\n\n---\n\nChloe wrote one concluding sentence at the bottom of her notebook page: \"Charcoal, pencil graphite, and diamond: one element, three completely different architectures.\" Mr. Miller smiled. \"Exactly right. And carbon's four hands don't stop at pure carbon—they link up with other atoms to build millions of compounds.\"\n\n**What's next?** With millions of carbon compounds in existence, how do chemists keep track of them without memorising millions of separate facts? In Topic 2, we discover how organic compounds are organised into **families (homologous series)** and how a tiny **functional group** dictates a molecule's chemical personality.",
            "bodyHinglish": "(Sab sawaal paper par hal karne ke liye hain. Mass: C 12, O 16. Molar volume 22.4 L at STP. Heera 3.5 g per cm³, graphite 2.2 g per cm³ lagbhag.)\n\n1. A carbon atom has electron arrangement 2, 4. Explain in 3 to 4 lines why carbon does not form C⁴⁺ or C⁴⁻ ions easily and so forms covalent bonds.\n2. In graphite, each carbon atom is joined to 3 other carbon atoms. How many valencies of each atom are left over after counting one bond to each neighbour? What does this leftover valency do for the properties of graphite?\n3. 0.6 g of lamp-black (assume it is pure carbon) is burnt completely: C + O₂ → CO₂. Find the mass of CO₂ and its volume at STP.\n4. 0.3 g of diamond and 0.3 g of graphite are burnt separately in excess oxygen. Find the mass of CO₂ from each. What does this show about the two substances?\n5. A sample of C₆₀ has a mass of 14.4 g. Find the number of moles of C₆₀, the number of moles of carbon atoms, and the mass of CO₂ obtained on burning it completely (C₆₀ + 60O₂ → 60CO₂).\n6. A 0.7 g piece of diamond and a 0.7 g piece of graphite are compared. Find the volume of each and say which one has its atoms packed closer together, and by what factor (ratio).\n7. A sample of coke is 90 percent carbon by mass (the rest does not burn to CO₂). Find the mass of CO₂ formed when 5.0 g of this coke is burnt completely.\n8. In C₆₀, each carbon is joined to 3 other carbons and has no hydrogen. (a) How many carbon-carbon links are there in total? (b) Every carbon needs one more unit of valency: how many double bonds are there? (c) How many single bonds?\n9. State True or False and correct the false ones: (a) Diamond is a good conductor of electricity. (b) The \"lead\" of a pencil is graphite and clay, not the metal lead. (c) Charcoal, coke and lamp-black are different elements. (d) C₆₀ has 60 carbon atoms arranged like a football. (e) Graphite and diamond give different products when burnt.\n10. Why is graphite used as a lubricant, while diamond is used on tools that cut glass? Use the arrangement of atoms in each.\n11. A student connects a 2 AA cell LED tester (with the 220 ohm resistor, adult nearby) with two wires touching a pencil: the LED glows when the wires touch the two sharpened ends but not when both wires touch the wooden part. Explain. Would a diamond piece in the circuit light the LED?\n12. A black solid X, when burnt in oxygen, gives only a gas that turns lime water milky. 0.24 g of X gives 0.88 g of this gas. Show whether X could be pure carbon, and say whether this test can tell if X is graphite, charcoal or lamp-black.\n13. Why is a closed room unsafe for a burning charcoal angithi? Name the gas, and give two reasons why it is dangerous.\n14. Why is lamp-black called amorphous but diamond crystalline? Give one use of lamp-black.\n\n### Answers\n\n1. C⁴⁺ banane ke liye 4 electron nikalne padenge: nucleus mein sirf 6 proton hain jo bahari electron ko kas ke pakadte hain, to bahut zyada energy lagti hai. C⁴⁻ banane ke liye 4 electron lene padenge: 6 proton ko 10 electron sambhalne padenge aur electron aapas mein dhakelte hain, ye bhi bahut mushkil. Isliye carbon apne 4 electron **share** karta hai aur **covalent** bond banata hai.\n2. Har carbon ki valency 4 hai, aur 3 padosi ke saath ek-ek bond ginne se 3 hi ho pati hai: **1 valency bachti hai**. Ye chauthi valency wala electron ek jagah nahi bandha, parat mein ghoom sakta hai, isliye graphite **bijli ka conductor** hai.\n3. C = 0.6 ÷ 12 = **0.05 mol**. Ratio 1 : 1: CO₂ = 0.05 mol × 44 = **2.2 g**; volume = 0.05 × 22.4 = **1.12 L**. Jaanch: C 1 = 1, O 2 = 2 ✅; oxygen = 0.05 × 32 = 1.6 g, 0.6 + 1.6 = 2.2 g ✅.\n4. Dono mein carbon = 0.3 ÷ 12 = **0.025 mol**. CO₂ = 0.025 × 44 = **1.1 g** (dono se). **Dono ke liye barabar**: matlab dono sirf carbon hain, yani ek hi tatva (allotrope). Jaanch: 0.3 + (0.025 × 32 = 0.8) = 1.1 g ✅.\n5. C₆₀ ka molar mass = 60 × 12 = **720** g/mol. Moles = 14.4 ÷ 720 = **0.02 mol**. Carbon atom = 0.02 × 60 = **1.2 mol**. CO₂ = 1.2 mol × 44 = **52.8 g**. Jaanch: C 60 = 60, O 120 = 120 ✅; mass: 14.4 + oxygen (1.2 × 32 = 38.4) = 52.8 g ✅.\n6. Heera: 0.7 ÷ 3.5 = **0.2 cm³**. Graphite: 0.7 ÷ 2.2 = **0.318 ≈ 0.32 cm³**. **Heere** mein atom zyada paas-paas hain: ratio graphite ÷ heera = 3.5 ÷ 2.2 = **lagbhag 1.6 guna**. Jaanch: 3.5 × 0.2 = 0.7 ✅.\n7. Carbon = 5.0 × 0.9 = **4.5 g**. Moles = 4.5 ÷ 12 = **0.375 mol**. CO₂ = 0.375 × 44 = **16.5 g**. Jaanch: 0.375 × 44 = 16.5 ✅ (baaki 0.5 g carbon-bina hissa CO₂ nahi banata).\n8. (a) Har carbon ke 3 padosi: 60 × 3 ÷ 2 = **90 links** (har link do atomon ne gina). (b) Har carbon ki ek valency baaki: ek double bond mein 2 carbon ki ek-ek valency lagti hai, to double bond = 60 ÷ 2 = **30**. (c) Single bond = 90 − 30 = **60**. Jaanch: har carbon: 1 double (2) + 2 single (2) = 4 ✅.\n9. (a) **False** ❌: heera bijli nahi chalata, har electron bond mein bandha hai. (b) **True** ✅: graphite aur chikni mitti; lead (Pb) hai hi nahi. (c) **False** ❌: teeno carbon ke hi roop hain, ek hi tatva. (d) **True** ✅. (e) **False** ❌: dono sirf CO₂ dete hain.\n10. Graphite mein parat ke beech kamzor jod hai, isliye parat phisalti hai: lubricant. Heere mein har C ke 4 mazboot bond poore jaal mein hain, isliye sabse kadak: glass kaat deta hai.\n11. Pencil ki nok graphite hai jo bijli chalata hai (chauthi valency ka electron ghoomta hai), isliye circuit poora hua. Lakdi ek kuchalak hai: circuit khula rahta hai. Heera ke saare 4 electron bond mein bandhe hain, to **LED nahi jalti**.\n12. CO₂ = 0.88 ÷ 44 = **0.02 mol**. Isme carbon = 0.02 × 12 = **0.24 g**, jo X ke mass 0.24 g ke barabar: X **shuddh carbon ho sakta hai** (poora X carbon). Par ye test sirf bata sakta hai ki X **carbon** hai; X graphite, charcoal ya lamp-black mein se kaun hai, ye nahi, kyunki teeno barabar mass par barabar CO₂ dete hain.\n13. Band kamre mein oxygen kam padti hai, to carbon se **carbon monoxide (CO)** banti hai. Khatra: (1) bina rang ki aur bina gandh ki hai, pata nahi chalta; (2) khoon mein oxygen ki jagah le leti hai aur jaan le sakti hai.\n14. Lamp-black mein atomon ka koi regular lambi duri tak pattern nahi (amorphous); heere mein atomon ka regular doharaya jaal (crystalline). Lamp-black ka upyog: ink, paint, shoe polish ya tyre (koi ek).\n\n---\n\nGayatri ne copy ke akhir mein ek line likhi: \"Koyla, pencil aur heera: ek tatva, tin kahaniyaan.\" Mr. Dhaygude ne use angithi ke paas se hataya aur kaha: \"Bilkul. Ek tatva, bas atomon ke jude hone ka tareeka alag. Aur ye carbon sirf in teeno tak nahi: uske haath chaar hain, aur wo sab jagah jud jaata hai.\"\n\n**Aage kya?** Topic 2 mein dekhenge ki carbon ke lakhon compounds ko **parivaar (homologous series)** mein kaise baandha jaata hai, aur wo **functional group** kya hota hai jo kisi compound ka swabhaav tay karta hai."
          }
