            "explanation": "Pitfall to avoid (\"Ethanol has an '–OH' group in its formula, so it must be a basic metal hydroxide like NaOH.\"): *Correction:* In sodium hydroxide ($\\text{Na}^+\\text{OH}^-$), the compound is ionic, dissociating in water to release free hydroxide ions ($\\text{OH}^-$), which turn red litmus blue. In ethanol ($\\text{C}_2\\text{H}_5\\text{OH}$), the oxygen and hydrogen atoms are linked to carbon by strong covalent bonds. Ethanol does not dissociate into hydroxide ions in water! - It has no effect on red or blue litmus paper (it is strictly neutral). - An aqueous solution of ethanol does not conduct electricity (tester bulb does not glow). | Test | Sodium Hydroxide (NaOH) | Ethanol (C₂H₅OH) | |---|---|---| | Bonding Type | Ionic lattice ($\\text{Na}^+$ and $\\text{OH}^-$ ions) | Covalent molecular compound | | Ions Formed in Water | Releases free aqueous $\\text{OH}^-$ ions | Zero ions; dissolves as intact neutral molecules | | Litmus Action | Basic: turns red litmus blue | Neutral: no colour change on either litmus | | Conductivity | Strong electrolyte (bulb shines brightly) | Non-electrolyte (bulb remains dark) |",
