            "explanation": "Pitfall to avoid (\"Glacial ethanoic acid is a strong acid because it burns skin and tastes intensely sour.\"): *Correction:* Never confuse concentration with acid strength! - Glacial ethanoic acid is extremely concentrated (>99% pure acid, virtually zero water). It causes painful skin burns through hygroscopic dehydration and protein irritation. - However, acid strength is strictly defined by the degree of ionisation in water ($\\alpha$). Even when diluted in water, ethanoic acid ionises only ~1.3% into $\\text{H}^+$ ions. A 0.1 M solution of ethanoic acid has a pH of ~2.9, whereas a 0.1 M solution of strong HCl has a pH of 1.0. Ethanoic acid is fundamentally a weak acid, regardless of whether it is diluted as vinegar or concentrated as glacial acid.",
