            "heading": "The Organic Chemistry Relay Race",
            "headingHinglish": "Organic Relay ka khel",
            "body": "On a sunny Friday afternoon, Mr. Foster visited the school athletic grounds to watch his nephew Lucas compete in the annual Science Olympiad \"Organic Relay Race.\" On the grass, five large chalk circles were drawn: **ALKENE, ALKANE, ALCOHOL, CARBOXYLIC ACID**, and **ESTER**, connected by bold directional chalk arrows. Each competing team held a shuffled deck of reaction flashcards: `+H₂, Ni catalyst`, `conc. H₂SO₄, ~170 °C`, `acidified K₂Cr₂O₇ or KMnO₄`, and `+ ethanol, conc. H₂SO₄, gentle heat`. The rules were straightforward: a runner picked a card from the deck, sprinted across the field, and laid the card on the **correct directional arrow**. A correct placement scored +1 point; an incorrect placement incurred a 1-point penalty.\n\nLucas's team stumbled in the opening round, losing two quick points. Lucas had sprinted across and placed the `conc. H₂SO₄` card on the arrow between ACID and ESTER, but that specific card belonged on the arrow between ALCOHOL and ALKENE. After the race, Lucas walked over to his uncle, visibly crestfallen. \n\n\"Uncle, I memorised all the chemical reagents,\" Lucas admitted. \"I just got confused because concentrated sulfuric acid appeared on **two completely different cards**, and I didn't know which one went where!\"\n\nMr. Foster smiled reassuringly. \"Lucas, rote memorisation of isolated flashcards will always leave you vulnerable. What you need is a **reaction roadmap**. Every directional arrow on that field must answer three critical questions:\n1. **Which two chemical families** are being connected?\n2. **What fundamental change occurred**: did atoms add across a bond, was an atom substituted, was water eliminated, or was oxygen gained?\n3. **What specific reagent and operating condition** governs that transformation? Concentrated sulfuric acid acts as a powerful **dehydrating agent** at 170 °C to turn an alcohol into an alkene, but it acts as a mild **acid catalyst** when reacting an acid with an alcohol to form an ester. Same chemical name, completely different chemical roles! Let us construct the master roadmap together.\"",
            "bodyHinglish": "Mr. Mahajan apne bhatije Vikrant ke school ki \"Organic Relay\" game day dekhne gaye. Ground par chalk se paanch bade gol bane the: **ALKENE, ALKANE, ALCOHOL, ACID** aur **ESTER**, aur unke beech teer ke nishaan khinche the. Har team ke paas reaction cards ka ek dher tha: \"+H₂, Ni\", \"gaadha H₂SO₄, lagbhag 170 °C\", \"K₂Cr₂O₇ ya KMnO₄\", \"+ ethanol, gaadha H₂SO₄\". Khel ka niyam seedha tha: team ka runner ek card uthakar daudta hai aur use **sahi teer** par rakhta hai. Sahi card par ek point, galat card par ek point kat.\n\nVikrant ki team ne shuruaat mein hi do point gawa diye. Wajah ye thi ki Vikrant ne \"gaadha H₂SO₄\" likha card ACID aur ESTER ke beech wale teer par rakh diya, jabki us card ki jagah ALCOHOL aur ALKENE ke beech wale teer par thi. Khel ke baad wo munh latkaye chacha ke paas aaya. \"Chacha, mujhe saare card yaad the. Bas ye samajh nahi aaya ki gaadha H₂SO₄ to **do jagah** aata hai, to kaun sa card kahan jaaye.\"\n\nMr. Mahajan muskuraye. \"Vikrant, cards ko ratne se kaam nahi chalta, ek **naksha** (map) chahiye. Har teer teen sawaal ka jawab deta hai: ek, **kaun se do compound** jud rahe hain? Do, **kya badla**: kuch juda, kuch badla gaya, paani nikla ya oxygen mila? Teen, **kaun sa reagent aur kaun si condition**? Gaadha H₂SO₄ ek jagah paani chheenne ka kaam karta hai aur doosri jagah sirf catalyst ka. Naam ek, kaam alag. Chalo, pehle naksha banate hain.\""
